ALSFRS-R questionnaire at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform ALSAQ-40 at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform 12. -lead ECG at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform ROADS at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform PGI-C at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform PGI-S at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Perform CGI-C at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At Visits after Week 52, collect vital signs including weight. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Perform SVC at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- At Visits after Week 52, collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- At Visits after Week 52, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Perform 12. -lead ECG at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- For early termination or consent withdrawal during ATE before Week 52, conduct all ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit assessments at the ET Visit. (Participants terminating early or withdrawing consent during ATE before Week 52) [regimen_g:6.10.13]\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C assessments are not required. (Early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.13]\n- Hand participants the ALSAQ-40 questionnaire and ask them to write their answers themselves. [regimen_g:7.1]\n- For ALSAQ-40, caregivers or study staff may help only if needed, transferring participant responses to the form without helping determine answers. (Participants needing help completing ALSAQ-40) [regimen_g:7.1]\n- Hand participants the CNS-BFS questionnaire and ask them to write their answers themselves. [regimen_g:7.2]\n- For CNS-BFS, caregivers or study staff may help only if needed, transferring participant responses to the form without helping determine answers. (Participants needing help completing CNS-BFS) [regimen_g:7.2]\n- Hand participants the ROADS questionnaire and ask them to write their answers themselves. [regimen_g:7.3]\n- For ROADS, caregivers or study staff may help only if needed, transferring participant responses to the form without helping determine answers. (Participants needing help completing ROADS) [regimen_g:7.3]\n- Site staff must record vital status for each randomized participant throughout placebo-controlled follow-up, generally through the Week 24 Visit as indicated in the SOA. (Each randomized participant during placebo-controlled follow-up) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- Site staff must record vital status for each randomized participant at the conclusion of ATE participation. (Each randomized participant concluding ATE participation) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- Vital status records must include dates of death and initiation of PAV, or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free. (Each randomized participant) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- Site staff must complete a second vital status check for each randomized participant approximately at the placebo-controlled portion''s last participant, last visit (LPLV). (Each randomized participant) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status. (All randomized participants) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, securely provide the vendor demographic information, such as participant name, date of birth, and last known address, for vital status ascertainment. (All randomized participants) [regimen_g:7.7]\n- Regimen G CSF biofluid collection is optional; collect at Baseline and Week 24 rather than Baseline and Week 16 in the Master Protocol. (Regimen G optional CSF collection) [regimen_g:8]\n- Azoospermia qualifies only if the man is the woman''s sole sexual partner and absence of sperm has been confirmed. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Site personnel may document a male participant''s azoospermia through review of medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. (Male participants) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Monitor participants taking permitted sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates for signs of loss of efficacy. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s094]\n- Monitor participants taking permitted BCRP or OAT3 substrates for signs of AEs noted in the respective medication''s prescribing information. (Participants taking BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s095]\n- Screening maps to Days -42 to -1 and includes ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, and Baseline Symptoms. (Screening) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- Baseline maps to Day 0 and includes Pre-dose Labs, ECG, Baseline PROs (ALSFRS-R, ROADS, ALSAQ-40). (Baseline) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- Week X (Placebo-Controlled) maps to Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 and includes Drug Administration, AEs, ConMeds, PK/Biomarkers, Safety Labs; Wks 2, 12, 20 are phone visits. (Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- If an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure is needed, add an Unscheduled Visit folder and do not alter scheduled visit dates. (out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure) [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders]\n- Patient Reported Outcomes (ALSFRS-R, ALSAQ-40, CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-S, PGI-C) must be transcribed exactly as completed; do not interpolate missing responses; if a patient skips a question, mark the field as ND to clear the system query. (patients completing PROs) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- Do not type ND or N/A into a numeric field; use the ND or UNK checkbox to override the numeric requirement and resolve the system query. (numeric fields with missing lab values) [site_edc_guide:8]\n- Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): collect at Screening (-42 to -1) only. (Screening (-42 to -1)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): collect at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), and Wk 24 / ET (Day 168). (Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): collect at Screening (-42 to -1) and Wk 16 (Day 112). (Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): collect at Baseline (Day 0) and Wk 24 / ET (Day 168). (Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Pharmacokinetics (PK Plasma) (Central): collect at Wk 4 (Day 28) Pre, Post; Wk 8 (Day 56) Post; Wk 16 (Day 112) Post; Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) Pre, Post. (Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): collect at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), and Wk 24 / ET (Day 168). (Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): collect at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), and Wk 24 / ET (Day 168). (Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- DNA Collection (Optional) (Central): collect at Baseline (Day 0) only. (Baseline (Day 0)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- CSF Collection (Optional) (Central): collect at Baseline (Day 0) X (Bio) and Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) X (Bio/PK). (Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): collect at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), and ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76). (ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): collect at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), and ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76). (ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): collect at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), and ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76). (ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Collect ACTH and Cortisol blood samples at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit. (each scheduled visit) [site_lab_manual:3]\n- Screening, Days -42 to -1: use KIT #1 for Clinical Safety & Pregnancy and Coagulation. (Screening, Days -42 to -1) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Baseline, Day 0: use KIT #2 for Clinical Safety & Endocrinology, Biomarker Blood, Optional Genomics / DNA, and Optional CSF. (Baseline, Day 0) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Week 4 & 24, Pre & Post Dose PK: use KIT #3; Week 24 includes additional tubes for Endocrinology, Biomarkers, and Optional CSF. (Week 4 & 24, Pre & Post Dose PK) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Week 8 & 16, Post-Dose PK: use KIT #4; Week 16 includes Coagulation and Biomarkers. (Week 8 & 16, Post-Dose PK) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- ATE Weeks (Standard Clinic Visits): use KIT #5 for Clinical Safety; selected ATE weeks include Biomarker Blood/Urine components. (ATE Weeks (Standard Clinic Visits)) [site_lab_manual:4]','\n',char(10)),'Assessments and procedures','["assessments"]',4006,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:concomitant_medications','brief','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)','',replace('- Participants must either not take riluzole or have a stable riluzole dose for ≥ 30 days before the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Participants must either not take edaravone or have completed at least one cycle (typically 14 days) of edaravone before the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Exclude investigational ALS treatments, off-label or active trial participation, within 5 half-lives (if known) or 30 days (whichever is longer) before Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.2]\n- Refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for the current list of experimental therapies when checking investigational ALS treatment exclusions. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude exposure at any time to gene therapies under investigation for ALS, whether off-label or investigational. [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking riluzole at study entry must have a stable dose for 30 days before Master Protocol Screening Visit and intend the same stable dosage throughout study. (Participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking edaravone at study entry must complete at least one cycle of treatment (typically 14 days) before Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking edaravone at study entry must intend to continue the same stable dosage throughout the study. (Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- SI-directed discontinuation or dose adjustment of riluzole or edaravone for medical reasons is not a protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2]\n- Missed riluzole or edaravone doses from unintended, non-medical extenuating circumstances, such as scheduling or drug availability, are not protocol deviations. [master:5.2.2]\n- Record stopping riluzole or edaravone for any non-medical reason, including participant preference, as a minor protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2]\n- Record initiation of riluzole or edaravone at any point during participation as a minor protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2]\n- Investigational product may need discontinuation if the SI or designee has concerns about prohibited medication use or safety, including missing safety data. [master:6.5.1]\n- At minimum, encourage collection of ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures after investigational product discontinuation. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Throughout the study, participants may receive concomitant medications deemed necessary for adequate care. (Throughout the study) [master:7.5]\n- Participants should not receive other investigational products during the study, including marketed agents at experimental dosages being tested for ALS. (Throughout the study) [master:7.5]\n- Record all concomitant medications and significant non-drug therapies, including supplements, on the appropriate source document and electronic Case Report Form (eCRF). [master:7.5]\n- Remote substituted visits should include ALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable. (Planned in-clinic visits conducted remotely) [master:8]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, review concomitant medications. (Regimen Specific Screening Visit) [master:8.2]\n- At the Baseline Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [master:8.7]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 28 Telephone Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 28 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9-60]\n- At the Week 32 Telephone Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 32 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9-61]\n- At the Week 36 Visit, review and document concomitant medications. (Week 36 Visit) [master:8.10-62]\n- Obtain participant demographics, past medical history including ALS, and concomitant medication usage. [master:9.1]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at Master Protocol Screening, Regimen Specific Screening, Baseline, Week 2, Week 4, and Week 8. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, and Week 24 or ET Visit. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at ATE Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- DNL343 may be coadministered with strong, moderate, or weak inhibitors of CYP3A4. [regimen_g:s017]\n- Moderate CYP3A4 inducers may be coadministered freely with DNL343 in this trial. [regimen_g:s017]\n- Exclude participants with epilepsy currently requiring antiepileptic therapy or seizure of any cause within 6 months of randomization. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude strong CYP3A4/5 inducer use within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose, or anticipated use during study treatment. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- The strong CYP3A4/5 inducer exclusion covers prescription and over-the-counter (OTC) medications, including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude prescription or OTC sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude participants anticipating sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrate use with a narrow therapeutic index during the study treatment period. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude prescription or OTC BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude participants anticipating BCRP or OAT3 substrate use with a narrow therapeutic index during the study treatment period. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; monitor participants taking them for signs of loss of efficacy. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index may require dose increase according to prescribing information and investigator and/or medical monitor discretion. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- The medical monitor may assess whether sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates have a narrow therapeutic index for an individual participant. (Participants taking these substrates) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- For all excluded therapies in Section 5.9, participants may be rescreened after the defined window has passed. (Participants excluded for prohibited therapies) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- Review Concomitant Medications at the Week 2 Telephone Visit. (Week 2 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.2]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, review Concomitant Medications. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.4]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.6]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.4]\n- At the Week 16 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.6]\n- At the Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.7]\n- At the Week 28 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 40 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At Visits after Week 52, review concomitant medications. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Implantable progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- Combined (estrogen- and progestogen-containing) hormonal contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation—oral, intravaginal, transdermal, injectable—must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- Progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation—Oral, injectable—must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- Excluded medications are prohibited during the study unless otherwise specified; see Section 5.9 for prohibited medications. (During the study) [regimen_g:s092]\n- Excluded medication lists are not exhaustive; any concomitant medication use is at the discretion of the investigator who knows the participant''s medical history. [regimen_g:s092]\n- Strong CYP3A4/5 inducers are excluded during the study, including drugs not listed in Table 4. (During the study) [regimen_g:s093]\n- For information on other strong CYP3A4/5 inducers, use of a tertiary source such as the Drug Interactions Flockhart Table is recommended if needed. [regimen_g:s093]\n- apalutamide: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking apalutamide) [regimen_g:s093]\n- carbamazepine: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking carbamazepine) [regimen_g:s093]\n- phenytoin: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking phenytoin) [regimen_g:s093]\n- rifampin: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking rifampin) [regimen_g:s093]\n- St. John’s wort: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking St. John’s wort) [regimen_g:s093]\n- bosentan: recommended washout is 2 weeks. (Participants taking bosentan) [regimen_g:s093]\n- Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index should be excluded during the study. (During the study) [regimen_g:s094]\n- Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s094]\n- Monitor participants taking permitted sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates for signs of loss of efficacy. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s094]\n- Permitted sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates may require dose increases based on the respective medication''s prescribing information. (Participants taking sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s094]\n- The medical monitor may be consulted about whether a Table 5 medication has a narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant. (Participants taking Table 5 medications) [regimen_g:s094]\n- buspirone: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking buspirone) [regimen_g:s094]\n- darunavir: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking darunavir) [regimen_g:s094]\n- dronedarone: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking dronedarone) [regimen_g:s094]\n- eplerenone: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking eplerenone) [regimen_g:s094]\n- felodipine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking felodipine) [regimen_g:s094]\n- indinavir: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking indinavir) [regimen_g:s094]\n- lurasidone: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking lurasidone) [regimen_g:s094]\n- maraviroc: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking maraviroc) [regimen_g:s094]\n- nisoldipine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking nisoldipine) [regimen_g:s094]\n- quetiapine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking quetiapine) [regimen_g:s094]\n- Quinine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking Quinine) [regimen_g:s094]\n- saquinavir: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking saquinavir) [regimen_g:s094]\n- sildenafil (when used for PAH): recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking sildenafil (when used for PAH)) [regimen_g:s094]\n- sirolimus: recommended washout is 13 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking sirolimus) [regimen_g:s094]\n- tacrolimus: recommended washout is 10 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking tacrolimus) [regimen_g:s094]\n- ticagrelor: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking ticagrelor) [regimen_g:s094]\n- tipranavir: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking tipranavir) [regimen_g:s094]\n- BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index should be excluded during the study. (During the study) [regimen_g:s095]\n- BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted. (Participants taking BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s095]\n- Monitor participants taking permitted BCRP or OAT3 substrates for signs of AEs noted in the respective medication''s prescribing information. (Participants taking BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a wide therapeutic index) [regimen_g:s095]\n- The medical monitor may be consulted about whether a Table 6 medication has a narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant. (Participants taking Table 6 medications) [regimen_g:s095]\n- rosuvastatin: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking rosuvastatin) [regimen_g:s095]\n- sulfasalazine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking sulfasalazine) [regimen_g:s095]\n- adefovir: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking adefovir) [regimen_g:s095]\n- cefaclor: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking cefaclor) [regimen_g:s095]\n- ceftizoxime: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking ceftizoxime) [regimen_g:s095]\n- famotidine: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking famotidine) [regimen_g:s095]\n- furosemide: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking furosemide) [regimen_g:s095]\n- methotrexate: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking methotrexate) [regimen_g:s095]\n- oseltamivir carboxylate: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking oseltamivir carboxylate) [regimen_g:s095]\n- penicillin G: recommended washout is 7 days if considered to have a narrow therapeutic index. (Participants taking penicillin G) [regimen_g:s095]\n- Week X (Placebo-Controlled) maps to Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 and includes Drug Administration, AEs, ConMeds, PK/Biomarkers, Safety Labs; Wks 2, 12, 20 are phone visits. (Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- Every Concomitant Medication log entry requires an Indication that logically maps to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. (every Concomitant Medication log entry) [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions]\n- Do not enter strong CYP3A4/5 inducers or sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates, including St. John''s wort, midazolam, itraconazole; flagged entries trigger a hard warning query to the CRA and Medical Monitor. (prohibited medications) [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions]\n- If day is unknown for concomitant medications or medical history, enter UNK in the day field; if month and day are unknown, enter UNK-UNK-YYYY; leave the date blank only if the entire date, including year, is unknown. (partial start dates for concomitant medications or medical history) [site_edc_guide:8]','\n',char(10)),'Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)','["concomitant_medications"]',4001,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:consent','brief','','Informed consent','',replace('- At the Master Protocol Screening Visit, obtain consent using the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF), then screen for Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:protocol-summary]\n- After regimen assignment, obtain regimen-specific consent and assess regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if applicable. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Obtain sIRB approval of the protocol, any amendments, and participant informed consent before study initiation. (Before study initiation) [master:1.2]\n- At the first visit, provide Master Protocol information before any study-related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care, such as vital signs or weight. (First visit) [master:1.3]\n- Explain the Master Protocol''s nature and purpose, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits before consent. (Master Protocol consent) [master:1.3]\n- Give participants adequate time to ask questions and become familiar with the Master Protocol before consent. (Master Protocol consent) [master:1.3]\n- Document Master Protocol informed consent and give participants a copy of the fully executed Master Protocol consent form. (Master Protocol consent) [master:1.3]\n- After regimen assignment, explain the assigned intervention and regimen-specific participation/termination conditions, risks and benefits. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:1.3]\n- Obtain written regimen-specific consent and give participants a copy of the fully executed regimen-specific consent form. (Regimen-specific consent) [master:1.3]\n- Master Protocol amendments may require written informed re-consent from already enrolled participants for continued participation. (Participants already enrolled) [master:1.4.1]\n- Adding regimens does not require re-consent. (Participants already enrolled) [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol participants must be capable of providing informed consent and complying with study procedures, in the SI''s opinion. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Exclude unstable psychiatric disease, cognitive impairment, dementia or substance abuse impairing ability to provide informed consent, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2]\n- Recruit participants from selected ALS centers in the US only after sIRB approval. (Participating sites) [master:6.1]\n- Obtain written informed consent before study procedures or assessments, except clinic visit standard of care, after explaining objectives, endpoints, methods, anticipated benefits, and potential hazards. [master:6.2]\n- Document willingness to participate on an sIRB-approved consent form signed and dated by the participant. [master:6.2]\n- The SI must retain original consent forms and give the participant a copy. [master:6.2]\n- Inform participants they may refuse trial entry or withdraw at any time without prejudice to future treatment. [master:6.2]\n- Consent must be obtained by the site investigator or other licensed staff trained on the study and delegated to obtain consent. (Staff obtaining consent) [master:6.2]\n- The SI or other licensed staff must explain the study, answer questions with the coordinator, and ensure the consent process is carried out correctly. (Staff obtaining consent) [master:6.2]\n- Offer every participant discussion with a licensed physician Investigator; document acceptance or refusal in the research file. [master:6.2]\n- Consent staff must provide adequate information, facilitate understanding, and allow ample time and opportunity for questions about the trial. (Staff obtaining consent) [master:6.2]\n- The SI and site staff must not coerce or unduly influence participation or continued participation. [master:6.2]\n- Site staff must continuously provide information as the clinical investigation progresses or as the participant or situation requires. [master:6.2]\n- Obtain Master Protocol consent first, then consent for the applicable assigned RSA. [master:6.2]\n- Do not consent participants for any regimen if they are ineligible for the Master Protocol. (Participants ineligible for the Master Protocol) [master:6.2]\n- Non-English speakers may consent using an sIRB-approved form translated into their spoken language; follow study MOPs and local translator policy. (Non-English speakers) [master:6.2]\n- If new procedures or information require Master Protocol or regimen reconsent, obtain it during the participant''s next visit. (Participants requiring reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- If the next visit is remote, reconsent may be completed remotely using the specified remote consent steps. (Participants requiring reconsent at a remote visit) [master:6.2]\n- For remote reconsent, site staff must provide the participant a copy of the informed consent form. (Remote reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- For remote reconsent, the participant must read the consent form without signing it first. (Remote reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- For remote reconsent, the Site Investigator or approved, delegated study staff must contact the participant and review the form by phone or telemedicine. (Remote reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- After remote consent review, the participant must sign the informed consent form and return the original signed form to the site. (Remote reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- After receiving the remote reconsent form, the person who consented the participant by phone or telemedicine must sign it. (Remote reconsent) [master:6.2]\n- Participants may discontinue investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1]\n- Participants may withdraw consent for study participation without providing justification. [master:6.5.2]\n- Obtain assigned-regimen informed consent at the scheduled Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:8.1]\n- Re-screening after the initial screening window requires a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. (Participants re-screened after the initial screening window) [master:8.1.2]\n- If regimen ineligibility is known at regimen assignment, the participant may be administratively screen-failed before regimen consent and screening. (Participants known to be ineligible for the assigned regimen) [master:8.1.2]\n- Discontinued randomized participants require written Master Protocol informed re-consent and continued Master Protocol eligibility before re-assignment to a different regimen, if available. (Randomized participants whose regimen participation was discontinued) [master:8.1.2]\n- At the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, obtain written regimen-specific informed consent from the participant. (Regimen Specific Screening Visit) [master:8.2]\n- Participants may decline biofluid research at any time by withdrawing consent for sample use; already-used samples cannot be destroyed. (Participants providing biofluid samples) [master:9.4]\n- Collect blood for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit from participants consenting to DNA collection. (Participants consenting to DNA collection) [master:9.3.1]\n- The SI must discuss all potential LP risks, including local injection-site pain, anesthetic reactions, needle entrance-site bleeding and infection, and post-LP low-pressure headache. (Participants undergoing LP) [master:9.3.4]\n- Monitor for adverse events from signing consent for the Master Protocol until completion of participation as defined in the RSA. (Master Protocol consent through completion of participation) [master:10.1.1]\n- With the participant''s permission, medical information may be given to their personal physician or other appropriate medical personnel responsible for their welfare. [master:12.6.1]\n- The SI must retain study-conduct and investigational-drug distribution records, including CRFs, consent forms, laboratory test results, and medical inventory records, for two years after marketing application approval. (SI) [master:12.6.2]\n- If no application is filed, retain study records for seven years after investigation discontinuation and notification of the US FDA and applicable national and local health authorities. (SI; if no application is filed) [master:12.6.2]\n- Obtain consent using the Platform Trial ICF during Master Protocol Screening Visit. [regimen_g:s010]\n- After random assignment to a regimen, obtain second consent using the regimen-specific ICF at Regimen Specific Screening. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Ask participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during ATE to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants withdrawing consent or terminating early during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- If new procedures or information require regimen reconsent, obtain reconsent at the participant’s next in-person visit. (Participants needing reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- If the next in-clinic visit is conducted remotely, regimen reconsent may also be completed remotely using the required consent procedure. (Participants needing reconsent at a remote visit) [regimen_g:6]\n- For remote reconsent, site staff must send the participant a copy of the informed consent form. (Remote reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- For remote reconsent, the participant must read the consent form but not sign before the consent review. (Remote reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- For remote reconsent, the Site Investigator or staff approved and delegated to obtain consent must contact the participant and review the informed consent form. (Remote reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- After remote consent review, the participant must sign the informed consent form and return the original signed form to the site. (Remote reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- Once the returned remote consent form is received, the individual who consented the participant must sign it. (Remote reconsent) [regimen_g:6]\n- Perform ET visit CSF collection only if the participant consented to lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and ET occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. (ET visit CSF collection) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Ask participants withdrawing consent or terminating early without completing the protocol to attend an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants withdrawing consent or terminating early without completing the protocol) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- For early termination or consent withdrawal during the placebo-controlled portion, conduct all Week 24 in-clinic visit assessments at the ET Visit. (Early termination or consent withdrawal during the placebo-controlled portion) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- For early termination or consent withdrawal during ATE before Week 52, conduct all ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit assessments at the ET Visit. (Participants terminating early or withdrawing consent during ATE before Week 52) [regimen_g:6.10.13]\n- Screening maps to Days -42 to -1 and includes ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, and Baseline Symptoms. (Screening) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- If a subject is re-consented, record it on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. (if a subject is re-consented) [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility]\n- Only subjects who signed the Master and Regimen-Specific Informed Consent Forms may be registered. (screening) [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- Enter the Date of Informed Consent and the subject''s Year of Birth, then review the summary and click Confirm. (screening) [site_irt_guide:3.1]','\n',char(10)),'Informed consent','["consent"]',4009,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:contraception_pregnancy','brief','','Contraception and pregnancy','',replace('- Perform serum pregnancy testing in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Women of child-bearing potential) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Repeat pregnancy testing during the study only as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy. (Women of child-bearing potential) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Exclude females who are breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, or planning pregnancy during the study. (Female participants) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude females of child-bearing potential unwilling to use effective contraception throughout the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. (Females of child-bearing potential) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude males of reproductive capacity unwilling to use effective contraception throughout the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. (Males of reproductive capacity) [master:5.2.2]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP). (Women of child bearing potential (WOCBP) at Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for WOCBP, if applicable. (WOCBP at Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for WOCBP, if applicable. (WOCBP at Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for WOCBP, if applicable. (WOCBP at Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for WOCBP, if applicable. (WOCBP at Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 36 Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for WOCBP, if applicable. (WOCBP at Week 36 Visit) [master:8.10-62]\n- Collect Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP only at Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary throughout the study. (WOCBP) [master:9.1.2]\n- The SI may order additional local testing or central laboratory retesting to assess an AE, suspected pregnancy, or an unobtainable result. (Participants needing additional safety laboratory testing) [master:9.1.2]\n- Classify an AE resulting in congenital anomaly or birth defect in a participant''s offspring as an SAE, whether the participant is male or female. [master:10.1.2]\n- Record Key Study Events, including Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy, in participant records. [master:10.2.3]\n- Enter AE and Key Study Event information into EDC as soon as possible after learning of an event or receiving an existing event update. [master:10.2.3]\n- Perform serum pregnancy testing at Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. (Participants of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform urine pregnancy testing at every in clinic visit. (Women of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Review contraception method at Regimen Specific Screening, Baseline, Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, and Week 20. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Review contraception method at Week 24 or ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- For contraception reviews, document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Review contraception method at ATE Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Review contraception method at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit, all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Apply RSA-specific contraceptive requirements in Section 5.10.2 when assessing Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Obtain verbal confirmation of participants’ contraceptive methods at Screening and each in clinic visit throughout the double-blind and ATE periods. [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Document contraceptive method(s) in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Female participants must meet the postmenopausal, surgical sterilization, or applicable childbearing-potential contraception requirements throughout the trial. (Female participants) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Postmenopausal eligibility requires no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without another medical cause and follicle-stimulating hormone > 40 IU/L or mIU/mL at screening. (Female participants qualifying as postmenopausal) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Surgical sterilization must be at least 6 months before dosing; examples include hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, and bilateral oophorectomy. (Female participants qualifying through surgical sterilization) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Participants of childbearing potential and their male partners must use highly effective contraception from dosing start, throughout study, and for 90 days after final study drug administration. (Participants of childbearing potential using the contraception option) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- For participants of childbearing potential, contraception requires two forms: a male barrier such as a latex or polyurethane condom and a highly effective method defined in Appendix III. (Participants of childbearing potential using the contraception option) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- For participants of childbearing potential, listed highly effective methods are intrauterine device, intrauterine hormone-releasing system, bilateral tubal ligation, or abstinence. (Participants of childbearing potential using the contraception option) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Abstinence requires no heterosexual intercourse during the entire study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug; periodic abstinence is unacceptable. (Participants using abstinence) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- For participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner, the partner must use a male barrier such as a latex or polyurethane condom. (Participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Azoospermia qualifies as highly effective contraception only if the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential. (Participants relying on partner azoospermia) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Male participant azoospermia documentation may come from site review of medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. (Azoospermic male participants) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Male participants having sex with a female partner of childbearing potential must use two birth-control forms: male barrier and partner’s highly effective method defined in Appendix III. (Male participants with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Male participant and female partner contraception is required from dosing start, throughout study, and for 90 days after final study drug administration. (Male participants with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Alternatively, azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential must use a male barrier such as a latex or polyurethane condom. (Azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Male participants must not donate sperm from dosing start, throughout study, and for 90 days after final study drug administration. (Male participants) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Perform Urine Pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential during the study. (Women of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:6]\n- Review contraceptive methods being used by the participant and partner during the study. [regimen_g:6]\n- Perform urine pregnancy testing at the Baseline Visit for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- At the Baseline Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- Review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner at the Week 2 Telephone Visit. (Week 2 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.2]\n- Perform urine pregnancy testing at Week 4 and 8 Visits for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- Review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner at Week 4 and 8 Visits. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.4]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 16 Visit) [regimen_g:6.5]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 16 Visit) [regimen_g:6.5]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.6]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Follow-Up Safety Call, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. [regimen_g:6.8]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.4]\n- At the Week 16 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 16 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.6]\n- At the Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.7]\n- At the Week 28 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 28 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 40 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- At the Week 40 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At Visits after Week 52, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- At Visits after Week 52, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Intrauterine device (IUD) must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Intrauterine hormone-releasing system (IUS) must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Bilateral tubal ligation must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- An azoospermic male partner is an acceptable contraceptive method when used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Female participants of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Azoospermia qualifies only if the man is the woman''s sole sexual partner and absence of sperm has been confirmed. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Use an additional highly effective contraceptive method if azoospermia is unconfirmed or the man is not the woman''s sole sexual partner. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Abstinence is an alternative contraceptive method only when heterosexual intercourse is avoided throughout the study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Male condom and female condom must not be used together. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Contraceptive use must follow local regulations for participants in clinical studies. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Essure fallopian tube coil placement is not an accepted contraceptive method. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Site personnel may document a male participant''s azoospermia through review of medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. (Male participants) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Periodic abstinence (calendar, symptothermal, post ovulation methods) is not an acceptable contraceptive method. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Withdrawal (coitus interruptus) is not an acceptable contraceptive method. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Spermicides only are not an acceptable contraceptive method. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Lactational amenorrhea method is not an acceptable contraceptive method. (Female participants of childbearing potential; male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s089]\n- Implantable progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- Combined (estrogen- and progestogen-containing) hormonal contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation—oral, intravaginal, transdermal, injectable—must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- Progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation—Oral, injectable—must be used with a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]\n- An azoospermic male participant must also use a male barrier method (e.g. condom). (Male participants with female partners of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s090]','\n',char(10)),'Contraception and pregnancy','["contraception_pregnancy"]',4004,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:dosing','brief','','Study drug dosing and dose changes','',replace('- Limit each regimen''s placebo-controlled treatment period to a maximum of 36-weeks. (Placebo-controlled period) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Use the respective RSAs for the duration of the Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period. (Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Administer the first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed. (Baseline Visit) [master:protocol-summary]\n- At phone visits, check study drug compliance to ensure dosing follows the dose regimen and note any report of missed doses. (Phone visits) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Keep participants, investigators and site staff blinded to active product or matching placebo assignment throughout the ATE period. (Participants, investigators and site staff) [master:4.1]\n- Participants must be able to swallow pills and liquids at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and, in the SI''s opinion, swallow for the study''s duration. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Investigational product may need discontinuation if the SI or designee has concerns about prohibited medication use or safety, including missing safety data. [master:6.5.1]\n- If a participant cannot swallow orally administered investigational product, use the RSA to determine whether alternative administration, such as via G-tube, is possible. (Participants unable to swallow oral investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Participants may discontinue investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1]\n- Under local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated staff must document investigational product amounts dispensed and/or administered, received from Central Pharmacy, and destroyed upon study completion. [master:7.1.3]\n- For investigational product non-compliance, remind the participant of the importance of taking investigational product per protocol. (Participants with non-compliance) [master:7.3]\n- Count visit windows as consecutive calendar days; calculate target visit dates from Baseline Visit - Day 0, the first day of investigational product administration. [master:8]\n- Remote substituted visits should include ALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable. (Planned in-clinic visits conducted remotely) [master:8]\n- At the Baseline Visit, administer the first dose of investigational product for the regimen per the RSA instructions. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- At the Baseline Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, perform Drug Compliance Check-In. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [master:8.7]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, perform Drug Compliance Check-In. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 28 Telephone Visit, perform Drug Compliance Check-In. (Week 28 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9-60]\n- At the Week 32 Telephone Visit, perform Drug Compliance Check-In. (Week 32 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9-61]\n- At the Week 36 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 36 Visit) [master:8.10-62]\n- Report investigational product compliance outside RSA limits as a protocol deviation; see the RSA for details. (Investigational product compliance outside RSA limits) [master:8.14]\n- For these positive C-SSRS responses, the SI must determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. (Participants with specified positive treatment-period C-SSRS responses) [master:9.1.6]\n- Study staff should document the time of the study drug dose administered. (Lumbar puncture) [master:9.3.4]\n- For a reported AE, probe for event type, onset and resolution dates, severity, seriousness, causality to investigational product and disease, investigational product action, and outcome. (Participants reporting an AE) [master:10.2.1]\n- Follow each applicable RSA''s timelines for reporting dosage changes. (Dosage changes) [master:10.3]\n- After completing a regimen, participants may be re-screened into the Master Protocol and assigned another regimen after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants who completed a regimen) [master:11.2.4]\n- The DNL343 dose during the placebo-controlled treatment period is 200 mg once daily (QD). (Regimen G participants assigned DNL343) [regimen_g:s004]\n- After completing the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all Regimen G participants enter the active treatment extension (ATE) and receive DNL343 200 mg QD. (Regimen G participants completing the placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s004]\n- Regimen G ATE treatment with DNL343 lasts up to an additional 78 weeks. (Regimen G ATE participants) [regimen_g:s004]\n- Regimen G ATE is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks, giving 76 weeks total treatment duration. (Regimen G ATE participants) [regimen_g:s007]\n- Continue Regimen G ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE. (Regimen G ATE) [regimen_g:s007]\n- Regimen G treatment must not exceed 102 weeks total from Baseline visit. (Regimen G participants) [regimen_g:s007]\n- Encourage participants stopping study drug early to remain in the study and follow the protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. (Regimen G participants stopping study drug early) [regimen_g:s008]\n- Placebo-controlled treatment must not exceed 24-week duration. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection at Week 4 (pre, post), Week 8 (post), Week 16 (post), and Week 24 or ET Visit (pre, post). (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Schedule Week 4 and Week 24 predose PK visits for the morning; participants should take their dose in clinic at approximately their usual morning time. (Participants having predose PK samples) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Collect predose PK samples before dosing, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose, and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. (Week 4 and Week 24) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Collect postdose PK samples anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:s010]\n- At Week 4 Visit, if predose PK collection is impossible, collect 2 postdose samples at least 1 hour apart. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Week 24 Visit is the first day of ATE; provide the ATE dose in clinic and obtain predose and postdose PK samples. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Obtain unscheduled PK samples as needed for TEAEs causing dose modification, reduction, or interruption, or severe TEAEs requiring in-clinic or unscheduled assessment. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Baseline CSF may be collected within one week before the first study medication dose, after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:s010]\n- CSF collection must occur before the first ATE study medication dose. (Participants continuing into ATE with baseline CSF collected) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug at Baseline, Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Administer the first study drug dose only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed. [regimen_g:s010]\n- At Week 24 or ET Visit, dispense study drug only if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/Compliance at Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, and Week 24 or ET Visit. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- At phone visits, do not perform drug accountability; check compliance with the dose regimen and note reports of missed doses. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform study drug compliance check-ins at Week 2, Week 12, and Week 20. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- ATE lasts a minimum of 52 weeks and maximum of 78 weeks per participant, ending when all active participants complete the 52-week Visit in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Obtain an unscheduled PK sample for a reported TEAE causing dose modification or interruption, or a severe TEAE requiring in-clinic evaluation. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug at ATE Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- At ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52, administer/dispense study drug only for participants returning for another visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform study drug compliance check-ins at ATE Week 2, Week 12, Week 20, and Week 24. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- DNL343 may be coadministered with strong, moderate, or weak inhibitors of CYP3A4. [regimen_g:s017]\n- Moderate CYP3A4 inducers may be coadministered freely with DNL343 in this trial. [regimen_g:s017]\n- Randomize Regimen G participants 3:1 active:placebo to oral DNL343 200 mg QD or placebo for 24 weeks of double-blinded treatment. (Placebo-controlled period) [regimen_g:3]\n- After double-blinded treatment, all participants enter ATE and receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks. (ATE period) [regimen_g:3]\n- DNL343 and placebo cartons contain 14 packets segmented into 7 doses containing 2 packets each; each packet contains DNL343 100 mg or placebo. [regimen_g:5.2]\n- Administer DNL343 or placebo reconstituted in water orally or through a feeding tube, or coadminister with soft food such as applesauce or yogurt. [regimen_g:5.4]\n- DNL343 or placebo may be administered through a gastrostomy tube [G-tube], jejunostomy tube [J-tube], or nasogastric tube. [regimen_g:5.4]\n- Participants may take study drug with fluid thickener as needed. [regimen_g:5.4]\n- Take study drug at approximately the same time each day in the morning, without regard to food. [regimen_g:5.4]\n- Complete all study drug dosing within 15 minutes. [regimen_g:5.4]\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, administer study drug in the clinic. (Clinic visits with predose PK assessments) [regimen_g:5.4]\n- Participants may take a missed study drug dose as soon as remembered in the same day. (Participants with a missed dose) [regimen_g:5.4]\n- If a missed study drug dose is remembered the day after, take only the next dose; take only one dose per day. (Participants with a missed dose) [regimen_g:5.4]\n- Maintain participants on randomized DNL343 or placebo treatment unless dose modification is needed for substantial intolerability. (Placebo-controlled period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- Substantial intolerability includes moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by investigator or medical monitor. (Participants considered for dose modification) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- For substantial intolerability, the site investigator, consulting the medical monitor, may interrupt study drug, temporarily reduce to one stick pack rather than two stick packs, or discontinue. (Placebo-controlled period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- Consider resuming study drug at the randomized dose within 28 days from dose reduction or dose interruption. (Placebo-controlled period dose modifications) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- Maintain the blind throughout the study drug dose modification procedure. (Placebo-controlled period dose modifications) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- ATE DNL343 200 mg QD applies even to participants who had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. (ATE period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- If starting ATE DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) may be necessary, the investigator must discuss with the medical monitor before ATE entry. (Participants needing reduced-dose ATE initiation) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- In ATE, the site investigator, consulting the medical monitor, may interrupt study drug, temporarily reduce to one stick pack rather than two stick packs, or discontinue. (ATE period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- After ATE dose modification, consider resuming DNL343 at the 200 mg dose level if possible. (ATE period dose modifications) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- For site dosing, participants must receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee under medical supervision. (Site dosing) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Record the date and time of each clinic study drug dose in source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). (Site dosing) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- At dosing, a site staff member other than the person administering study drug must confirm the dose and participant identification. (Site dosing) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Assess home study drug compliance at each study visit. (Participants self-administering study drug at home) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Instruct participants to bring all unused study drug packets to all clinic visits. (Participants self-administering study drug at home) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Study staff must count unused study drug packets to assess home administration compliance and record compliance. (Participants self-administering study drug at home) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Record deviations from the prescribed study drug dosage regimen. [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Report accidental or intentional DNL343 overdose to the Coordination Center whether or not it results in an AE/SAE. (DNL343 overdose) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- Report suspected DNL343 abuse/misuse to the Coordination Center whether or not it results in an AE/SAE. (Suspected DNL343 abuse/misuse) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- Report inadvertent or accidental DNL343 exposure to the Coordination Center whether or not it results in an AE/SAE. (Inadvertent or accidental DNL343 exposure) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- Report study drug medication errors to the Coordination Center, with or without participant DNL343 exposure and whether or not they result in an AE/SAE. (Study drug medication errors) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- Also report DNL343 overdose, abuse/misuse, accidental exposure, or study drug medication errors associated with an AE/SAE in the electronic data capture. (Safety events associated with an AE/SAE) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- For DNL343 overdose, study staff must monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. (DNL343 overdose) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- For DNL343 overdose, the Site Investigator must contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. (DNL343 overdose) [regimen_g:5.8]\n- Collect additional unscheduled PK samples as needed for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption, or severe TEAE requiring an unscheduled assessment visit. (Participants with these TEAEs) [regimen_g:6]\n- Baseline CSF may be collected separately, within one week prior to first study medication dose, after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. (Baseline CSF collection) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- Administer the first study drug dose in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed. (Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- At the Baseline Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- At the Week 2 Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in and remind participants of the importance of compliance. (Week 2 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.2]\n- At the Week 2 Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring any unused study drug to the next visit. (Week 2 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.2]\n- Schedule the Week 4 visit in the morning for pre/post dose PK collection. (Week 4 Visit) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, do not administer the daily study drug dose until study visit procedures are complete. (Week 4 Visit with clinic dosing) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, participants should take study drug at home at their usual dosing time rather than in clinic. (Week 4 Visit not occurring in the morning) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- If Week 4 dosing occurred before clinic arrival, collect Two post dose PK blood samples at least one hour apart, without a dose between them. (Week 4 Visit after home dosing) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- Record a minor protocol deviation when Week 4 home dosing requires two post dose PK collections instead of predose and postdose collections. (Week 4 Visit after home dosing) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, participants may take their daily study drug dose at home or in clinic, depending on visit time. (Week 8 Visit) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- Collect predose and postdose PK blood at Week 4; collect one postdose PK blood sample at Week 8. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- For predose PK collection visits, administer study drug at approximately the participant’s usual morning dosing time. (Visits with predose PK sample collection) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- Collect the predose PK sample before dosing, 0 to 2 hours before the dose and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. (Visits with predose PK sample collection) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- Collect the postdose PK sample anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At Week 4 and 8 Visits, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in and remind the participant of the importance of compliance. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.4]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, collect PK blood postdose only, anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. (Week 16 Visit) [regimen_g:6.5]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in and remind the participant of the importance of compliance. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.6]\n- On the Week 24 Visit day, participants must not take any placebo-controlled period dose; take the ATE dose in-clinic. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, do not take study drug until study visit procedures are complete. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Collect predose and postdose PK blood at the Week 24 Visit. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- For Week 24 predose PK collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately their usual morning time. (Participants continuing into ATE at the Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Collect the Week 24 predose PK sample 0 to 2 hours before the dose and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Collect the Week 24 postdose PK sample anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- For participants continuing into ATE, optional Week 24 CSF collection must occur before the first ATE dose. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- For participants discontinuing study drug but completing the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call are unnecessary. (Participants discontinuing study drug but completing the protocol) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- Collect additional unscheduled PK samples as needed when assessing TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption. (ATE participants with TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in. (Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.4]\n- Instruct participants to hold study drug on the Week 16 ATE Visit day until study visit procedures are complete. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 16 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in. (Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.6]\n- At the Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in. (Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.7]\n- Instruct participants to hold study drug on the Week 28 ATE Visit day until study visit procedures are complete. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 28 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 40 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- Instruct participants to hold study drug on the Week 52 ATE Visit day until study visit procedures are complete. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At the Week 52 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- At Visits after Week 52, check study drug compliance. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- Week X (Placebo-Controlled) maps to Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 and includes Drug Administration, AEs, ConMeds, PK/Biomarkers, Safety Labs; Wks 2, 12, 20 are phone visits. (Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- DNL343 (or placebo) is administered as oral granules, 200 mg QD via two 100 mg stick packs. (DNL343 (or placebo)) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- If the dose is adjusted, update the Planned Dose Level field, check Yes for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason, e.g., Adverse Event. (if the dose is adjusted) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format, e.g., 09:15. (all dosing visits) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing; post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose; violations require a protocol deviation explanation. (PK samples around dosing) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply. (baseline / Day 0) [site_irt_guide:3.2]\n- Enter the required Stratification variables and the Subject Weight (kg) before randomization. (baseline / Day 0) [site_irt_guide:3.2]\n- If the subject has no dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction, dispense the standard 200 mg QD supply. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- If the subject has a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction, the system logs 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, complete the double-blind period and transition to the Open-Label ATE period receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks. (Week 24 and ATE) [site_irt_guide:3.4]\n- DNL343 (Active) and Matching Placebo are granules for oral/enteral suspension; DNL343 is 100 mg per stick pack and Matching Placebo is 0 mg per stick pack. [site_pharmacy_manual:2]\n- Subjects consume 200 mg Once Daily (QD) by taking two (2) stick packs simultaneously each day. [site_pharmacy_manual:2]\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. (Depending on the visit schedule) [site_pharmacy_manual:5]\n- Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. [site_pharmacy_manual:5]\n- Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5]\n- Use two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. (Method A) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en]\n- Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. (Method A) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en]\n- Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended; granules may not dissolve completely and a suspension is expected. (Method A) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en]\n- If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. (Method A) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en]\n- Use two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt); do not mix with hot foods. (Method B) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a]\n- Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. (Method B) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a]\n- Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. (Method B) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a]\n- The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). (Method B) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a]\n- Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. (Method B) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a]\n- Provide the subject/caregiver with thorough training on the preparation methods (Water or Soft Food) and emphasize the 15-minute post-mixing administration window. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- For visits requiring in-clinic dosing (e.g., Baseline, Week 4, Week 24 PK sampling), ensure the subject is ready to receive the dose before opening the stick packs. (Baseline, Week 4, Week 24 PK sampling) [site_pharmacy_manual:6]\n- If administering via an enteral feeding tube, flush the administration cup/syringe with an additional 15–30 mL of water, draw it up, and flush the tube to ensure 100% of the dose is delivered and the tube remains patent. (If administering via an enteral feeding tube) [site_pharmacy_manual:6]\n- If administered orally via cup, add a small amount of water to the empty cup, swirl, and have the subject drink it to ensure no residual granules are left behind. (If administered orally via cup) [site_pharmacy_manual:6]','\n',char(10)),'Study drug dosing and dose changes','["dosing"]',4002,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:drug_supply','brief','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability','',replace('- Do not perform drug accountability at phone visits. (Phone visits) [master:protocol-summary]\n- At phone visits, check study drug compliance to ensure dosing follows the dose regimen and note any report of missed doses. (Phone visits) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Keep participants, investigators and site staff blinded to active product or matching placebo assignment throughout the ATE period. (Participants, investigators and site staff) [master:4.1]\n- Upon investigational product discontinuation, instruct participants to return unused investigational product, including empty packaging if applicable. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Upon study termination, instruct participants to return unused investigational product, including empty packaging material if applicable. (Participants terminating study participation) [master:6.5.2]\n- Investigational product packaging and labeling must follow Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) regulations. [master:7.1.2]\n- File samples of investigational product labels in the Trial Master File (TMF). [master:7.1.2]\n- Receive, handle, and store investigational product safely and properly at the site pharmacy or other designated location. [master:7.1.3]\n- Keep investigational product secure; restrict access to the trial pharmacist, designated pharmacy staff, SI, and clinical staff. [master:7.1.3]\n- Upon receipt, store investigational product according to label instructions. [master:7.1.3]\n- Adequately monitor investigational product storage conditions; control, monitor, and record storage-area temperature at a minimum daily. [master:7.1.3]\n- Under local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated staff must document investigational product amounts dispensed and/or administered, received from Central Pharmacy, and destroyed upon study completion. [master:7.1.3]\n- The SI must ensure investigational product accountability records are maintained throughout the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The research pharmacist or designated staff must maintain accurate investigational product shipment and dispensing records in a drug accountability log. [master:7.1.3]\n- Follow the MOP or site specific policies for investigational product destruction, responsible personnel, and retention of destruction documentation. [master:7.1.4]\n- Remote substituted visits should include ALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable. (Planned in-clinic visits conducted remotely) [master:8]\n- At the Baseline Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- At the Baseline Visit, instruct the participant to bring investigational product to the Week 4 visit. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 4 Visit, remind the participant to bring investigational product to the Week 8 Visit. (Week 4 Visit) [master:8.5]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 8 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 8 Visit) [master:8.6]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, remind the participant to bring investigational product to the Week 16 Visit. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [master:8.7]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 16 Visit) [master:8.8]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, remind the participant to bring investigational product to the Week 24 Visit. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, dispense investigational product to the participant per the RSA instructions. (Week 24 Visit) [master:8.10]\n- At the Week 32 Telephone Visit, remind the participant to bring investigational product to the Week 36 Visit. (Week 32 Telephone Visit) [master:8.9-61]\n- At the Week 36 Visit, perform investigational product accountability and compliance. (Week 36 Visit) [master:8.10-62]\n- SIs must allow monitors access to clinical supplies and dispensing and storage areas. (SI) [master:12.3]\n- The SI must retain study-conduct and investigational-drug distribution records, including CRFs, consent forms, laboratory test results, and medical inventory records, for two years after marketing application approval. (SI) [master:12.6.2]\n- If no application is filed, retain study records for seven years after investigation discontinuation and notification of the US FDA and applicable national and local health authorities. (SI; if no application is filed) [master:12.6.2]\n- Week 24 Visit is the first day of ATE; provide the ATE dose in clinic and obtain predose and postdose PK samples. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug at Baseline, Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- At Week 24 or ET Visit, dispense study drug only if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/Compliance at Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, and Week 24 or ET Visit. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- At phone visits, do not perform drug accountability; check compliance with the dose regimen and note reports of missed doses. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug at ATE Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- At ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52, administer/dispense study drug only for participants returning for another visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- DNL343 and placebo cartons contain 14 packets segmented into 7 doses containing 2 packets each; each packet contains DNL343 100 mg or placebo. [regimen_g:5.2]\n- Control all DNL343 and placebo shipments to clinical sites using an Integrated Response Technology solution. [regimen_g:5.2]\n- Store DNL343 and placebo at room temperature, below 30°C, in a secure, temperature-controlled area with limited access; follow batch-specific packaging instructions. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- Transport, receive, store, and handle study drug according to container or product labels, supplied site and pharmacy instructions, site standard operating procedures, and applicable country regulations. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- Maintain appropriate study drug storage temperature and transportation conditions from manufacture through delivery to each clinical site. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- On receipt, research pharmacy staff must transfer study drug to appropriate environmentally controlled storage and examine the shipment and temperature monitoring devices, if applicable. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- After inspection confirms acceptable condition, store study drug under its required physical conditions, secured with access limited to authorized research site staff. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- The investigator or designee must confirm appropriate transit temperatures for all received study drug; report and resolve discrepancies before use. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- Follow the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for additional study drug handling and storage instructions. [regimen_g:5.3]\n- For site dosing, participants must receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee under medical supervision. (Site dosing) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- At dosing, a site staff member other than the person administering study drug must confirm the dose and participant identification. (Site dosing) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Instruct participants to bring all unused study drug packets to all clinic visits. (Participants self-administering study drug at home) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Study staff must count unused study drug packets to assess home administration compliance and record compliance. (Participants self-administering study drug at home) [regimen_g:5.7]\n- Follow MOP details for remote CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S collection and study drug dispensing. (Remote visits) [regimen_g:s049]\n- Dispense study drug at the Baseline Visit. (Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- At the Baseline Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Baseline Visit) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- At the Week 2 Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring any unused study drug to the next visit. (Week 2 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.2]\n- Dispense study drug at Week 4 and 8 Visits. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At Week 4 and 8 Visits, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 4 and 8 Visits) [regimen_g:6.3]\n- At the Week 12 Telephone Visit, remind the participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit. (Week 12 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.4]\n- Dispense study drug at the Week 16 Visit. (Week 16 Visit) [regimen_g:6.5]\n- At the Week 16 Visit, remind the participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 16 Visit) [regimen_g:6.5]\n- At the Week 20 Telephone Visit, remind the participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit. (Week 20 Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.6]\n- Dispense study drug at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug containers, to the next visit. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.4]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 16 ATE Visit. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 16 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 16 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.5]\n- At the Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.6]\n- At the Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.7]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 28 ATE Visit. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- At the Week 28 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 28 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.8]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 40 ATE Visit. (Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- At the Week 40 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 40 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.9]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 52 ATE Visit. (Week 52 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.10]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at Visits after Week 52. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- At Visits after Week 52, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Visits after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.11]\n- DNL343 (or placebo) is administered as oral granules, 200 mg QD via two 100 mg stick packs. (DNL343 (or placebo)) [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]\n- Re-enter username and password to finalize Randomization, Dispensing, or Unblinding actions. (Suvoda critical transactions) [site_irt_guide:2]\n- Never dispense an IMP kit unless it has been explicitly assigned by Suvoda. (all dispensing) [site_irt_guide:2]\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply. (baseline / Day 0) [site_irt_guide:3.2]\n- Retrieve the exact assigned cartons from the pharmacy, and print the Dispensation Confirmation PDF for the subject binder. (baseline / Day 0) [site_irt_guide:3.2]\n- Use the current visit dropdown for Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24; Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- If the subject has no dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction, dispense the standard 200 mg QD supply. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- If the subject has a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction, the system logs 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- Authenticate with your password and dispense the newly assigned Kit Numbers. (Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24) [site_irt_guide:3.3]\n- Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. (Week 24 and ATE) [site_irt_guide:3.4]\n- Confirming Discontinue Subject stops all future automated resupply shipments for that subject. (end of study) [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- Stop the TempTale monitor included in the physical shipment and download the PDF data. (shipment receipt) [site_irt_guide:4.1]\n- When a shipment arrives, receive it in the Shipments tab by clicking the In Transit shipment number and then Receive Shipment. (shipment receipt) [site_irt_guide:4.1]\n- If there were any temperature excursions, place the entire shipment in Quarantined, physically quarantine the drug, and email the TempTale PDF to the CDMO for disposition. (shipment receipt) [site_irt_guide:4.1]\n- If you find a physically damaged kit, receive the shipment without an excursion, then change the damaged Kit Number from Available to Damaged. (shipment receipt) [site_irt_guide:4.1]\n- Subjects must return all used and unused stick packs/cartons to the clinic. (drug accountability) [site_irt_guide:4.2]\n- Site staff must physically count the returns and update Suvoda by entering the number of Unused Stick Packs returned under Kit Accountability. (drug accountability) [site_irt_guide:4.2]\n- Only if knowledge of the subject''s treatment assignment is absolutely necessary for immediate medical management may Emergency Unblind be performed. (emergency unblinding) [site_irt_guide:5]\n- After Emergency Unblind, enter the Reason for Unblinding, authenticate with your username and password, and click Reveal Treatment. (emergency unblinding) [site_irt_guide:5]\n- Unblinding automatically discontinues further double-blind protocol treatment, and Suvoda prevents future double-blind kits from being dispensed. (emergency unblinding) [site_irt_guide:5]\n- Do not give a damaged kit to the subject. (kit replacement) [site_irt_guide:6]\n- Use Replace Kit, select the damaged kit, choose the reason, and enter your password to get a replacement Kit Number. (kit replacement) [site_irt_guide:6]\n- Missed Visit for Week 4 skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. (visit missed) [site_irt_guide:6]\n- Do not manually order extra drug for your site; if inventory is low after an unexpected surge in screening, contact the Suvoda Helpdesk or the CRA. (inventory shortage) [site_irt_guide:6]\n- Kits are pre-assembled specifically for each visit; do not substitute tubes between kits. [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Screening, Days -42 to -1: use KIT #1 for Clinical Safety & Pregnancy and Coagulation. (Screening, Days -42 to -1) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Baseline, Day 0: use KIT #2 for Clinical Safety & Endocrinology, Biomarker Blood, Optional Genomics / DNA, and Optional CSF. (Baseline, Day 0) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Week 4 & 24, Pre & Post Dose PK: use KIT #3; Week 24 includes additional tubes for Endocrinology, Biomarkers, and Optional CSF. (Week 4 & 24, Pre & Post Dose PK) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Week 8 & 16, Post-Dose PK: use KIT #4; Week 16 includes Coagulation and Biomarkers. (Week 8 & 16, Post-Dose PK) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- ATE Weeks (Standard Clinic Visits): use KIT #5 for Clinical Safety; selected ATE weeks include Biomarker Blood/Urine components. (ATE Weeks (Standard Clinic Visits)) [site_lab_manual:4]\n- Use the specific supplies provided by Labcorp/World Courier. [site_lab_manual:6]\n- Complete Appendix 1: Kit Supply Order Form and fax it to Labcorp at +1-317-555-0999. [site_lab_manual:appendix-1-kit-supply-order-form]\n- DNL343 (Active) and Matching Placebo are granules for oral/enteral suspension; DNL343 is 100 mg per stick pack and Matching Placebo is 0 mg per stick pack. [site_pharmacy_manual:2]\n- Both products use aluminum foil stick pack and cardboard carton containing 14 stick packs segmented into 7 daily doses of 2 packets each; labeling complies with Annex 13 and local regulations. [site_pharmacy_manual:2]\n- Every shipment includes a TempTale continuous temperature monitor. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- Upon arrival, immediately inspect the outer shipping container for physical damage. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- Press and hold the "STOP" button on the TempTale device for 3-5 seconds until the stop icon appears. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- Plug the TempTale USB into a computer and save the automatically generated PDF report to your site''s regulatory binder. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- Verify whether the TempTale triggered a temperature alarm. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- If alarm triggered, immediately isolate the affected kits in the pharmacy under appropriate storage conditions, mark them "Quarantined" in the IRT, email the PDF report to the CDMO Supply Chain Manager for disposition, and do NOT dispense. (If alarm triggered) [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- Log into the IRT system, enter the shipment number, and confirm receipt of all physical kit numbers; the system will transition the kits from "In Transit" to "Available" inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:3]\n- During the 24-week placebo-controlled phase, the active and placebo stick packs are visually indistinguishable. (24-week placebo-controlled phase) [site_pharmacy_manual:critical-blinding-maintenance]\n- Site staff must never attempt to unblind a kit. [site_pharmacy_manual:critical-blinding-maintenance]\n- Dispense strictly by the Kit Number assigned by the IRT system. [site_pharmacy_manual:critical-blinding-maintenance]\n- Store both DNL343 and Placebo at Controlled Room Temperature (below 30°C / 86°F) and protect them from extreme heat and moisture. [site_pharmacy_manual:4]\n- Store kits in a secure, limited-access area (e.g., locked pharmacy) in accordance with ICH-GCP. [site_pharmacy_manual:4]\n- If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C, move the IMP to an alternate compliant storage location if the primary area cannot be rapidly corrected. (If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C) [site_pharmacy_manual:4]\n- If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C, quarantine the affected kits physically and systematically in the IRT. (If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C) [site_pharmacy_manual:4]\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. (Depending on the visit schedule) [site_pharmacy_manual:5]\n- Use two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. (Method A) [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en]\n- For take-home dosing, the Pharmacist or authorized Coordinator will dispense intact cartons to the subject. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- Log into the IRT to register the dispensing visit; the IRT will assign specific Kit Number(s) to the subject. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- Retrieve the exact matching Kit Number(s) from the pharmacy inventory. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- Affix a site-specific dispensing label to the secondary carton, and do not obscure the original clinical label. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- The label must include Subject ID Number, Protocol Number (NCT05842941), Date Dispensed & Dispenser Initials, and Instructions: Take 2 stick packs once daily. (For take-home dosing) [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use]\n- The Investigator is responsible for ensuring that all IMP is adequately accounted for throughout the trial. [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- The Pharmacy must maintain a master Drug Accountability Log (DAL) tracking the receipt, overall inventory, and final disposition of all cartons. [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- Maintain a subject-specific log tracking the exact dates and kit numbers dispensed to, and returned by, each subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- Instruct subjects to return all unused stick packs, as well as all empty cartons and empty stick pack wrappers, to every clinic visit. (Every clinic visit) [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- Site staff must count the returned intact packets and wrappers to calculate dosing compliance and record this in the subject''s eCRF and DAL. [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- IMP must NOT be destroyed on site without prior written authorization. [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- Used/Empty packaging may be destroyed on site according to standard hospital biohazardous waste policies after reconciliation is verified by the CRA. (After reconciliation is verified by the CRA) [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- Unused, expired, or quarantined kits must be returned to the CDMO depot (Fisher Clinical Services) using the pre-supplied return shipping materials, following IRT return authorization. (Unused, expired, or quarantined kits) [site_pharmacy_manual:7]\n- You must mark the shipment as containing an excursion during receipt, which automatically places the kits into Quarantined status in Suvoda. [site_irt_guide:6]','\n',char(10)),'Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability','["drug_supply"]',4008,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:early_termination','brief','','Stopping treatment and early termination','',replace('- Explain the Master Protocol''s nature and purpose, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits before consent. (Master Protocol consent) [master:1.3]\n- After regimen assignment, explain the assigned intervention and regimen-specific participation/termination conditions, risks and benefits. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:1.3]\n- Stopping regimens for futility or ending them early for success (if applicable) does not require a Master Protocol amendment. (Regimens stopped for futility or early success) [master:1.4.1]\n- The Sponsor may terminate individual sites, including for insufficient protocol adherence or failure to enter participants at an acceptable rate. (Participating sites) [master:1.4.2]\n- A regimen may be discontinued early due to futility or safety concerns. (Regimens) [master:2.3]\n- Inform participants they may refuse trial entry or withdraw at any time without prejudice to future treatment. [master:6.2]\n- Investigational product may need discontinuation if the SI or designee has concerns about prohibited medication use or safety, including missing safety data. [master:6.5.1]\n- Participants may discontinue investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1]\n- After investigational product discontinuation, the SI or designee should encourage continued protocol participation under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT). (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Encourage off-treatment study visits through the week 36 visit and the ATE period, following the Schedule of Activities. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- At minimum, encourage collection of ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures after investigational product discontinuation. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Prevent loss to follow-up whenever possible after investigational product discontinuation. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Complete an in-person Early Termination Visit after participants discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. (Participants followed under the ITT principle) [master:6.5.1]\n- A Follow-Up Safety Call when study drug is discontinued is not required if ITT follow-up continues for a minimum of 28 days. (Participants discontinuing study drug) [master:6.5.1]\n- Upon investigational product discontinuation, instruct participants to return unused investigational product, including empty packaging if applicable. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- Record every permanent investigational product discontinuation reason in the Case Report Form (CRF); include these data in the trial database and report them. (Participants permanently discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- The SI or designee may terminate participation when a medical condition or other situation makes continued participation contrary to the participant''s best interest. [master:6.5.2]\n- Participants may withdraw consent for study participation without providing justification. [master:6.5.2]\n- Upon study termination, instruct participants to return unused investigational product, including empty packaging material if applicable. (Participants terminating study participation) [master:6.5.2]\n- Discontinued randomized participants require written Master Protocol informed re-consent and continued Master Protocol eligibility before re-assignment to a different regimen, if available. (Randomized participants whose regimen participation was discontinued) [master:8.1.2]\n- After a regimen stops for futility or success, Master Protocol re-screening may occur after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants in a regimen stopped for futility or success) [master:8.1.2]\n- After completing the Week 36 Visit and terminating participation, Master Protocol re-screening may occur after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants completing the placebo-controlled period and terminating participation) [master:8.1.2]\n- After discontinuation for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI, no Master Protocol re-screening until after the would-be 36-Week Visit date. (Participants discontinuing for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI) [master:8.1.2]\n- Ask participants terminating early to attend an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants terminating participation early) [master:8.12]\n- For early terminations, follow the RSA for required outcome measures and timing and activities of the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants terminating participation early) [master:8.12]\n- Perform clinical safety laboratory tests at Week 36/ET, including ET testing not specified in earlier visit rules. (ET) [master:9.1.2]\n- For these positive C-SSRS responses, the SI must determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. (Participants with specified positive treatment-period C-SSRS responses) [master:9.1.6]\n- Report all AEs, especially those causing permanent discontinuation of the investigational product, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10]\n- After the final ATE visit and study-drug discontinuation, schedule a 28-day follow-up safety phone call. (Regimen G participants completing ATE) [regimen_g:s008]\n- Encourage participants stopping study drug early to remain in the study and follow the protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. (Regimen G participants stopping study drug early) [regimen_g:s008]\n- If a participant discontinues study medication and declines intent-to-treat follow-up, complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call. (Regimen G participants discontinuing study medication and declining continued follow-up) [regimen_g:s008]\n- Conduct Week 24 or ET Visit in clinic; the Week 24 window is Day 168 ± 7. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Complete ET visit CSF collection for participants with Baseline CSF collection only if ET occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Adverse Event Review at Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit, and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- At Week 24 or ET Visit, dispense study drug only if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Review contraception method at Week 24 or ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Complete Exit Questionnaire at Week 24 or ET Visit. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Complete the listed Week 24 or ET Visit assessments for Early Termination Visit occurring before Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period. (Participants terminating early) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Conduct Follow-Up Safety Call by phone 28 days after last dose ± 3 days only for early study termination; otherwise conduct it after the last ATE dose. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Conduct ATE Week 52 or ET Visit / Week 76 in clinic; the Week 52 window is Day 364 ± 14. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ALSFRS-R at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ALSAQ-40 at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ROADS at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform PGI-C at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform PGI-S at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform CGI-C at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Biomarker Blood Collection at ATE Week 16, Week 28, Week 52 or ET Visit, and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Biomarker Urine Collection at ATE Week 16, Week 28, Week 52 or ET Visit, and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Adverse Event Review at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit, all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), and Follow-Up Safety Call. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- At ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52, administer/dispense study drug only for participants returning for another visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Review contraception method at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit, all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Vital Status Determination at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Ask participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during ATE to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants withdrawing consent or terminating early during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- All ATE participants must have a Follow-Up Safety Call by phone 28 days after last dose ± 3 days. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- For substantial intolerability, the site investigator, consulting the medical monitor, may interrupt study drug, temporarily reduce to one stick pack rather than two stick packs, or discontinue. (Placebo-controlled period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- In ATE, the site investigator, consulting the medical monitor, may interrupt study drug, temporarily reduce to one stick pack rather than two stick packs, or discontinue. (ATE period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- Perform ALSAQ-40 at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform ROADS at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform PGI-C at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform PGI-S at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform CGI-C at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform 12. -lead ECG at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Optional lumbar puncture at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit must collect both biomarker and PK CSF samples. (Participants undergoing optional lumbar puncture) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Perform ET visit CSF collection only if the participant consented to lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and ET occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. (ET visit CSF collection) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Dispense study drug at the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Conduct the Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after the last dose of study drug. (Placebo-controlled phase follow-up) [regimen_g:6.8]\n- Ask participants withdrawing consent or terminating early without completing the protocol to attend an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants withdrawing consent or terminating early without completing the protocol) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- Schedule the in-person ET Visit as soon as possible after early termination. (Participants terminating early) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- For early termination or consent withdrawal during the placebo-controlled portion, conduct all Week 24 in-clinic visit assessments at the ET Visit. (Early termination or consent withdrawal during the placebo-controlled portion) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- After early termination, complete the Follow-Up Safety Call approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. (Participants terminating early) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- If ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose, collect Follow-Up Safety Call information during ET; a separate call is unnecessary. (Participants terminating early) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- If in-person ET Visit is outside 28±3 days after the last dose, conduct Follow-Up Safety Call approximately 28 days after the last dose; complete ET Visit afterward. (Participants terminating early) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- For participants discontinuing study drug but completing the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call are unnecessary. (Participants discontinuing study drug but completing the protocol) [regimen_g:6.9]\n- Make every effort to retain participants in the study, including ATE, regardless of study drug discontinuation. [regimen_g:6.9]\n- During phone or telemedicine and remote visits, complete ALSFRS-R at Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET. (Phone or telemedicine and remote visits) [regimen_g:s060]\n- During phone or telemedicine and remote visits, complete ROADS at Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET. (Phone or telemedicine and remote visits) [regimen_g:s060]\n- During phone or telemedicine and remote visits, complete PGI-C at Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET. (Phone or telemedicine and remote visits) [regimen_g:s060]\n- During phone or telemedicine and remote visits, complete PGI-S at Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET. (Phone or telemedicine and remote visits) [regimen_g:s060]\n- Conduct the ATE Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after the last dose of study drug. (ATE participants) [regimen_g:6.10.12]\n- Participants whose last dose precedes ATE completion, including early discontinuations, must complete the ATE Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants whose last dose precedes ATE completion) [regimen_g:6.10.12]\n- For early termination or consent withdrawal during ATE before Week 52, conduct all ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit assessments at the ET Visit. (Participants terminating early or withdrawing consent during ATE before Week 52) [regimen_g:6.10.13]\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C assessments are not required. (Early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE) [regimen_g:6.10.13]\n- Early Termination (ET) is out of sequence ET and is triggered via the Study Completion / ET form if subject withdraws early. (out of sequence ET) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- During ATE withdrawal of consent, complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form with Withdrawal of Consent as primary reason, and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are marked Ongoing at Study Exit. (during ATE withdrawal of consent) [site_edc_guide:8]\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial, enter the Date of Discontinuation and the primary reason, then confirm. (end of study) [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- Confirming Discontinue Subject stops all future automated resupply shipments for that subject. (end of study) [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- Unblinding automatically discontinues further double-blind protocol treatment, and Suvoda prevents future double-blind kits from being dispensed. (emergency unblinding) [site_irt_guide:5]','\n',char(10)),'Stopping treatment and early termination','["early_termination"]',4010,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:eligibility','brief','','Eligibility (who can join)','',replace('- At the Master Protocol Screening Visit, obtain consent using the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF), then screen for Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Randomly assign participants meeting Master Protocol eligibility criteria with equal probability among all active regimens. (Participants meeting Master Protocol eligibility criteria) [master:protocol-summary]\n- After regimen assignment, obtain regimen-specific consent and assess regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if applicable. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Within each regimen, randomize eligible participants a second time in a 3:1 ratio to active treatment or matching placebo. (Participants eligible for their assigned regimen) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Continue trial enrollment only while regimens are enrolling. (Enrollment) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Stop regimen enrollment when pre-defined futility or success criteria (if applicable) are met, or the target number of randomized participants is reached. (Regimen enrollment) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Conduct Regimen Specific Screening in clinic according to the RSA. (Regimen Specific Screening) [master:protocol-summary]\n- An individual may be re-assigned to multiple different regimens if eligible; follow Section 8.1.2 Screen Failures and Re-Assignment. (Participants considered for re-assignment) [master:1.3]\n- Add each regimen as an appendix to the Master Protocol before assigning any participant to that regimen. (Added regimens) [master:1.4.1]\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must enroll only participants meeting all Master Protocol eligibility criteria and, for Regimen enrollment, all additional criteria of the corresponding RSA. (Site Investigators) [master:1.5]\n- Use the sample size specified in each RSA for regimen enrollment. (Regimen enrollment) [master:5.1]\n- Master Protocol participants must have sporadic or familial ALS diagnosed as clinically possible, probable, lab-supported probable, or definite ALS under revised El Escorial criteria (Appendix I). (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Master Protocol participants must be Age 18 years or older. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Master Protocol participants must be capable of providing informed consent and complying with study procedures, in the SI''s opinion. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Time since onset of weakness due to ALS must be ≤ 24 months at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Vital Capacity must be ≥ 50% of predicted capacity at Master Protocol Screening, measured by Slow Vital Capacity (SVC). (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- At Master Protocol Screening, Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) measured in person may replace Slow Vital Capacity (SVC) if required due to pandemic-related restrictions. (Master Protocol candidates affected by pandemic-related restrictions) [master:5.2.1]\n- Participants must either not take riluzole or have a stable riluzole dose for ≥ 30 days before the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Participants must either not take edaravone or have completed at least one cycle (typically 14 days) of edaravone before the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Participants must be able to swallow pills and liquids at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and, in the SI''s opinion, swallow for the study''s duration. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Master Protocol participants must be geographically accessible to the site. (Master Protocol candidates) [master:5.2.1]\n- Exclude clinically significant unstable medical conditions other than ALS posing risk in the SI''s judgment, or clinically significant laboratory abnormalities or EKG changes. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude clinically significant abnormal liver or kidney function. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude alanine aminotransferase (ALT) or aspartate aminotransferase (AST) > 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN), regardless of clinical symptoms. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR) < 30 mL/min/1.73m°, regardless of clinical symptoms. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude unstable psychiatric disease, cognitive impairment, dementia or substance abuse impairing ability to provide informed consent, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude active cancer or history of cancer unless an allowed cancer exception applies. [master:5.2.2]\n- Cancer exceptions allow basal cell carcinoma, successfully treated squamous cell carcinoma of the skin, cervical carcinoma in situ, or prostatic carcinoma in situ. (Participants with cancer history) [master:5.2.2]\n- Other malignancies are allowed only if curatively treated with no evidence of disease recurrence for at least 3 years. (Participants with other malignancies) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude investigational ALS treatments, off-label or active trial participation, within 5 half-lives (if known) or 30 days (whichever is longer) before Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.2]\n- Refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for the current list of experimental therapies when checking investigational ALS treatment exclusions. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude exposure at any time to gene therapies under investigation for ALS, whether off-label or investigational. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude females who are breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, or planning pregnancy during the study. (Female participants) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude females of child-bearing potential unwilling to use effective contraception throughout the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. (Females of child-bearing potential) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude males of reproductive capacity unwilling to use effective contraception throughout the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. (Males of reproductive capacity) [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude anything increasing participant risk or preventing full study compliance or completion, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2]\n- Exclude re-screened participants if the disqualifying condition remains unresolved or the mandatory wash-out duration has not occurred. (Participants being re-screened) [master:5.2.2]\n- Apply additional inclusion or exclusion criteria listed in the RSA for the regimen''s specific investigational product. [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking riluzole at study entry must have a stable dose for 30 days before Master Protocol Screening Visit and intend the same stable dosage throughout study. (Participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking edaravone at study entry must complete at least one cycle of treatment (typically 14 days) before Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- Participants taking edaravone at study entry must intend to continue the same stable dosage throughout the study. (Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry) [master:5.2.2]\n- For eligibility, ALS symptom onset is first muscle weakness and must be no greater than 24 months before the Master Protocol Screening Visit date. [master:5.2.2]\n- Sites participating in the Master Protocol must be members of the Northeast ALS Consortium (NEALS). (Participating sites) [master:6.1]\n- Record screen failure reasons in EDC for participants not assigned to a regimen. (Participants not assigned to a regimen) [master:6.1]\n- Do not consent participants for any regimen if they are ineligible for the Master Protocol. (Participants ineligible for the Master Protocol) [master:6.2]\n- After regimen assignment, confirm regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if any, during the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:6.3]\n- Document reasons for regimen ineligibility. (Participants ineligible for their assigned regimen) [master:6.3]\n- After confirming Master Protocol eligibility, randomly assign participants equally among available recruiting regimens to which they have not previously been assigned. (Master Protocol-eligible participants) [master:6.4.1]\n- After confirming all regimen inclusion criteria and no regimen exclusion criteria, randomize participants 3:1 to active intervention or placebo. (Regimen-eligible participants) [master:6.4.1]\n- Perform Master Protocol Screening Visit procedures in-person, in the listed preferred order, to determine Master Protocol eligibility. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, assess Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, obtain ALS and medical history. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, collect blood samples for clinical screening assessments, including coagulation parameters. (Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- At Master Protocol Screening Visit, collect blood for pregnancy test for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP). (Women of child bearing potential (WOCBP) at Master Protocol Screening Visit) [master:8.1]\n- Schedule the Regimen Specific Screening Visit if the participant passes Master Protocol screening. (Participants passing Master Protocol screening) [master:8.1]\n- For Master Protocol screening failures, capture and enter demographics, including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP, in the EDC system. (Master Protocol screening failures) [master:8.1.2]\n- For Master Protocol screening failures, capture and enter the reason for screen failure in the EDC system. (Master Protocol screening failures) [master:8.1.2]\n- For Master Protocol screening failures, enter eligibility criteria assessed before an inclusion criterion failed or an exclusion criterion was met in the EDC system. (Master Protocol screening failures) [master:8.1.2]\n- A time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility failure may resolve immediately within the screening period without re-screening. (Participants failing a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion) [master:8.1.2]\n- If vital capacity fails on the Master Protocol Screening visit day, vital capacity may be reassessed on a different day during the initial screening period. (Participants failing the vital capacity screening criterion) [master:8.1.2]\n- Safety labs needed by the site investigator to determine eligibility may be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. (Master Protocol screening period) [master:8.1.2]\n- Re-screening after the initial screening window requires a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. (Participants re-screened after the initial screening window) [master:8.1.2]\n- Participants meeting Master Protocol eligibility but failing assigned regimen-specific criteria may be immediately re-assigned to a different regimen. (Participants failing assigned regimen-specific eligibility) [master:8.1.2]\n- If regimen ineligibility is known at regimen assignment, the participant may be administratively screen-failed before regimen consent and screening. (Participants known to be ineligible for the assigned regimen) [master:8.1.2]\n- Discontinued randomized participants require written Master Protocol informed re-consent and continued Master Protocol eligibility before re-assignment to a different regimen, if available. (Randomized participants whose regimen participation was discontinued) [master:8.1.2]\n- After a regimen stops for futility or success, Master Protocol re-screening may occur after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants in a regimen stopped for futility or success) [master:8.1.2]\n- After completing the Week 36 Visit and terminating participation, Master Protocol re-screening may occur after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants completing the placebo-controlled period and terminating participation) [master:8.1.2]\n- After discontinuation for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI, no Master Protocol re-screening until after the would-be 36-Week Visit date. (Participants discontinuing for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI) [master:8.1.2]\n- At the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, assess regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Regimen Specific Screening Visit) [master:8.2]\n- At the Baseline Visit, randomize within the regimen. (Baseline Visit) [master:8.3]\n- Perform clinical safety laboratory tests at Master Protocol Screening for eligibility and Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA. (Master Protocol Screening and Screening/Baseline) [master:9.1.2]\n- Perform a standard 12-lead ECG at the screening visit for eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. (Screening and scheduled ECG assessments) [master:9.1.3]\n- Use the highest recorded VC for eligibility. (VC eligibility assessments) [master:9.2.2]\n- Randomize participants first to an actively enrolling regimen only after determining eligibility under Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Participants entering a regimen) [master:11.1]\n- Randomize participants 3:1 to active treatment or placebo within the regimen only after determining eligibility under any regimen-specific criteria. (Participants assigned to a regimen) [master:11.1]\n- After completing a regimen, participants may be re-screened into the Master Protocol and assigned another regimen after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. (Participants who completed a regimen) [master:11.2.4]\n- Re-screened participants meeting eligibility for subsequent randomization in a different regimen must have new baseline visits and sets of observations for each assigned regimen. (Eligible repeat participants entering a different regimen) [master:11.2.4]\n- ALS diagnosis requires evidence of lower motor neuron (LMN) degeneration by clinical, electrophysiological or neuropathologic examination. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- ALS diagnosis requires evidence of upper motor neuron (UMN) degeneration by clinical examination. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- ALS diagnosis requires progressive spread of symptoms or signs within a region or to other regions, determined by history or examination. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- ALS diagnosis requires absence of electrophysiological and pathological evidence of other disease processes explaining LMN and/or UMN degeneration signs. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- ALS diagnosis requires absence of neuroimaging evidence of other disease processes explaining the observed clinical and electrophysiological signs. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- For ALS diagnosis, carefully take history and perform physical and neurological examination for UMN and LMN signs in four regions: brainstem, cervical, thoracic, or lumbosacral spinal cord. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- For ALS diagnosis, use ancillary tests as clinically indicated to exclude other diseases, including electrodiagnostic, neurophysiological, neuroimaging and clinical laboratory studies. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- ALS diagnosis requires clinical evidence of both LMN and UMN degeneration. (ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- For Clinically Probable ALS - Laboratory-supported, properly apply neuroimaging and clinical laboratory protocols to exclude other causes. (Clinically Probable ALS - Laboratory-supported diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- Exclude other diagnoses before accepting a diagnosis of Clinically Possible ALS. (Clinically Possible ALS diagnosis) [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-]\n- Screen participants under the Master Protocol before random assignment to a regimen. (Participants being considered for regimen assignment) [regimen_g:s005]\n- Stop assignment to the regimen if pre-defined futility criteria are met or the target number of randomized participants has been reached. (Regimen assignment) [regimen_g:s005]\n- Regimen G planning assumes a 6-week screening window. (Regimen G screening) [regimen_g:s009]\n- Complete Master Protocol Screening in clinic within –42 to –1 Days relative to Baseline (Day 0). [regimen_g:s010]\n- Conduct Regimen Specific Screening in clinic within –41 to 0 Day relative to Baseline (Day 0). [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Inclusion/Exclusion Review at Master Protocol Screening and assess regimen-specific eligibility criteria at Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Obtain ALS & Medical History at Master Protocol Screening. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Baseline CSF may be collected within one week before the first study medication dose, after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Perform Assignment to the Regimen at Master Protocol Screening. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Before randomization to Regimen G, confirm participants meet Master Protocol eligibility criteria and all Regimen G inclusion and exclusion criteria. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2]\n- Exclude participants with epilepsy currently requiring antiepileptic therapy or seizure of any cause within 6 months of randomization. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude participants with hypersensitivity to DNL343 or any excipients in the DNL343 drug product. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude strong CYP3A4/5 inducer use within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose, or anticipated use during study treatment. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- The strong CYP3A4/5 inducer exclusion covers prescription and over-the-counter (OTC) medications, including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude prescription or OTC sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude participants anticipating sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrate use with a narrow therapeutic index during the study treatment period. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude prescription or OTC BCRP or OAT3 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of first dose. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Exclude participants anticipating BCRP or OAT3 substrate use with a narrow therapeutic index during the study treatment period. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- Apply RSA-specific contraceptive requirements in Section 5.10.2 when assessing Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6. (Participants entering Regimen G) [regimen_g:4.2.2]\n- The medical monitor may assess whether sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates have a narrow therapeutic index for an individual participant. (Participants taking these substrates) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- For all excluded therapies in Section 5.9, participants may be rescreened after the defined window has passed. (Participants excluded for prohibited therapies) [regimen_g:5.9]\n- Female participants must meet the postmenopausal, surgical sterilization, or applicable childbearing-potential contraception requirements throughout the trial. (Female participants) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Postmenopausal eligibility requires no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without another medical cause and follicle-stimulating hormone > 40 IU/L or mIU/mL at screening. (Female participants qualifying as postmenopausal) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Surgical sterilization must be at least 6 months before dosing; examples include hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, and bilateral oophorectomy. (Female participants qualifying through surgical sterilization) [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- Baseline CSF may be collected separately, within one week prior to first study medication dose, after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. (Baseline CSF collection) [regimen_g:6.1]\n- Screening maps to Days -42 to -1 and includes ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, and Baseline Symptoms. (Screening) [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping]\n- Master and Regimen-Specific Inclusion/Exclusion criteria must be evaluated; if Not Met is selected for any criterion, enter the specific failure reason to satisfy the hard edit check. (Inclusion/Exclusion) [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility]\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply. (baseline / Day 0) [site_irt_guide:3.2]','\n',char(10)),'Eligibility (who can join)','["eligibility"]',4000,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','brief:extension_period','brief','','Extension period (ATE)','',replace('- Use the respective RSAs for the duration of the Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period. (Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period) [master:protocol-summary]\n- Keep participants, investigators and site staff blinded to active product or matching placebo assignment throughout the ATE period. (Participants, investigators and site staff) [master:4.1]\n- Participants, SIs, and everyone conducting a regimen are unblinded to regimen assignment but must remain blinded to randomized treatment assignments through the ATE period. [master:6.4.2]\n- Maintain treatment blinding according to the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.2]\n- Encourage off-treatment study visits through the week 36 visit and the ATE period, following the Schedule of Activities. (Participants discontinuing investigational product) [master:6.5.1]\n- After completing the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all Regimen G participants enter the active treatment extension (ATE) and receive DNL343 200 mg QD. (Regimen G participants completing the placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s004]\n- Regimen G ATE treatment with DNL343 lasts up to an additional 78 weeks. (Regimen G ATE participants) [regimen_g:s004]\n- Regimen G ATE is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks, giving 76 weeks total treatment duration. (Regimen G ATE participants) [regimen_g:s007]\n- Continue Regimen G ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE. (Regimen G ATE) [regimen_g:s007]\n- Regimen G treatment must not exceed 102 weeks total from Baseline visit. (Regimen G participants) [regimen_g:s007]\n- After the final ATE visit and study-drug discontinuation, schedule a 28-day follow-up safety phone call. (Regimen G participants completing ATE) [regimen_g:s008]\n- Plan Regimen G participation for a minimum of approximately 86 weeks and a maximum of approximately 112 weeks. (Regimen G participants) [regimen_g:s009]\n- Perform Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) at Baseline and Week 24 or ET Visit, for all participants prior to ATE. (Placebo-controlled treatment period) [regimen_g:s010]\n- Week 24 Visit is the first day of ATE; provide the ATE dose in clinic and obtain predose and postdose PK samples. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:s010]\n- CSF collection must occur before the first ATE study medication dose. (Participants continuing into ATE with baseline CSF collected) [regimen_g:s010]\n- At Week 24 or ET Visit, dispense study drug only if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Conduct Follow-Up Safety Call by phone 28 days after last dose ± 3 days only for early study termination; otherwise conduct it after the last ATE dose. [regimen_g:s010]\n- Conduct ATE Week 2 / Week 26 by phone on Day 14 ± 3. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 4 / Week 28 in clinic on Day 28 ± 7. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 8 / Week 32 in clinic on Day 56 ± 7. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 12 / Week 36 by phone on Day 84 ± 3. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 16 / Week 40 in clinic on Day 112 ± 7. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 20 / Week 44 by phone on Day 140 ± 3. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 24 / Week 48 by phone on Day168 ± 3. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 28 / Week 52 in clinic on Day 196 ± 14. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 40 / Week 64 in clinic on Day 280 ± 14. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Conduct ATE Week 52 or ET Visit / Week 76 in clinic; the Week 52 window is Day 364 ± 14. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- The ATE schedule lists visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), Week 89 and Week 102, in clinic 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks. (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- ATE footnote 8 requires in-clinic visits after the 52-week Visit at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. (ATE after the 52-week Visit) [regimen_g:s011]\n- ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, and Week 40 may use phone or telemedicine with remote services for pandemic, disability, or medically necessary safety reasons. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- ATE visits after week 52 may use phone or telemedicine with remote services for pandemic, disability, or medically necessary safety reasons. (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- ATE lasts a minimum of 52 weeks and maximum of 78 weeks per participant, ending when all active participants complete the 52-week Visit in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Measure Vital Signs at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Measure Vital Signs at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform 12-lead ECG at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform 12-lead ECG at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Slow Vital Capacity at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Slow Vital Capacity at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ALSFRS-R at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ALSFRS-R at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ALSAQ-40 at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at ATE Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform ROADS at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform PGI-C at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform PGI-S at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform CGI-C at ATE Week 28 and Week 52 or ET Visit; not required for early terminations after Week 52 in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Biomarker Blood Collection at ATE Week 16, Week 28, Week 52 or ET Visit, and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Biomarker Urine Collection at ATE Week 16, Week 28, Week 52 or ET Visit, and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at ATE Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Concomitant Medication Review at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Adverse Event Review at ATE Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Adverse Event Review at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit, all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), and Follow-Up Safety Call. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Obtain an unscheduled PK sample for a reported TEAE causing dose modification or interruption, or a severe TEAE requiring in-clinic evaluation. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug at ATE Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, and Week 40. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- At ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52, administer/dispense study drug only for participants returning for another visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at ATE Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40, and Week 52 or ET Visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance at all ATE visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE after week 52) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform study drug compliance check-ins at ATE Week 2, Week 12, Week 20, and Week 24. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Review contraception method at ATE Week 2, Week 4, Week 8, Week 12, Week 16, Week 20, Week 24, Week 28, and Week 40. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Review contraception method at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit, all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks), and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Perform Vital Status Determination at ATE Week 52 or ET Visit and all visits after week 52 (every 13weeks). (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Determine date of death, PAV status, or date last known alive for each participant at the end of ATE follow-up. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- If alive at the end of ATE follow-up, determine vital status again at the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- Ask participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during ATE to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call. (Participants withdrawing consent or terminating early during ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- All ATE participants must have a Follow-Up Safety Call by phone 28 days after last dose ± 3 days. (ATE) [regimen_g:s011]\n- After double-blinded treatment, all participants enter ATE and receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks. (ATE period) [regimen_g:3]\n- ATE DNL343 200 mg QD applies even to participants who had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. (ATE period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- If starting ATE DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) may be necessary, the investigator must discuss with the medical monitor before ATE entry. (Participants needing reduced-dose ATE initiation) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- In ATE, the site investigator, consulting the medical monitor, may interrupt study drug, temporarily reduce to one stick pack rather than two stick packs, or discontinue. (ATE period) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- After ATE dose modification, consider resuming DNL343 at the 200 mg dose level if possible. (ATE period dose modifications) [regimen_g:5.6.1]\n- Obtain verbal confirmation of participants’ contraceptive methods at Screening and each in clinic visit throughout the double-blind and ATE periods. [regimen_g:5.10.2]\n- On the Week 24 Visit day, participants must not take any placebo-controlled period dose; take the ATE dose in-clinic. (Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- For Week 24 predose PK collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately their usual morning time. (Participants continuing into ATE at the Week 24 Visit) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- At the Week 24 Visit, perform endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) for participants continuing in the ATE. (Participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- For participants continuing into ATE, optional Week 24 CSF collection must occur before the first ATE dose. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:6.7]\n- Participants continuing into ATE do not complete the Follow-Up Safety Call during the placebo-controlled phase. (Participants continuing into ATE) [regimen_g:6.8]\n- Make every effort to retain participants in the study, including ATE, regardless of study drug discontinuation. [regimen_g:6.9]\n- Following the placebo-controlled period, participants continue in ATE as outlined in the SOA. (Participants completing the placebo-controlled period) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- Continue ATE until all active participants complete a minimum of 52 weeks in ATE. (ATE) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- No participant may exceed 78 weeks in ATE, or 102 weeks total from the Baseline visit. (ATE) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- Unscheduled safety assessment visits may occur anytime, independently of scheduled in-clinic visits, if the Investigator deems them medically appropriate and necessary. (ATE) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- Collect additional unscheduled PK samples as needed when assessing TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption. (ATE participants with TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- Collect additional unscheduled PK samples as needed for any severe TEAE requiring an unscheduled assessment visit. (ATE participants with a severe TEAE requiring an unscheduled visit) [regimen_g:6.10]\n- SOA visits Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE may use telemedicine with remote services when pandemic, disability, or medical necessity requires participant protection. (Designated SOA visits needing remote services for participant safety) [regimen_g:s060]\n- For permitted remote SOA visits, use phone with remote services if telemedicine is unavailable. (Designated SOA visits conducted remotely) [regimen_g:s060]\n- Conduct the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events; see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, perform drug compliance check-in. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug containers, to the next visit. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- At the Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.1]\n- Conduct the Week 4 ATE Visit in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, collect vital signs including weight. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Perform SVC at the Week 4 ATE Visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire at the Week 4 ATE Visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events; see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire at the Week 4 ATE Visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Perform 12. -lead ECG at the Week 4 ATE Visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- At the Week 4 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 4 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.2]\n- Conduct the Week 8 ATE Visit in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, collect vital signs including weight. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Perform SVC at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Perform CNS-BFS at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, perform urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential. (Women of childbearing potential at the Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, review contraceptive methods used by the participant and partner. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, review concomitant medications. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events; see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Perform 12. -lead ECG at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Dispense study drug to the participant at the Week 8 ATE Visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, check study drug compliance. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- At the Week 8 ATE Visit, remind participants to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit. (Week 8 ATE Visit) [regimen_g:6.10.3]\n- Conduct the Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion. (Week 12 ATE 