y visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs, including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. ROADS\n16. PGI-C\n17. PGI-S\n18. CGI-C\n19. Dispense study drug to participant\n20. Perform study drug compliance\n21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 28)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. -lead ECG\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 40)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n16. ROADS\n17. PGI-C\n18. PGI-S\n19. CGI-C\n20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 52)\n## ATE assessment mapping\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [9], All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- [9]For participants returning for another visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE dispensing at Week 52 or later)\n- 2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, CGI-C)\n- 3. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE telephone visits)\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants subject to contraception review)\n## Remote-visit allowances\n- If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. The following procedures should be completed in the suggested order and as per the Schedule of Activities:\nALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable; each RA may describe additional activities. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G designated placebo-controlled clinic visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nIn addition to the procedures in the Master Protocol that should be conducted during the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. CNS-BFS (Weeks 8 and 16 only)\n2. ROADS (Week 16 and 24 only)\n3. PGI-C (Week 16 and 24 only)\n4. PGI-S (Week 16 and 24 only)\nDetails on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled remote visits)\n- 7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G designated ATE visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nDuring the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. ALSFRS-R (Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET)\n2. CNS-BFS (Week 8, 16, 28, 40, 52 only)\n3. ROADS (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n4. PGI-C (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n5. PGI-S (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time 12 Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant. [master:protocol-summary]\n## Follow-up after stopping treatment\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants stopping study drug but completing protocol)\n## Safety-call timing and procedures\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety after their last dose of study drug. Additional details, including the timing of this call, can be found in the RSA. The following procedure will be performed at a minimum:\n• Assess and document AEs [master:8.11] (applies to: all participants)\n- 10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug.\nParticipants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase.\nThe following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.12)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.8)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early)\n2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n## Early-termination visits and combined safety follow-up\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n## Unscheduled visits and repeat assessments\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- All participants will have clinical safety laboratory tests at the designated visits outlined in the schedule of activities. The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix.\n2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen.\n3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add.\n4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n## Missed visits and procedures\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Q: A subject missed their Week 4 visit entirely. How do I record this?\nA: Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: Regimen G missed Week 4)\n## PK sampling and clinic dosing timing\n- 15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)\n## CSF and DNA collection windows\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional DNA collection)\n- 16. CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional CSF collection) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination CSF)\n- It should be noted that the CSF biofluid collection is optional and collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24 (rather than Baseline and Week 16 as noted in the Master Protocol). Additional biofluid samples (blood) will be collected for Regimen G for evaluation of plasma PK at scheduled timepoints described in the SOA. [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing second lumbar puncture)\n## Laboratory assessment calendar\n- Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections. [site_lab_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G laboratory schedule)\n- - Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1)\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112)\n- Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Pharmacokinetics (PK Plasma) (Central): done at Wk 4 (Day 28) Pre, Post; Wk 8 (Day 56) Post; Wk 16 (Day 112) Post; Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) Pre, Post\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- DNA Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0)\n- CSF Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0) X (Bio), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) X (Bio/PK) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled laboratory assessments) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G ATE laboratory assessments)\n- * Only collected at specific ATE visits (Wk 16, 28, 52). [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: Regimen G ATE biomarker blood)\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol clinical safety laboratory tests)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: WOCBP pregnancy tests; all participants coagulation tests)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n## Other assessment timing\n- Assessments will be performed at designated time-points throughout the study for clinical evaluation. [master:9.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details of any additional specific clinical assessments required for a regimen will be reported in the corresponding RSA [master:9.1] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Height in em or in will be measured and recorded at the Master Protocol Screening Visit (Visit 1) only. This one-time height measurement must be used throughout the duration of the study. [master:9.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. [master:9.1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. -lead ECG (to be done at all in-clinic visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 8. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood adrenocorticotropic hormone [ACTH], cortisol, free thyroxine [fT4]) (Baseline and Week 24 Visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010, regimen_g:s011)\n- Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n## Vital-status follow-up timing\n- Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive and date last known to be PAV-free, will be determined by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 visit, as indicated in the SOA. At approximately the time of the last patient last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants to assess vital status. [master:8.15] (applies to: all randomized participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:7.7)\n- Vital status may also be ascertained at later time points. [master:8.15] (applies to: all randomized participants)\n- 11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- 1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. At approximately the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled portion of the regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status.\nWe may also ascertain vital status at later time points. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebo- of the last participant''s last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen. [master:protocol-summary]\n## Reconsent at scheduled or remote visits\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. If the participant’s next in-clinic visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants requiring reconsent)\n- a) The site staff sends copy of the informed consent form to the participant.\nb) The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign.\nc) The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant.\nd) The participant signs the informed consent form and returns the original signed consent form back to the site.\ne) Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant signs the informed consent form. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)\n## Visit-system mapping and transition steps\n- - Rave Folder Name: Screening | Protocol Equivalent: Days -42 to -1 | Key eCRFs Included: ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, Baseline Symptoms.\n- Rave Folder Name: Baseline | Protocol Equivalent: Day 0 | Key eCRFs Included: Pre-dose Labs, ECG, Baseline PROs (ALSFRS-R, ROADS, ALSAQ-40).\n- Rave Folder Name: Week X (Placebo-Controlled) | Protocol Equivalent: Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 | Key eCRFs Included: Drug Administration, AEs, ConMeds, PK/Biomarkers, Safety Labs. (Note: Wks 2, 12, 20 are phone visits).\n- Rave Folder Name: ATE Week X (Open-Label) | Protocol Equivalent: Weeks 26 through 102 | Key eCRFs Included: Dynamically populates only after the Week 24 visit is marked complete.\n- Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Regimen G EDC visit folders)\n- 2. Select the current visit from the dropdown (e.g., Week 4). (Note: Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G IRT scheduled visits)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks).\n\n1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit.\n2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply.\n3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period.\n4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition and ATE visit sequence)\n## Screening timing and visits\n- Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days [master:protocol-summary]\n## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]\n## Vital signs and examinations\n- Height in cm measured at Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [master:protocol-summary]\n## DNA and blood biomarkers\n- Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [master:protocol-summary]\n## Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary]\n## Telephone compliance checks and return reminders\n- A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 Visit, as indicated). [master:protocol-summary]\n## CSF collection schedule and conditions\n- " CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]\n## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When visits or procedures are missed or PK timing departs from protocol, record reportable deviations while applying the specified illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]\n## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a scheduled visit includes clinic dosing, follow clinic preparation requirements, including the second-person check and in-use stability limits.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Remote dispensing (When an eligible clinic visit is conducted remotely, follow the remote dispensing requirements for any study drug supplied.)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Details on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (When the Week 24 visit starts ATE, obtain open-label kit assignment through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind kits.)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)\n## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (When study drug is discontinued, arrange unused-product returns and complete discontinuation documentation even if scheduled follow-up continues.)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participants terminating participation)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all research participants)\n## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a scheduled, remote or unscheduled assessment identifies a reportable safety event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]\n## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When a visit''s C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety assessment and response procedures.)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:s011)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n## Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (When screening restarts after the initial window or a previously randomized participant is considered for reassignment, obtain the required renewed Master Protocol consent.)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants rescreened after the initial screening window)\n- The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants whose regimen participation is discontinued)','\n',char(10)),'Schedule framework and visit-date calculation; Assessment order and time of day; Screening and baseline timing; Repeat screening and reassignment; Placebo-controlled visit calendar; Regimen G placebo-controlled visit procedures; Placebo-controlled assessment mapping; ATE duration and completion; ATE calendar and later-visit intervals; ATE scheduled visits; ATE assessment mapping; Remote-visit allowances; Follow-up after stopping treatment; Safety-call timing and procedures; Early-termination visits and combined safety follow-up; Unscheduled visits and repeat assessments; Missed visits and procedures; PK sampling and clinic dosing timing; CSF and DNA collection windows; Laboratory assessment calendar; Other assessment timing; Vital-status follow-up timing; Reconsent at scheduled or remote visits; Visit-system mapping and transition steps; Screening timing and visits; Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent; Vital signs and examinations; DNA and blood biomarkers; Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception; Telephone compliance checks and return reminders; CSF collection schedule and conditions; Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When visits or procedures are missed or PK timing departs from protocol, record reportable deviations while applying the specified illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.); Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a scheduled visit includes clinic dosing, follow clinic preparation requirements, including the second-person check and in-use stability limits.); Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Remote dispensing (When an eligible clinic visit is conducted remotely, follow the remote dispensing requirements for any study drug supplied.); Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (When the Week 24 visit starts ATE, obtain open-label kit assignment through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind kits.); Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (When study drug is discontinued, arrange unused-product returns and complete discontinuation documentation even if scheduled follow-up continues.); Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a scheduled, remote or unscheduled assessment identifies a reportable safety event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.); Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When a visit''s C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety assessment and response procedures.); Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (When screening restarts after the initial window or a previously randomized participant is considered for reassignment, obtain the required renewed Master Protocol consent.)','["visit_schedule"]',2005,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:1','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Consent and assessment framework','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Consent and assessment framework\n- This section describes trial procedures that are common to all regimens tested in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial as detailed in the Master Protocol. Each regimen will follow these procedures and may add additional procedures as described in the corresponding RSA. [master:8] (applies to: all regimens)\n- No study procedures are performed prior to the signing of the Master Protocol ICF. All participants will sign the Master Protocol ICF prior to undergoing any study tests or procedures. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- Assessments will be performed at designated time-points throughout the study for clinical evaluation. In addition to the assessments evaluated below, participants will provide information on their demographics, past medical history, including ALS, as well as concomitant medication usage. [master:9.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details of any additional specific clinical assessments required for a regimen will be reported in the corresponding RSA [master:9.1]\n- Details of any additional specific endpoints required for a regimen will be described in the corresponding RSA. [master:9.2]','\n',char(10)),'This section describes trial procedures that are common to all regimens tested in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial as detailed in the Master Pr','["assessments"]',3601,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:10','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 16 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 16 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.5)\n- Collect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs (including coagulation parameters) and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform LP for CSF collection (or as described in the RSA) Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16) (note: differs from regimen_g:8)\n- This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- 1. CNS-BFS\n2. ALSAQ-40\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (postdose only on Week 16) o Postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n9. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the','["assessments"]',3610,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:11','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 20 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 20 assessments\n- This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 24 Visit [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in','["assessments"]',3611,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:12','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 24 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 24 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- Collect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24)\n- This visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET) (note: differs from master:8.10)\n- 1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET)\n- 9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- 10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in pre','["assessments"]',3612,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:13','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Weeks 28 and 32 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Weeks 28 and 32 assessments\n- This visit will take place via telephone 196 - 3 days after the Bascline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28)\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 224 + 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 36 Visit [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 196 - 3 days after the Bascline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in','["assessments"]',3613,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:14','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 36 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 36 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)\n- Collect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nPerform 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG)\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Ask participant to complete the Exit Questionnaire [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in pre','["assessments"]',3614,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:15','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Regimen G placebo-controlled assessment schedule','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Regimen G placebo-controlled assessment schedule\n- As per the Schedule of Activities (SOA) below, visits must occur every 4 weeks and will be clinic-, phone-, or telemedicine based, as applicable. There is a maximum 24-week duration of placebo-controlled treatment for a regimen. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 Days [13] | Regimen Specific Screening [1] / Clinic / –41 to 0 Day [13] | Baseline [1] / Clinic / Day 0 | Week 2 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [12] / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [12] / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [12] / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 or ET Visit [19] / Clinic / Day 168 ± 7 | Follow-Up Safety Call 10 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:8.7; differs from master:8.9)\n- - Written Informed Consent [2] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1]\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1] [3]\n- ALS & Medical History Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Demographics Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Physical Examination Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Neurological Exam Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Muscle Strength Assessment Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-C 17 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-S 17 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CGI-C 18 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - 12-Lead ECG Regimen: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Week 16 [12] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all participants prior to ATE) [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- - Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] Regimen: done at Week 4 [12] (pre, post), Week 8 [12] (post), Week 16 [12] (post), Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (pre, post) [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24)\n- - DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1]\n- CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Study Drug Accountability/Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [20], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12 [20], Week 16 [12], Week 20 [20], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Exit Questionnaire Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Vital Status Determination [11] Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 19. The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G early termination before Week 24)\n- [21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G participants with reproductive potential)','\n',char(10)),'As per the Schedule of Activities (SOA) below, visits must occur every 4 weeks and will be clinic-, phone-, or telemedicine based, as applic','["assessments"]',3615,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:16','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Remote assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Remote assessments\n- If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. The following procedures should be completed in the suggested order and as per the Schedule of Activities:\nALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable; each RA may describe additional activities. [master:8] (applies to: remote visits)\n- 12. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G designated clinic visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- In addition to the procedures in the Master Protocol that should be conducted during the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed: [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled remote visits)\n- 1. CNS-BFS (Weeks 8 and 16 only)\n2. ROADS (Week 16 and 24 only)\n3. PGI-C (Week 16 and 24 only)\n4. PGI-S (Week 16 and 24 only) [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled remote visits)\n- Details on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled remote visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- During the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed: [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- 1. ALSFRS-R (Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET)\n2. CNS-BFS (Week 8, 16, 28, 40, 52 only)\n3. ROADS (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n4. PGI-C (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n5. PGI-S (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- 7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE designated visits)','\n',char(10)),'If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not av','["assessments"]',3616,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:17','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Vital signs and examinations','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Vital signs and examinations\n- Vital signs include weight in kg or Ibs, systolic and diastolic blood pressure in mmHg, pulse rate (radial artery/minute, respiratory rate/minute, and temperature in °C or °F. Height in em or in will be measured and recorded at the Master Protocol Screening Visit (Visit 1) only. This one-time height measurement must be used throughout the duration of the study. [master:9.1.1] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- 4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.1)\n- A physical examination will be performed and recorded. [master:9.1.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- A neurological examination will be performed and recorded. [master:9.1.5] (applies to: all participants)\n- Height in cm measured at Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Vital signs include weight in kg or Ibs, systolic and diastolic blood pressure in mmHg, pulse rate (radial artery/minute, respiratory rate/m','["assessments"]',3617,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:18','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Safety laboratories','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Safety laboratories\n- Clinical Safety Labs: the default and preferred method for collecting clinical safety labs is by using the study specific central laboratory as described in the study MOP. If clinical safety labs are needed and it is not feasible to use the central laboratory, clinical safety labs can be collected using one of the following methods: [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- Local site laboratory resources, or\n• Independent labs offering such services as described in this protocol and/or local to the participant (e.g. primary care physician) [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Hematology with differential panel: complete blood count with differential (hematocrit, hemoglobin, platelet count, RBC indices, Total RBC, Total WBC, and WBC & differential)\n• Blood chemistry panel/Liver function tests (LFTs): alanine aminotransferase (ALT (SGPT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST (SGOT)), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate, blood urea nitrogen, calcium, chloride, creatinine (estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate [eGFR] will be calculated using the using the CKD-EPI 2021 equation), cystatin C, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, potassium, sodium, total bilirubin and total protein [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants; hCG for WOCBP)\n- All participants will have clinical safety laboratory tests at the designated visits outlined in the schedule of activities. The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections. [site_lab_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Clinical Safety Labs: the default and preferred method for collecting clinical safety labs is by using the study specific central laboratory','["assessments"]',3618,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:19','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Endocrinology laboratories','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Endocrinology laboratories\n- 14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- * Endocrinology notes: Blood samples for ACTH and Cortisol should be collected at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit to control for diurnal variation. [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated','["assessments"]',3619,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:2','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Assessment order and evaluators','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Assessment order and evaluators\n- Study Visit Recommendations: [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Visit windows are consecutive calendar days and the target visit dates are calculated from the Baseline Visit - Day 0 (first day of investigational product administration). [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 2. Assessment of vital capacity should be performed at the start of a visit so as not to fatigue the participant with other testing. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. The remaining clinical assessments should be administered in the same sequence as listed in the sections below and at approximately the same time of the day. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- a. Any additional assessments that are included in an RSA for a given visit should be completed once the Master Protocol SOA for that visit is complete, unless otherwise specified in the RSA. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Outcomes for individual participants should be measured by the same evaluator throughout their participation in a regimen. Evaluators must be qualified, trained, certified, and delegated. Training documentation must be filed in the Investigator Site File (ISF). [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 17. PGI-C and PGI-S should be completed by the participant prior to other clinical assessments scheduled during the visit whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 18. CGI-C should be completed after all clinical assessments are completed by the investigator whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Study Visit Recommendations:','["assessments"]',3602,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:20','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Central laboratory collection schedules','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Central laboratory collection schedules\n- - Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1)\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112)\n- Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Pharmacokinetics (PK Plasma) (Central): done at Wk 4 (Day 28) Pre, Post; Wk 8 (Day 56) Post; Wk 16 (Day 112) Post; Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) Pre, Post\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Biomarker Urine (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- - DNA Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0)\n- CSF Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0) X (Bio), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) X (Bio/PK) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),replace('- Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1)\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1','\n',char(10)),'["assessments"]',3620,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:21','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ECG procedures','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ECG procedures\n- A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. The ECG should be collected using the study provided ECG device. Tracings will be reviewed by a central ECG reader and a copy of the tracings will be kept on site as part of the source documents. Additional guidance is included in the MOP. [master:9.1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. -lead ECG (to be done at all in-clinic visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedu','["assessments"]',3621,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:22','piece','','Assessments and procedures > C-SSRS procedures and safety response','Assessments and procedures',replace('## C-SSRS procedures and safety response\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: Baseline)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: visits other than Baseline)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: positive C-SSRS responses during treatment)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)','\n',char(10)),'At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s l','["assessments"]',3622,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:23','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ALSFRS-R and SVC methods and certification','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ALSFRS-R and SVC methods and certification\n- The primary endpoint will be assessed for all interventions every 4 weeks, either on-site or via phone. [master:4.1] (applies to: all interventions)\n- Each functional activity is rated 0-4 for a total score that ranges from 0 to 48. [master:9.2.1] (applies to: ALSFRS-R)\n- All ALSFRS-R evaluators must be NEALS certified. [master:9.2.1] (applies to: ALSFRS-R evaluators)\n- 5. Vital Capacity ≥ 50% of predicted capacity at the time of Master Protocol Screening measured by Slow Vital Capacity (SVC), or, if required due to pandemic-related restrictions, Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) measured in person. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- The vital capacity (VC) will be determined using the upright slow VC (SVC) method. All evaluators performing S VC, must be NEALS certified. The SVC will be measured using the study-approved portable spirometer, and assessments will be performed using a face mask. A printout from the spirometer of all VC trials will be retained. Three VC trials are required for each testing session, however up to 5 trials may be performed if the variability between the highest and second highest VC is 10% or greater for the first 3 trials. Only the 3 best trials are recorded on the CRF. The highest VC recorded is utilized for eligibility. At least 3 measurable VC trials must be completed to score VC for all visits after screening. Predicted VC values and percent-predicted VC values will be calculated as described in the MOP. [master:9.2.2] (applies to: SVC assessments)\n- All evaluators must be NEALS trained and certified and delegated to perform the ALSFRS-R and SVC assessments. Certification and training will be provided by the Barrow Outcome Center. Certification occurs via a formal evaluation of reliability and accuracy of performance of these measures. Repeat certification will be required on an annual basis for ALSFRS-R and every two years for SVC. It is strongly preferred that a single evaluator performs all measures throughout the study, as much as possible. [master:9.2.2] (applies to: ALSFRS-R and SVC evaluators)','\n',char(10)),'The primary endpoint will be assessed for all interventions every 4 weeks, either on-site or via phone.','["assessments"]',3623,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:24','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Patient and clinician reported outcomes','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Patient and clinician reported outcomes\n- Patient reported outcomes will be assessed in this trial and will be defined in the RSA. [master:9.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form, and should not provide any assistance with determining answers. [regimen_g:7.1] (applies to: Regimen G ALSAQ-40)\n- Participants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed, but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form, and should not provide any assistance with determining answers. [regimen_g:7.2] (applies to: Regimen G CNS-BFS)\n- Participants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form and should not provide any assistance with determining answers. [regimen_g:7.3] (applies to: Regimen G ROADS)\n- Respondents rate their change relative to baseline as “very much improved,” “much improved,” “minimally improved,” “no change,” “minimally worse,” “much worse,” or “very much worse” (Hurst and Bolton 2004). [regimen_g:7.4] (applies to: Regimen G PGI-C)\n- The PGI-S item asks the respondent to best describe the level of severity of their symptoms now on a 4-point scale scored as: “normal” (1), “mild” (2), “moderate” (3), or “severe” (4) (Viktrup et al 2012). [regimen_g:7.5] (applies to: Regimen G PGI-S)\n- CGI-C asks the clinician one question, which is rated on a seven-point scale: “Compared to the patient’s condition at admission the project (prior to study drug initiation), this patient’s condition is 1=very much improved; 2=much improved; 3=minimally improved; 4=no change from baseline; 5=minimally worse; 6=much worse; 7=very much worse since the initiation of study drug treatment”. [regimen_g:7.6] (applies to: Regimen G CGI-C)\n- - PRO Transcription Rule: Patient Reported Outcomes (ALSFRS-R, ALSAQ-40, CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-S, PGI-C) must be transcribed into Rave exactly as completed by the patient. Do not interpolate missing responses. If a patient skips a question, mark the field as "ND" (Not Done) to clear the system query. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: PRO data entry)','\n',char(10)),'Patient reported outcomes will be assessed in this trial and will be defined in the RSA.','["assessments"]',3624,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:25','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Biomarker and DNA collection','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Biomarker and DNA collection\n- All participants will provide blood samples for biomarker assessments. [master:9.3.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional DNA collection)\n- Biofluid samples (blood, urine, and CSF) that will be used for biomarker assessments for Regimen G are captured in the Master Protocol. It should be noted that the CSF biofluid collection is optional and collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24 (rather than Baseline and Week 16 as noted in the Master Protocol). Additional biofluid samples (blood) will be collected for Regimen G for evaluation of plasma PK at scheduled timepoints described in the SOA. [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:8.8)','\n',char(10)),'All participants will provide blood samples for biomarker assessments.','["assessments"]',3625,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:26','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Lumbar puncture procedures','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Lumbar puncture procedures\n- The lumbar puncture (LP) procedure will be performed to collect CSF, as described in each RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: CSF collection)\n- Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: CSF collection)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: second lumbar puncture)\n- The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including: [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Local pain at injection site\nReaction to anesthetic agents\n• Bleeding at needle entrance site\n• Infection at needle entrance site\n• Post-LP low-pressure headache [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- The procedure must be performed by the SI or another staff member with the appropriate qualifications, experience, and training in performing LPs per institutional policy. When possible, LPs should be performed with an atraumatic Sprotte needle to reduce the risk of post-LP headache. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: LP procedures)\n- 16. CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional CSF)\n- a) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 CSF)\n- b) CSF collection for the ET visit will be performed only if the participant has consented to the lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G ET CSF)','\n',char(10)),'The lumbar puncture (LP) procedure will be performed to collect CSF, as described in each RSA.','["assessments"]',3626,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:27','piece','','Assessments and procedures > PK collection and dosing coordination','Assessments and procedures',replace('## PK collection and dosing coordination\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)\n- 15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G PK collection)\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 not in morning)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times.\n- Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15).\n- Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK data entry)','\n',char(10)),'On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic.','["assessments"]',3627,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:28','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Unscheduled safety assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Unscheduled safety assessments\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: unscheduled assessments)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix.\n2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen.\n3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add.\n4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: unscheduled assessments)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["assessments"]',3628,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:29','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE assessment schedule','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE assessment schedule\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [7] / Week 32 / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Week 36 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [7] / Week 40 / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Week 44 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 / Week 48 / Phone / Day168 ± 3 | Week 28 [7] / Week 52 / Clinic / Day 196 ± 14 | Week 40 [7] / Week 64 / Clinic / Day 280 ± 14 | Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 / Week 76 / Clinic / Day 364 ± 14 | All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] / Week 89 and Week 102 / Clinic / 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks | Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11)\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants with reproductive potential)','\n',char(10)),'Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 ','["assessments"]',3629,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:3','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Screening assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Screening assessments\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Assess Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria:\nObtain ALS and medical history\nObtain demographics\nPerform Vital Capacity\nPerform physical examination\nPerform neurological examination\nMeasure vital signs including height and weight\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nPerform 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG)\nCollect blood samples for clinical screening assessments (including coagulation parameters) and, for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP), for pregnancy test Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood biomarker sample Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Schedule the Regimen Specific Screening Visit if the participant passes screening for the Master Protocol [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: ineligible participants)\n- Once assigned to a regimen, participant eligibility will be confirmed using regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if any, during the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: regimen-specific screening)\n- 1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. At the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit, participants will have regimen-specific eligibility criteria assessed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 13. Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0). [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eli','["assessments"]',3603,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:30','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 4 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 4 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 4)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n7. Review concomitant medications\n8. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n9. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n11. Perform study drug compliance\n12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 4)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following proce','["assessments"]',3630,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:31','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 8 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 8 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 8)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 8)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following pro','["assessments"]',3631,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:32','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE telephone assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE telephone assessments\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 12)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 12)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 20)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 20)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 24)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit • Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 24)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following','["assessments"]',3632,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:33','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 16 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 16 assessments\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 16)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 16)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n12. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n14. Dispense study drug to participant\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 16)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["assessments"]',3633,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:34','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 28 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 28 assessments\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 28)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 28)\n- 1. Collect vital signs, including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. ROADS\n16. PGI-C\n17. PGI-S\n18. CGI-C\n19. Dispense study drug to participant\n20. Perform study drug compliance\n21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 28)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["assessments"]',3634,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:35','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 40 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 40 assessments\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 40)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. -lead ECG\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 40)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The fo','["assessments"]',3635,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:36','piece','','Assessments and procedures > ATE Week 52 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## ATE Week 52 assessments\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 52)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 52)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n16. ROADS\n17. PGI-C\n18. PGI-S\n19. CGI-C\n20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["assessments"]',3636,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:37','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Assessments after ATE Week 52','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Assessments after ATE Week 52\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after ATE Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- 8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. Participants will continue in the ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G after ATE Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after ATE Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have c','["assessments"]',3637,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:38','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Off-treatment follow-up','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Off-treatment follow-up\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing study drug) (note: differs from master:6.5.1)','\n',char(10)),'These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period','["assessments"]',3638,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:39','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Early termination and safety calls','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Early termination and safety calls\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G ET CSF)\n- 10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G end of placebo-controlled period)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- 2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, CGI-C) (note: Footnote associated with table marker [11].)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["assessments"]',3639,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:4','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Repeat screening and failed screening','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Repeat screening and failed screening\n- If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP)\n• Reason for screen failure\n• Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to enrollment, interval following last use of an investigational treatment, or other criteria as applicable), then that participant may become eligible immediately after resolution of the disqualifying condition if resolution occurs within the screening period and re-screening is not required. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: screen failures)\n- If a participant fails to meet the vital capacity screening criterion on the day of the Master Protocol Screening visit, vital capacity may be reassessed on a different day during the initial screening period. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: vital capacity screen failures)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: re-screened participants)','\n',char(10)),'If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system:','["assessments"]',3604,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:40','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Missed assessments and deviations','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Missed assessments and deviations\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14] (applies to: pandemic-related missed assessments)','\n',char(10)),'Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., parti','["assessments"]',3640,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:41','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Survival and vital status','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Survival and vital status\n- The primary survival endpoint will be the composite of death or death equivalent. Death equivalent will be defined as use of permanent assisted ventilation (PAV), where PAV is defined as more than 22 hours per day of noninvasive or invasive mechanical ventilation for more than seven consecutive days. The date of PAV initiation is the first day of the consecutive days. The date of PAV initiation, where applicable, will be imputed as the fifteenth day of a month if not specified more precisely. [master:9.2.4] (applies to: survival assessments)\n- Information on whether a participant has died may be obtained from the participant''s family, from clinic notes, or from a publicly available data source like the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) National Death Index or the Social Security Death Index. [master:8.15] (applies to: all participants)\n- Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive and date last known to be PAV-free, will be determined by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 visit, as indicated in the SOA. At approximately the time of the last patient last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants to assess vital status. [master:8.15] (applies to: randomized participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:7.7)\n- Vital status may also be ascertained at later time points. An outside vendor will be used to ascertain death or date of last known alive for all randomized participants by using publicly available data sources. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g. participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method. [master:8.15] (applies to: randomized participants)\n- 11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. At approximately the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled portion of the regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points. An outside vendor will be used to ascertain death or date of last known alive for all randomized participants by using publicly available data sources. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g., participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants)\n- 1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: Footnote associated with table marker [10].)','\n',char(10)),'The primary survival endpoint will be the composite of death or death equivalent. Death equivalent will be defined as use of permanent assis','["assessments"]',3641,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:42','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Adverse event assessment','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Adverse event assessment\n- Examples of adverse events include: new conditions, worsening of pre-existing conditions, clinically significant abnormal physical examination signs (e.g., skin rash, peripheral edema, etc.), or clinically significant abnormal test results (e.g., lab values or vital signs), with the exception of outcome measure results, which are not being recorded as adverse events in this trial (they are being collected, but analyzed separately). Stable chronic conditions (e.g., diabetes, arthritis) that are present prior to the start of the study and do not worsen during the trial are NOT considered adverse events. Chronic conditions that occur more frequently (for intermittent conditions) or with greater severity, would be considered as worsened and therefore would be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Symptoms of progression/worsening of ALS, including ''normal'' progression, will not be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If discernible at the time of completing the AE log, a specific disease or syndrome rather than individual associated signs and symptoms should be identified by the SI and recorded on the AE log. However, if an observed or reported sign, symptom, or clinically significant laboratory anomaly is not considered by the SI to be a component of a specific disease or syndrome, then it should be recorded as a separate AE on the AE log. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- At each visit (including telephone interviews), the participant will be asked if they have had any problems or symptoms since their last visit in order to determine the occurrence of adverse events. If the participant reports an adverse event, site staff will probe further to determine: [master:10.2.1] (applies to: all visits)\n- 1. Type of event\n2. Date of onset and resolution (duration)\n3. Severity (mild, moderate, severe)\n4. Seriousness (does the event meet the above definition for an SAE)\n5. Causality, relation to investigational product and disease\n6. Action taken regarding investigational product\n7. Outcome [master:10.2.1] (applies to: reported adverse events)\n- The following guidelines will be used to describe severity. [master:10.2.1] (applies to: adverse event severity)\n- Mild - Events require minimal or no treatment and do not interfere with the participant''s daily activities.\n• Moderate - Events result in a low level of inconvenience or concern with the therapeutic measures. Moderate events may cause some interference with functioning.\n• Severe - Events interrupt a participant''s usual daily activity and may require systemic drug therapy or other treatment. Severe events are usually potentially life-threatening or incapacitating. Of note, the term "severe" does not necessarily equate to "serious". [master:10.2.1] (applies to: adverse event severity)\n- The relationship of the AE to the investigational product is specified by the SI based on temporal relationship and his/her clinical judgment, using the following definitions: [master:10.2.2] (applies to: adverse event relatedness)\n- Related - The AE is known to occur with the study intervention, there is a reasonable possibility that the study intervention caused the AE, or there is a temporal relationship between the study intervention and event. Reasonable possibility means that there is evidence to suggest a causal relationship between the study intervention and the AE.\n• Not Related - There is not a reasonable possibility that the administration of the study intervention caused the event, there is no temporal relationship between the study intervention and event onset, or an alternate etiology has been established. [master:10.2.2] (applies to: adverse event relatedness)','\n',char(10)),'Examples of adverse events include: new conditions, worsening of pre-existing conditions, clinically significant abnormal physical examinati','["assessments"]',3642,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:43','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Drug compliance assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Drug compliance assessments\n- The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- When participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 20. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled phone visits)\n- 3. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE phone visits) (note: Footnote associated with table marker [12].)','\n',char(10)),'The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). T','["assessments"]',3643,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:44','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Diagnostic assessment requirements','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Diagnostic assessment requirements\n- The diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis [ALS] requires: [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: ALS diagnosis)\n- A - The presence of:\n(A:1) evidence of lower motor neuron (LMN) degeneration by clinical, electrophysiology cal or neuropathologic examination, (A:2) evidence of upper motor neuron (UMN) degeneration by clinical examination, and (A:3) progressive spread of symptoms or signs within a region or to other regions, as determined by history or examination, together with B - The absence of:\n(B:I) electrophysiological and pathological evidence of other disease processes that might explain the signs of LMN and/or UMN degeneration, and (B:2) neuroimaging evidence of other disease processes that might explain the observed clinical and electrophysiological signs. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: ALS diagnosis)\n- A careful history, physical and neurological examination must search for clinical evidence of UMN and LMN signs in four regions [brainstem, cervical, thoracic, or lumbosacral spinal cord] (see Table 1) of the central nervous system [CNS]. Ancillary tests should be reasonably applied, as clinically indicated, to exclude other disease processes. These should include electrodiagnostic, neurophysiological, neuroimaging and clinical laboratory studies. Clinical evidence of LMN and UMN degeneration is required for the diagnosis of ALS. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: ALS diagnostic assessments)\n- A. Clinically Definite ALS is defined on clinical evidence alone by the presence of UMN, as well as LMN signs, in three regions.\nB. Clinically Probable ALS is defined on clinical evidence alone by UMN and LMN signs in at least two regions with some UMN signs necessarily rostral to (above) the LMN signs.\nC. Clinically Probable ALS - Laboratory-supported is defined when clinical signs of UMN and LMN dysfunction are in only one region, or when UMN signs alone are present in one region, and LMN signs defined by EMG criteria are present in at least two limbs, with proper application of neuroimaging and clinical laboratory protocols to exclude other causes.\nD. Clinically Possible ALS is defined when clinical signs of UMN and LMN dysfunction are found together in only one region or UMN signs are found alone in two or more regions; or LMN signs are found rostral to UMN signs and the diagnosis of Clinically Probable -Laboratory-supported ALS cannot be proven by evidence on clinical grounds in conjunction with electrodiagnostic, neurophysiologic, neuroimaging or clinical laboratory studies. Other diagnoses must have been excluded to accept a diagnosis of Clinically Possible ALS. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: ALS diagnostic classification)','\n',char(10)),'The diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis [ALS] requires:','["assessments"]',3644,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:45','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Regimen-specific additional assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Regimen-specific additional assessments\n- Additional assessments may be added and will be described in each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Additional assessments may be added and will be described in each RSA.','["assessments"]',3645,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:46','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Schedule framework and visit-date calculation','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Schedule framework and visit-date calculation\n- The Master Protocol allows a 36-week duration of placebo-Safety Call Follow-Up Refer to Phone RSA The Master Protocol sets out the minimum visit and assessment requirements that must be incorporated into each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'The Master Protocol allows a 36-week duration of placebo-Safety Call Follow-Up Refer to Phone RSA The Master Protocol sets out the minimum v','["assessments"]',3646,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:47','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Master screening assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Master screening assessments\n- ALS & Medical History Screening procedure(s) Version date 10/14/2024 Physical Examination Clinical Safety Labs Slow Vital Capacity Inclusion/Exclusion Neurological Exam Regimen Specific Dutcomesported Demographics 12-Lead ECG Vital Signst ALSERS-R Activity Review Safety Call Follow-Up Protocol Screening and as specified in the RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'ALS & Medical History Screening procedure(s) Version date 10/14/2024 Physical Examination Clinical Safety Labs Slow Vital Capacity Inclusion','["assessments"]',3647,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:48','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["assessments"]',3648,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:49','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Clinical safety laboratory tests','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Clinical safety laboratory tests\n- After a participant is assigned to a regimen, RSA " Clinical safety labs include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, liver function tests, and urinalysis. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'After a participant is assigned to a regimen, RSA " Clinical safety labs include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry pane','["assessments"]',3649,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:5','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Baseline assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Baseline assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, please refer to the RA for applicability. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- Randomize within the regimen\nCollect vital signs\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs, biomarkers, and optional DNA sequencing Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood biomarker sample Perform LP for CSF collection (as described in the RSA) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Baseline questionnaire Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-S\n12. -lead ECG\n6. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4)\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (biomarkers only; no CSF PK sample collected at baseline) [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline CSF)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 8. Administer the first dose of study drug only after the Baseline Visit procedures are completed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screen','["assessments"]',3605,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:50','piece','','Assessments and procedures > DNA and blood biomarkers','Assessments and procedures',replace('## DNA and blood biomarkers\n- Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is no','["assessments"]',3650,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:51','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed\n- Pregnancy testing is only repeated as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Pregnancy testing is only repeated as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy.','["assessments"]',3651,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:52','piece','','Assessments and procedures > First dose','Assessments and procedures',replace('## First dose\n- S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 17 Baseline Clinic Day 0 x8 Screening'' Regimen Refer to Specific Clinic RSA Screening'' Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days DNA Collection'' (optional) Administer/Dispense Study Accountability/Compliance Columbia-Suicide Severity Vital Status Determination if any, should be performed. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 ','["assessments"]',3652,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:53','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Vital-status follow-up timing','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Vital-status follow-up timing\n- Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebo- of the last participant''s last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each r','["assessments"]',3653,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:54','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone vi','["assessments"]',3654,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:55','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Telephone compliance checks and return reminders','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Telephone compliance checks and return reminders\n- A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 Visit, as indicated). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period','["assessments"]',3655,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:56','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Remote-visit allowances','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Remote-visit allowances\n- If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time 12 Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time 12 Visit may be conducted via phone','["assessments"]',3656,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:57','piece','','Assessments and procedures > CSF collection schedule and conditions','Assessments and procedures',replace('## CSF collection schedule and conditions\n- " CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'" CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA.','["assessments"]',3657,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:58','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Consent-linked adverse event recording','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Consent-linked adverse event recording\n- Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Participants will be monitored for adverse events from the time they sign consent for the Master Protocol until completion of their participation as defined in the RSA. [master:10.1.1]','\n',char(10)),'Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Parti','["assessments"]',3658,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:59','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required visits or assessments are missed or PK timing falls outside allowed windows, record protocol deviations subject to the stated illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required visits or assessments are missed or PK timing falls outside allowed windows, record protocol deviations subject to the stated illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["assessments"]',3659,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:6','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 4 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 4 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Collect vital signs\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done at Baseline) Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Schedule next Study Visit Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 8 Visit [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the ','["assessments"]',3606,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:60','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When assessments or vital-status checks identify a reportable safety event, notify the Coordinating Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When assessments or vital-status checks identify a reportable safety event, notify the Coordinating Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["assessments"]',3660,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:61','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When assessments identify clinically significant abnormalities or other AEs, document them on the designated AE CRFs and follow safety recording requirements.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When assessments identify clinically significant abnormalities or other AEs, document them on the designated AE CRFs and follow safety recording requirements.)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible AEs. All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. The Master Protocol MM shall promptly review all information relevant to the safety of the investigational product, including all SAEs. Special attention will be paid to those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational products) being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- - The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: SAE EDC entry) (note: differs from master:10.2.1; master:10.2.2)\n- A: Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log line)\n- A: You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: concomitant medication entry)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed','["assessments"]',3661,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:62','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When safety assessments reveal substantial intolerance or clinically significant abnormalities, follow the regimen''s dose-modification and interruption rules.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When safety assessments reveal substantial intolerance or clinically significant abnormalities, follow the regimen''s dose-modification and interruption rules.)\n- Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA. [master:7.2]\n- To adequately evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 participants should be maintained on the randomized treatment. For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Dose Modifications: The protocol permits a dose reduction to 100 mg due to intolerability. If the dose is adjusted, you must update the Planned Dose Level field, check "Yes" for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason (e.g., Adverse Event). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA.','["assessments"]',3662,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:63','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Draw order and specimen processing (When assessment blood samples are collected, follow the required draw order and specimen-specific processing instructions.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Draw order and specimen processing (When assessment blood samples are collected, follow the required draw order and specimen-specific processing instructions.)\n- Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- Blood must be collected in the sequence: Light Blue → Gold SST → Lavender EDTA. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- Always program centrifuges using Relative Centrifugal Force (RCF or g), not RPM. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- - Tube Type / Color: Light Blue (Sodium Citrate) | Target Assay: Coagulation | Processing Instructions: Invert 3-4x. Centrifuge at 1,500 x g for 15 min at Room Temp (20-25°C). Transfer plasma to cryovial. Freeze immediately. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: coagulation samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Gold SST (Serum Separator) | Target Assay: Safety Chem, Endocrinology | Processing Instructions: Invert 5x. Allow to clot upright for 30 min. Centrifuge at 1,500 - 2,000 x g for 10-15 min at Room Temp. Ship ambient. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety chemistry and endocrinology samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Lavender EDTA (3.0 mL) | Target Assay: Safety Hematology | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Do Not Centrifuge. Ship as ambient whole blood on day of collection. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety hematology samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Lavender EDTA (4.0 mL / 6.0 mL) | Target Assay: PK Plasma & Biomarkers | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Centrifuge at 1,500 - 2,000 x g for 10-15 min at 4°C (Refrigerated). Separate plasma into cryovials. Freeze at -70°C within 60 mins. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: PK plasma and biomarker samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Specialty Tubes | Target Assay: Genomics / DNA | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Do Not Centrifuge. Freeze immediately at -70°C. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: genomics and DNA samples)','\n',char(10)),'Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required.','["assessments"]',3663,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:64','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Informed consent: Sample consent and withdrawal (When collecting optional DNA or CSF samples, verify the relevant sample consent and respect any withdrawal of that consent.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Sample consent and withdrawal (When collecting optional DNA or CSF samples, verify the relevant sample consent and respect any withdrawal of that consent.)\n- Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their sample used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- However, it will not be possible to destroy samples that may have already been used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid sample consent withdrawal)\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- - DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 11. Optional CSF collection [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their ','["assessments"]',3664,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:65','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At the Week 24 transition assessment, follow ATE supply-transition requirements before administering the ATE dose.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At the Week 24 transition assessment, follow ATE supply-transition requirements before administering the ATE dose.)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD','["assessments"]',3665,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:66','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): EDC prohibited medication checks (When visit medication review identifies a potentially prohibited drug, perform the required prohibited-medication checks rather than merely recording its use.)','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): EDC prohibited medication checks (When visit medication review identifies a potentially prohibited drug, perform the required prohibited-medication checks rather than merely recording its use.)\n- Prohibited Medications Check: DNL343 protocol excludes the use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates (e.g., St. John''s wort, midazolam, itraconazole). [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: DNL343 regimen) (note: differs from regimen_g:s017; differs from regimen_g:4.2.2; differs from regimen_g:5.9; differs from regimen_g:s094)\n- Entering flagged medications into the ConMed log will fire a hard warning query alerting the CRA and Medical Monitor of a potential protocol violation. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: DNL343 regimen ConMed log)','\n',char(10)),'Prohibited Medications Check: DNL343 protocol excludes the use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates (e.g., St. John','["assessments"]',3666,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:7','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 8 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 8 assessments\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Collect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the ','["assessments"]',3607,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:8','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Regimen G Week 4 and 8 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Regimen G Week 4 and 8 assessments\n- These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8) (note: differs from master:8.5; differs from master:8.6)\n- 1. CNS-BFS [Week 8 only]\n12. -lead ECG\n3. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 4, postdose only on Week 8) o For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n4. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)','\n',char(10)),'These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedu','["assessments"]',3608,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:assessments:9','piece','','Assessments and procedures > Week 12 assessments','Assessments and procedures',replace('## Week 12 assessments\n- This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 16 Visit [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in ','["assessments"]',3609,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:1','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > General concomitant medication allowance and recording','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## General concomitant medication allowance and recording\n- Throughout the study, participants may be prescribed concomitant medications deemed necessary to provide adequate care. [master:7.5] (applies to: all participants throughout the study)\n- All concomitant medications and significant non-drug therapies, including supplements, received by a participant should be recorded on the appropriate source document and electronic Case Report Form (eCRF). [master:7.5] (applies to: all participants)\n- In addition to the assessments evaluated below, participants will provide information on their demographics, past medical history, including ALS, as well as concomitant medication usage. [master:9.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details on medications that may not be taken during the trial will be included in the RSA. [master:7.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- The medications indicated below are excluded during the study (unless otherwise specified) (See Section 5.9 for prohibited medications). [regimen_g:s092] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The lists below are not exhaustive; the use of any concomitant medication is at the discretion of the investigator who knows the participant’s medical history. [regimen_g:s092] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'Throughout the study, participants may be prescribed concomitant medications deemed necessary to provide adequate care.','["concomitant_medications"]',3101,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:10','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > BCRP and OAT3 substrates','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## BCRP and OAT3 substrates\n- 5. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for breast cancer resistance protein (BCRP) or organic anion transporter (OAT)3 transporters and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for BCRP or OAT3 and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug, and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 6 for common examples of sensitive substrates). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The medications in Table 6 are examples of substrates of BCRP or OAT3. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: DNL343 regimen; Table 6 medications)\n- Medications that are considered to have a wide therapeutic index are permitted, and participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of AEs noted in the respective medication’s prescribing information. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: DNL343 regimen participants taking wide therapeutic index BCRP or OAT3 substrates)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 6 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- Table 6: Substrates of BCRP or OAT3 Transporters\nTable columns: Substrate | Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-Life | Recommended Washout\n- Substrate: BCRP Substrates | Generic Drug Name: rosuvastatin | Drug Half-Life: 7.48 ± 1.65 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: BCRP Substrates | Generic Drug Name: sulfasalazine | Drug Half-Life: 22.45 ± 6.366 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: adefovir | Drug Half-Life: 7.48 ± 1.65 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: cefaclor | Drug Half-Life: 11 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: ceftizoxime | Drug Half-Life: 1.8 ± 0.8 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: famotidine | Drug Half-Life: 0.8 ± 0.2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: furosemide | Drug Half-Life: 20 ± 6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: methotrexate | Drug Half-Life: 15 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: oseltamivir carboxylate | Drug Half-Life: 6 to 10 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: penicillin G | Drug Half-Life: 7.6 ± 3.4 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'5. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for breast cancer resistance protein (BCRP) or organic anion transporter (OAT)','["concomitant_medications"]',3110,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:11','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Therapeutic index assessment','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Therapeutic index assessment\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates (See Appendix IV, Table 5 and 6 for common examples) would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates (See Appendix I','["concomitant_medications"]',3111,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:12','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Rescreening after washout','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Rescreening after washout\n- 9. If a participant is being re-screened, the disqualifying condition has not been resolved, or the mandatory wash-out duration has not occurred. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants being re-screened)\n- For all excluded therapies described in this section, rescreening of participants is allowed after the defined window has passed. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen participants excluded for therapies described in Section 5.9)','\n',char(10)),'9. If a participant is being re-screened, the disqualifying condition has not been resolved, or the mandatory wash-out duration has not occu','["concomitant_medications"]',3112,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:13','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants who discontinue investigational product and are followed under the ITT principle)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["concomitant_medications"]',3113,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:14','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Screening and baseline medication review','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Screening and baseline medication review\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person after random assignment to a regimen. This visit may be combined with the Baseline Visit, please refer to the RSA for applicability. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- Concomitant medication review [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, please refer to the RA for applicability. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eli','["concomitant_medications"]',3114,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:15','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Scheduled medication review','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Scheduled medication review\n- This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 2 Telephone Visit)\n- 3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 2 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12 Telephone Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.4)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 12 Telephone Visit) (note: differs from master:8.7)\n- 3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 12 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20 Telephone Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.6)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 20 Telephone Visit) (note: differs from master:8.9)\n- 3. Review of concomitant medications and adverse events [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 20 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 196 - 3 days after the Bascline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28 Telephone Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 224 + 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32 Telephone Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32 Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)\n- Review and document concomitant medications [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen sc','["concomitant_medications"]',3115,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:16','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > ATE medication review','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## ATE medication review\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- 1. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 4 ATE Visit)\n- 7. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 4 ATE Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 8 ATE Visit)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 8 ATE Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 16 ATE Visit)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 16 ATE Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 28 ATE Visit)\n- 9. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 28 ATE Visit)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 40 ATE Visit)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 40 ATE Visit)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 52 ATE Visit)\n- 9. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: DNL343 regimen Week 52 ATE Visit)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: DNL343 regimen ATE visits after Week 52)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: DNL343 regimen ATE visits after Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following pr','["concomitant_medications"]',3116,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:17','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > EDC indications','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## EDC indications\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: all Concomitant Medication log entries)\n- This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: all Concomitant Medication log entries)','\n',char(10)),'Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication.','["concomitant_medications"]',3117,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:18','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > EDC prohibited medication checks','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## EDC prohibited medication checks\n- Prohibited Medications Check: DNL343 protocol excludes the use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates (e.g., St. John''s wort, midazolam, itraconazole). [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: DNL343 regimen) (note: differs from regimen_g:s017; differs from regimen_g:4.2.2; differs from regimen_g:5.9; differs from regimen_g:s094)\n- Entering flagged medications into the ConMed log will fire a hard warning query alerting the CRA and Medical Monitor of a potential protocol violation. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: DNL343 regimen ConMed log)','\n',char(10)),'Prohibited Medications Check: DNL343 protocol excludes the use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates (e.g., St. John','["concomitant_medications"]',3118,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:19','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > EDC partial and unknown medication start dates','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## EDC partial and unknown medication start dates\n- Q: How do I handle partial or completely unknown start dates for concomitant medications or medical history? [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: concomitant medication start dates)\n- A: Rave utilizes a standard partial date format. If the day is unknown, enter UNK in the day field (e.g., UNK-AUG-2021). If both the month and day are unknown, enter UNK-UNK-YYYY. Leave the date completely blank only if the entire date, including the year, is unknown. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: concomitant medication start dates)','\n',char(10)),'Q: How do I handle partial or completely unknown start dates for concomitant medications or medical history?','["concomitant_medications"]',3119,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:2','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Riluzole','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Riluzole\n- 6. Participants must either not take riluzole or be on a stable dose of riluzole for ≥ 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants entering the Master Protocol)\n- Riluzole. Participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry must be on a stable dose for 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit and must intend to continue taking the same stable dosage throughout the study. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry)','\n',char(10)),'6. Participants must either not take riluzole or be on a stable dose of riluzole for ≥ 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit.','["concomitant_medications"]',3102,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:20','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Record deviations for non-medical stopping or new initiation of riluzole or edaravone, and assess prohibited medication use for protocol deviation reporting.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Record deviations for non-medical stopping or new initiation of riluzole or edaravone, and assess prohibited medication use for protocol deviation reporting.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["concomitant_medications"]',3120,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:21','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When a medication treats a new or worsening condition or causes an adverse event, document the event on the AE form rather than only in the medication log.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When a medication treats a new or worsening condition or causes an adverse event, document the event on the AE form rather than only in the medication log.)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible AEs. All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. The Master Protocol MM shall promptly review all information relevant to the safety of the investigational product, including all SAEs. Special attention will be paid to those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational products) being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- - The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: SAE EDC entry) (note: differs from master:10.2.1; master:10.2.2)\n- A: Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log line)\n- A: You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: concomitant medication entry)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed','["concomitant_medications"]',3121,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:22','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (If a concomitant medication-related event meets expedited reporting criteria, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (If a concomitant medication-related event meets expedited reporting criteria, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["concomitant_medications"]',3122,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:23','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Queries and missing data (When a medication entry triggers a warning or has an inconsistent indication, resolve the EDC query and reconcile the supporting records.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Queries and missing data (When a medication entry triggers a warning or has an inconsistent indication, resolve the EDC query and reconcile the supporting records.)\n- To ensure accuracy and completeness of the data set, logic and range checks as well as in-form rules will be built into the EDC system, and electronic queries will be created to track potential data issues. [master:12.2.2]\n- The sites will only have access to the queries concerning their own participants. [master:12.2.2]\n- They must be resolved promptly prior to data locks. [site_edc_guide:6] (applies to: queries)\n- 1. To correct erroneous data: Click the edit pencil next to the field. Select "Entry Error" or "New Information" from the dropdown. Input the correct data and click Save. The query will automatically resolve. Do not type the corrected data into the query comment box. [site_edc_guide:6]\n- 2. To explain data without changing it: If the data is correct but unusual (e.g., a clinically verified extreme heart rate), leave the data field alone. Type your explanation in the Query Response text box beneath the query message and click Save. The query status will change to "Answered" pending DM review. [site_edc_guide:6]\n- Rave utilizes a standard partial date format. If the day is unknown, enter UNK in the day field (e.g., UNK-AUG-2021). If both the month and day are unknown, enter UNK-UNK-YYYY. Leave the date completely blank only if the entire date, including the year, is unknown. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: concomitant medication or medical history start dates)\n- Do not type text (like "ND" or "N/A") into a numeric field. Look for the checkbox next to the field typically labeled "ND" (Not Done) or "UNK" (Unknown). Checking this box overrides the numeric requirement and resolves the system query. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: laboratory parameter not done)','\n',char(10)),'To ensure accuracy and completeness of the data set, logic and range checks as well as in-form rules will be built into the EDC system, and ','["concomitant_medications"]',3123,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:24','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Stopping investigational product (When prohibited medication use raises safety concerns, assess whether investigational product must be discontinued.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Stopping investigational product (When prohibited medication use raises safety concerns, assess whether investigational product must be discontinued.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product, the possibility of alternative modes of administration (e.g., via G-tube) will be detailed in the RSA. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants receiving oral investigational product)\n- A research participant may choose to discontinue the investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["concomitant_medications"]',3124,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:25','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (If investigational product is stopped because of concomitant medication concerns, document discontinuation and arrange return of unused product.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (If investigational product is stopped because of concomitant medication concerns, document discontinuation and arrange return of unused product.)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participants terminating participation)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all research participants)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["concomitant_medications"]',3125,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:26','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Repeat assessments during screening (If medication stability or washout requirements prevent eligibility, follow repeat-screening requirements before reconsidering enrollment.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Repeat assessments during screening (If medication stability or washout requirements prevent eligibility, follow repeat-screening requirements before reconsidering enrollment.)\n- If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to enrollment, interval following last use of an investigational treatment, or other criteria as applicable), then that participant may become eligible immediately after resolution of the disqualifying condition if resolution occurs within the screening period and re-screening is not required. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing time-dependent criteria)\n- If a participant fails to meet the vital capacity screening criterion on the day of the Master Protocol Screening visit, vital capacity may be reassessed on a different day during the initial screening period. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing vital capacity screening)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants re-screened after the initial screening window)','\n',char(10)),'If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to','["concomitant_medications"]',3126,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:27','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (If medication washout delays rescreening beyond the initial screening window, follow the rescreening consent requirements.)','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (If medication washout delays rescreening beyond the initial screening window, follow the rescreening consent requirements.)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants rescreened after the initial screening window)\n- The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants whose regimen participation is discontinued)','\n',char(10)),'If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Pr','["concomitant_medications"]',3127,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:3','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Edaravone','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Edaravone\n- 7. Participants must either not take edaravone or have completed at least one cycle (typically 14 days) of edaravone prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants entering the Master Protocol)\n- Edaravone. Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry must have completed at least one cycle of treatment (typically 14 days) prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit and must intend to continue taking the same stable dosage throughout the study. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry)','\n',char(10)),'7. Participants must either not take edaravone or have completed at least one cycle (typically 14 days) of edaravone prior to the Master Pro','["concomitant_medications"]',3103,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:4','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Riluzole and edaravone changes and missed doses','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Riluzole and edaravone changes and missed doses\n- If the SI determines that riluzole or edaravone should be discontinued or dose-adjusted for medical reasons, this will not be considered a protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking riluzole or edaravone)\n- If extenuating circumstances cause doses to be missed for reasons not intended or medically indicated (scheduling, drug availability, etc.), this will not be considered a protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking riluzole or edaravone)\n- If riluzole or edaravone is stopped for any non-medical reason (participant preference), this will be recorded as a minor protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking riluzole or edaravone)\n- If initiation of riluzole or edaravone therapy begins at any point during participation, this will be recorded as a minor protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants during participation)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI determines that riluzole or edaravone should be discontinued or dose-adjusted for medical reasons, this will not be considered a p','["concomitant_medications"]',3104,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:5','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Other investigational treatments and gene therapies','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Other investigational treatments and gene therapies\n- These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants entering the Master Protocol)\n- 4. Use of investigational treatments for ALS (off-label use or active participation in a clinical trial within 5 half-lives (if known) or 30 days (whichever is longer) prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Please refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for current list of experimental therapies) [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants entering the Master Protocol)\n- 5. Exposure at any time to any gene therapies under investigation for the treatment of ALS (off-label use or investigational). [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants entering the Master Protocol)\n- Participants should not receive other investigational products during the study. This includes marketed agents at experimental dosages that are being tested for the treatment of ALS. [master:7.5] (applies to: all participants during the study)','\n',char(10)),'These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens:','["concomitant_medications"]',3105,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:6','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Antiepileptic therapy','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Antiepileptic therapy\n- Participants who meet any of the following criteria will be excluded from study entry: [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen entry)\n- 1. Diagnosis of epilepsy currently requiring antiepileptic therapy, or seizure of any cause within 6 months of randomization [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen entry)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who meet any of the following criteria will be excluded from study entry:','["concomitant_medications"]',3106,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:7','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > CYP3A4 inhibitors and moderate inducers','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## CYP3A4 inhibitors and moderate inducers\n- The results indicate that DNL343 is not a sensitive substrate of CYP3A4 and that DNL343 can be coadministered with strong, moderate, or weak inhibitors of CYP3A4. [regimen_g:s017] (applies to: DNL343 regimen) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Additionally, these preliminary results show a minor contribution of CYP3A4 to the metabolism of DNL343, indicating that coadministration with moderate CYP3A4 inducers are unlikely to affect the PK of DNL343 and can be coadministered with DNL343 freely in this trial. [regimen_g:s017] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'The results indicate that DNL343 is not a sensitive substrate of CYP3A4 and that DNL343 can be coadministered with strong, moderate, or weak','["concomitant_medications"]',3107,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:8','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Strong CYP3A4/5 inducers','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Strong CYP3A4/5 inducers\n- 3. Use of prescription or over-the-counter (OTC) medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV Table 4). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The medications in Table 4 are examples of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and are excluded during the study. [regimen_g:s093] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- If needed, it is recommended that a tertiary source, such as the Drug Interactions Flockhart Table (found at https://drug-interactions.medicine.iu.edu/MainTable.aspx), be used for more information regarding other medications. [regimen_g:s093] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- Table 4: Strong CYP3A4/5 Inducers with the Drug Half-Life and Washout Periods\nTable columns: Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-life | Recommended Washout\n- Generic Drug Name: apalutamide | Drug Half-life: 4.2 d | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: carbamazepine | Drug Half-life: 15 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: phenytoin | Drug Half-life: 24 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: rifampin | Drug Half-life: 3.5 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: St. John’s wort | Drug Half-life: 36 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: bosentan | Drug Half-life: 5 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\nAbbreviations: CYP, cytochrome P450. [regimen_g:s093] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'3. Use of prescription or over-the-counter (OTC) medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 i','["concomitant_medications"]',3108,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:concomitant_medications:9','piece','','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned) > Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates','Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned)',replace('## Sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates\n- 4. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: DNL343 regimen) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 5 for common examples of sensitive substrates). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increase based on the respective medication’s prescribing information and discretion of the investigator and/or medical monitor. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: DNL343 regimen participants taking wide therapeutic index sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- The medications in Table 5 are examples of sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: DNL343 regimen; Table 5 medications) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increases based on the respective medication’s prescribing information. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: DNL343 regimen participants taking wide therapeutic index sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 5 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- Table 5: Sensitive CYP3A4/5 Substrates\nTable columns: Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-Life | Recommended Washout\n- Generic Drug Name: buspirone | Drug Half-Life: 5.0 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: darunavir | Drug Half-Life: 3–5 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: dronedarone | Drug Half-Life: 1.6 ± 0.2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: eplerenone | Drug Half-Life: 3–6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: felodipine | Drug Half-Life: 4.8 min, 1.5 h, and 9.1 h (IV); 11–16 h (IR tablet) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: indinavir | Drug Half-Life: 1.8 ± 0.4 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: lurasidone | Drug Half-Life: 18 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: maraviroc | Drug Half-Life: 13.2 (10.3–8.3) h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: nisoldipine | Drug Half-Life: 3.2 h (young), 6.5 h (elderly) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: quetiapine | Drug Half-Life: 6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: Quinine | Drug Half-Life: 11 ± 2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: saquinavir | Drug Half-Life: 7–12 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: sildenafil (when used for PAH) | Drug Half-Life: 2.4 ± 1.0 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: sirolimus | Drug Half-Life: 62.3 ± 16.2 h | Recommended Washout: 13 days\n- Generic Drug Name: tacrolimus | Drug Half-Life: 31.9 ± 10.5 h (based on radioactivity); 48.4 ± 12.3 h (based on tacrolimus concentrations) | Recommended Washout: 10 days\n- Generic Drug Name: ticagrelor | Drug Half-Life: 6.8 h (IV); 8.1 h (oral) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: tipranavir | Drug Half-Life: 4.8 h (healthy volunteers); 5.5 h (female patients); 6.0 h (male patients) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\nAbbreviations: CYP, cytochrome P450; IM, intramuscular; IR, immediate release; IV, intravenous; PAH, pulmonary arterial hypertension.\nNote: Sensitive substrates are drugs that demonstrate an increase in area under the concentration-time curve of ≥ 5-fold with strong inhibitors of a given metabolic pathway in clinical drug-drug interaction studies. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'4. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-liv','["concomitant_medications"]',3109,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:1','piece','','Informed consent > Consent standards and approval','Informed consent',replace('## Consent standards and approval\n- This study will be conducted in compliance with Title 21 Part 50 of the United States of America CFR and ICH Guidance Documents pertaining to informed consent. [master:1.3]\n- The protocol and any amendments as well as the participant informed consent will receive sIRB approval prior to initiation of the study. [master:1.2]','\n',char(10)),'This study will be conducted in compliance with Title 21 Part 50 of the United States of America CFR and ICH Guidance Documents pertaining t','["consent"]',3901,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:10','piece','','Informed consent > Rescreening and reassignment consent','Informed consent',replace('## Rescreening and reassignment consent\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants rescreened after the initial screening window)\n- The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants whose regimen participation is discontinued)','\n',char(10)),'If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Pr','["consent"]',3910,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:11','piece','','Informed consent > Voluntary participation and withdrawal','Informed consent',replace('## Voluntary participation and withdrawal\n- The participant will also be informed that they may refuse entry into the trial and are free to withdraw from the trial at any time without prejudice to future treatment. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- A research participant may withdraw consent for study participation. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- No justification is required for the decision. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participants withdrawing consent)','\n',char(10)),'The participant will also be informed that they may refuse entry into the trial and are free to withdraw from the trial at any time without ','["consent"]',3911,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:12','piece','','Informed consent > Follow-up after consent withdrawal','Informed consent',replace('## Follow-up after consent withdrawal\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE before Week 52)\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants withdrawing consent or terminating early)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-pers','["consent"]',3912,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:13','piece','','Informed consent > Sample consent and withdrawal','Informed consent',replace('## Sample consent and withdrawal\n- Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their sample used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- However, it will not be possible to destroy samples that may have already been used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid sample consent withdrawal)\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- - DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 11. Optional CSF collection [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their ','["consent"]',3913,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:14','piece','','Informed consent > Lumbar puncture risk discussion','Informed consent',replace('## Lumbar puncture risk discussion\n- The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including: [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)\n- Local pain at injection site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)\n- Reaction to anesthetic agents [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)\n- Bleeding at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)\n- Infection at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)\n- Post-LP low-pressure headache [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing lumbar puncture)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including:','["consent"]',3914,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:15','piece','','Informed consent > Consent-linked adverse event recording','Informed consent',replace('## Consent-linked adverse event recording\n- 6 Adverse events that occur after signing Master Protocol consent form will be recorded. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Participants will be monitored for adverse events from the time they sign consent for the Master Protocol until completion of their participation as defined in the RSA. [master:10.1.1]','\n',char(10)),'6 Adverse events that occur after signing Master Protocol consent form will be recorded.','["consent"]',3915,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:16','piece','','Informed consent > Electronic consent records and registration','Informed consent',replace('## Electronic consent records and registration\n- - Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: reconsented participants)\n- To register a subject who has signed the Master and Regimen-Specific Informed Consent Forms: [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration; Days -42 to -1)\n- 1. Click the Add Subject button on the Home Dashboard. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Informed Consent and the subject''s Year of Birth. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 3. Review the summary screen and click Confirm. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 4. The system will generate a unique Subject ID. Print or save the automated Screening Confirmation PDF for your source records. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)','\n',char(10)),'- Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent l','["consent"]',3916,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:17','piece','','Informed consent > Consent form monitoring','Informed consent',replace('## Consent form monitoring\n- Study Monitors will visit each study site during the course of the study to review source documentation, ICF, and the clinical facilities to ensure the study is conducted in accordance with the study protocol and compliance with ICH/GP and regulatory guidelines. [master:12.3.1]','\n',char(10)),'Study Monitors will visit each study site during the course of the study to review source documentation, ICF, and the clinical facilities to','["consent"]',3917,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:18','piece','','Informed consent > Permission for medical information disclosure','Informed consent',replace('## Permission for medical information disclosure\n- Upon the participant''s permission, medical information may be given to his or her personal physician or other appropriate medical personnel responsible for his or her welfare. [master:12.6.1] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'Upon the participant''s permission, medical information may be given to his or her personal physician or other appropriate medical personnel ','["consent"]',3918,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:19','piece','','Informed consent > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Informed consent',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["consent"]',3919,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:2','piece','','Informed consent > Capacity to consent','Informed consent',replace('## Capacity to consent\n- These are the inclusion criteria that research participants must meet to be eligible to enter the Master Protocol and are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. Capable of providing informed consent and complying with study procedures, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'These are the inclusion criteria that research participants must meet to be eligible to enter the Master Protocol and are common for all reg','["consent"]',3902,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:20','piece','','Informed consent > Recruitment after sIRB approval','Informed consent',replace('## Recruitment after sIRB approval\n- Research participants will be recruited from these centers, after approval by the sIRB. [master:6.1]','\n',char(10)),'Research participants will be recruited from these centers, after approval by the sIRB.','["consent"]',3920,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:21','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When consent is missed, late, or otherwise noncompliant, follow protocol-deviation documentation and reporting requirements.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When consent is missed, late, or otherwise noncompliant, follow protocol-deviation documentation and reporting requirements.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["consent"]',3921,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:22','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Enrollment, eligibility and consent records (When a participant signs consent or is reconsented, complete the required enrollment and consent records in EDC.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Enrollment, eligibility and consent records (When a participant signs consent or is reconsented, complete the required enrollment and consent records in EDC.)\n- Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. [master:6.1] (applies to: all enrolled participants)\n- This information will include reasons for screen failure for participants not assigned to a regimen, as well as a log of all participants randomized within a regimen, irrespective of whether they have been treated with the intervention or not. [master:6.1]\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: regimen screen failures)\n- If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP) [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reason for screen failure [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Inclusion/Exclusion: Master and Regimen-Specific criteria must be evaluated. If "Not Met" is selected for any criterion, the system will fire a hard edit check requiring the selection of a specific failure reason. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility]\n- The willingness of the participant to participate in the study will be documented in writing on a consent form approved by the sIRB, which will be signed and dated by the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will keep the original consent forms and a copy will be given to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All participants will be offered the opportunity to discuss participation with a licensed physician Investigator and the participant''s decision to accept or decline this opportunity will be documented in the research file. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the participant''s next visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 1. The site staff provides a copy of the informed consent form to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 2. The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 3. The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant over the phone or via telemedicine. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 4. The participant signs the informed consent form and provides the original signed consent form back to the site. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant via phone or telemedicine signs the informed consent form. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: reconsented participants)','\n',char(10)),'Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system.','["consent"]',3922,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:23','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Regimen re-assignment and re-screening (When a participant is rescreened or reassigned after reconsent, follow the repeat eligibility and regimen-reassignment requirements.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Regimen re-assignment and re-screening (When a participant is rescreened or reassigned after reconsent, follow the repeat eligibility and regimen-reassignment requirements.)\n- In some situations, an individual may be re-assigned to multiple different regimens, if eligible.\nThe procedures detailing the re-assignment process can be found in Section 8.1.2 Screen Failures and Re-Assignment. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants considered for re-assignment)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a specific regimen, but does not meet additional regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria for that regimen, then the research participant may be re-assigned immediately to a different regimen. If it is known at the time of regimen assignment that the participant is not eligible for the regimen, a participant may be administratively screen-failed for a regimen prior to regimen consent and screening. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants ineligible for their assigned regimen)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a regimen, and is randomized within that regimen, but their participation in that regimen is discontinued, that participant may have the opportunity to be re-screened for the Master Protocol. The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. Re-screening for the Master Protocol and potential re-assignment into a different regimen, if available, may occur due to the following situations: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- A regimen is stopped due to futility or success [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- The research participant may be re-screened after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants whose regimen stopped due to futility or success)\n- A research participant completes their Week 36 Visit in the placebo-controlled period and terminates their participation in the study [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- A research participant discontinues participation in a regimen for personal reasons (e.g., inconvenience of the intervention, mode of administration, required assessments) or due to an adverse event, or is discontinued by the SI [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- The research participant may not be re-screened until after the date that their 36-Week Visit would have occurred, had they completed participation in that regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants discontinuing for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI)\n- Upon completion of a regimen, a research participant may be re-screened into the Master Protocol and assigned to another regimen after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. [master:11.2.4] (applies to: participants completing a regimen)','\n',char(10)),replace('In some situations, an individual may be re-assigned to multiple different regimens, if eligible.\nThe procedures detailing the re-assignment','\n',char(10)),'["consent"]',3923,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:24','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (Once Master Protocol consent is signed, document adverse events in the required CRFs, including events occurring before treatment.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (Once Master Protocol consent is signed, document adverse events in the required CRFs, including events occurring before treatment.)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible AEs. All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. The Master Protocol MM shall promptly review all information relevant to the safety of the investigational product, including all SAEs. Special attention will be paid to those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational products) being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- - The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: SAE EDC entry) (note: differs from master:10.2.1; master:10.2.2)\n- A: Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log line)\n- A: You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: concomitant medication entry)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed','["consent"]',3924,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:25','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When an event after consent meets expedited reporting criteria, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When an event after consent meets expedited reporting criteria, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["consent"]',3925,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:26','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: EDC and IRT termination steps (When consent withdrawal ends study participation, complete the EDC and IRT termination steps.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: EDC and IRT termination steps (When consent withdrawal ends study participation, complete the EDC and IRT termination steps.)\n- - Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded subjects)','\n',char(10)),'- Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completi','["consent"]',3926,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:27','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Discontinuation returns and supply closure (When consent withdrawal results in study-drug discontinuation, arrange return of unused product and close out supply accountability.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Discontinuation returns and supply closure (When consent withdrawal results in study-drug discontinuation, arrange return of unused product and close out supply accountability.)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: investigational product discontinuation)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participant termination)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["consent"]',3927,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:28','piece','','Informed consent > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample identification, storage and consent (When a participant declines or withdraws consent for sample use, follow the sample storage and consent restrictions.)','Informed consent',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample identification, storage and consent (When a participant declines or withdraws consent for sample use, follow the sample storage and consent restrictions.)\n- All samples will be labeled with a code. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- The code will not include any identifiable information but will be linked to a specific participant, visit, and sample by data entry of the code into the EDC. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- There is no scheduled date on which the samples will be destroyed. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- Samples may be stored for research until they are used, damaged, decayed or otherwise unfit for analysis. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their sample used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- However, it will not be possible to destroy samples that may have already been used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- Specific instructions regarding the collection, processing, storage, and shipment of these samples will be provided in the study Laboratory Manual. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid samples)\n- Details of any additional specific biofluid samples required for a regimen will be described in the corresponding RSA. [master:9.4] (applies to: regimen-specific biofluid samples)\n- A participant Neurological Global Unique Identifier (NeuroGUID), or its derivitives'', will be used as the identifier for participant''s samples participating in this study. [master:12.5] (applies to: participant samples)','\n',char(10)),'All samples will be labeled with a code.','["consent"]',3928,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:3','piece','','Informed consent > Information and consent before procedures','Informed consent',replace('## Information and consent before procedures\n- At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), participants will be informed about the nature and purpose of the Master Protocol, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- Participants will be given adequate time to ask questions and become familiar with the Master Protocol prior to providing consent to participate. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Written informed consent will be obtained from participants before any study procedures or assessments, not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), are done and after the study objectives and endpoints, methods, anticipated benefits, and potential hazards are explained. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- No study procedures are performed prior to the signing of the Master Protocol ICF. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- All participants will sign the Master Protocol ICF prior to undergoing any study tests or procedures. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)','\n',char(10)),'At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, w','["consent"]',3903,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:4','piece','','Informed consent > Consent documentation and copies','Informed consent',replace('## Consent documentation and copies\n- Participants will give their documented informed consent to participate in the Master Protocol and will be provided with a copy of the fully executed Master Protocol consent form for their records. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- The willingness of the participant to participate in the study will be documented in writing on a consent form approved by the sIRB, which will be signed and dated by the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will keep the original consent forms and a copy will be given to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants will give their written consent to participate in their assigned regimen and will be provided with a copy of the fully executed regimen-specific consent form for their records. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will give their documented informed consent to participate in the Master Protocol and will be provided with a copy of the fully','["consent"]',3904,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:5','piece','','Informed consent > Consent personnel and ongoing discussion','Informed consent',replace('## Consent personnel and ongoing discussion\n- Informed consent will be obtained by the site investigator or other licensed staff who are trained on the study and delegated to obtain consent. [master:6.2]\n- The SI, or other licensed staff, will be involved in explaining the study and will play a role during the consent process, answering any questions in conjunction with the coordinator and ensuring that the process is carried out correctly. [master:6.2]\n- All participants will be offered the opportunity to discuss participation with a licensed physician Investigator and the participant''s decision to accept or decline this opportunity will be documented in the research file. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Site study staff performing consent must provide participants adequate information to allow for an informed decision about participation, facilitate the potential participant''s understanding of the information, and provide ample time and opportunity to inquire about details of the trial. [master:6.2]\n- The SI, nor the site study staff, should coerce or unduly influence a potential participant to participate or to continue to participate in the study. [master:6.2]\n- Site study staff must continuously provide information as the clinical investigation progresses or as the participant or situation requires. [master:6.2]','\n',char(10)),'Informed consent will be obtained by the site investigator or other licensed staff who are trained on the study and delegated to obtain cons','["consent"]',3905,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:6','piece','','Informed consent > Master and regimen consent sequence','Informed consent',replace('## Master and regimen consent sequence\n- Participants that provide consent to the Master Protocol will be screened for Master Protocol-level inclusion and exclusion criteria. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants will then be informed about the intervention specific to their assigned regimen and any participation/termination conditions or risks and benefits specific to that regimen. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- Informed consent will be conducted for the Master Protocol and subsequently for the applicable RSA to which that participant was assigned. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Those participants who are not found eligible for the Master Protocol will not be consented for any regimen. [master:6.2] (applies to: participants ineligible for the Master Protocol)\n- Once a participant''s Master Protocol eligibility is confirmed the participant will be randomly assigned to a regimen and will undergo informed consent for that regimen at their scheduled Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. [master:8.1] (applies to: participants eligible for the Master Protocol)\n- This visit will take place in-person after random assignment to a regimen. [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- This visit may be combined with the Baseline Visit, please refer to the RSA for applicability. [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- Obtain written regimen-specific informed consent from participant [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- - Written Informed Consent [2] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Any participant who signs the Master Protocol consent form will be considered enrolled in the Platform Trial. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If it is known at the time of regimen assignment that the participant is not eligible for the regimen, a participant may be administratively screen-failed for a regimen prior to regimen consent and screening. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants known to be ineligible at regimen assignment)\n- Phone 196+3 Week Day 28 participants will be consented a second time via the neg win-save rec ice-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria assessed, if applicable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Participants that provide consent to the Master Protocol will be screened for Master Protocol-level inclusion and exclusion criteria.','["consent"]',3906,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:7','piece','','Informed consent > Non-English consent','Informed consent',replace('## Non-English consent\n- Consent for non-English speakers may be obtained using an sIRB approved consent form that has been translated into the participant''s spoken language. [master:6.2] (applies to: non-English speakers)\n- Sites should follow study MOPs and local policy on the use of translators. [master:6.2] (applies to: non-English speakers)','\n',char(10)),'Consent for non-English speakers may be obtained using an sIRB approved consent form that has been translated into the participant''s spoken ','["consent"]',3907,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:8','piece','','Informed consent > Reconsent triggers and timing','Informed consent',replace('## Reconsent triggers and timing\n- Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: participants already enrolled; Master Protocol amendments)\n- Addition of regimens will not require re-consent. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: addition of regimens)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the Master Protocol or a regimen if new procedures or information is added in the future. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant''s next visit. [master:6.2] (applies to: participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from regimen_g:6)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from master:6.2)','\n',char(10)),'Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol','["consent"]',3908,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:consent:9','piece','','Informed consent > Remote reconsent','Informed consent',replace('## Remote reconsent\n- If the participant''s next visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [master:6.2] (applies to: participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from regimen_g:6)\n- 1. The site staff provides a copy of the informed consent form to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 2. The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 3. The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant over the phone or via telemedicine. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 4. The participant signs the informed consent form and provides the original signed consent form back to the site. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant via phone or telemedicine signs the informed consent form. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- If the participant’s next in-clinic visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from master:6.2)\n- a) The site staff sends copy of the informed consent form to the participant. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)\n- b) The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)\n- c) The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)\n- d) The participant signs the informed consent form and returns the original signed consent form back to the site. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)\n- e) Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant signs the informed consent form. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)','\n',char(10)),'If the participant''s next visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures:','["consent"]',3909,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:1','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Eligibility and contraception duration','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Eligibility and contraception duration\n- These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. If female, breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, planning to become pregnant during the study, or of child-bearing potential and unwilling to use effective contraception, for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: female participants)\n- 7. If male of reproductive capacity, unwilling to use effective contraception for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: male participants of reproductive capacity)\n- There are RSA-specific requirements that apply to Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6. [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- For further details of contraceptive requirements for this RSA, please refer to Section 5.10.2. [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens:','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3401,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:10','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Placebo-controlled clinic testing and review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Placebo-controlled clinic testing and review\n- These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- 4. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Weeks 4 and 8)\n- 5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16)\n- 9. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 16)\n- 10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16)\n- This visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination) (note: differs from master:8.10)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination)\n- 10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 24 or Early Termination)\n- 11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination)','\n',char(10)),'These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3410,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:11','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Placebo-controlled telephone contraception review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Placebo-controlled telephone contraception review\n- This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- 4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- 4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)\n- 4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3411,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:12','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > ATE clinic pregnancy testing and review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## ATE clinic pregnancy testing and review\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- 5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 4 ATE)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 8 ATE)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 16 ATE)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 28 ATE)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 40 ATE)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Week 52 ATE)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)\n- Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; after Week 52 ATE)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3412,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:13','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > ATE telephone contraception review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## ATE telephone contraception review\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2 ATE)\n- 5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12 ATE)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20 ATE)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 ATE)\n- Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3413,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:14','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Safety follow-up contraception review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Safety follow-up contraception review\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G participants continuing into ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE follow-up)\n- Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE follow-up)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early) [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early discontinuation)\n- 2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE completion)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3414,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:15','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Early termination testing and review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Early termination testing and review\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G early termination or consent withdrawal)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early termination before Week 52)\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early discontinuation after Week 52)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-pers','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3415,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:16','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Pregnancy event recording and congenital anomalies','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Pregnancy event recording and congenital anomalies\n- A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria: [master:10.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 5. Results in congenital anomaly or birth defect in the offspring of the participant (whether the participant is male or female. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: offspring of male or female participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: AE recording)','\n',char(10)),'A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria:','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3416,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:17','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3417,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:18','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a pregnancy or congenital anomaly is learned of, apply expedited reporting requirements, including notification to the CC within 24 hours for reportable events.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a pregnancy or congenital anomaly is learned of, apply expedited reporting requirements, including notification to the CC within 24 hours for reportable events.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3418,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:19','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: SAE criteria (When offspring of a male or female participant has a congenital anomaly or birth defect, classify the event as an SAE.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: SAE criteria (When offspring of a male or female participant has a congenital anomaly or birth defect, classify the event as an SAE.)\n- A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria: [master:10.1.2]\n- 1. Results in death. [master:10.1.2]\n- 2. Is life threatening: that is, poses an immediate risk of death as the event occurred. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This serious criterion applies if the study participant, in the view of the SI or Sponsor, is at immediate risk of death from the AE as it occurs. It does not apply if an AE that might hypothetically have caused death if it were more severe. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: life-threatening criterion)\n- 3. Requires inpatient hospitalization or prolongation of existing hospitalization. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. Hospitalization for an elective procedure (including elective PEG tube/g-tube/feeding tube placement) or a routinely scheduled treatment is not an SAE by this criterion because an elective or scheduled "procedure" or a "treatment" is not an untoward medical occurrence. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: hospitalization criterion)\n- 4. Results in persistent or significant disability or incapacity. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This criterion applies if the "disability" caused by the reported AE results in a substantial disruption of the participant''s ability to carry out normal life functions. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: disability criterion)\n- 5. Results in congenital anomaly or birth defect in the offspring of the participant (whether the participant is male or female. [master:10.1.2]\n- 6. Necessitates medical or surgical intervention to preclude permanent impairment of a body function or permanent damage to a body structure. [master:10.1.2]\n- 7. Important medical events that may not result in death, are not life-threatening, or do not require hospitalization may also be considered SAEs when, based upon appropriate medical judgment, they may jeopardize the participant and may require medical or surgical intervention to prevent one of the outcomes listed in this definition. Examples of such medical events include blood dyscrasias or convulsions that do not result in inpatient hospitalization, or the development of drug dependency or drug abuse. [master:10.1.2]\n- An inpatient hospital admission in the absence of a precipitating, treatment-emergent, clinical adverse event may meet criteria for "seriousness" but is not an adverse experience, and will therefore, not be considered an SAE. An example of this would include a social admission (participant admitted for other reasons than medical, e.g., lives far from the hospital, has no place to sleep). [master:10.1.2]\n- A serious, suspected adverse drug reaction is an SAE that, in the opinion of the SI or Sponsor, there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event. [master:10.1.2]\n- The SI is responsible for classifying adverse events as serious or non-serious. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: SI)','\n',char(10)),'A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria:','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3419,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:2','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Female contraceptive requirements','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Female contraceptive requirements\n- Participants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- 1. For female participants, one of the following must apply: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants)\n- 1. Postmenopausal (defined as no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without an alternative medical cause, with a follicle-stimulating hormone level of > 40\nIU/L or mIU/mL at screening) [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants)\n- 2. Surgically sterilized (eg, hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, bilateral oophorectomy) at least 6 months before dosing [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants)\n- c. For participants of childbearing potential: both the participant and the male partner must use highly effective contraception at the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. Highly effective contraception consists of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. The other must be a highly effective method (as defined in Appendix III), which includes one of the following: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential and male partners)\n- 1. Intrauterine device [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 2. Intrauterine hormone-releasing system [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 3. Bilateral tubal ligation [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 4. Abstinence, defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ (calendar, symptothermal, postovulation methods) is considered not reliable and would not be acceptable. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- d. For participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner, the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential. Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential with azoospermic male partners)','\n',char(10)),'Participants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3402,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:20','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required contraception, pregnancy testing, or contraception review is not followed, apply protocol-deviation documentation and reporting rules.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required contraception, pregnancy testing, or contraception review is not followed, apply protocol-deviation documentation and reporting rules.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3420,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:21','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Informed consent: Information and consent before procedures (Before study-specific screening pregnancy testing, obtain informed consent.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Information and consent before procedures (Before study-specific screening pregnancy testing, obtain informed consent.)\n- At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), participants will be informed about the nature and purpose of the Master Protocol, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- Participants will be given adequate time to ask questions and become familiar with the Master Protocol prior to providing consent to participate. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Written informed consent will be obtained from participants before any study procedures or assessments, not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), are done and after the study objectives and endpoints, methods, anticipated benefits, and potential hazards are explained. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- No study procedures are performed prior to the signing of the Master Protocol ICF. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- All participants will sign the Master Protocol ICF prior to undergoing any study tests or procedures. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)','\n',char(10)),'At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, w','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3421,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:22','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: First dose (Before the first dose, complete Baseline pregnancy testing and contraception review along with all other Baseline procedures.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: First dose (Before the first dose, complete Baseline pregnancy testing and contraception review along with all other Baseline procedures.)\n- Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants at Baseline)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline optional CSF collection)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 17 Baseline Clinic Day 0 x8 Screening'' Regimen Refer to Specific Clinic RSA Screening'' Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days DNA Collection'' (optional) Administer/Dispense Study Accountability/Compliance Columbia-Suicide Severity Vital Status Determination if any, should be performed. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3422,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:23','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Screen failure documentation (When pregnancy, breastfeeding, pregnancy plans, or unwillingness to use required contraception causes screening failure, complete screen-failure documentation.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Screen failure documentation (When pregnancy, breastfeeding, pregnancy plans, or unwillingness to use required contraception causes screening failure, complete screen-failure documentation.)\n- Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. This information will include reasons for screen failure for participants not assigned to a regimen, as well as a log of all participants randomized within a regimen, irrespective of whether they have been treated with the intervention or not. [master:6.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: participants ineligible for the Master Protocol)\n- Once assigned to a regimen, participant eligibility will be confirmed using regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if any, during the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- - Inclusion/Exclusion: Master and Regimen-Specific criteria must be evaluated. If "Not Met" is selected for any criterion, the system will fire a hard edit check requiring the selection of a specific failure reason. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: all participants)\n- Any participant who signs the Master Protocol consent form will be considered enrolled in the Platform Trial. If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP) [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reason for screen failure [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)','\n',char(10)),'Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. This infor','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3423,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:24','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Safety-related dose changes (When suspected pregnancy or contraceptive failure raises a treatment-safety concern, apply the safety-related treatment interruption or discontinuation process.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Safety-related dose changes (When suspected pregnancy or contraceptive failure raises a treatment-safety concern, apply the safety-related treatment interruption or discontinuation process.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- A SI or designee may terminate a research participant if a medical condition or other situation occurs such that continued participation would not be in the best interest of the participant. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3424,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:25','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When pregnancy concerns require repeated screening tests or out-of-window safety laboratory testing, use the required unscheduled-visit EDC entry process.)','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When pregnancy concerns require repeated screening tests or out-of-window safety laboratory testing, use the required unscheduled-visit EDC entry process.)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK reconciliation)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK entry)','\n',char(10)),'If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit f','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3425,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:3','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Male contraceptive requirements and sperm donation','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Male contraceptive requirements and sperm donation\n- 2. For male participants: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants)\n- a. When engaging in sex with a female partner of childbearing potential, both the male participant and his female partner must use highly effective contraception consisting of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom, and the other of which must be a highly effective form of birth control (as defined in Appendix III) used by the female partner. This is required from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- b. For azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G azoospermic male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Male participants must not donate sperm at any time from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants)','\n',char(10)),'2. For male participants:','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3403,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:4','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Female contraception guidance','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Female contraception guidance\n- Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom) [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Intrauterine device (IUD) [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Intrauterine hormone-releasing system (IUS) b [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Bilateral tubal ligation c [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Azoospermic male partner of woman of childbearing potential participantd [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Alternative Highly Effective Method [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Abstinence defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire duration of the study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ is considered not reliable and would not be acceptablee [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- Notes: Male condom and female condom should not be used together (due to risk of failure with friction). [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 1. Contraceptive use by participants should be consistent with local regulations regarding the use of contraceptive methods for those participating in clinical studies. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 2. Failure rate of < 1% per year when used consistently and correctly. Typical use failure rates differ from those when used consistently and correctly. c Essure fallopian tube coil placement is not accepted, as the permanent contraception failure rate is likely greater than 1%. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 1. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential and the absence of sperm has been confirmed. If not, an additional highly effective method of contraception should be used. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential with azoospermic partners)\n- Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Periodic abstinence (calendar, symptothermal, post ovulation methods), withdrawal (coitus interruptus),\nspermicides only, and lactational amenorrhea method are NOT acceptable methods of contraception. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom)','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3404,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:5','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Male contraception guidance','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Male contraception guidance\n- Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom) [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Intrauterine device (IUD) [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Intrauterine hormone-releasing system (IUS) b [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Bilateral tubal ligation c [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Implantable progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Combined (estrogen- and progestogen-containing) hormonal contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation − oral − intravaginal − transdermal − injectable [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation) − Oral − injectable [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Azoospermic male participantd [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Alternative Highly Effective Method [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Abstinence defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire duration of the study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ is considered not reliable and would not be acceptablee. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Notes: Male condom and female condom should not be used together (due to risk of failure with friction). [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- 1. Contraceptive use by participants should be consistent with local regulations regarding the use of contraceptive methods for those participating in clinical studies. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- 2. Failure rate of < 1% per year when used consistently and correctly. Typical use failure rates differ from those when used consistently and correctly. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- 3. Essure fallopian tube coil placement is not accepted, as the permanent contraception failure rate is likely greater than 1%. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- 4. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential and the absence of sperm has been confirmed. If not, an additional highly effective method of contraception should be used. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G azoospermic male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)\n- Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants)\n- 5. Periodic abstinence (calendar, symptothermal, post ovulation methods), withdrawal (coitus interruptus), spermicides only, and lactational amenorrhea method are NOT acceptable methods of contraception. [regimen_g:s090] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with female partners of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom)','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3405,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:6','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Collect blood samples for clinical screening assessments (including coagulation parameters) and, for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP), for pregnancy test [master:8.1] (applies to: women of child bearing potential at Master Protocol Screening)\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: clinical safety laboratory tests)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) [master:9.1.2] (applies to: WOCBP) (note: differs from master:8.5, master:8.6, master:8.8, master:8.10, master:8.10-62)\n- The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: participants requiring additional testing)\n- Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: WOCBP; placebo-controlled period)\n- Pregnancy testing is only repeated as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eli','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3406,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:7','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Master Protocol follow-up pregnancy testing','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Master Protocol follow-up pregnancy testing\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.5] (applies to: WOCBP; Week 4) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.6] (applies to: WOCBP; Week 8) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.5)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs (including coagulation parameters) and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.8] (applies to: WOCBP; Week 16) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.10] (applies to: WOCBP; Week 24) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.10-62] (applies to: WOCBP; Week 36) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3407,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:8','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Contraceptive confirmation and documentation','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Contraceptive confirmation and documentation\n- Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- In addition to procedures in the Master Protocol, the following DNL343-specific procedures will be conducted during the study: [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 9. Urine Pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential)\n- 10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants and partners)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3408,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:contraception_pregnancy:9','piece','','Contraception and pregnancy > Baseline testing and contraception review','Contraception and pregnancy',replace('## Baseline testing and contraception review\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G women of childbearing potential; Baseline)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place on Day 0.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',3409,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:1','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Dose and treatment duration','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Dose and treatment duration\n- Treatment duration of the placebo-controlled period is a maximum of 36-weeks for each regimen. The duration of the Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period will be described in the respective RSAs. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s007)\n- The planned duration of the placebo-controlled treatment period of the study is 24 weeks. The duration of the ATE period is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks (i.e., 76 weeks total treatment duration). The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks of ATE (i.e., 102 weeks total from Baseline visit). [regimen_g:s007] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:protocol-summary)\n- Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extension (ATE) where all participants will receive DNL343 200 mg QD for up to an additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:s004] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Regimen G is a multi-center, randomized, placebo-controlled trial, testing active dose of DNL343 200 mg administered orally QD versus placebo. [regimen_g:3] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Following 24 weeks of double-blinded treatment, all participants will enter the active treatment extension (ATE) period to receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:3] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Each participant who meets all eligibility criteria for the Regimen will be randomized to receive either DNL343 200 mg or placebo for approximately 24 weeks of placebo-controlled treatment and will continue on to the ATE period for up to an additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:4.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Study drug administered are described in Table 2. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Dose Formulation | Placebo-Controlled Period: Granules in stick pack | ATE Period: Granules in stick pack\n- Unit Dose Strength(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 100 mg | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo | ATE Period: 100 mg\n- Dose Level(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 200 mg QD | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo QD | ATE Period: 200 mg QD\n- Route of Administration | Placebo-Controlled Period: Oral or feeding tube | ATE Period: Oral or feeding tube [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Abbreviations: ATE, active treatment extension; QD, once daily. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- DNL343 (or placebo) is administered as oral granules (200 mg QD via two 100 mg stick packs). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Regimen G utilizes a double-blind, placebo-controlled design for the initial 24-week period, followed by an open-label Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period where all subjects receive active DNL343. [site_pharmacy_manual:2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Parameter: Formulation | DNL343 (Active): Granules for oral/enteral suspension | Matching Placebo: Granules for oral/enteral suspension\n- Parameter: Strength | DNL343 (Active): 100 mg per stick pack | Matching Placebo: 0 mg per stick pack [site_pharmacy_manual:2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Parameter: Secondary Packaging | Both: Cardboard carton containing 14 stick packs (segmented into 7 daily doses of 2 packets each). Labeling complies with Annex 13 and local regulations.\n- Parameter: Dosing Regimen | Both: 200 mg Once Daily (QD). Subjects consume two (2) stick packs simultaneously each day. [site_pharmacy_manual:2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE (ie, 102 weeks total from the Baseline visit). The Industry Partner may consider extending or discontinuing the ATE period depending on the results from the primary analysis. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Treatment duration of the placebo-controlled period is a maximum of 36-weeks for each regimen. The duration of the Active Treatment Extensio','["dosing"]',3201,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:10','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Placebo-controlled visit dispensing and compliance','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Placebo-controlled visit dispensing and compliance\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Instruct participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 4 visit [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Schedule next Study Visit Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 8 Visit [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.4)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 16 Visit [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12)\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.6)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 24 Visit [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20)\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 196 - 3 days after the Bascline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Week 28)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 224 + 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 36 Visit [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)\n- 10. Dispense study drug [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- 6. Dispense study drug\n7. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12) (note: differs from master:8.7)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20) (note: differs from master:8.9)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)\n- 13. Dispense study drug\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)','\n',char(10)),'Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Instruct participant to bring in investigational product to the','["dosing"]',3210,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:11','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > ATE visit dispensing and compliance','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## ATE visit dispensing and compliance\n- Following the placebo-controlled period, participants will continue in the ATE as outlined in the SOA. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2 ATE)\n- 3. Perform drug compliance check-in\n4. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, containers, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- 11. Perform study drug compliance\n12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- 14. Dispense study drug to participant\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit • Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 ATE)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- 19. Dispense study drug to participant\n20. Perform study drug compliance\n21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- 13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)\n- 15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after Week 52 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Following the placebo-controlled period, participants will continue in the ATE as outlined in the SOA.','["dosing"]',3211,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:12','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > IRT dose assignment and dispensing','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## IRT dose assignment and dispensing\n- At subsequent in-clinic visits, the subject will return unused drug and receive new supply. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID from the dashboard and click Dispense. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 2. Select the current visit from the dropdown (e.g., Week 4). (Note: Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 4. Authenticate with your password and dispense the newly assigned Kit Numbers. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- For take-home dosing, the Pharmacist or authorized Coordinator will dispense intact cartons to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Log into the IRT to register the dispensing visit. The IRT will assign specific Kit Number(s) to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Retrieve the exact matching Kit Number(s) from the pharmacy inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Affix a site-specific dispensing label to the secondary carton. The label must not obscure the original clinical label and must include: Subject ID Number; Protocol Number (NCT05842941); Date Dispensed & Dispenser Initials; Instructions: "Take 2 stick packs once daily. Store below 30°C." [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Provide the subject/caregiver with thorough training on the preparation methods (Water or Soft Food) and emphasize the 15-minute post-mixing administration window. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)','\n',char(10)),'At subsequent in-clinic visits, the subject will return unused drug and receive new supply.','["dosing"]',3212,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:13','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Treatment randomization','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Treatment randomization\n- Within cach regimen, participants will then be randomized a second time in a 3:1 ratio to active treatment or matching placebo. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Within cach regimen, participants will then be randomized a second time in a 3:1 ratio to active treatment or matching placebo.','["dosing"]',3213,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:14','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Telephone compliance checks and return reminders','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Telephone compliance checks and return reminders\n- A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 Visit, as indicated). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period','["dosing"]',3214,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:15','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization\n- Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system. [master:6.4.1]','\n',char(10)),'Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system.','["dosing"]',3215,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:16','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (Any overdose, misuse, accidental exposure or medication error requires Coordination Center reporting even without an AE, with overdose monitoring and Medical Monitor contact within 24 hours of investigator awareness.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (Any overdose, misuse, accidental exposure or medication error requires Coordination Center reporting even without an AE, with overdose monitoring and Medical Monitor contact within 24 hours of investigator awareness.)\n- Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA. [master:7.4]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA.','["dosing"]',3216,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:17','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Safety-related dose changes (Safety-driven dose reduction, interruption or discontinuation requires investigator consultation with the Medical Monitor and assessment of the underlying safety issue.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Safety-related dose changes (Safety-driven dose reduction, interruption or discontinuation requires investigator consultation with the Medical Monitor and assessment of the underlying safety issue.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- A SI or designee may terminate a research participant if a medical condition or other situation occurs such that continued participation would not be in the best interest of the participant. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["dosing"]',3217,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:18','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Unscheduled safety and PK collection (In ATE, a TEAE causing dose reduction or interruption, or a severe TEAE requiring an unscheduled visit, triggers consideration of additional unscheduled PK sampling.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Unscheduled safety and PK collection (In ATE, a TEAE causing dose reduction or interruption, or a severe TEAE requiring an unscheduled visit, triggers consideration of additional unscheduled PK sampling.)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["dosing"]',3218,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:19','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Dosing noncompliance, incorrect administration or PK timing outside protocol requirements must be assessed and recorded as protocol deviations.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Dosing noncompliance, incorrect administration or PK timing outside protocol requirements must be assessed and recorded as protocol deviations.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["dosing"]',3219,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:2','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Administration routes and preparation','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Administration routes and preparation\n- If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product, the possibility of alternative modes of administration (e.g., via G-tube) will be detailed in the RSA. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants losing ability to swallow oral investigational product)\n- DNL343 drug product and placebo will be administered reconstituted in water for oral or feeding tube administration (eg, gastrostomy tube [G-tube], jejunostomy tube [J-tube], or nasogastric tube) or coadministered with soft food (eg, applesauce or yogurt). Participants can take the drug with fluid thickener as needed. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- DNL343/Placebo is supplied as oral granules. Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G feeding tube administration)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- For visits requiring in-clinic dosing (e.g., Baseline, Week 4, Week 24 PK sampling): [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- - Ensure the subject is ready to receive the dose before opening the stick packs, due to the strict 15-minute stability window. [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- - Post-Dose Flushing: If administering via an enteral feeding tube, flush the administration cup/syringe with an additional 15–30 mL of water, draw it up, and flush the tube to ensure 100% of the dose is delivered and the tube remains patent. If administered orally via cup, add a small amount of water to the empty cup, swirl, and have the subject drink it to ensure no residual granules are left behind. [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)','\n',char(10)),'If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product','["dosing"]',3202,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:20','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Accountability records and compliance (For clinic dosing, a second staff member must confirm participant identity and dose, and dosing and accountability records must be maintained.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Accountability records and compliance (For clinic dosing, a second staff member must confirm participant identity and dose, and dosing and accountability records must be maintained.)\n- In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed and/or administered to study participants, the amount received from the Central Pharmacy, and the amount destroyed upon completion of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The SI is responsible for ensuring product accountability records are maintained throughout the course of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The research pharmacist or designated study staff will be responsible for maintaining an accurate record of the shipment and dispensing of investigational product in a drug accountability log. [master:7.1.3]\n- When participants are dosed at the site, they will receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee, under medical supervision. The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G site dosing)\n- When participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing)\n- Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Under ICH-GCP, the Investigator is responsible for ensuring that all IMP is adequately accounted for throughout the trial. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Master Drug Accountability Log (DAL): The Pharmacy must maintain a master log tracking the receipt, overall inventory, and final disposition of all cartons. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Subject Dispensing Log: Maintain a subject-specific log tracking the exact dates and kit numbers dispensed to, and returned by, each subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Returns & Compliance: Instruct subjects to return all unused stick packs, as well as all empty cartons and empty stick pack wrappers, to every clinic visit. Site staff must count the returned intact packets and wrappers to calculate dosing compliance and record this in the subject''s eCRF and DAL. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- Subjects must return all used and unused stick packs/cartons to the clinic. Site staff must physically count the returns and update the Suvoda system: [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject ID and select Kit Accountability. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Locate the previously dispensed Kit Number. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Enter the number of Unused Stick Packs returned. The system updates the kit status to Returned, ensuring complete lifecycle tracking for the Sponsor. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed ','["dosing"]',3220,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:21','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At Week 24 ATE entry, assign open-label kits through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind supply.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At Week 24 ATE entry, assign open-label kits through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind supply.)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD','["dosing"]',3221,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:22','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: PK collection and dosing coordination (On PK assessment days, coordinate dosing with required predose and postdose collections and reconcile actual dose and draw times.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: PK collection and dosing coordination (On PK assessment days, coordinate dosing with required predose and postdose collections and reconcile actual dose and draw times.)\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)\n- 15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G PK collection)\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 not in morning)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times.\n- Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15).\n- Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK data entry)','\n',char(10)),'On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic.','["dosing"]',3222,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:23','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit timing and call consolidation (If stopping drug also ends study follow-up, arrange prompt ET assessment and approximately 28-day safety follow-up, combining them only within the permitted window.)','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit timing and call consolidation (If stopping drug also ends study follow-up, arrange prompt ET assessment and approximately 28-day safety follow-up, combining them only within the permitted window.)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)','\n',char(10)),'The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates.','["dosing"]',3223,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:3','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Daily timing and missed doses','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Daily timing and missed doses\n- Participants should take the study drug at approximately the same time each day in the morning without regard to food. All dosing should be completed within 15 minutes. On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Participants may take their missed dose as soon as they remember to take it in the same day. If the participant remembers the day after the dose was missed, only the next dose should be taken. Only one dose is to be taken per day. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should take the study drug at approximately the same time each day in the morning without regard to food. All dosing should be ','["dosing"]',3203,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:4','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > First dose','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## First dose\n- Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants at Baseline)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline optional CSF collection)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 17 Baseline Clinic Day 0 x8 Screening'' Regimen Refer to Specific Clinic RSA Screening'' Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days DNA Collection'' (optional) Administer/Dispense Study Accountability/Compliance Columbia-Suicide Severity Vital Status Determination if any, should be performed. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed','["dosing"]',3204,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:5','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Clinic dosing and PK timing','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Clinic dosing and PK timing\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- 3. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 4, postdose only on Week 8) o For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- a) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 predose PK)\n- b) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8 postdose PK)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- This visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination)\n- 8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- a) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- b) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- a) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 optional CSF collection)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture)\n- When participants are dosed at the site, they will receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee, under medical supervision. The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the parti','["dosing"]',3205,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:6','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Dose modification and interruption','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Dose modification and interruption\n- Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA. [master:7.2]\n- To adequately evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 participants should be maintained on the randomized treatment. For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Dose Modifications: The protocol permits a dose reduction to 100 mg due to intolerability. If the dose is adjusted, you must update the Planned Dose Level field, check "Yes" for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason (e.g., Adverse Event). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA.','["dosing"]',3206,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:7','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Discontinuation and follow-up','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Discontinuation and follow-up\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- A research participant may choose to discontinue the investigational product at any time for any reason. However, the SI or designee will encourage the research participant to follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT). These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: participants with specified positive C-SSRS responses during treatment)\n- At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing study drug)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["dosing"]',3207,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:8','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Overdose and administration errors','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Overdose and administration errors\n- Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA. [master:7.4]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA.','["dosing"]',3208,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:dosing:9','piece','','Study drug dosing and dose changes > Compliance rules','Study drug dosing and dose changes',replace('## Compliance rules\n- Compliance for each regimen will be defined in the RSA. In cases of non-compliance, the participant will be reminded of the importance of taking the investigational product per protocol. [master:7.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: phone visits)\n- When participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home administration)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]','\n',char(10)),'Compliance for each regimen will be defined in the RSA. In cases of non-compliance, the participant will be reminded of the importance of ta','["dosing"]',3209,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:1','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Product supply, packaging and labeling','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Product supply, packaging and labeling\n- Each active agent will have a matching placebo. [master:7.1]\n- Details identifying the investigational product manufacturer will be included in the corresponding RSA. [master:7.1.1]\n- Investigational product for each regimen will be provided by the regimen industry partner and will be described in the corresponding RSA. [master:7.1.2]\n- Packaging and labeling will follow Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) regulations. [master:7.1.2]\n- Samples of labels will be filed in the Trial Master File (TMF). [master:7.1.2]\n- Details for packaging, labeling, and re-supply will be described in the corresponding RSA. [master:7.1.2]\n- DNL343 drug product and placebo will be provided for this trial; both are comprised of granules filled into stick packs. [regimen_g:5.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Secondary packaging, labeling, and initial distribution of DNL343 drug product and placebo is performed at Fisher Clinical Services located at 7554 Schantz Rd, Allentown, PA 18106 USA. [regimen_g:5.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Both DNL343 drug product and placebo are packaged into individual stick packs made from aluminum foil at Catalent. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The individual stick packs, or packets, are packaged into secondary cartons at Fisher Clinical Services and each carton contains 14 packets segmented into 7 doses containing 2 packets each. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Each packet contains DNL343 100 mg or placebo. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Each packet and each carton are labeled at Fisher Clinical Services. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The final labeled cartons are shipped to the Central pharmacy for distribution to clinical sites. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- All shipments to clinical sites are controlled using an Integrated Response Technology solution. [regimen_g:5.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Each active agent will have a matching placebo.','["drug_supply"]',3801,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:10','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Baseline dispensing','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Baseline dispensing\n- This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, please refer to the RA for applicability. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Instruct participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 4 visit [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 10. Dispense study drug [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screen','["drug_supply"]',3810,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:11','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > In-clinic dispensing and accountability visits','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## In-clinic dispensing and accountability visits\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 8 Visit [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8)\n- These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8) (note: differs from master:8.5; differs from master:8.6)\n- 6. Dispense study drug [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- 7. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8)\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.5)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16)\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16)\n- This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- 11. Dispense study drug [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16)\n- 12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16)\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24)\n- Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)\n- Perform investigational product accountability and compliance [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the ','["drug_supply"]',3811,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:12','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Telephone compliance checks and return reminders','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Telephone compliance checks and return reminders\n- This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance) [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- 2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.4)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 16 Visit [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12)\n- This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12) (note: differs from master:8.7)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance) [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- 2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.6)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 24 Visit [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20)\n- This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20) (note: differs from master:8.9)\n- 1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance) [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)\n- 2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 224 + 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- Drug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 36 Visit [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Week 32)\n- A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 Visit, as indicated). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen sc','["drug_supply"]',3812,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:13','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Remote dispensing','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Remote dispensing\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Details on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not availabl','["drug_supply"]',3813,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:14','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Clinic dose timing','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Clinic dose timing\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Week 4 dosing before clinic arrival)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Week 16 ATE)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Week 28 ATE)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Week 52 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the parti','["drug_supply"]',3814,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:15','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > ATE dispensing and compliance visits','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## ATE dispensing and compliance visits\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Week 2 ATE)\n- 3. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Week 2 ATE)\n- 4. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, containers, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Week 2 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Week 4 ATE)\n- 11. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Week 4 ATE)\n- 12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Week 4 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Week 8 ATE)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Week 8 ATE)\n- 13. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Week 8 ATE)\n- 14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Week 8 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Week 12 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Week 12 ATE)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Week 12 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Week 16 ATE)\n- 14. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Week 16 ATE)\n- 15. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Week 16 ATE)\n- 16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Week 16 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Week 20 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Week 20 ATE)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Week 20 ATE)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Week 24 ATE)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Week 24 ATE)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Week 24 ATE)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Week 28 ATE)\n- 19. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Week 28 ATE)\n- 20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Week 28 ATE)\n- 21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Week 28 ATE)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Week 40 ATE)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Week 40 ATE)\n- 13. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Week 40 ATE)\n- 14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Week 40 ATE)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Week 52 ATE)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Week 52 ATE)\n- 20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Week 52 ATE)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 15. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following pr','["drug_supply"]',3815,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:16','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Discontinuation returns and supply closure','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Discontinuation returns and supply closure\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: investigational product discontinuation)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participant termination)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["drug_supply"]',3816,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:17','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Destruction and depot returns','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Destruction and depot returns\n- Details on how the investigational product will be destroyed, who is responsible for the destruction, and how long documentation will be retained at sites, will be provided in the MOP or defer to site specific policies. [master:7.1.4]\n- - Destruction: IMP must NOT be destroyed on site without prior written authorization. Used/Empty packaging may be destroyed on site according to standard hospital biohazardous waste policies after reconciliation is verified by the Clinical Research Associate (CRA). Unused, expired, or quarantined kits must be returned to the CDMO depot (Fisher Clinical Services) using the pre-supplied return shipping materials, following IRT return authorization. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on how the investigational product will be destroyed, who is responsible for the destruction, and how long documentation will be ret','["drug_supply"]',3817,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:18','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Monitor access','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Monitor access\n- The SIs will agree to allow monitors access to the clinical supplies, dispensing and storage areas, and to the clinical files of the study participants, and, if requested, agree to assist the monitors. [master:12.3]','\n',char(10)),'The SIs will agree to allow monitors access to the clinical supplies, dispensing and storage areas, and to the clinical files of the study p','["drug_supply"]',3818,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:19','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > First dose','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## First dose\n- S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 17 Baseline Clinic Day 0 x8 Screening'' Regimen Refer to Specific Clinic RSA Screening'' Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days DNA Collection'' (optional) Administer/Dispense Study Accountability/Compliance Columbia-Suicide Severity Vital Status Determination if any, should be performed. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 ','["drug_supply"]',3819,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:2','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Storage and transport','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Storage and transport\n- Investigational product for all regimens will be received at the study site, handled and stored safely and properly at the site pharmacy or other designated location, and kept in a secure location to which only the trial pharmacist and designated pharmacy staff, SI, and clinical staff have access. [master:7.1.3] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Upon receipt, the investigational product will be stored according to the instructions specified on the labels. [master:7.1.3] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Storage conditions will be adequately monitored and temperature in the area in which the investigational product is stored will be controlled, monitored and recorded, at a minimum daily. [master:7.1.3] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Additional investigational product-specific details will be provided in the RA and Regimen study drug MOP. [master:7.1.3]\n- DNL343 drug product and placebo should be stored at room temperature (store below 30°C). [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- All drug supplies must be stored in a secure, temperature-controlled area with limited access. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- For batch-specific storage instructions, see the packaging. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- All study drug will be transported, received, stored, and handled in accordance with the container or product label, the instructions supplied to the research site and its designated pharmacy, the site’s standard operating procedures, and the applicable country regulations. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Appropriate storage temperature and transportation conditions will be maintained for the study drug from the point of manufacture up to delivery of the study drug to each participating clinical site. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for additional instructions on handling and storage of study drug. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Storage Conditions: Both DNL343 and Placebo must be stored at Controlled Room Temperature (below 30°C / 86°F). Protect from extreme heat and moisture. Kits must be stored in a secure, limited-access area (e.g., locked pharmacy) in accordance with ICH-GCP. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Investigational product for all regimens will be received at the study site, handled and stored safely and properly at the site pharmacy or ','["drug_supply"]',3802,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:20','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone vi','["drug_supply"]',3820,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:21','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Eligibility confirmation before randomization (Before initial IRT randomization and dispensing, confirm that the participant meets all required eligibility criteria.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Eligibility (who can join): Eligibility confirmation before randomization (Before initial IRT randomization and dispensing, confirm that the participant meets all required eligibility criteria.)\n- Participants meeting additional eligibility criteria required by their assigned regimen, if any, will be randomized in a 3:1 ratio to receive either active treatment or matching placebo in that regimen. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- In the first stage, after a participant is determined eligible according to the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria, a research participant will be randomly assigned equally among all available regimens to which the participant has not previously been assigned. In the second stage, if a participant has been determined eligible by meeting all inclusion and no exclusion criteria specific to their assigned regimen, the research participant will be randomized 3:1 to the active intervention or the placebo for the regimen. [master:6.4.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Randomization into treatment group within a regimen will be facilitated via the IRT system after a participant has been determined eligible for any inclusion and exclusion criteria specific to their assigned regimen. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants in the master protocol will be randomized in two stages; first to one of the actively enrolling regimens after the participant is determined to be eligible based on Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria; and then 3:1 to active treatment or placebo within the regimen after the participant is determined to be eligible based on any regimen-specific eligibility criteria. [master:11.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply: [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Randomization (Day 0))\n- Stratification Data: [Standard Study Default – Biostats Confirmation Required]. During the Randomization step, Suvoda will prompt you to enter the subject''s stratification factors (e.g., Time since symptom onset [<18 months vs. >18 months] and Baseline Bulbar function). Ensure this data is verified in the EDC prior to executing IRT randomization. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: randomization)\n- Those determined eligible will be randomly assigned with equal probability among all active regimens. [master:protocol-summary]\n- Those participants that meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria will be randomly assigned to a regimen. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Participants meeting additional eligibility criteria required by their assigned regimen, if any, will be randomized in a 3:1 ratio to receiv','["drug_supply"]',3821,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:22','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When toxicity prompts reduced-dose dispensing, follow the clinical dose-modification and interruption rules rather than relying on the IRT selection alone.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When toxicity prompts reduced-dose dispensing, follow the clinical dose-modification and interruption rules rather than relying on the IRT selection alone.)\n- Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA. [master:7.2]\n- To adequately evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 participants should be maintained on the randomized treatment. For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Dose Modifications: The protocol permits a dose reduction to 100 mg due to intolerability. If the dose is adjusted, you must update the Planned Dose Level field, check "Yes" for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason (e.g., Adverse Event). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA.','["drug_supply"]',3822,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:23','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (If dispensing or preparation errors lead to an overdose or medication error, follow the required safety assessment and reporting procedures.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (If dispensing or preparation errors lead to an overdose or medication error, follow the required safety assessment and reporting procedures.)\n- Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA. [master:7.4]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA.','["drug_supply"]',3823,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:24','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (If emergency unblinding is contemplated, follow the medical-emergency authorization and notification requirements before executing the IRT action.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (If emergency unblinding is contemplated, follow the medical-emergency authorization and notification requirements before executing the IRT action.)\n- In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a participant, emergency unblinding of that single participant may be undertaken as outlined below. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Emergency unblinding for a research participant will only be undertaken when it is essential to treat the participant safely. It must only be used in an emergency when the identity of the treatment arm must be known to the SI to provide appropriate medical treatment or otherwise ensure the safety of research participants or others exposed to investigational products. In most cases, investigational product discontinuation and knowledge of the possible treatment assignments will be sufficient to treat a study participant who presents with an emergency condition. However, if unblinding is necessary, the blind may be broken only for that participant. [master:6.4.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Detailed unblinding information for the trial is detailed in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.4]\n- Emergency unblinding should only be performed if knowledge of the subject''s treatment assignment is absolutely necessary for their immediate medical management. Only the Principal Investigator (PI) or authorized Sub-Investigator has the system permissions to execute a code break. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Log into Suvoda. From the Home Dashboard, navigate to the Subjects list. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 2. Click on the relevant Subject ID. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 3. Under the Action menu, select Emergency Unblind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 4. Acknowledge the warning prompt detailing the severity of breaking the blind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 5. Enter the Reason for Unblinding (e.g., Severe Adverse Event requiring emergency intervention). [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 6. Authentication: You must enter your Suvoda username and password to execute this transaction. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 7. Click Reveal Treatment. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 8. The system will display whether the subject was on DNL343 200 mg or Placebo. An automated, unblinded alert email is instantly dispatched to the Sponsor Medical Monitor and the Suvoda Helpdesk. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded participants)','\n',char(10)),'In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a partic','["drug_supply"]',3824,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:25','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When storage, dispensing, administration, compliance, or visit procedures depart from protocol, assess and document the protocol deviation.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When storage, dispensing, administration, compliance, or visit procedures depart from protocol, assess and document the protocol deviation.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["drug_supply"]',3825,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:26','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: PK collection and dosing coordination (When administering clinic doses on PK sampling days, coordinate dosing with the required predose and postdose collections and document dose timing.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: PK collection and dosing coordination (When administering clinic doses on PK sampling days, coordinate dosing with the required predose and postdose collections and document dose timing.)\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)\n- 15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G PK collection)\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 not in morning)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times.\n- Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15).\n- Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK data entry)','\n',char(10)),'On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic.','["drug_supply"]',3826,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:27','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Administration routes and preparation (When preparing drug for oral or feeding-tube administration, follow the route-specific administration requirements in addition to pharmacy preparation instructions.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Administration routes and preparation (When preparing drug for oral or feeding-tube administration, follow the route-specific administration requirements in addition to pharmacy preparation instructions.)\n- If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product, the possibility of alternative modes of administration (e.g., via G-tube) will be detailed in the RSA. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants losing ability to swallow oral investigational product)\n- DNL343 drug product and placebo will be administered reconstituted in water for oral or feeding tube administration (eg, gastrostomy tube [G-tube], jejunostomy tube [J-tube], or nasogastric tube) or coadministered with soft food (eg, applesauce or yogurt). Participants can take the drug with fluid thickener as needed. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- DNL343/Placebo is supplied as oral granules. Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: Regimen G feeding tube administration)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: Regimen G preparation in soft food)\n- For visits requiring in-clinic dosing (e.g., Baseline, Week 4, Week 24 PK sampling): [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- - Ensure the subject is ready to receive the dose before opening the stick packs, due to the strict 15-minute stability window. [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- - Post-Dose Flushing: If administering via an enteral feeding tube, flush the administration cup/syringe with an additional 15–30 mL of water, draw it up, and flush the tube to ensure 100% of the dose is delivered and the tube remains patent. If administered orally via cup, add a small amount of water to the empty cup, swirl, and have the subject drink it to ensure no residual granules are left behind. [site_pharmacy_manual:6] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)','\n',char(10)),'If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product','["drug_supply"]',3827,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:28','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit and safety call requirements (When drug returns and IRT closure reflect early termination, arrange the required early-termination visit and safety follow-up rather than treating supply closure as complete follow-up.)','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit and safety call requirements (When drug returns and IRT closure reflect early termination, arrange the required early-termination visit and safety follow-up rather than treating supply closure as complete follow-up.)\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [master:8.12] (applies to: participants terminating participation early)\n- The outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: all regimens)\n- For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants followed under the ITT principle)\n- A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing study drug and continuing ITT follow-up) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing medication and ITT follow-up)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing drug but completing the protocol) (note: differs from master:6.5.1; differs from regimen_g:6.10.12; differs from regimen_g:s011)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["drug_supply"]',3828,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:3','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Shipment receipt and inspection','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Shipment receipt and inspection\n- Upon receipt by the study site, the research pharmacy staff will transfer the shipment to the appropriate environmentally controlled storage area. The shipment and temperature monitoring devices (if applicable) will then be examined to verify that the study drug was received in acceptable condition. Once inspected and deemed acceptable, the study drug will then be stored in a restricted access, secured area with access limited to authorized research site staff, under physical conditions consistent with the study drug’s specific requirements. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The investigator or designee must confirm that appropriate temperature conditions have been maintained during transit for all study drug received and that any discrepancies are reported and resolved before use of the study drug. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- IMP is shipped via validated temperature-controlled couriers from the CDMO regional depots. Every shipment includes a TempTale continuous temperature monitor. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Inspect Shipment: Upon arrival, immediately inspect the outer shipping container for physical damage. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Stop TempTale: Press and hold the "STOP" button on the TempTale device for 3-5 seconds until the stop icon appears. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Download Data: Plug the TempTale USB into a computer. Save the automatically generated PDF report to your site''s regulatory binder. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Check for Alarms: Verify if the TempTale triggered a temperature alarm. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - No Alarm: Proceed to step 5. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Alarm Triggered: Immediately isolate the affected kits in the pharmacy under appropriate storage conditions. Mark them as "Quarantined" in the IRT and email the PDF report to the CDMO Supply Chain Manager for disposition. Do NOT dispense. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 5. IRT Registration: Log into the IRT system. Enter the shipment number and confirm receipt of all physical kit numbers. The system will transition the kits from "In Transit" to "Available" inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Suvoda automatically triggers predictive resupply shipments to your site based on your screening/randomization activity. When a shipment arrives: [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Stop the TempTale monitor included in the physical shipment and download the PDF data. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Log into Suvoda and navigate to the Shipments tab. Click on the In Transit shipment number. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Click Receive Shipment. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Answer the prompt: "Were there any temperature excursions?" [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - If No, all kits are moved to Available status. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - If Yes, the entire shipment is placed in Quarantined status. You must physically quarantine the drug and email the TempTale PDF to the CDMO for disposition. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Upon receipt by the study site, the research pharmacy staff will transfer the shipment to the appropriate environmentally controlled storage','["drug_supply"]',3803,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:4','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Excursions, damaged kits and resupply','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Excursions, damaged kits and resupply\n- Site Temperature Excursions: If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C: [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Move the IMP to an alternate compliant storage location if the primary area cannot be rapidly corrected. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Quarantine the affected kits physically and systematically in the IRT. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Damaged Kits: If you open a shipment and find a physically damaged kit, receive the shipment without an excursion, but immediately navigate to the Inventory tab, select the damaged Kit Number, and change its status from Available to Damaged. Suvoda will automatically trigger a replacement shipment if your site''s buffer falls below the required threshold. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- A: Do not give the damaged kit to the subject. Log into Suvoda, navigate to the Subject ID, and select the Replace Kit action. Select the damaged kit, choose the reason (e.g., Damaged at Site), and enter your password. Suvoda will assign a new, replacement Kit Number to the subject and change the damaged kit''s status to Damaged. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: kit damaged after IRT dispensing)\n- A: No. Suvoda uses a predictive algorithm to manage your site''s inventory based on your current screening and active subject data. If you have an unexpected surge in screening and believe your inventory is low, contact the Suvoda Helpdesk or the CRA, who can authorize a manual supplementary shipment. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: manual orders for extra drug)\n- A: NO. You must mark the shipment as containing an excursion during receipt, which automatically places the kits into Quarantined status in Suvoda. The system will physically block you from dispensing these kits until the Sponsor/CDMO reviews the TempTale data and explicitly changes their status to Available in the system. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: TempTale alarm; disposition pending)','\n',char(10)),'Site Temperature Excursions: If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C:','["drug_supply"]',3804,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:5','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > IRT dispensing and blinding','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## IRT dispensing and blinding\n- All critical transactions in Suvoda require 21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Signatures, meaning you must re-enter your username and password to finalize Randomization, Dispensing, or Unblinding actions. [site_irt_guide:2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Important: Never dispense an IMP kit to a subject unless it has been explicitly assigned to them by Suvoda. [site_irt_guide:2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Site staff must never attempt to unblind a kit. Dispense strictly by the Kit Number assigned by the IRT system. [site_pharmacy_manual:critical-blinding-maintenance] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply: [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subjects list and click on the specific Subject ID. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 2. Click the Randomize action button. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 3. Enter the required Stratification variables and the Subject Weight (kg). [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 4. Electronic Signature: Enter your Suvoda username and password to authenticate the randomization transaction. Click Confirm. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 5. Suvoda executes the 3:1 (Active:Placebo) blinding algorithm and displays the assigned Kit Numbers. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- 6. Retrieve the exact assigned cartons from the pharmacy. Print the Dispensation Confirmation PDF and file it in the subject binder. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Day 0)\n- At subsequent in-clinic visits, the subject will return unused drug and receive new supply. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID from the dashboard and click Dispense. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 2. Select the current visit from the dropdown (e.g., Week 4). (Note: Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 4. Authenticate with your password and dispense the newly assigned Kit Numbers. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- A: Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)','\n',char(10)),'All critical transactions in Suvoda require 21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Signatures, meaning you must re-enter your username and password to fi','["drug_supply"]',3805,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:6','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > ATE supply transition','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## ATE supply transition\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD','["drug_supply"]',3806,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:7','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Preparation and in-use stability','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Preparation and in-use stability\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["drug_supply"]',3807,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:8','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Take-home dispensing','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Take-home dispensing\n- For take-home dosing, the Pharmacist or authorized Coordinator will dispense intact cartons to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: take-home dosing)\n- - Log into the IRT to register the dispensing visit. The IRT will assign specific Kit Number(s) to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: take-home dosing)\n- - Retrieve the exact matching Kit Number(s) from the pharmacy inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: take-home dosing)\n- - Affix a site-specific dispensing label to the secondary carton. The label must not obscure the original clinical label and must include: Subject ID Number; Protocol Number (NCT05842941); Date Dispensed & Dispenser Initials; Instructions: "Take 2 stick packs once daily. Store below 30°C." [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: take-home dosing)\n- - Provide the subject/caregiver with thorough training on the preparation methods (Water or Soft Food) and emphasize the 15-minute post-mixing administration window. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: take-home dosing)','\n',char(10)),'For take-home dosing, the Pharmacist or authorized Coordinator will dispense intact cartons to the subject.','["drug_supply"]',3808,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:drug_supply:9','piece','','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability > Accountability records and compliance','Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability',replace('## Accountability records and compliance\n- In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed and/or administered to study participants, the amount received from the Central Pharmacy, and the amount destroyed upon completion of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The SI is responsible for ensuring product accountability records are maintained throughout the course of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The research pharmacist or designated study staff will be responsible for maintaining an accurate record of the shipment and dispensing of investigational product in a drug accountability log. [master:7.1.3]\n- When participants are dosed at the site, they will receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee, under medical supervision. The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G site dosing)\n- When participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing)\n- Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Under ICH-GCP, the Investigator is responsible for ensuring that all IMP is adequately accounted for throughout the trial. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Master Drug Accountability Log (DAL): The Pharmacy must maintain a master log tracking the receipt, overall inventory, and final disposition of all cartons. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Subject Dispensing Log: Maintain a subject-specific log tracking the exact dates and kit numbers dispensed to, and returned by, each subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Returns & Compliance: Instruct subjects to return all unused stick packs, as well as all empty cartons and empty stick pack wrappers, to every clinic visit. Site staff must count the returned intact packets and wrappers to calculate dosing compliance and record this in the subject''s eCRF and DAL. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- Subjects must return all used and unused stick packs/cartons to the clinic. Site staff must physically count the returns and update the Suvoda system: [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject ID and select Kit Accountability. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Locate the previously dispensed Kit Number. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Enter the number of Unused Stick Packs returned. The system updates the kit status to Returned, ensuring complete lifecycle tracking for the Sponsor. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed ','["drug_supply"]',3809,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:1','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Stopping investigational product','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Stopping investigational product\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product, the possibility of alternative modes of administration (e.g., via G-tube) will be detailed in the RSA. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants receiving oral investigational product)\n- A research participant may choose to discontinue the investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["early_termination"]',4001,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:10','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > ET assessment details and ordering','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## ET assessment details and ordering\n- 4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- 14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G endocrinology laboratory assessments)\n- 17. PGI-C and PGI-S should be completed by the participant prior to other clinical assessments scheduled during the visit whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G ET assessments)\n- 18. CGI-C should be completed after all clinical assessments are completed by the investigator whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G ET assessments)\n- [21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination Visit)\n- 1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination Visit)\n- 9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- 10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination Visit)\n- 13. Dispense study drug\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or Early Termination Visit)','\n',char(10)),'4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature.','["early_termination"]',4010,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:11','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > CSF collection at early termination','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## CSF collection at early termination\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled CSF collection)\n- Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G post-treatment CSF collection)\n- For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G ET Visit)\n- b) CSF collection for the ET visit will be performed only if the participant has consented to the lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G ET Visit)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G ET Visit)','\n',char(10)),'CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits.','["early_termination"]',4011,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:12','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Vital status after termination','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Vital status after termination\n- 11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants ending placebo-controlled follow-up)\n- 1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G participants ending ATE follow-up) (note: Footnote referenced as [10] in the table.)','\n',char(10)),'11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized','["early_termination"]',4012,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:13','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Completion definitions','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Completion definitions\n- A participant is considered to have completed the placebo-controlled period of the Regimen when they have completed Week 24 or the Early Termination (ET) Visit. [regimen_g:s025] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants are considered to have completed their participation in the ATE period of\nRegimen G if planned ATE period visits, including the last visit, ET Visit, or the last scheduled procedure shown in the Schedule of Activities (SOA), have been completed. [regimen_g:s026] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'A participant is considered to have completed the placebo-controlled period of the Regimen when they have completed Week 24 or the Early Ter','["early_termination"]',4013,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:14','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > EDC and IRT termination steps','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## EDC and IRT termination steps\n- - Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded subjects)','\n',char(10)),'- Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completi','["early_termination"]',4014,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:15','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Regimen and trial termination','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Regimen and trial termination\n- During the trial, a regimen may be discontinued early due to futility or safety concerns. [master:2.3] (applies to: all regimens)\n- The IND-holder/Sponsor reserves the right to terminate the overall Master Protocol or any individual regimen at any time. [master:6.5.3] (applies to: Master Protocol and individual regimens)\n- If there are regimen-specific criteria for terminating a regimen, such criterion is detailed in the RSA or Regimen-Statiscal Analys Plan. [master:6.5.3] (applies to: individual regimens)\n- Recommendations for modification or termination of the trial based on safety data will be made by the DSMB to the Sponsor. [master:12.4] (applies to: trial)\n- The DSMB will review safety data throughout the trial and may stop enrollment into a treatment arm for safety if they determine that there is a significant difference in the rate of a particular adverse event that would indicate a risk that is greater than the possible benefit of the investigational product. [master:12.4] (applies to: treatment arms)\n- Regimens exit the platform either after completing all planned accrual and follow-up or by meeting the criteria for early stopping of the trial at pre-defined interim analyses. [master:11.1]','\n',char(10)),'During the trial, a regimen may be discontinued early due to futility or safety concerns.','["early_termination"]',4015,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:16','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Study site termination','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Study site termination\n- The Sponsor reserves the right to terminate the participation of individual study sites. [master:1.4.2] (applies to: study sites)\n- Conditions that may warrant termination include, but are not limited to, insufficient adherence to protocol requirements and failure to enter participants at an acceptable rate. [master:1.4.2] (applies to: study sites)','\n',char(10)),'The Sponsor reserves the right to terminate the participation of individual study sites.','["early_termination"]',4016,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:17','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (If the event prompting discontinuation meets expedited-reporting criteria, notify the Coordinating Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (If the event prompting discontinuation meets expedited-reporting criteria, notify the Coordinating Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["early_termination"]',4017,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:18','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When an AE causes discontinuation or is identified during off-treatment follow-up, the ET visit or safety call, document it on the AE CRF.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Recording and EDC (When an AE causes discontinuation or is identified during off-treatment follow-up, the ET visit or safety call, document it on the AE CRF.)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible AEs. All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. The Master Protocol MM shall promptly review all information relevant to the safety of the investigational product, including all SAEs. Special attention will be paid to those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational products) being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- - The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: SAE EDC entry) (note: differs from master:10.2.1; master:10.2.2)\n- A: Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log line)\n- A: You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: concomitant medication entry)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed','["early_termination"]',4018,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:19','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: Missed assessments and deviations (If an ET or off-treatment visit or required procedure is missed, apply the missed-assessment deviation rules and their illness, injury or progressive-disability exceptions.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: Missed assessments and deviations (If an ET or off-treatment visit or required procedure is missed, apply the missed-assessment deviation rules and their illness, injury or progressive-disability exceptions.)\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14] (applies to: pandemic-related missed assessments)','\n',char(10)),'Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., parti','["early_termination"]',4019,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:2','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Continued follow-up off treatment','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Continued follow-up off treatment\n- However, the SI or designee will encourage the research participant to follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G participants stopping study drug early)\n- Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'However, the SI or designee will encourage the research participant to follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT).','["early_termination"]',4002,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:20','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Accountability records and compliance (When discontinued participants return unused drug and packaging, reconcile and document drug accountability and compliance.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Accountability records and compliance (When discontinued participants return unused drug and packaging, reconcile and document drug accountability and compliance.)\n- In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed and/or administered to study participants, the amount received from the Central Pharmacy, and the amount destroyed upon completion of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The SI is responsible for ensuring product accountability records are maintained throughout the course of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The research pharmacist or designated study staff will be responsible for maintaining an accurate record of the shipment and dispensing of investigational product in a drug accountability log. [master:7.1.3]\n- When participants are dosed at the site, they will receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee, under medical supervision. The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G site dosing)\n- When participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing)\n- Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:7)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Under ICH-GCP, the Investigator is responsible for ensuring that all IMP is adequately accounted for throughout the trial. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Master Drug Accountability Log (DAL): The Pharmacy must maintain a master log tracking the receipt, overall inventory, and final disposition of all cartons. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Subject Dispensing Log: Maintain a subject-specific log tracking the exact dates and kit numbers dispensed to, and returned by, each subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Returns & Compliance: Instruct subjects to return all unused stick packs, as well as all empty cartons and empty stick pack wrappers, to every clinic visit. Site staff must count the returned intact packets and wrappers to calculate dosing compliance and record this in the subject''s eCRF and DAL. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- Subjects must return all used and unused stick packs/cartons to the clinic. Site staff must physically count the returns and update the Suvoda system: [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.7)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject ID and select Kit Accountability. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Locate the previously dispensed Kit Number. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Enter the number of Unused Stick Packs returned. The system updates the kit status to Returned, ensuring complete lifecycle tracking for the Sponsor. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed ','["early_termination"]',4020,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:21','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (If study drug is prepared or administered in clinic during an ET or transition assessment, follow preparation, stability and second-person verification requirements.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (If study drug is prepared or administered in clinic during an ET or transition assessment, follow preparation, stability and second-person verification requirements.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["early_termination"]',4021,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:22','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Contraception and pregnancy: Eligibility and contraception duration (After study drug stops, ensure applicable contraception requirements continue for the required post-treatment period.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Contraception and pregnancy: Eligibility and contraception duration (After study drug stops, ensure applicable contraception requirements continue for the required post-treatment period.)\n- These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. If female, breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, planning to become pregnant during the study, or of child-bearing potential and unwilling to use effective contraception, for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: female participants)\n- 7. If male of reproductive capacity, unwilling to use effective contraception for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: male participants of reproductive capacity)\n- There are RSA-specific requirements that apply to Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6. [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- For further details of contraceptive requirements for this RSA, please refer to Section 5.10.2. [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens:','["early_termination"]',4022,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:23','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Lumbar puncture safety (If an eligible participant undergoes optional CSF collection at ET, complete the required lumbar-puncture safety checks.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Lumbar puncture safety (If an eligible participant undergoes optional CSF collection at ET, complete the required lumbar-puncture safety checks.)\n- Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture participants)\n- - Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Week 16 [12] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including: [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture participants)\n- Local pain at injection site [master:9.3.4]\n- Reaction to anesthetic agents [master:9.3.4]\n- Bleeding at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4]\n- Infection at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4]\n- Post-LP low-pressure headache [master:9.3.4]\n- The procedure must be performed by the SI or another staff member with the appropriate qualifications, experience, and training in performing LPs per institutional policy. When possible, LPs should be performed with an atraumatic Sprotte needle to reduce the risk of post-LP headache. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture)','\n',char(10)),'Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized rat','["early_termination"]',4023,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:24','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (If an ET or off-treatment C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety-response procedures.)','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (If an ET or off-treatment C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety-response procedures.)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:s011)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)','\n',char(10)),'At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s l','["early_termination"]',4024,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:3','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Individual participant termination and withdrawal','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Individual participant termination and withdrawal\n- A SI or designee may terminate a research participant if a medical condition or other situation occurs such that continued participation would not be in the best interest of the participant. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- A research participant may withdraw consent for study participation. No justification is required for the decision. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'A SI or designee may terminate a research participant if a medical condition or other situation occurs such that continued participation wou','["early_termination"]',4003,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:4','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Product return and discontinuation documentation','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Product return and discontinuation documentation\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participants terminating participation)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all research participants)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["early_termination"]',4004,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:5','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Early termination visit and safety call requirements','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Early termination visit and safety call requirements\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [master:8.12] (applies to: participants terminating participation early)\n- The outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: all regimens)\n- For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants followed under the ITT principle)\n- A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing study drug and continuing ITT follow-up) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing medication and ITT follow-up)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing drug but completing the protocol) (note: differs from master:6.5.1; differs from regimen_g:6.10.12; differs from regimen_g:s011)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["early_termination"]',4005,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:6','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Early termination visit timing and call consolidation','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Early termination visit timing and call consolidation\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)','\n',char(10)),'The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates.','["early_termination"]',4006,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:7','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Follow-up safety call eligibility and procedures','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Follow-up safety call eligibility and procedures\n- 10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early) [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- 2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Assess and document AEs [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)','\n',char(10)),'10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will ha','["early_termination"]',4007,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:8','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > Placebo-controlled early termination assessments','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## Placebo-controlled early termination assessments\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- 19. The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- - Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Muscle Strength Assessment Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - ALSFRS-R Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - ROADS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - PGI-C 17 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - PGI-S 17 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - CGI-C 18 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - 12-Lead ECG Regimen: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all participants prior to ATE) [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- - Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] Regimen: done at Week 4 [12] (pre, post), Week 8 [12] (post), Week 16 [12] (post), Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (pre, post) [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10 [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Administer/Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Baseline [1] [8], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [9] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- 9. Study drug will only be dispensed at this visit if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET Visit)\n- - Study Drug Accountability/Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [20], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12 [20], Week 16 [12], Week 20 [20], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10 [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- - Exit Questionnaire Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)\n- - Vital Status Determination [11] Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled ET Visit)','\n',char(10)),'If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are coll','["early_termination"]',4008,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:early_termination:9','piece','','Stopping treatment and early termination > ATE early termination assessments','Stopping treatment and early termination',replace('## ATE early termination assessments\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE before Week 52)\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- 2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, CGI-C) (note: Footnote referenced as [11] in the table.)\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Administer/ Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [9], All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [9] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- [9]For participants returning for another visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE study drug dispensing)\n- - Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)\n- - Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- - Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE ET Visit)','\n',char(10)),'If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected a','["early_termination"]',4009,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:1','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Master and regimen eligibility requirements','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Master and regimen eligibility requirements\n- Each SI will be responsible for enrolling into the Master Protocol only those study participants who have met all Master Protocol eligibility criteria, and for enrolling into a Regimen only those study participants who have met the additional eligibility criteria (if any) of the corresponding RSA. [master:1.5] (applies to: all participants)\n- Research participants that meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria listed below will be further screened and randomly assigned to a regimen. [master:5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- In order to be randomized to Regimen G, participants must meet the Master Protocol eligibility criteria. In addition, participants meeting all of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria will be allowed to enroll in Regimen G. [regimen_g:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- There are no additional RSA Inclusion Criteria beyond those described in the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:4.2.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If justified based on regimen''s specific investigational product, additional inclusion or exclusion criteria (c.g., contraindications, laboratory parameters) will be listed in the RSA. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants must first be screened under the Master Protocol and before they are randomly assigned to a regimen. [regimen_g:s005] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Assignment to the regimen will stop if pre-defined criteria for futility for the regimen are met or after the target number of randomized participants has been reached. [regimen_g:s005] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- As soon as pre-defined criteria for futility or success (if applicable) are met, or the target number of randomized participants in a regimen has been reached, enrollment will stop in that regimen. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all regimens)','\n',char(10)),'Each SI will be responsible for enrolling into the Master Protocol only those study participants who have met all Master Protocol eligibilit','["eligibility"]',3001,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:10','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Consent before eligibility screening','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Consent before eligibility screening\n- At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), participants will be informed about the nature and purpose of the Master Protocol, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits. Participants will be given adequate time to ask questions and become familiar with the Master Protocol prior to providing consent to participate. Participants will give their documented informed consent to participate in the Master Protocol and will be provided with a copy of the fully executed Master Protocol consent form for their records. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants meeting Master Protocol eligibility criteria will be randomly assigned to a regimen. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants will then be informed about the intervention specific to their assigned regimen and any participation/termination conditions or risks and benefits specific to that regimen.\nParticipants will give their written consent to participate in their assigned regimen and will be provided with a copy of the fully executed regimen-specific consent form for their records. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- 2. During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, w','["eligibility"]',3010,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:11','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Screening timing and visits','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Screening timing and visits\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 Days [13] | Regimen Specific Screening [1] / Clinic / –41 to 0 Day [13] | Baseline [1] / Clinic / Day 0 | Week 2 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [12] / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [12] / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [12] / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 or ET Visit [19] / Clinic / Day 168 ± 7 | Follow-Up Safety Call 10 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- 13. Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0). [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- This visit will take place in-person after random assignment to a regimen. This visit may be combined with the Baseline Visit, please refer to the RSA for applicability. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- Obtain written regimen-specific informed consent from participant Assess regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria Concomitant medication review Assess and document AEs [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- 3. At the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit, participants will have regimen-specific eligibility criteria assessed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 D','["eligibility"]',3011,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:12','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Master screening assessments','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Master screening assessments\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Assess Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Obtain ALS and medical history [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Obtain demographics [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Perform Vital Capacity [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Perform physical examination [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Perform neurological examination [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Measure vital signs including height and weight [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Perform 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Collect blood samples for clinical screening assessments (including coagulation parameters) and, for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP), for pregnancy test Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood biomarker sample Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Schedule the Regimen Specific Screening Visit if the participant passes screening for the Master Protocol 8.1.I Regimen Assignment Once a participant''s Master Protocol eligibility is confirmed the participant will be randomly assigned to a regimen and will undergo informed consent for that regimen at their scheduled Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- ALS & Medical History Screening procedure(s) Version date 10/14/2024 Physical Examination Clinical Safety Labs Slow Vital Capacity Inclusion/Exclusion Neurological Exam Regimen Specific Dutcomesported Demographics 12-Lead ECG Vital Signst ALSERS-R Activity Review Safety Call Follow-Up Protocol Screening and as specified in the RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eli','["eligibility"]',3012,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:13','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Eligibility laboratory tests and pregnancy tests','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Eligibility laboratory tests and pregnancy tests\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Hematology with differential panel: complete blood count with differential (hematocrit, hemoglobin, platelet count, RBC indices, Total RBC, Total WBC, and WBC & differential) [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Blood chemistry panel/Liver function tests (LFTs): alanine aminotransferase (ALT (SGPT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST (SGOT)), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate, blood urea nitrogen, calcium, chloride, creatinine (estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate [eGFR] will be calculated using the using the CKD-EPI 2021 equation), cystatin C, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, potassium, sodium, total bilirubin and total protein [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants; hCG for WOCBP) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G; pregnancy testing for participants of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:9.1.2)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline women of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following v','["eligibility"]',3013,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:14','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Eligibility ECG','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Eligibility ECG\n- A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. The ECG should be collected using the study provided ECG device. Tracings will be reviewed by a central ECG reader and a copy of the tracings will be kept on site as part of the source documents. Additional guidance is included in the MOP. [master:9.1.3] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedu','["eligibility"]',3014,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:15','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Vital capacity eligibility assessment','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Vital capacity eligibility assessment\n- The vital capacity (VC) will be determined using the upright slow VC (SVC) method. All evaluators performing S VC, must be NEALS certified. The SVC will be measured using the study-approved portable spirometer, and assessments will be performed using a face mask. A printout from the spirometer of all VC trials will be retained. Three VC trials are required for each testing session, however up to 5 trials may be performed if the variability between the highest and second highest VC is 10% or greater for the first 3 trials. Only the 3 best trials are recorded on the CRF. The highest VC recorded is utilized for eligibility. [master:9.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Predicted VC values and percent-predicted VC values will be calculated as described in the MOP. [master:9.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All evaluators must be NEALS trained and certified and delegated to perform the ALSFRS-R and SVC assessments. Certification and training will be provided by the Barrow Outcome Center. Certification occurs via a formal evaluation of reliability and accuracy of performance of these measures. Repeat certification will be required on an annual basis for ALSFRS-R and every two years for SVC. It is strongly preferred that a single evaluator performs all measures throughout the study, as much as possible. [master:9.2.2] (applies to: ALSFRS-R and SVC evaluators)','\n',char(10)),'The vital capacity (VC) will be determined using the upright slow VC (SVC) method. All evaluators performing S VC, must be NEALS certified. ','["eligibility"]',3015,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:16','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Eligibility confirmation before randomization','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Eligibility confirmation before randomization\n- Participants meeting additional eligibility criteria required by their assigned regimen, if any, will be randomized in a 3:1 ratio to receive either active treatment or matching placebo in that regimen. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- In the first stage, after a participant is determined eligible according to the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria, a research participant will be randomly assigned equally among all available regimens to which the participant has not previously been assigned. In the second stage, if a participant has been determined eligible by meeting all inclusion and no exclusion criteria specific to their assigned regimen, the research participant will be randomized 3:1 to the active intervention or the placebo for the regimen. [master:6.4.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Randomization into treatment group within a regimen will be facilitated via the IRT system after a participant has been determined eligible for any inclusion and exclusion criteria specific to their assigned regimen. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Participants in the master protocol will be randomized in two stages; first to one of the actively enrolling regimens after the participant is determined to be eligible based on Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria; and then 3:1 to active treatment or placebo within the regimen after the participant is determined to be eligible based on any regimen-specific eligibility criteria. [master:11.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Once eligibility is confirmed, randomize the subject and dispense the initial drug supply: [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline / Randomization (Day 0))\n- Stratification Data: [Standard Study Default – Biostats Confirmation Required]. During the Randomization step, Suvoda will prompt you to enter the subject''s stratification factors (e.g., Time since symptom onset [<18 months vs. >18 months] and Baseline Bulbar function). Ensure this data is verified in the EDC prior to executing IRT randomization. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: randomization)\n- Those determined eligible will be randomly assigned with equal probability among all active regimens. [master:protocol-summary]\n- Those participants that meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria will be randomly assigned to a regimen. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Participants meeting additional eligibility criteria required by their assigned regimen, if any, will be randomized in a 3:1 ratio to receiv','["eligibility"]',3016,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:17','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Screen failure documentation','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Screen failure documentation\n- Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. This information will include reasons for screen failure for participants not assigned to a regimen, as well as a log of all participants randomized within a regimen, irrespective of whether they have been treated with the intervention or not. [master:6.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: participants ineligible for the Master Protocol)\n- Once assigned to a regimen, participant eligibility will be confirmed using regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria, if any, during the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: participants assigned to a regimen)\n- - Inclusion/Exclusion: Master and Regimen-Specific criteria must be evaluated. If "Not Met" is selected for any criterion, the system will fire a hard edit check requiring the selection of a specific failure reason. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: all participants)\n- Any participant who signs the Master Protocol consent form will be considered enrolled in the Platform Trial. If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP) [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reason for screen failure [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)','\n',char(10)),'Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. This infor','["eligibility"]',3017,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:18','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Repeat assessments during screening','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Repeat assessments during screening\n- If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to enrollment, interval following last use of an investigational treatment, or other criteria as applicable), then that participant may become eligible immediately after resolution of the disqualifying condition if resolution occurs within the screening period and re-screening is not required. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing time-dependent criteria)\n- If a participant fails to meet the vital capacity screening criterion on the day of the Master Protocol Screening visit, vital capacity may be reassessed on a different day during the initial screening period. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing vital capacity screening)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants re-screened after the initial screening window)','\n',char(10)),'If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to','["eligibility"]',3018,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:19','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Regimen re-assignment and re-screening','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Regimen re-assignment and re-screening\n- In some situations, an individual may be re-assigned to multiple different regimens, if eligible.\nThe procedures detailing the re-assignment process can be found in Section 8.1.2 Screen Failures and Re-Assignment. [master:1.3] (applies to: participants considered for re-assignment)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a specific regimen, but does not meet additional regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria for that regimen, then the research participant may be re-assigned immediately to a different regimen. If it is known at the time of regimen assignment that the participant is not eligible for the regimen, a participant may be administratively screen-failed for a regimen prior to regimen consent and screening. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants ineligible for their assigned regimen)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a regimen, and is randomized within that regimen, but their participation in that regimen is discontinued, that participant may have the opportunity to be re-screened for the Master Protocol. The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. Re-screening for the Master Protocol and potential re-assignment into a different regimen, if available, may occur due to the following situations: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- A regimen is stopped due to futility or success [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- The research participant may be re-screened after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants whose regimen stopped due to futility or success)\n- A research participant completes their Week 36 Visit in the placebo-controlled period and terminates their participation in the study [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- A research participant discontinues participation in a regimen for personal reasons (e.g., inconvenience of the intervention, mode of administration, required assessments) or due to an adverse event, or is discontinued by the SI [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants seeking re-assignment)\n- The research participant may not be re-screened until after the date that their 36-Week Visit would have occurred, had they completed participation in that regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants discontinuing for personal reasons, an adverse event, or by the SI)\n- Upon completion of a regimen, a research participant may be re-screened into the Master Protocol and assigned to another regimen after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. [master:11.2.4] (applies to: participants completing a regimen)','\n',char(10)),replace('In some situations, an individual may be re-assigned to multiple different regimens, if eligible.\nThe procedures detailing the re-assignment','\n',char(10)),'["eligibility"]',3019,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:2','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Master inclusion criteria','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Master inclusion criteria\n- These are the inclusion criteria that research participants must meet to be eligible to enter the Master Protocol and are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Sporadic or familial ALS diagnosed as clinically possible, probable, lab-supported probable, or definite ALS defined by revised El Escorial criteria (Appendix I). [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 2. Age 18 years or older. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. Capable of providing informed consent and complying with study procedures, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Time since onset of weakness due to ALS ≤ 24 months at the time of the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 5. Vital Capacity ≥ 50% of predicted capacity at the time of Master Protocol Screening measured by Slow Vital Capacity (SVC), or, if required due to pandemic-related restrictions, Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) measured in person. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. Participants must either not take riluzole or be on a stable dose of riluzole for ≥ 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 7. Participants must either not take edaravone or have completed at least one cycle (typically 14 days) of edaravone prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 8. Participants must have the ability to swallow pills and liquids at the time of the Master Protocol Screening Visit and, in the SI''s opinion, have the ability to swallow for the duration of the study. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 9. Geographically accessible to the site. [master:5.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- For the purposes of this study, the date of symptom onset will be defined as the date the participant first had symptoms of muscle weakness. To be eligible for this study, the date of symptom onset must be no greater than 24 months prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit date. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'These are the inclusion criteria that research participants must meet to be eligible to enter the Master Protocol and are common for all reg','["eligibility"]',3002,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:20','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["eligibility"]',3020,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:21','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Master and regimen consent sequence','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Master and regimen consent sequence\n- Phone 196+3 Week Day 28 participants will be consented a second time via the neg win-save rec ice-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria assessed, if applicable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Phone 196+3 Week Day 28 participants will be consented a second time via the neg win-save rec ice-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria ','["eligibility"]',3021,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:22','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization\n- Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system. [master:6.4.1]','\n',char(10)),'Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system.','["eligibility"]',3022,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:23','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Record eligibility violations, non-medical riluzole or edaravone discontinuation, and initiation of either therapy during participation as applicable protocol deviations.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (Record eligibility violations, non-medical riluzole or edaravone discontinuation, and initiation of either therapy during participation as applicable protocol deviations.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["eligibility"]',3023,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:24','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Consent-linked adverse event recording (Assess screening abnormalities and other events after Master Protocol consent for required AE recording, even if the participant fails screening.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Consent-linked adverse event recording (Assess screening abnormalities and other events after Master Protocol consent for required AE recording, even if the participant fails screening.)\n- Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Participants will be monitored for adverse events from the time they sign consent for the Master Protocol until completion of their participation as defined in the RSA. [master:10.1.1]','\n',char(10)),'Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Parti','["eligibility"]',3024,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:25','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation (If prohibited medication use is discovered after enrollment, follow the safety review and treatment-discontinuation requirements rather than treating it only as an eligibility issue.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation (If prohibited medication use is discovered after enrollment, follow the safety review and treatment-discontinuation requirements rather than treating it only as an eligibility issue.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants who discontinue investigational product and are followed under the ITT principle)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["eligibility"]',3025,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:26','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (Obtain renewed Master Protocol consent when re-screening after the initial screening window or seeking reassignment after prior randomization.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (Obtain renewed Master Protocol consent when re-screening after the initial screening window or seeking reassignment after prior randomization.)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants rescreened after the initial screening window)\n- The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants whose regimen participation is discontinued)','\n',char(10)),'If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Pr','["eligibility"]',3026,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:27','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (For repeated screening procedures or out-of-window safety laboratories, add the required Unscheduled Visit in EDC.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (For repeated screening procedures or out-of-window safety laboratories, add the required Unscheduled Visit in EDC.)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK reconciliation)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK entry)','\n',char(10)),'If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit f','["eligibility"]',3027,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:28','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: Assessment order and evaluators (When performing screening assessments, follow required assessment ordering, including measuring vital capacity before other testing fatigues the participant.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Assessments and procedures: Assessment order and evaluators (When performing screening assessments, follow required assessment ordering, including measuring vital capacity before other testing fatigues the participant.)\n- Study Visit Recommendations: [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Visit windows are consecutive calendar days and the target visit dates are calculated from the Baseline Visit - Day 0 (first day of investigational product administration). [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 2. Assessment of vital capacity should be performed at the start of a visit so as not to fatigue the participant with other testing. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. The remaining clinical assessments should be administered in the same sequence as listed in the sections below and at approximately the same time of the day. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- a. Any additional assessments that are included in an RSA for a given visit should be completed once the Master Protocol SOA for that visit is complete, unless otherwise specified in the RSA. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Outcomes for individual participants should be measured by the same evaluator throughout their participation in a regimen. Evaluators must be qualified, trained, certified, and delegated. Training documentation must be filed in the Investigator Site File (ISF). [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 17. PGI-C and PGI-S should be completed by the participant prior to other clinical assessments scheduled during the visit whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 18. CGI-C should be completed after all clinical assessments are completed by the investigator whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Study Visit Recommendations:','["eligibility"]',3028,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:29','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: First dose (After eligibility confirmation and randomization, give the first study dose only after all Baseline procedures are completed.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: First dose (After eligibility confirmation and randomization, give the first study dose only after all Baseline procedures are completed.)\n- Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all participants at Baseline)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline optional CSF collection)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- S Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed Week Clinic Clinic Day 812 56 +7 x Week Day 412 28 17 Baseline Clinic Day 0 x8 Screening'' Regimen Refer to Specific Clinic RSA Screening'' Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days DNA Collection'' (optional) Administer/Dispense Study Accountability/Compliance Columbia-Suicide Severity Vital Status Determination if any, should be performed. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Administer first dose of study drug only after Baseline Visit procedures are completed','["eligibility"]',3029,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:3','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > ALS diagnostic requirements','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## ALS diagnostic requirements\n- The diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis [ALS] requires: [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: all participants)\n- A - The presence of: [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: all participants)\n- (A:1) evidence of lower motor neuron (LMN) degeneration by clinical, electrophysiology cal or neuropathologic examination, (A:2) evidence of upper motor neuron (UMN) degeneration by clinical examination, and (A:3) progressive spread of symptoms or signs within a region or to other regions, as determined by history or examination, together with B - The absence of: [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: all participants)\n- (B:I) electrophysiological and pathological evidence of other disease processes that might explain the signs of LMN and/or UMN degeneration, and (B:2) neuroimaging evidence of other disease processes that might explain the observed clinical and electrophysiological signs. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: all participants)\n- A careful history, physical and neurological examination must search for clinical evidence of UMN and LMN signs in four regions [brainstem, cervical, thoracic, or lumbosacral spinal cord] (see Table 1) of the central nervous system [CNS]. Ancillary tests should be reasonably applied, as clinically indicated, to exclude other disease processes. These should include electrodiagnostic, neurophysiological, neuroimaging and clinical laboratory studies. Clinical evidence of LMN and UMN degeneration is required for the diagnosis of ALS. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: all participants)\n- A. Clinically Definite ALS is defined on clinical evidence alone by the presence of UMN, as well as LMN signs, in three regions. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: clinically definite ALS)\n- B. Clinically Probable ALS is defined on clinical evidence alone by UMN and LMN signs in at least two regions with some UMN signs necessarily rostral to (above) the LMN signs. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: clinically probable ALS)\n- C. Clinically Probable ALS - Laboratory-supported is defined when clinical signs of UMN and LMN dysfunction are in only one region, or when UMN signs alone are present in one region, and LMN signs defined by EMG criteria are present in at least two limbs, with proper application of neuroimaging and clinical laboratory protocols to exclude other causes. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: clinically probable ALS - laboratory-supported)\n- D. Clinically Possible ALS is defined when clinical signs of UMN and LMN dysfunction are found together in only one region or UMN signs are found alone in two or more regions; or LMN signs are found rostral to UMN signs and the diagnosis of Clinically Probable -Laboratory-supported ALS cannot be proven by evidence on clinical grounds in conjunction with electrodiagnostic, neurophysiologic, neuroimaging or clinical laboratory studies. Other diagnoses must have been excluded to accept a diagnosis of Clinically Possible ALS. [master:appendix-i-el-escorial-world-federation-] (applies to: clinically possible ALS)','\n',char(10)),'The diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis [ALS] requires:','["eligibility"]',3003,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:30','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Enrollment, eligibility and consent records (Document consent, assessed eligibility criteria, and specific screen-failure reasons in EDC for participants who do not qualify.)','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Enrollment, eligibility and consent records (Document consent, assessed eligibility criteria, and specific screen-failure reasons in EDC for participants who do not qualify.)\n- Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. [master:6.1] (applies to: all enrolled participants)\n- This information will include reasons for screen failure for participants not assigned to a regimen, as well as a log of all participants randomized within a regimen, irrespective of whether they have been treated with the intervention or not. [master:6.1]\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: regimen screen failures)\n- If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP) [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reason for screen failure [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Inclusion/Exclusion: Master and Regimen-Specific criteria must be evaluated. If "Not Met" is selected for any criterion, the system will fire a hard edit check requiring the selection of a specific failure reason. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility]\n- The willingness of the participant to participate in the study will be documented in writing on a consent form approved by the sIRB, which will be signed and dated by the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will keep the original consent forms and a copy will be given to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All participants will be offered the opportunity to discuss participation with a licensed physician Investigator and the participant''s decision to accept or decline this opportunity will be documented in the research file. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the participant''s next visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 1. The site staff provides a copy of the informed consent form to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 2. The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 3. The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant over the phone or via telemedicine. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 4. The participant signs the informed consent form and provides the original signed consent form back to the site. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant via phone or telemedicine signs the informed consent form. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: reconsented participants)','\n',char(10)),'Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system.','["eligibility"]',3030,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:4','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Master exclusion criteria','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Master exclusion criteria\n- These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Clinically significant unstable medical condition (other than ALS) that would pose a risk to the participant, according to SI''s judgment (e.g., cardiovascular instability, systemic infection,), or clinically significant laboratory abnormality or EKG changes. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Clinically significant abnormal liver or kidney function is exclusionary. The following values [alanine aminotransferase (ALT) or aspartate aminotransferase (AST) > 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN) or estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR) < 30 mL/min/1.73m°] are exclusionary regardless of clinical symptoms. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 2. Presence of unstable psychiatric disease, cognitive impairment, dementia or substance abuse that would impair ability of the participant to provide informed consent, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. Active cancer or history of cancer, except for the following: basal cell carcinoma or successfully treated squamous cell carcinoma of the skin, cervical carcinoma in situ, prostatic carcinoma in situ, or other malignancies curatively treated and with no evidence of disease recurrence for at least 3 years. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Use of investigational treatments for ALS (off-label use or active participation in a clinical trial within 5 half-lives (if known) or 30 days (whichever is longer) prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Please refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for current list of experimental therapies) [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 5. Exposure at any time to any gene therapies under investigation for the treatment of ALS (off-label use or investigational). [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. If female, breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, planning to become pregnant during the study, or of child-bearing potential and unwilling to use effective contraception, for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: female participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.10.2)\n- 7. If male of reproductive capacity, unwilling to use effective contraception for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: male participants of reproductive capacity) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.10.2)\n- 8. Anything that would place the participant at increased risk or preclude the participant''s full compliance with or completion of the study, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 9. If a participant is being re-screened, the disqualifying condition has not been resolved, or the mandatory wash-out duration has not occurred. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: re-screened participants)','\n',char(10)),'These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens:','["eligibility"]',3004,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:5','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Riluzole and edaravone','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Riluzole and edaravone\n- Participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry must be on a stable dose for 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit and must intend to continue taking the same stable dosage throughout the study. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking riluzole at study entry)\n- Participants taking concomitant edaravone at study entry must have completed at least one cycle of treatment (typically 14 days) prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit and must intend to continue taking the same stable dosage throughout the study. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants taking edaravone at study entry)\n- If the SI determines that riluzole or edaravone should be discontinued or dose-adjusted for medical reasons, this will not be considered a protocol deviation. If extenuating circumstances cause doses to be missed for reasons not intended or medically indicated (scheduling, drug availability, etc.), this will not be considered a protocol deviation. If riluzole or edaravone is stopped for any non-medical reason (participant preference), this will be recorded as a minor protocol deviation. If initiation of riluzole or edaravone therapy begins at any point during participation, this will be recorded as a minor protocol deviation. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: participants using riluzole or edaravone)','\n',char(10)),'Participants taking concomitant riluzole at study entry must be on a stable dose for 30 days prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit an','["eligibility"]',3005,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:6','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Regimen G exclusion criteria','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Regimen G exclusion criteria\n- Participants who meet any of the following criteria will be excluded from study entry: [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Diagnosis of epilepsy currently requiring antiepileptic therapy, or seizure of any cause within 6 months of randomization [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Hypersensitivity to DNL343 or any of the excipients contained within the DNL343 drug product [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Use of prescription or over-the-counter (OTC) medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 5. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for breast cancer resistance protein (BCRP) or organic anion transporter (OAT)3 transporters and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9) [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- There are RSA-specific requirements that apply to Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6.\nFor further details of contraceptive requirements for this RSA, please refer to Section 5.10.2. [regimen_g:4.2.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who meet any of the following criteria will be excluded from study entry:','["eligibility"]',3006,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:7','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Regimen G prohibited medication windows and allowances','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Regimen G prohibited medication windows and allowances\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV Table 4). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 5 for common examples of sensitive substrates). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increase based on the respective medication’s prescribing information and discretion of the investigator and/or medical monitor. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for BCRP or OAT3 and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug, and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 6 for common examples of sensitive substrates). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates (See Appendix IV, Table 5 and 6 for common examples) would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- For all excluded therapies described in this section, rescreening of participants is allowed after the defined window has passed. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G re-screening)','\n',char(10)),'Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 ','["eligibility"]',3007,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:8','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Regimen G contraception requirements','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Regimen G contraception requirements\n- Participants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. For female participants, one of the following must apply: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 1. Postmenopausal (defined as no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without an alternative medical cause, with a follicle-stimulating hormone level of > 40\nIU/L or mIU/mL at screening) [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 2. Surgically sterilized (eg, hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, bilateral oophorectomy) at least 6 months before dosing [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G female participants) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- OR\nc. For participants of childbearing potential: both the participant and the male partner must use highly effective contraception at the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. Highly effective contraception consists of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. The other must be a highly effective method (as defined in Appendix III), which includes one of the following: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential and male partners) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 1. Intrauterine device [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 2. Intrauterine hormone-releasing system [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 3. Bilateral tubal ligation [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 4. Abstinence, defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ (calendar, symptothermal, postovulation methods) is considered not reliable and would not be acceptable. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- OR\nd. For participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner, the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential. Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- 2. For male participants: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- a. When engaging in sex with a female partner of childbearing potential, both the male participant and his female partner must use highly effective contraception consisting of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom, and the other of which must be a highly effective form of birth control (as defined in Appendix III) used by the female partner. This is required from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants with a female partner of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- OR\nb. For azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential) (note: differs from master:5.2.2)\n- Male participants must not donate sperm at any time from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G male participants)','\n',char(10)),'Participants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial.','["eligibility"]',3008,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:eligibility:9','piece','','Eligibility (who can join) > Contraception review and documentation','Eligibility (who can join)',replace('## Contraception review and documentation\n- Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G double-blind and ATE periods)\n- - Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10 [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- [21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic','["eligibility"]',3009,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:1','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > ATE entry, treatment and duration','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## ATE entry, treatment and duration\n- Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extension (ATE) where all participants will receive DNL343 200 mg QD for up to an additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:s004] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- The duration of the ATE period is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks (i.e., 76 weeks total treatment duration). [regimen_g:s007] (applies to: ATE only)\n- The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks of ATE (i.e., 102 weeks total from Baseline visit). [regimen_g:s007] (applies to: ATE only)\n- At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Including the placebo-controlled treatment and the ATE periods, the total planned amount of time for a participant in Regimen G is a minimum of approximately 86 weeks and a maximum of approximately 112 weeks. [regimen_g:s009] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- This duration assumes a 6-week screening window, 24-week placebocontrolled treatment period, 52- to 78-week ATE period, and 4-week safety follow-up period. [regimen_g:s009] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- 5. The duration of the ATE is a minimum of 52 weeks with a maximum of 78 weeks per participant and will be completed when all active participants have completed the 52-week Visit in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- Following 24 weeks of double-blinded treatment, all participants will enter the active treatment extension (ATE) period to receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:3] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Each participant who meets all eligibility criteria for the Regimen will be randomized to receive either DNL343 200 mg or placebo for approximately 24 weeks of placebo-controlled treatment and will continue on to the ATE period for up to an additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:4.3] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Following the placebo-controlled period, participants will continue in the ATE as outlined in the SOA. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: ATE only)\n- The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE (ie, 102 weeks total from the Baseline visit). [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: ATE only)\n- The Industry Partner may consider extending or discontinuing the ATE period depending on the results from the primary analysis. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: ATE only)','\n',char(10)),'Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extens','["extension_period"]',4101,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:10','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Unscheduled safety visits and PK','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Unscheduled safety visits and PK\n- 3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE safety monitoring)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: ATE only)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: ATE only)','\n',char(10)),'3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dos','["extension_period"]',4110,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:11','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Remote visit allowances','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Remote visit allowances\n- 7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE visits marked [7]) (note: differs from regimen_g:s060)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- During the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed: [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- 1. ALSFRS-R (Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- 2. CNS-BFS (Week 8, 16, 28, 40, 52 only) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- 3. ROADS (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- 4. PGI-C (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)\n- 5. PGI-S (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: ATE remote visits)','\n',char(10)),'7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the','["extension_period"]',4111,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:12','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 2 ATE telephone visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 2 ATE telephone visit\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- 1. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- 2. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- 3. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- 4. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, containers, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)\n- 5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: ATE Week 2)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4112,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:13','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 4 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 4 ATE visit\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 4. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4; women of childbearing potential)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 7. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 8. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 9. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 11. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)\n- 12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: ATE Week 4)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4113,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:14','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 8 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 8 ATE visit\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 4. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8; women of childbearing potential)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 13. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)\n- 14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: ATE Week 8)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4114,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:15','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 12 ATE telephone visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 12 ATE telephone visit\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: ATE Week 12)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4115,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:16','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 16 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 16 ATE visit\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 4. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16; women of childbearing potential)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 11. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 12. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 14. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 15. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)\n- 16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: ATE Week 16)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit.','["extension_period"]',4116,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:17','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 20 ATE telephone visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 20 ATE telephone visit\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)\n- 6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: ATE Week 20)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4117,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:18','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 24 ATE telephone visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 24 ATE telephone visit\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- 1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- 2. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- 3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- 4. Perform drug compliance check-in [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)\n- 5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit • Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: ATE Week 24)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4118,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:19','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 28 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 28 ATE visit\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 1. Collect vital signs, including weight [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 4. ALSAQ-40 [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 5. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28; women of childbearing potential)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 9. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 15. ROADS [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 16. PGI-C [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 17. PGI-S [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 18. CGI-C [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 19. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)\n- 21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: ATE Week 28)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit.','["extension_period"]',4119,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:2','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Blinding and open-label designation','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Blinding and open-label designation\n- Research participants, investigators and site staff will not be blinded to the regimen assignment, but they will be blinded to active product or matching placebo assignment and this blind will be maintained throughout the ATE period of the study. [master:4.1] (applies to: Research participants, investigators and site staff) (note: differs from site_irt_guide:3.4)\n- Research participants, SIs, and everyone involved in the conduct of a given trial regimen will not be blinded to regimen assignments but will be blinded to the randomized treatment assignments within a regimen, and this blind will be maintained through the ATE period of the study, as outlined in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: Research participants, SIs, and everyone involved in a trial regimen) (note: differs from site_pharmacy_manual:2)\n- In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a participant, emergency unblinding of that single participant may be undertaken as outlined below. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: Medical emergency)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition) (note: differs from master:4.1; differs from master:6.4.2)\n- Regimen G utilizes a double-blind, placebo-controlled design for the initial 24-week period, followed by an open-label Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period where all subjects receive active DNL343. [site_pharmacy_manual:2] (applies to: Regimen G participants) (note: differs from master:4.1; differs from master:6.4.2)\n- - Rave Folder Name: ATE Week X (Open-Label) | Protocol Equivalent: Weeks 26 through 102 | Key eCRFs Included: Dynamically populates only after the Week 24 visit is marked complete. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: ATE EDC folders) (note: differs from master:4.1; differs from master:6.4.2)','\n',char(10)),'Research participants, investigators and site staff will not be blinded to the regimen assignment, but they will be blinded to active produc','["extension_period"]',4102,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:20','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 40 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 40 ATE visit\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 4. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40; women of childbearing potential)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 13. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)\n- 14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: ATE Week 40)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial.','["extension_period"]',4120,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:21','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 52 ATE visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 52 ATE visit\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 4. ALSAQ-40 [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 5. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52; women of childbearing potential)\n- 8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 9. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 12. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 16. ROADS [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 17. PGI-C [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 18. PGI-S [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 19. CGI-C [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)\n- 20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: ATE Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit.','["extension_period"]',4121,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:22','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Visits after Week 52','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Visits after Week 52\n- 8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE after Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11; differs from regimen_g:s011 schedule table)\n- Participants will continue in the ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 1. Collect vital signs including weight [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 2. Perform SVC [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 4. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52; women of childbearing potential)\n- 7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 8. Review concomitant medications [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol) [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 11. Dispense study drug to participant [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 12. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 15. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)\n- 16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: ATE after Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals.','["extension_period"]',4122,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:23','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Contraception requirements and documentation','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Contraception requirements and documentation\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential)\n- Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Participants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- 1. For female participants, one of the following must apply: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants)\n- 1. Postmenopausal (defined as no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without an alternative medical cause, with a follicle-stimulating hormone level of > 40\nIU/L or mIU/mL at screening) [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants)\n- 2. Surgically sterilized (eg, hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, bilateral oophorectomy) at least 6 months before dosing [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants)\n- OR\nc. For participants of childbearing potential: both the participant and the male partner must use highly effective contraception at the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. Highly effective contraception consists of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. The other must be a highly effective method (as defined in Appendix III), which includes one of the following: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential and male partners)\n- 1. Intrauterine device [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 2. Intrauterine hormone-releasing system [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 3. Bilateral tubal ligation [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential)\n- 4. Abstinence, defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ (calendar, symptothermal, postovulation methods) is considered not reliable and would not be acceptable. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential)\n- OR\nd. For participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner, the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential. Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Female participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner)\n- 2. For male participants: [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Male participants)\n- a. When engaging in sex with a female partner of childbearing potential, both the male participant and his female partner must use highly effective contraception consisting of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom, and the other of which must be a highly effective form of birth control (as defined in Appendix III) used by the female partner. This is required from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Male participants with a female partner of childbearing potential)\n- OR\nb. For azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Azoospermic male participants with a female partner of childbearing potential)\n- Male participants must not donate sperm at any time from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Male participants)','\n',char(10)),'[13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document curre','["extension_period"]',4123,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:24','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Drug discontinuation and continued follow-up','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Drug discontinuation and continued follow-up\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: All participants)\n- A research participant may choose to discontinue the investigational product at any time for any reason. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: All participants)\n- However, the SI or designee will encourage the research participant to follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants followed under ITT)\n- A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants followed under ITT for at least 28 days) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.12; differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: All participants permanently discontinuing investigational product)\n- Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Participants stopping study drug early)\n- If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Participants discontinuing medication and ITT follow-up)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Participants discontinuing drug but completing protocol) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.12; differs from regimen_g:s011)\n- Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Participants discontinuing study drug)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["extension_period"]',4124,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:25','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Early termination','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Early termination\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE early termination or consent withdrawal)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination or consent withdrawal)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Early termination; optional CSF collection)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination or consent withdrawal)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination before Week 52)\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early discontinuation after Week 52)\n- The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: ATE early termination)\n- - Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Early termination EDC folder)','\n',char(10)),'6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Sa','["extension_period"]',4125,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:26','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Follow-up safety call','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Follow-up safety call\n- Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: ATE completers)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: All ATE participants) (note: differs from master:6.5.1; differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.12)\n- Participants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Participants continuing into ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Assess and document AEs [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE safety follow-up) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.8; differs from master:6.5.1; differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE safety follow-up)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early) [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE early drug discontinuation)\n- 2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE completers)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Assess and document AEs [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)','\n',char(10)),'Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled.','["extension_period"]',4126,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:27','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > ATE completion and end definition','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## ATE completion and end definition\n- Participants are considered to have completed their participation in the ATE period of\nRegimen G if planned ATE period visits, including the last visit, ET Visit, or the last scheduled procedure shown in the Schedule of Activities (SOA), have been completed. [regimen_g:s026] (applies to: ATE completion)\n- The end of the ATE period in Regimen G occurs when all participants who initiated study drug in the ATE period have completed their participation in that period as defined in Section 3.2. [regimen_g:3.3] (applies to: End of Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),replace('Participants are considered to have completed their participation in the ATE period of\nRegimen G if planned ATE period visits, including the','\n',char(10)),'["extension_period"]',4127,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:28','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Vital status determination','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Vital status determination\n- 1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE participants)\n- Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Randomized participants)\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Randomized participants)\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Randomized participants)\n- An outside vendor will be used to ascertain death or date of last known alive for all randomized participants by using publicly available data sources. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Randomized participants)\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g., participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Randomized participants; vital status ascertainment)','\n',char(10)),'1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant','["extension_period"]',4128,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:29','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When preparing an ATE dose in clinic, follow preparation and in-use stability requirements and obtain the required second-person check.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When preparing an ATE dose in clinic, follow preparation and in-use stability requirements and obtain the required second-person check.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["extension_period"]',4129,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:3','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > ATE transition and kit assignment','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## ATE transition and kit assignment\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE only)','\n',char(10)),'1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit.','["extension_period"]',4103,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:30','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When an ATE assessment or follow-up identifies an expedited-reportable event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When an ATE assessment or follow-up identifies an expedited-reportable event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["extension_period"]',4130,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:31','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (If an ATE participant takes an extra dose or receives the wrong dose or kit, follow overdose and medication-error assessment and reporting requirements.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Overdose and medication errors (If an ATE participant takes an extra dose or receives the wrong dose or kit, follow overdose and medication-error assessment and reporting requirements.)\n- Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA. [master:7.4]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA.','["extension_period"]',4131,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:32','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (If emergency care during ATE requires disclosure of the participant''s randomized treatment, follow the emergency-unblinding procedure rather than treating ATE entry as permission to unblind.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (If emergency care during ATE requires disclosure of the participant''s randomized treatment, follow the emergency-unblinding procedure rather than treating ATE entry as permission to unblind.)\n- In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a participant, emergency unblinding of that single participant may be undertaken as outlined below. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Emergency unblinding for a research participant will only be undertaken when it is essential to treat the participant safely. It must only be used in an emergency when the identity of the treatment arm must be known to the SI to provide appropriate medical treatment or otherwise ensure the safety of research participants or others exposed to investigational products. In most cases, investigational product discontinuation and knowledge of the possible treatment assignments will be sufficient to treat a study participant who presents with an emergency condition. However, if unblinding is necessary, the blind may be broken only for that participant. [master:6.4.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Detailed unblinding information for the trial is detailed in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.4]\n- Emergency unblinding should only be performed if knowledge of the subject''s treatment assignment is absolutely necessary for their immediate medical management. Only the Principal Investigator (PI) or authorized Sub-Investigator has the system permissions to execute a code break. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Log into Suvoda. From the Home Dashboard, navigate to the Subjects list. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 2. Click on the relevant Subject ID. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 3. Under the Action menu, select Emergency Unblind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 4. Acknowledge the warning prompt detailing the severity of breaking the blind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 5. Enter the Reason for Unblinding (e.g., Severe Adverse Event requiring emergency intervention). [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 6. Authentication: You must enter your Suvoda username and password to execute this transaction. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 7. Click Reveal Treatment. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 8. The system will display whether the subject was on DNL343 200 mg or Placebo. An automated, unblinded alert email is instantly dispatched to the Sponsor Medical Monitor and the Suvoda Helpdesk. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded participants)','\n',char(10)),'In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a partic','["extension_period"]',4132,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:33','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When ATE dosing, visit timing, or required procedures depart from the protocol, assess and document the departure under the protocol-deviation rules.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When ATE dosing, visit timing, or required procedures depart from the protocol, assess and document the departure under the protocol-deviation rules.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["extension_period"]',4133,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:34','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Informed consent: Electronic consent records and registration (If a participant is reconsented at ATE transition or during ATE, record the reconsent on the electronic Informed Consent log.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Electronic consent records and registration (If a participant is reconsented at ATE transition or during ATE, record the reconsent on the electronic Informed Consent log.)\n- - Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: reconsented participants)\n- To register a subject who has signed the Master and Regimen-Specific Informed Consent Forms: [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration; Days -42 to -1)\n- 1. Click the Add Subject button on the Home Dashboard. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Informed Consent and the subject''s Year of Birth. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 3. Review the summary screen and click Confirm. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)\n- 4. The system will generate a unique Subject ID. Print or save the automated Screening Confirmation PDF for your source records. [site_irt_guide:3.1] (applies to: IRT registration)','\n',char(10)),'- Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent l','["extension_period"]',4134,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:35','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When ATE safety monitoring requires an unscheduled PK draw or out-of-window safety laboratory collection, add the required Unscheduled Visit folder and enter the sample data.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When ATE safety monitoring requires an unscheduled PK draw or out-of-window safety laboratory collection, add the required Unscheduled Visit folder and enter the sample data.)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK reconciliation)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK entry)','\n',char(10)),'If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit f','["extension_period"]',4135,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:36','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When an ATE C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety-response procedures rather than only recording the score.)','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When an ATE C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety-response procedures rather than only recording the score.)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:s011)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)','\n',char(10)),'At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s l','["extension_period"]',4136,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:4','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Dose, formulation and administration','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Dose, formulation and administration\n- DNL343 drug product and placebo will be administered reconstituted in water for oral or feeding tube administration (eg, gastrostomy tube [G-tube], jejunostomy tube [J-tube], or nasogastric tube) or coadministered with soft food (eg, applesauce or yogurt). [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Participants can take the drug with fluid thickener as needed. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- - Dose Formulation | Placebo-Controlled Period: Granules in stick pack | ATE Period: Granules in stick pack [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G study drug)\n- - Unit Dose Strength(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 100 mg | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo | ATE Period: 100 mg [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G study drug)\n- - Dose Level(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 200 mg QD | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo QD | ATE Period: 200 mg QD [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G study drug)\n- - Route of Administration | Placebo-Controlled Period: Oral or feeding tube | ATE Period: Oral or feeding tube [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G study drug)\n- Participants should take the study drug at approximately the same time each day in the morning without regard to food. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- All dosing should be completed within 15 minutes. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Clinic visits with predose PK assessments)\n- - Parameter: Dosing Regimen | Both: 200 mg Once Daily (QD). Subjects consume two (2) stick packs simultaneously each day. [site_pharmacy_manual:2] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- If the investigational product for an RSA is administered orally, and a participant loses the ability to swallow the investigational product, the possibility of alternative modes of administration (e.g., via G-tube) will be detailed in the RSA. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: Participants unable to swallow oral investigational product)','\n',char(10)),'DNL343 drug product and placebo will be administered reconstituted in water for oral or feeding tube administration (eg, gastrostomy tube [G','["extension_period"]',4104,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:5','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Missed doses and compliance','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Missed doses and compliance\n- Participants may take their missed dose as soon as they remember to take it in the same day. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- If the participant remembers the day after the dose was missed, only the next dose should be taken. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Only one dose is to be taken per day. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- 3. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE phone visits)','\n',char(10)),'Participants may take their missed dose as soon as they remember to take it in the same day.','["extension_period"]',4105,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:6','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Dose modification','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Dose modification\n- For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G participants)\n- The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Dose modification)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: ATE only)\n- In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: ATE entry at reduced dose)\n- As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: ATE only)\n- Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: ATE only)','\n',char(10)),'For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test resul','["extension_period"]',4106,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:7','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Week 24 transition visit','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Week 24 transition visit\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 1. ALSAQ-40 [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 2. CNS-BFS [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 12. -lead ECG [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- a) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- b) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Participants continuing in ATE)\n- 10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24; women of childbearing potential)\n- 11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- a) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit; optional CSF collection)\n- 13. Dispense study drug [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)\n- 14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Week 24 transition visit)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection.','["extension_period"]',4107,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:8','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > ATE schedule of activities','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## ATE schedule of activities\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [7] / Week 32 / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Week 36 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [7] / Week 40 / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Week 44 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 / Week 48 / Phone / Day168 ± 3 | Week 28 [7] / Week 52 / Clinic / Day 196 ± 14 | Week 40 [7] / Week 64 / Clinic / Day 280 ± 14 | Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 / Week 76 / Clinic / Day 364 ± 14 | All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] / Week 89 and Week 102 / Clinic / 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks | Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11)\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE and safety follow-up)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Administer/ Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [9], All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [9] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- [9]For participants returning for another visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE study drug dispensing)\n- - Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE and safety follow-up)\n- - Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE only)\n- 2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S and CGI-C at early termination)','\n',char(10)),'Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 ','["extension_period"]',4108,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:extension_period:9','piece','','Extension period (ATE) > Laboratory and vital-sign requirements','Extension period (ATE)',replace('## Laboratory and vital-sign requirements\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE vital signs)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE safety laboratory testing)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Biomarker Blood (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE only)\n- - Biomarker Urine (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE only)\n- * Endocrinology notes: Blood samples for ACTH and Cortisol should be collected at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit to control for diurnal variation. [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Scheduled endocrinology sampling)','\n',char(10)),'1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm','["extension_period"]',4109,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:1','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Source data and data entry','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Source data and data entry\n- All data will be managed in compliance with NCRI policies and applicable regulatory requirements. [master:12.2]\n- Site personnel will collect data onto source documents as appropriate and into electronic source documents (e.g., voice sample data, EMR data, etc). [master:12.2.1]\n- Values from source documents will be transcribed into the corresponding eCRFs in the EDC system by site personnel; electronically captured data will be transmitted to their respective vendors for consolidation and reconciliation with the study EDC system. [master:12.2.1]\n- Site personnel are instructed to enter collected data into the EDC system shortly after a visit. [master:12.2.2]\n- Data collection is the responsibility of the staff at the site under the supervision of the SI as specified in the delegation log for that site. [master:12.2.2]\n- During the study, the SI must maintain complete and accurate documentation for the study. [master:12.2.2]\n- The SI is responsible for ensuring the accuracy, completeness, legibility, originality and timeliness of the data reported. [master:12.6]\n- All source documents should be completed in a neat, legible manner to ensure accurate interpretation of data. [master:12.6]\n- Dark ink is required to ensure clarity of reproduced copies. [master:12.6]\n- When making changes or corrections, cross out the original entry with a single line, and initial and date the change. [master:12.6]\n- Do not erase, overwrite, or use correction fluid or tape on the original. [master:12.6]\n- Source document templates (STs) will be provided for use and maintained for recording data for each participant enrolled in the study. [master:12.6]\n- Data reported in the eCRF derived from source documents should be consistent with the source documents and discrepancies should be explained [master:12.6]\n- PRO Transcription Rule: Patient Reported Outcomes (ALSFRS-R, ALSAQ-40, CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-S, PGI-C) must be transcribed into Rave exactly as completed by the patient. Do not interpolate missing responses. If a patient skips a question, mark the field as "ND" (Not Done) to clear the system query. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation]','\n',char(10)),'All data will be managed in compliance with NCRI policies and applicable regulatory requirements.','["records_reporting"]',4201,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:10','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > IRT transaction records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## IRT transaction records\n- All critical transactions in Suvoda require 21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Signatures, meaning you must re-enter your username and password to finalize Randomization, Dispensing, or Unblinding actions. [site_irt_guide:2]\n- To register a subject who has signed the Master and Regimen-Specific Informed Consent Forms: [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- 1. Click the Add Subject button on the Home Dashboard. [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- 2. Enter the Date of Informed Consent and the subject''s Year of Birth. [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- 3. Review the summary screen and click Confirm. [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- 4. The system will generate a unique Subject ID. Print or save the automated Screening Confirmation PDF for your source records. [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- Stratification Data: [Standard Study Default – Biostats Confirmation Required]. During the Randomization step, Suvoda will prompt you to enter the subject''s stratification factors (e.g., Time since symptom onset [<18 months vs. >18 months] and Baseline Bulbar function). Ensure this data is verified in the EDC prior to executing IRT randomization. [site_irt_guide:3.1]\n- 3. Enter the required Stratification variables and the Subject Weight (kg). [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline randomization)\n- 4. Electronic Signature: Enter your Suvoda username and password to authenticate the randomization transaction. Click Confirm. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline randomization)\n- 6. Retrieve the exact assigned cartons from the pharmacy. Print the Dispensation Confirmation PDF and file it in the subject binder. [site_irt_guide:3.2] (applies to: Baseline randomization)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5]\n- 5. Enter the Reason for Unblinding (e.g., Severe Adverse Event requiring emergency intervention). [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 6. Authentication: You must enter your Suvoda username and password to execute this transaction. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 8. The system will display whether the subject was on DNL343 200 mg or Placebo. An automated, unblinded alert email is instantly dispatched to the Sponsor Medical Monitor and the Suvoda Helpdesk. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)','\n',char(10)),'All critical transactions in Suvoda require 21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Signatures, meaning you must re-enter your username and password to fi','["records_reporting"]',4210,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:11','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Queries and missing data','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Queries and missing data\n- To ensure accuracy and completeness of the data set, logic and range checks as well as in-form rules will be built into the EDC system, and electronic queries will be created to track potential data issues. [master:12.2.2]\n- The sites will only have access to the queries concerning their own participants. [master:12.2.2]\n- They must be resolved promptly prior to data locks. [site_edc_guide:6] (applies to: queries)\n- 1. To correct erroneous data: Click the edit pencil next to the field. Select "Entry Error" or "New Information" from the dropdown. Input the correct data and click Save. The query will automatically resolve. Do not type the corrected data into the query comment box. [site_edc_guide:6]\n- 2. To explain data without changing it: If the data is correct but unusual (e.g., a clinically verified extreme heart rate), leave the data field alone. Type your explanation in the Query Response text box beneath the query message and click Save. The query status will change to "Answered" pending DM review. [site_edc_guide:6]\n- Rave utilizes a standard partial date format. If the day is unknown, enter UNK in the day field (e.g., UNK-AUG-2021). If both the month and day are unknown, enter UNK-UNK-YYYY. Leave the date completely blank only if the entire date, including the year, is unknown. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: concomitant medication or medical history start dates)\n- Do not type text (like "ND" or "N/A") into a numeric field. Look for the checkbox next to the field typically labeled "ND" (Not Done) or "UNK" (Unknown). Checking this box overrides the numeric requirement and resolves the system query. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: laboratory parameter not done)','\n',char(10)),'To ensure accuracy and completeness of the data set, logic and range checks as well as in-form rules will be built into the EDC system, and ','["records_reporting"]',4211,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:12','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Electronic signatures and locks','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Electronic signatures and locks\n- Under 21 CFR Part 11, Principal Investigators must electronically sign clinical data. [site_edc_guide:7]\n- Forms will display the purple Requires Signature icon only after all mandatory fields are complete and all queries on that form are closed. [site_edc_guide:7]\n- 1. The PI logs into iMedidata and navigates to the Subject Matrix. [site_edc_guide:7] (applies to: batch signing)\n- 2. Click the Requires Signature icon at the top of the column header to filter for all signable forms across the subject. [site_edc_guide:7] (applies to: batch signing)\n- 3. Review the data presented. [site_edc_guide:7] (applies to: batch signing)\n- 4. Check the authorization acknowledgment box at the bottom of the page, enter your Medidata login password, and click Sign. [site_edc_guide:7] (applies to: batch signing)\n- Signature Invalidation Warning: If a CRC or CRA modifies a single data point or responds to a query on a form that has already been signed, the signature is immediately broken. The frozen/padlock icon will lift, and the form will revert to "Requires Signature" status, requiring the PI to re-sign the updated form. [site_edc_guide:7]\n- The EDC system will have the ability to lock each trial regimen independently to prevent any modification of data once a trial regimen is closed. [master:12.2.3]\n- Once this option is activated, every user will have read-only access to the data until all access is revoked following NCRI DM procedures. [master:12.2.3]\n- The specific database lock procedures are detailed in the DM Standard Operating Procedures (SOP). [master:12.2.3]\n- No. A Padlock indicates Data Management has hard-locked the form, typically for interim analysis or study closeout. If you discover a critical source data error on a locked form, you must contact the Lead Data Manager to request the form be temporarily unlocked. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: locked forms)','\n',char(10)),'Under 21 CFR Part 11, Principal Investigators must electronically sign clinical data.','["records_reporting"]',4212,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:13','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Monitoring access and reports','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Monitoring access and reports\n- The SIs will agree to allow monitors access to the clinical supplies, dispensing and storage areas, and to the clinical files of the study participants, and, if requested, agree to assist the monitors. [master:12.3]\n- Investigators are responsible for allowing and ensuring access to all source documents and medical records related to the participant''s participation in the study. [master:12.3.1]\n- Study monitors) will identify study non-compliance and if appropriate, the study monitor(s) will assist the site with developing a corrective and preventative action plan. [master:12.3.1]\n- All significant noncompliance will be communicated to the Sponsor. [master:12.3.1]\n- The Study Monitors will provide monitoring reports to the Sponsor and, if requested, and when required, will provide reports of protocol compliance to the Sponsor and other applicable parties as detailed in the Clinical Monitoring Plan. [master:12.3.2]\n- AE(s) of Special Interest events occurring within 24 hours of dosing for any given treatment arm, and any severe unexpected serious adverse events for a treatment arm are considered events of interest and will be reported in real-time (within 1 business day of CC awareness) to the DSMB. [master:12.4]\n- Prior to each DSMB meeting, the CC will provide an update to the DSMB on enrollment, data quality (missing data), and protocol adherence for each treatment arm. [master:12.4]\n- The CC will be responsible for communication with the DSMB. [master:12.4]\n- Clinical sites will be monitored to ensure compliance with the study protocol, data management requirements, and GCP. [master:12.2.1]','\n',char(10)),'The SIs will agree to allow monitors access to the clinical supplies, dispensing and storage areas, and to the clinical files of the study p','["records_reporting"]',4213,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:14','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Confidentiality and disclosure','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Confidentiality and disclosure\n- Study participant medical information obtained by this study is confidential, and disclosure to third parties other than those noted below is prohibited. [master:12.6.1]\n- Upon the participant''s permission, medical information may be given to his or her personal physician or other appropriate medical personnel responsible for his or her welfare. [master:12.6.1]\n- All local and federal guidelines and regulations regarding maintaining study participant confidentiality of data will be adhered to. [master:12.6.1]\n- Data generated by this study must be available for inspection by representatives of the US FDA, the Office for Human Research Protections (OHRP), the sponsor, all pertinent national and local health and regulatory authorities, the CC or their representative, Study Monitoring personnel, and the siRB. [master:12.6.1]\n- By signing the RSA, I agree to keep all information provided in strict confidence and to request the same from my staff. [regimen_g:s002] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Study documents will be stored appropriately to ensure their confidentiality. [regimen_g:s002] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- I will not disclose such information to others without authorization, except to the extent necessary to conduct the study. [regimen_g:s002] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- No data or results generated from the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial or its RSAs may be published without written agreement from the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Executive Committee. [master:12.7.1]\n- Data and sample sharing policies will be specified in the legal agreements governing the trial. [master:12.7.2]\n- The Master Protocol and each RSA will be registered on www.ClinicalTrials.gov. [master:12.7.3]\n- Results will be posted to ClinicalTrials. gov according to applicable regulations. [master:12.7.3]','\n',char(10)),'Study participant medical information obtained by this study is confidential, and disclosure to third parties other than those noted below i','["records_reporting"]',4214,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:15','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Record retention and transfer','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Record retention and transfer\n- US FDA regulations (21 CFR 312.62[c]) require that records and documents pertaining to the conduct of this study and the distribution of investigational drugs, including CRFs (if applicable), consent forms, laboratory test results, and medical inventory records, must be retained by the SI for two years after marketing application approval. [master:12.6.2]\n- If no application is filed, these records must be kept for seven years after the investigation is discontinued and the US FDA and the applicable national and local health authorities are notified. [master:12.6.2]\n- The CC or their representative will notify the Sis of these events. [master:12.6.2]\n- The SIs should retain all study documents and records until they are notified in writing by the Sponsor or their representative. [master:12.6.2]\n- No records may be disposed of without the written approval of the Sponsor. [master:12.6.2]\n- Written notification should be provided to the Sponsor prior to transferring any records to another party or moving them to another location. [master:12.6.2]','\n',char(10)),'US FDA regulations (21 CFR 312.62[c]) require that records and documents pertaining to the conduct of this study and the distribution of inv','["records_reporting"]',4215,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:16','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Vital-status follow-up timing','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Vital-status follow-up timing\n- Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebo- of the last participant''s last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each r','["records_reporting"]',4216,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:17','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone vi','["records_reporting"]',4217,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:18','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Site authorization and reliance agreements','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Site authorization and reliance agreements\n- Sites participating in this Master Protocol must have an executed sIRB Authorization Agreement (Reliance Agreement or RA) to rely on the MGB HRS to participate. [master:1.1]','\n',char(10)),'Sites participating in this Master Protocol must have an executed sIRB Authorization Agreement (Reliance Agreement or RA) to rely on the MGB','["records_reporting"]',4218,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:19','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Study personnel qualifications and restrictions','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Study personnel qualifications and restrictions\n- This study will not use the services of study personnel where sanctions have been invoked or where there has been scientific misconduct or fraud (e.g., loss of medical licensure, debarment). [master:1.2]','\n',char(10)),'This study will not use the services of study personnel where sanctions have been invoked or where there has been scientific misconduct or f','["records_reporting"]',4219,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:2','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Enrollment, eligibility and consent records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Enrollment, eligibility and consent records\n- Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system. [master:6.1] (applies to: all enrolled participants)\n- This information will include reasons for screen failure for participants not assigned to a regimen, as well as a log of all participants randomized within a regimen, irrespective of whether they have been treated with the intervention or not. [master:6.1]\n- All sites will be required to collect demographic information including age, gender, race, and ethnicity, and reasons for incligibility for the Master Protocol for participants that have signed the Master Protocol consent form and are deemed ineligible for the Master Protocol. [master:6.3] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reasons for ineligibility for the regimen must be documented [master:6.3] (applies to: regimen screen failures)\n- If a participant fails the Master Protocol screening, at a minimum the following information is captured and entered into the EDC system: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Demographics (including the participant''s NeuroSTAMP) [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Reason for screen failure [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Any eligibility criteria that were assessed prior to when the research participant failed to meet an inclusion criterion or met an exclusion criterion. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screen failures)\n- Inclusion/Exclusion: Master and Regimen-Specific criteria must be evaluated. If "Not Met" is selected for any criterion, the system will fire a hard edit check requiring the selection of a specific failure reason. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility]\n- The willingness of the participant to participate in the study will be documented in writing on a consent form approved by the sIRB, which will be signed and dated by the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will keep the original consent forms and a copy will be given to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All participants will be offered the opportunity to discuss participation with a licensed physician Investigator and the participant''s decision to accept or decline this opportunity will be documented in the research file. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the participant''s next visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 1. The site staff provides a copy of the informed consent form to the participant. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 2. The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 3. The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant over the phone or via telemedicine. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- 4. The participant signs the informed consent form and provides the original signed consent form back to the site. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant via phone or telemedicine signs the informed consent form. [master:6.2] (applies to: remote reconsent)\n- Re-Consent: If a subject is re-consented (e.g., transition to the ATE period or protocol amendment), record this on the Informed Consent log form by clicking Add a new Log line. [site_edc_guide:informed-consent-eligibility] (applies to: reconsented participants)','\n',char(10)),'Information about all research participants enrolled into the trial will be recorded in the Electronic Data Capture (EDC) system.','["records_reporting"]',4202,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:20','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Site participation requirements','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Site participation requirements\n- Sites participating in this Master Protocol must be members of the Northeast ALS Consortium (NEALS). [master:6.1]','\n',char(10)),'Sites participating in this Master Protocol must be members of the Northeast ALS Consortium (NEALS).','["records_reporting"]',4220,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:21','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Recruitment after sIRB approval','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Recruitment after sIRB approval\n- Research participants will be recruited from these centers, after approval by the sIRB. [master:6.1]','\n',char(10)),'Research participants will be recruited from these centers, after approval by the sIRB.','["records_reporting"]',4221,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:22','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## IRT regimen assignment and treatment randomization\n- Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system. [master:6.4.1]','\n',char(10)),'Regimen assignment and treatment randomization will be conducted using an interactive response technology (IRT) system.','["records_reporting"]',4222,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:23','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Pre-study protocol training','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Pre-study protocol training\n- Protocol procedures are reviewed with the SI and associated personnel prior to the study to ensure the accuracy and reliability of data. [master:12.1]','\n',char(10)),'Protocol procedures are reviewed with the SI and associated personnel prior to the study to ensure the accuracy and reliability of data.','["records_reporting"]',4223,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:24','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: SAE criteria (When recording an AE, assess the SAE criteria to determine whether expedited reporting is required.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: SAE criteria (When recording an AE, assess the SAE criteria to determine whether expedited reporting is required.)\n- A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria: [master:10.1.2]\n- 1. Results in death. [master:10.1.2]\n- 2. Is life threatening: that is, poses an immediate risk of death as the event occurred. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This serious criterion applies if the study participant, in the view of the SI or Sponsor, is at immediate risk of death from the AE as it occurs. It does not apply if an AE that might hypothetically have caused death if it were more severe. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: life-threatening criterion)\n- 3. Requires inpatient hospitalization or prolongation of existing hospitalization. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. Hospitalization for an elective procedure (including elective PEG tube/g-tube/feeding tube placement) or a routinely scheduled treatment is not an SAE by this criterion because an elective or scheduled "procedure" or a "treatment" is not an untoward medical occurrence. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: hospitalization criterion)\n- 4. Results in persistent or significant disability or incapacity. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This criterion applies if the "disability" caused by the reported AE results in a substantial disruption of the participant''s ability to carry out normal life functions. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: disability criterion)\n- 5. Results in congenital anomaly or birth defect in the offspring of the participant (whether the participant is male or female. [master:10.1.2]\n- 6. Necessitates medical or surgical intervention to preclude permanent impairment of a body function or permanent damage to a body structure. [master:10.1.2]\n- 7. Important medical events that may not result in death, are not life-threatening, or do not require hospitalization may also be considered SAEs when, based upon appropriate medical judgment, they may jeopardize the participant and may require medical or surgical intervention to prevent one of the outcomes listed in this definition. Examples of such medical events include blood dyscrasias or convulsions that do not result in inpatient hospitalization, or the development of drug dependency or drug abuse. [master:10.1.2]\n- An inpatient hospital admission in the absence of a precipitating, treatment-emergent, clinical adverse event may meet criteria for "seriousness" but is not an adverse experience, and will therefore, not be considered an SAE. An example of this would include a social admission (participant admitted for other reasons than medical, e.g., lives far from the hospital, has no place to sleep). [master:10.1.2]\n- A serious, suspected adverse drug reaction is an SAE that, in the opinion of the SI or Sponsor, there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event. [master:10.1.2]\n- The SI is responsible for classifying adverse events as serious or non-serious. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: SI)','\n',char(10)),'A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria:','["records_reporting"]',4224,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:25','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (Before executing or documenting emergency unblinding in IRT, follow the medical-emergency unblinding requirements.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Emergency unblinding (Before executing or documenting emergency unblinding in IRT, follow the medical-emergency unblinding requirements.)\n- In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a participant, emergency unblinding of that single participant may be undertaken as outlined below. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Emergency unblinding for a research participant will only be undertaken when it is essential to treat the participant safely. It must only be used in an emergency when the identity of the treatment arm must be known to the SI to provide appropriate medical treatment or otherwise ensure the safety of research participants or others exposed to investigational products. In most cases, investigational product discontinuation and knowledge of the possible treatment assignments will be sufficient to treat a study participant who presents with an emergency condition. However, if unblinding is necessary, the blind may be broken only for that participant. [master:6.4.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Detailed unblinding information for the trial is detailed in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.4]\n- Emergency unblinding should only be performed if knowledge of the subject''s treatment assignment is absolutely necessary for their immediate medical management. Only the Principal Investigator (PI) or authorized Sub-Investigator has the system permissions to execute a code break. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Log into Suvoda. From the Home Dashboard, navigate to the Subjects list. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 2. Click on the relevant Subject ID. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 3. Under the Action menu, select Emergency Unblind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 4. Acknowledge the warning prompt detailing the severity of breaking the blind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 5. Enter the Reason for Unblinding (e.g., Severe Adverse Event requiring emergency intervention). [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 6. Authentication: You must enter your Suvoda username and password to execute this transaction. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 7. Click Reveal Treatment. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 8. The system will display whether the subject was on DNL343 200 mg or Placebo. An automated, unblinded alert email is instantly dispatched to the Sponsor Medical Monitor and the Suvoda Helpdesk. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded participants)','\n',char(10)),'In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a partic','["records_reporting"]',4225,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:26','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When documenting a dose reduction or interruption, follow the regimen''s permitted dose-modification and interruption rules.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Dose modification and interruption (When documenting a dose reduction or interruption, follow the regimen''s permitted dose-modification and interruption rules.)\n- Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA. [master:7.2]\n- To adequately evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 participants should be maintained on the randomized treatment. For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Dose Modifications: The protocol permits a dose reduction to 100 mg due to intolerability. If the dose is adjusted, you must update the Planned Dose Level field, check "Yes" for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason (e.g., Adverse Event). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Any changes in dosage, along with the circumstances surrounding those for such, will be described in the RSA.','["records_reporting"]',4226,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:27','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Excursions, damaged kits and resupply (When a temperature excursion or damaged kit is documented, follow quarantine, disposition and resupply requirements before using affected drug.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Excursions, damaged kits and resupply (When a temperature excursion or damaged kit is documented, follow quarantine, disposition and resupply requirements before using affected drug.)\n- Site Temperature Excursions: If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C: [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Move the IMP to an alternate compliant storage location if the primary area cannot be rapidly corrected. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Quarantine the affected kits physically and systematically in the IRT. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Damaged Kits: If you open a shipment and find a physically damaged kit, receive the shipment without an excursion, but immediately navigate to the Inventory tab, select the damaged Kit Number, and change its status from Available to Damaged. Suvoda will automatically trigger a replacement shipment if your site''s buffer falls below the required threshold. [site_irt_guide:4.1] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- A: Do not give the damaged kit to the subject. Log into Suvoda, navigate to the Subject ID, and select the Replace Kit action. Select the damaged kit, choose the reason (e.g., Damaged at Site), and enter your password. Suvoda will assign a new, replacement Kit Number to the subject and change the damaged kit''s status to Damaged. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: kit damaged after IRT dispensing)\n- A: No. Suvoda uses a predictive algorithm to manage your site''s inventory based on your current screening and active subject data. If you have an unexpected surge in screening and believe your inventory is low, contact the Suvoda Helpdesk or the CRA, who can authorize a manual supplementary shipment. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: manual orders for extra drug)\n- A: NO. You must mark the shipment as containing an excursion during receipt, which automatically places the kits into Quarantined status in Suvoda. The system will physically block you from dispensing these kits until the Sponsor/CDMO reviews the TempTale data and explicitly changes their status to Available in the system. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: TempTale alarm; disposition pending)','\n',char(10)),'Site Temperature Excursions: If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C:','["records_reporting"]',4227,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:28','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a dose is prepared or administered in clinic, follow preparation, in-use stability and second-person verification requirements.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a dose is prepared or administered in clinic, follow preparation, in-use stability and second-person verification requirements.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["records_reporting"]',4228,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:29','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit and safety call requirements (When recording early termination or withdrawal, arrange the required Early Termination Visit and safety follow-up rather than only closing EDC and IRT records.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Early termination visit and safety call requirements (When recording early termination or withdrawal, arrange the required Early Termination Visit and safety follow-up rather than only closing EDC and IRT records.)\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [master:8.12] (applies to: participants terminating participation early)\n- The outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: all regimens)\n- For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants followed under the ITT principle)\n- A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing study drug and continuing ITT follow-up) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing medication and ITT follow-up)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing drug but completing the protocol) (note: differs from master:6.5.1; differs from regimen_g:6.10.12; differs from regimen_g:s011)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["records_reporting"]',4229,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:3','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Protocol amendments and deviations','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Protocol amendments and deviations\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["records_reporting"]',4203,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:30','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Informed consent: Reconsent triggers and timing (When an amendment introduces new information or procedures, assess and meet reconsent requirements before continuing affected study activities.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Reconsent triggers and timing (When an amendment introduces new information or procedures, assess and meet reconsent requirements before continuing affected study activities.)\n- Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: participants already enrolled; Master Protocol amendments)\n- Addition of regimens will not require re-consent. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: addition of regimens)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the Master Protocol or a regimen if new procedures or information is added in the future. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant''s next visit. [master:6.2] (applies to: participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from regimen_g:6)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from master:6.2)','\n',char(10)),'Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol','["records_reporting"]',4230,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:31','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation (When medication entry identifies a prohibited medication or other safety concern, follow the required clinical review and treatment-discontinuation rules rather than merely answering the EDC warning.)','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): Prohibited medication concerns and treatment discontinuation (When medication entry identifies a prohibited medication or other safety concern, follow the required clinical review and treatment-discontinuation rules rather than merely answering the EDC warning.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants who discontinue investigational product and are followed under the ITT principle)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["records_reporting"]',4231,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:4','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Adverse event records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Adverse event records\n- All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. [master:10]\n- It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10]\n- Examples of adverse events include: new conditions, worsening of pre-existing conditions, clinically significant abnormal physical examination signs (e.g., skin rash, peripheral edema, etc.), or clinically significant abnormal test results (e.g., lab values or vital signs), with the exception of outcome measure results, which are not being recorded as adverse events in this trial (they are being collected, but analyzed separately). [master:10.1.1]\n- Stable chronic conditions (e.g., diabetes, arthritis) that are present prior to the start of the study and do not worsen during the trial are NOT considered adverse events. [master:10.1.1]\n- Chronic conditions that occur more frequently (for intermittent conditions) or with greater severity, would be considered as worsened and therefore would be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1]\n- Symptoms of progression/worsening of ALS, including ''normal'' progression, will not be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1]\n- If discernible at the time of completing the AE log, a specific disease or syndrome rather than individual associated signs and symptoms should be identified by the SI and recorded on the AE log. [master:10.1.1]\n- However, if an observed or reported sign, symptom, or clinically significant laboratory anomaly is not considered by the SI to be a component of a specific disease or syndrome, then it should be recorded as a separate AE on the AE log. [master:10.1.1]\n- All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. [master:10.2]\n- All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. [master:10.2]\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. [master:10.2.3]\n- The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3]\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3]\n- Clinically significant out-of-range laboratory tests are recorded as adverse events and will be documented in the TEA summaries. [master:11.5]\n- Medical History vs. AEs: Pre-existing conditions must be logged on the Baseline Medical History form. If a pre-existing condition worsens in severity or frequency post-dose, log the worsening as a new event on the Adverse Events Log. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger]\n- Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log lines)\n- You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)','\n',char(10)),'All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose.','["records_reporting"]',4204,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:5','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Expedited safety notifications','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Expedited safety notifications\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3]\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2]\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3]\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3]\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. [master:10.3]\n- The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]\n- The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G overdose)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["records_reporting"]',4205,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:6','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Vital status records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Vital status records\n- Information on whether a participant has died may be obtained from the participant''s family, from clinic notes, or from a publicly available data source like the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) National Death Index or the Social Security Death Index. [master:8.15]\n- Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive and date last known to be PAV-free, will be determined by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 visit, as indicated in the SOA. [master:8.15] (applies to: randomized participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:7.7)\n- Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- At approximately the time of the last patient last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. [master:8.15] (applies to: randomized participants)\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants to assess vital status. [master:8.15] (applies to: randomized participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:7.7)\n- At approximately the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled portion of the regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g. participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method. [master:8.15]\n- When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g., participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Information on whether a participant has died may be obtained from the participant''s family, from clinic notes, or from a publicly available','["records_reporting"]',4206,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:7','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Medication and dosing records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Medication and dosing records\n- All concomitant medications and significant non-drug therapies, including supplements, received by a participant should be recorded on the appropriate source document and electronic Case Report Form (eCRF). [master:7.5]\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions]\n- Entering flagged medications into the ConMed log will fire a hard warning query alerting the CRA and Medical Monitor of a potential protocol violation. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions]\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: permanent investigational product discontinuation)\n- The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G in-clinic dosing)\n- Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G home dosing)\n- Deviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded. [regimen_g:5.7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Dose Modifications: The protocol permits a dose reduction to 100 mg due to intolerability. If the dose is adjusted, you must update the Planned Dose Level field, check "Yes" for Was the dose adjusted?, and select a reason (e.g., Adverse Event). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G double-blind and ATE periods)\n- Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview. [regimen_g:s089] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'All concomitant medications and significant non-drug therapies, including supplements, received by a participant should be recorded on the a','["records_reporting"]',4207,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:8','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Assessment and sample documentation','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Assessment and sample documentation\n- Training documentation must be filed in the Investigator Site File (ISF). [master:8] (applies to: evaluators)\n- Study personnel involved in conducting this study will be qualified to perform their respective tasks) as confirmed by the site and collection of required documentation. [master:1.2] (applies to: study personnel)\n- Height in em or in will be measured and recorded at the Master Protocol Screening Visit (Visit 1) only. [master:9.1.1]\n- This one-time height measurement must be used throughout the duration of the study. [master:9.1.1]\n- Tracings will be reviewed by a central ECG reader and a copy of the tracings will be kept on site as part of the source documents. [master:9.1.3]\n- A physical examination will be performed and recorded. [master:9.1.4]\n- A neurological examination will be performed and recorded. [master:9.1.5]\n- A printout from the spirometer of all VC trials will be retained. [master:9.2.2]\n- Only the 3 best trials are recorded on the CRF. [master:9.2.2]\n- All samples will be labeled with a code. [master:9.4]\n- The code will not include any identifiable information but will be linked to a specific participant, visit, and sample by data entry of the code into the EDC. [master:9.4]\n- A participant Neurological Global Unique Identifier (NeuroGUID), or its derivitives'', will be used as the identifier for participant''s samples participating in this study. [master:12.5]\n- Each shipment tendered to the courier must be accompanied by the following documentation: [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: sample shipments)\n- House Air Waybill (HAWB): Affix to the exterior of the shipment. This is your internal tracking form provided by World Courier. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: sample shipments)\n- Sample Requisition Form (PRF/GSR): Place the completed Requisition Form inside the shipping container, but outside the sealed biohazard bag. Retain a photocopy for the subject''s chart. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: sample shipments)\n- Customs Invoice (International Only): Print the Customs Invoice (Appendix 3) on your hospital/institution letterhead and sign it. Required for cross-border shipments to declare "No Commercial Value". [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: international sample shipments)\n- USDA Statement (International to USA): Required for animal/human material transit to the US. See Appendix 3. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: international sample shipments to USA)\n- Include this form in the shipping container. [site_lab_manual:appendix-2-sample-requisition-form-templ] (applies to: sample requisition form)\n- Form fields: Patient ID Number; Patient Initials; Date of Birth; Sex (Male / Female); Date Collected; Time Collected; Hospital Name; Investigator Name; Visit Name; Fasting Status (Yes / No); Discrepancies / Notes. [site_lab_manual:appendix-2-sample-requisition-form-templ] (applies to: sample requisition form)\n- Note: This document must be printed on your hospital''s letterhead paper, signed, and included in triplicate for any shipments crossing international borders into the United States. [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: Customs Invoice / USDA Statement)\n- Customs Invoice & USDA Statement. Fields: Date; Shipper (Site Name & Address); Consignee: Labcorp Central Laboratory Services, 8211 SciCor Drive, Indianapolis, IN 46214, USA; Contents: Human Other: Blood / Serum / Plasma / CSF / Urine samples; Quantity (mL). [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: international sample shipments to USA)\n- Value: No Commercial Value - For Customs Purposes Only $10 USD. Signature and Job Title. [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: Customs Invoice / USDA Statement)','\n',char(10)),'Training documentation must be filed in the Investigator Site File (ISF).','["records_reporting"]',4208,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:records_reporting:9','piece','','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications > Drug accountability and excursion records','Records, data entry, deviations and notifications',replace('## Drug accountability and excursion records\n- Samples of labels will be filed in the Trial Master File (TMF). [master:7.1.2]\n- Storage conditions will be adequately monitored and temperature in the area in which the investigational product is stored will be controlled, monitored and recorded, at a minimum daily. [master:7.1.3]\n- In accordance with local regulatory requirements, the SI or delegated study staff documents the amount of investigational product dispensed and/or administered to study participants, the amount received from the Central Pharmacy, and the amount destroyed upon completion of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The SI is responsible for ensuring product accountability records are maintained throughout the course of the study. [master:7.1.3]\n- The research pharmacist or designated study staff will be responsible for maintaining an accurate record of the shipment and dispensing of investigational product in a drug accountability log. [master:7.1.3]\n- Details on how the investigational product will be destroyed, who is responsible for the destruction, and how long documentation will be retained at sites, will be provided in the MOP or defer to site specific policies. [master:7.1.4]\n- The investigator or designee must confirm that appropriate temperature conditions have been maintained during transit for all study drug received and that any discrepancies are reported and resolved before use of the study drug. [regimen_g:5.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Download Data: Plug the TempTale USB into a computer. Save the automatically generated PDF report to your site''s regulatory binder. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: IMP receipt)\n- - Alarm Triggered: Immediately isolate the affected kits in the pharmacy under appropriate storage conditions. Mark them as "Quarantined" in the IRT and email the PDF report to the CDMO Supply Chain Manager for disposition. Do NOT dispense. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: IMP receipt temperature alarm)\n- 5. IRT Registration: Log into the IRT system. Enter the shipment number and confirm receipt of all physical kit numbers. The system will transition the kits from "In Transit" to "Available" inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:3] (applies to: IMP receipt)\n- Site Temperature Excursions: If the pharmacy environmental monitoring system detects a temperature > 30°C: [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Quarantine the affected kits physically and systematically in the IRT. [site_pharmacy_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G site temperature excursions)\n- Master Drug Accountability Log (DAL): The Pharmacy must maintain a master log tracking the receipt, overall inventory, and final disposition of all cartons. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Subject Dispensing Log: Maintain a subject-specific log tracking the exact dates and kit numbers dispensed to, and returned by, each subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Returns & Compliance: Instruct subjects to return all unused stick packs, as well as all empty cartons and empty stick pack wrappers, to every clinic visit. Site staff must count the returned intact packets and wrappers to calculate dosing compliance and record this in the subject''s eCRF and DAL. [site_pharmacy_manual:7] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Site staff must physically count the returns and update the Suvoda system: [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject ID and select Kit Accountability. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Locate the previously dispensed Kit Number. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Enter the number of Unused Stick Packs returned. The system updates the kit status to Returned, ensuring complete lifecycle tracking for the Sponsor. [site_irt_guide:4.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Samples of labels will be filed in the Trial Master File (TMF).','["records_reporting"]',4209,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:1','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > AE definitions and exclusions','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## AE definitions and exclusions\n- An AE is any unfavorable and unintended sign (including a clinically significant abnormal laboratory finding, for example, symptom, or disease temporally associated with a study, use of a drug product or device whether or not considered related to the drug product or device. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Adverse drug reactions (ADR) are all noxious and unintended responses to a medicinal product related to any dose. The phrase "responses to a medicinal product" means that a causal relationship between a medicinal product and an adverse event is at least a reasonable possibility, i.e., the relationship cannot be ruled out. Therefore, a subset of AEs can be classified as suspected ADRs, if there is a causal relationship to the medicinal product. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Examples of adverse events include: new conditions, worsening of pre-existing conditions, clinically significant abnormal physical examination signs (e.g., skin rash, peripheral edema, etc.), or clinically significant abnormal test results (e.g., lab values or vital signs), with the exception of outcome measure results, which are not being recorded as adverse events in this trial (they are being collected, but analyzed separately). [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Stable chronic conditions (e.g., diabetes, arthritis) that are present prior to the start of the study and do not worsen during the trial are NOT considered adverse events. Chronic conditions that occur more frequently (for intermittent conditions) or with greater severity, would be considered as worsened and therefore would be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger)\n- - Medical History vs. AEs: Pre-existing conditions must be logged on the Baseline Medical History form. If a pre-existing condition worsens in severity or frequency post-dose, log the worsening as a new event on the Adverse Events Log. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:10.1.1)\n- Adverse events are generally detected in two ways: [master:10.1.1]\n- 1. Clinical: symptoms reported by the participant or signs detected on examination. [master:10.1.1]\n- 2. Ancillary Tests: abnormalities of vital signs, laboratory tests, and other diagnostic procedures (other than the outcome measures, the results of which are not being captured as AEs). [master:10.1.1]\n- Symptoms of progression/worsening of ALS, including ''normal'' progression, will not be recorded as adverse events. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If discernible at the time of completing the AE log, a specific disease or syndrome rather than individual associated signs and symptoms should be identified by the SI and recorded on the AE log. However, if an observed or reported sign, symptom, or clinically significant laboratory anomaly is not considered by the SI to be a component of a specific disease or syndrome, then it should be recorded as a separate AE on the AE log. [master:10.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- An unexpected adverse event is any adverse event, the specificity or severity of which is not consistent with the current regimen applicable Investigator''s Brochure. An unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction is any unexpected adverse event that for which, in the opinion of the SI or Sponsor, there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event. [master:10.1.1]\n- Clinically significant out-of-range laboratory tests are recorded as adverse events and will be documented in the TEA summaries. [master:11.5] (applies to: all participants)','\n',char(10)),'An AE is any unfavorable and unintended sign (including a clinically significant abnormal laboratory finding, for example, symptom, or disea','["safety"]',3301,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:10','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Emergency unblinding','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Emergency unblinding\n- In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a participant, emergency unblinding of that single participant may be undertaken as outlined below. [master:6.4.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Emergency unblinding for a research participant will only be undertaken when it is essential to treat the participant safely. It must only be used in an emergency when the identity of the treatment arm must be known to the SI to provide appropriate medical treatment or otherwise ensure the safety of research participants or others exposed to investigational products. In most cases, investigational product discontinuation and knowledge of the possible treatment assignments will be sufficient to treat a study participant who presents with an emergency condition. However, if unblinding is necessary, the blind may be broken only for that participant. [master:6.4.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Detailed unblinding information for the trial is detailed in the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial Blinding and Unblinding Plan. [master:6.4.4]\n- Emergency unblinding should only be performed if knowledge of the subject''s treatment assignment is absolutely necessary for their immediate medical management. Only the Principal Investigator (PI) or authorized Sub-Investigator has the system permissions to execute a code break. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Log into Suvoda. From the Home Dashboard, navigate to the Subjects list. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 2. Click on the relevant Subject ID. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 3. Under the Action menu, select Emergency Unblind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 4. Acknowledge the warning prompt detailing the severity of breaking the blind. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 5. Enter the Reason for Unblinding (e.g., Severe Adverse Event requiring emergency intervention). [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 6. Authentication: You must enter your Suvoda username and password to execute this transaction. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 7. Click Reveal Treatment. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- 8. The system will display whether the subject was on DNL343 200 mg or Placebo. An automated, unblinded alert email is instantly dispatched to the Sponsor Medical Monitor and the Suvoda Helpdesk. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: emergency unblinding)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded participants)','\n',char(10)),'In the event of a medical emergency that necessitates the unblinding of the Medical Monitor (MM) or a SI to safely provide care for a partic','["safety"]',3310,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:11','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Suicidality assessment and response','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Suicidality assessment and response\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:s011)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)','\n',char(10)),'At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s l','["safety"]',3311,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:12','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Clinical safety laboratory tests','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Clinical safety laboratory tests\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Hematology with differential panel: complete blood count with differential (hematocrit, hemoglobin, platelet count, RBC indices, Total RBC, Total WBC, and WBC & differential) [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Blood chemistry panel/Liver function tests (LFTs): alanine aminotransferase (ALT (SGPT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST (SGOT)), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate, blood urea nitrogen, calcium, chloride, creatinine (estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate [eGFR] will be calculated using the using the CKD-EPI 2021 equation), cystatin C, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, potassium, sodium, total bilirubin and total protein [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants; hCG for WOCBP)\n- All participants will have clinical safety laboratory tests at the designated visits outlined in the schedule of activities. The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: screening)\n- Clinical Safety Labs: the default and preferred method for collecting clinical safety labs is by using the study specific central laboratory as described in the study MOP. If clinical safety labs are needed and it is not feasible to use the central laboratory, clinical safety labs can be collected using one of the following methods: [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- Local site laboratory resources, or [master:8]\n- Independent labs offering such services as described in this protocol and/or local to the participant (e.g. primary care physician) [master:8]\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- A: Do not type text (like "ND" or "N/A") into a numeric field. Look for the checkbox next to the field typically labeled "ND" (Not Done) or "UNK" (Unknown). Checking this box overrides the numeric requirement and resolves the system query. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: laboratory parameter not done)\n- After a participant is assigned to a regimen, RSA " Clinical safety labs include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, liver function tests, and urinalysis. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following v','["safety"]',3312,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:13','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Endocrinology safety tests','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Endocrinology safety tests\n- - Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all participants prior to ATE) [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- 14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 6. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Day 0)\n- 9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24)\n- - Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- * Endocrinology notes: Blood samples for ACTH and Cortisol should be collected at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit to control for diurnal variation. [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'- Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all pa','["safety"]',3313,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:14','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > ECG safety assessments','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## ECG safety assessments\n- A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. The ECG should be collected using the study provided ECG device. Tracings will be reviewed by a central ECG reader and a copy of the tracings will be kept on site as part of the source documents. Additional guidance is included in the MOP. [master:9.1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. -lead ECG (to be done at all in-clinic visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - 12-Lead ECG Regimen: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedu','["safety"]',3314,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:15','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Vital signs','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Vital signs\n- - Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'- Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [','["safety"]',3315,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:16','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Lumbar puncture safety','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Lumbar puncture safety\n- Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture participants)\n- - Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Week 16 [12] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including: [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture participants)\n- Local pain at injection site [master:9.3.4]\n- Reaction to anesthetic agents [master:9.3.4]\n- Bleeding at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4]\n- Infection at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4]\n- Post-LP low-pressure headache [master:9.3.4]\n- The procedure must be performed by the SI or another staff member with the appropriate qualifications, experience, and training in performing LPs per institutional policy. When possible, LPs should be performed with an atraumatic Sprotte needle to reduce the risk of post-LP headache. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: lumbar puncture)','\n',char(10)),'Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized rat','["safety"]',3316,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:17','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Safety follow-up','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Safety follow-up\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety after their last dose of study drug. Additional details, including the timing of this call, can be found in the RSA. The following procedure will be performed at a minimum: [master:8.11] (applies to: all participants)\n- Assess and document AEs [master:8.11] (applies to: Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants followed under ITT) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.9)\n- Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:6.10.12)\n- Participants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase. [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G continuing into ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Assess and document AEs [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.8)\n- Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early) [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 1. Assess and document AEs [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)\n- 2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety after their last dose of study drug. Additional details, including the timing of this call, can be','["safety"]',3317,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:18','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Early termination safety procedures','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Early termination safety procedures\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [master:8.12] (applies to: early terminations)\n- The outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G off-treatment follow-up) (note: differs from master:6.5.1)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations before Week 52)\n- For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["safety"]',3318,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:19','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > AE review schedule','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## AE review schedule\n- - Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10 [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- - Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 19. The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G early terminations)\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 Days [13] | Regimen Specific Screening [1] / Clinic / –41 to 0 Day [13] | Baseline [1] / Clinic / Day 0 | Week 2 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [12] / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [12] / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [12] / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 or ET Visit [19] / Clinic / Day 168 ± 7 | Follow-Up Safety Call 10 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled safety assessments) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.8)\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [7] / Week 32 / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Week 36 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [7] / Week 40 / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Week 44 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 / Week 48 / Phone / Day168 ± 3 | Week 28 [7] / Week 52 / Clinic / Day 196 ± 14 | Week 40 [7] / Week 64 / Clinic / Day 280 ± 14 | Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 / Week 76 / Clinic / Day 364 ± 14 | All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] / Week 89 and Week 102 / Clinic / 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks | Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE safety assessments) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11)\n- 8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)','\n',char(10)),'- Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12],','["safety"]',3319,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:2','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > SAE criteria','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## SAE criteria\n- A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria: [master:10.1.2]\n- 1. Results in death. [master:10.1.2]\n- 2. Is life threatening: that is, poses an immediate risk of death as the event occurred. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This serious criterion applies if the study participant, in the view of the SI or Sponsor, is at immediate risk of death from the AE as it occurs. It does not apply if an AE that might hypothetically have caused death if it were more severe. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: life-threatening criterion)\n- 3. Requires inpatient hospitalization or prolongation of existing hospitalization. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. Hospitalization for an elective procedure (including elective PEG tube/g-tube/feeding tube placement) or a routinely scheduled treatment is not an SAE by this criterion because an elective or scheduled "procedure" or a "treatment" is not an untoward medical occurrence. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: hospitalization criterion)\n- 4. Results in persistent or significant disability or incapacity. [master:10.1.2]\n- a. This criterion applies if the "disability" caused by the reported AE results in a substantial disruption of the participant''s ability to carry out normal life functions. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: disability criterion)\n- 5. Results in congenital anomaly or birth defect in the offspring of the participant (whether the participant is male or female. [master:10.1.2]\n- 6. Necessitates medical or surgical intervention to preclude permanent impairment of a body function or permanent damage to a body structure. [master:10.1.2]\n- 7. Important medical events that may not result in death, are not life-threatening, or do not require hospitalization may also be considered SAEs when, based upon appropriate medical judgment, they may jeopardize the participant and may require medical or surgical intervention to prevent one of the outcomes listed in this definition. Examples of such medical events include blood dyscrasias or convulsions that do not result in inpatient hospitalization, or the development of drug dependency or drug abuse. [master:10.1.2]\n- An inpatient hospital admission in the absence of a precipitating, treatment-emergent, clinical adverse event may meet criteria for "seriousness" but is not an adverse experience, and will therefore, not be considered an SAE. An example of this would include a social admission (participant admitted for other reasons than medical, e.g., lives far from the hospital, has no place to sleep). [master:10.1.2]\n- A serious, suspected adverse drug reaction is an SAE that, in the opinion of the SI or Sponsor, there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event. [master:10.1.2]\n- The SI is responsible for classifying adverse events as serious or non-serious. [master:10.1.2] (applies to: SI)','\n',char(10)),'A SAE is defined as an adverse event that meets any of the following criteria:','["safety"]',3302,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:20','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Remote safety visits','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Remote safety visits\n- If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. The following procedures should be completed in the suggested order and as per the Schedule of Activities: [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- ALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable; each RA may describe additional activities. [master:8] (applies to: remote visits)\n- 12. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G designated clinic visits)\n- 7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G designated ATE clinic visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not av','["safety"]',3320,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:21','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Safety-related eligibility','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Safety-related eligibility\n- These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens: [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Clinically significant unstable medical condition (other than ALS) that would pose a risk to the participant, according to SI''s judgment (e.g., cardiovascular instability, systemic infection,), or clinically significant laboratory abnormality or EKG changes. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Clinically significant abnormal liver or kidney function is exclusionary. The following values [alanine aminotransferase (ALT) or aspartate aminotransferase (AST) > 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN) or estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR) < 30 mL/min/1.73m°] are exclusionary regardless of clinical symptoms. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 2. Presence of unstable psychiatric disease, cognitive impairment, dementia or substance abuse that would impair ability of the participant to provide informed consent, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. Active cancer or history of cancer, except for the following: basal cell carcinoma or successfully treated squamous cell carcinoma of the skin, cervical carcinoma in situ, prostatic carcinoma in situ, or other malignancies curatively treated and with no evidence of disease recurrence for at least 3 years. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Use of investigational treatments for ALS (off-label use or active participation in a clinical trial within 5 half-lives (if known) or 30 days (whichever is longer) prior to the Master Protocol Screening Visit. (Please refer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for current list of experimental therapies) [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 5. Exposure at any time to any gene therapies under investigation for the treatment of ALS (off-label use or investigational). [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 6. If female, breastfeeding, known to be pregnant, planning to become pregnant during the study, or of child-bearing potential and unwilling to use effective contraception, for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: female participants)\n- 7. If male of reproductive capacity, unwilling to use effective contraception for the duration of the trial and for 3 months, or as specified in each RSA, after discontinuing study treatment. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: male participants of reproductive capacity)\n- 8. Anything that would place the participant at increased risk or preclude the participant''s full compliance with or completion of the study, in the SI''s opinion. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- 9. If a participant is being re-screened, the disqualifying condition has not been resolved, or the mandatory wash-out duration has not occurred. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: re-screening)\n- If justified based on regimen''s specific investigational product, additional inclusion or exclusion criteria (c.g., contraindications, laboratory parameters) will be listed in the RSA. [master:5.2.2] (applies to: all regimens)\n- A research participant discontinues participation in a regimen for personal reasons (e.g., inconvenience of the intervention, mode of administration, required assessments) or due to an adverse event, or is discontinued by the SI [master:8.1.2] (applies to: re-screening after discontinuation)\n- The research participant may not be re-screened until after the date that their 36-Week Visit would have occurred, had they completed participation in that regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: re-screening after personal-reason, AE, or SI discontinuation)','\n',char(10)),'These following exclusion criteria are common for all regimens:','["safety"]',3321,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:22','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Contraception monitoring','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Contraception monitoring\n- - Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10 [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- [21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential)\n- - Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G female participants of childbearing potential and men with a female partner of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'- Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12,','["safety"]',3322,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:23','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Medication interaction safety','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Medication interaction safety\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV Table 4). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 5 for common examples of sensitive substrates). Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increase based on the respective medication’s prescribing information and discretion of the investigator and/or medical monitor. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions)\n- Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for BCRP or OAT3 and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug, and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 6 for common examples of sensitive substrates). [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates (See Appendix IV, Table 5 and 6 for common examples) would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- For all excluded therapies described in this section, rescreening of participants is allowed after the defined window has passed. [regimen_g:5.9] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Prohibited Medications Check: DNL343 protocol excludes the use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates (e.g., St. John''s wort, midazolam, itraconazole). Entering flagged medications into the ConMed log will fire a hard warning query alerting the CRA and Medical Monitor of a potential protocol violation. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:5.9)\n- The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increases based on the respective medication’s prescribing information. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: Regimen G sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 5 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:s094] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. Medications that are considered to have a wide therapeutic index are permitted, and participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of AEs noted in the respective medication’s prescribing information. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: Regimen G BCRP or OAT3 substrates)\n- The medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 6 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication. [regimen_g:s095] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 ','["safety"]',3323,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:24','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Safety specimen processing','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Safety specimen processing\n- Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required. Blood must be collected in the sequence: Light Blue → Gold SST → Lavender EDTA. Always program centrifuges using Relative Centrifugal Force (RCF or g), not RPM. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: blood collection)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Light Blue (Sodium Citrate) | Target Assay: Coagulation | Processing Instructions: Invert 3-4x. Centrifuge at 1,500 x g for 15 min at Room Temp (20-25°C). Transfer plasma to cryovial. Freeze immediately. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: coagulation specimens)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Gold SST (Serum Separator) | Target Assay: Safety Chem, Endocrinology | Processing Instructions: Invert 5x. Allow to clot upright for 30 min. Centrifuge at 1,500 - 2,000 x g for 10-15 min at Room Temp. Ship ambient. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety chemistry and endocrinology specimens)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Lavender EDTA (3.0 mL) | Target Assay: Safety Hematology | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Do Not Centrifuge. Ship as ambient whole blood on day of collection. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety hematology specimens)\n- - TOTALS (mL): Screening 14.2 mL | Baseline 40.5 mL | Wk 4 19.5 mL | Wk 8 27.5 mL | Wk 16 30.2 mL | Wk 24 40.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) 11.5 - 23.5 mL [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: Regimen G research blood volumes)\n- * Only collected at specific ATE visits (Wk 16, 28, 52). [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: ATE biomarker blood)','\n',char(10)),'Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required. Blood must be collected in the sequence: Light Blue → Gold SST → Lavender ED','["safety"]',3324,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:25','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Consent-linked adverse event recording','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Consent-linked adverse event recording\n- Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Participants will be monitored for adverse events from the time they sign consent for the Master Protocol until completion of their participation as defined in the RSA. [master:10.1.1]','\n',char(10)),'Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Parti','["safety"]',3325,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:26','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When safety assessment requires an out-of-window laboratory test or unscheduled PK draw, add an Unscheduled Visit in EDC to record it.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Sample and PK entry in EDC (When safety assessment requires an out-of-window laboratory test or unscheduled PK draw, add an Unscheduled Visit in EDC to record it.)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK reconciliation)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK entry)','\n',char(10)),'If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit f','["safety"]',3326,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:27','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Unscheduled safety and PK collection (When a TEAE causes dose reduction or interruption, or a severe TEAE requires an unscheduled evaluation, obtain the required unscheduled PK sample.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping: Unscheduled safety and PK collection (When a TEAE causes dose reduction or interruption, or a severe TEAE requires an unscheduled evaluation, obtain the required unscheduled PK sample.)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["safety"]',3327,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:28','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: IRT dose assignment and dispensing (When toxicity requires a dose reduction, record the reduced dose in IRT so dispensing and resupply match the prescribed dose.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: IRT dose assignment and dispensing (When toxicity requires a dose reduction, record the reduced dose in IRT so dispensing and resupply match the prescribed dose.)\n- At subsequent in-clinic visits, the subject will return unused drug and receive new supply. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID from the dashboard and click Dispense. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 2. Select the current visit from the dropdown (e.g., Week 4). (Note: Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- 4. Authenticate with your password and dispense the newly assigned Kit Numbers. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, 24)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition to ATE)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- For take-home dosing, the Pharmacist or authorized Coordinator will dispense intact cartons to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Log into the IRT to register the dispensing visit. The IRT will assign specific Kit Number(s) to the subject. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Retrieve the exact matching Kit Number(s) from the pharmacy inventory. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Affix a site-specific dispensing label to the secondary carton. The label must not obscure the original clinical label and must include: Subject ID Number; Protocol Number (NCT05842941); Date Dispensed & Dispenser Initials; Instructions: "Take 2 stick packs once daily. Store below 30°C." [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)\n- - Provide the subject/caregiver with thorough training on the preparation methods (Water or Soft Food) and emphasize the 15-minute post-mixing administration window. [site_pharmacy_manual:dispensing-to-subjects-for-home-use] (applies to: Regimen G take-home dosing)','\n',char(10)),'At subsequent in-clinic visits, the subject will return unused drug and receive new supply.','["safety"]',3328,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:29','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Discontinuation returns and supply closure (When a safety issue leads to permanent drug discontinuation, arrange return of unused investigational product and close out supply accountability.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Discontinuation returns and supply closure (When a safety issue leads to permanent drug discontinuation, arrange return of unused investigational product and close out supply accountability.)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: investigational product discontinuation)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participant termination)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: permanent discontinuation or trial completion)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["safety"]',3329,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:3','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > AE assessment','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## AE assessment\n- At each visit (including telephone interviews), the participant will be asked if they have had any problems or symptoms since their last visit in order to determine the occurrence of adverse events. [master:10.2.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If the participant reports an adverse event, site staff will probe further to determine: [master:10.2.1] (applies to: site staff)\n- 1. Type of event [master:10.2.1]\n- 2. Date of onset and resolution (duration) [master:10.2.1]\n- 3. Severity (mild, moderate, severe) [master:10.2.1] (note: differs from site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger)\n- 4. Seriousness (does the event meet the above definition for an SAE) [master:10.2.1]\n- 5. Causality, relation to investigational product and disease [master:10.2.1]\n- 6. Action taken regarding investigational product [master:10.2.1]\n- 7. Outcome [master:10.2.1]\n- The following guidelines will be used to describe severity. [master:10.2.1]\n- Mild - Events require minimal or no treatment and do not interfere with the participant''s daily activities. [master:10.2.1]\n- Moderate - Events result in a low level of inconvenience or concern with the therapeutic measures. Moderate events may cause some interference with functioning. [master:10.2.1]\n- Severe - Events interrupt a participant''s usual daily activity and may require systemic drug therapy or other treatment. Severe events are usually potentially life-threatening or incapacitating. Of note, the term "severe" does not necessarily equate to "serious". [master:10.2.1]\n- The relationship of the AE to the investigational product is specified by the SI based on temporal relationship and his/her clinical judgment, using the following definitions: [master:10.2.2] (applies to: SI) (note: differs from site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger)\n- Related - The AE is known to occur with the study intervention, there is a reasonable possibility that the study intervention caused the AE, or there is a temporal relationship between the study intervention and event. Reasonable possibility means that there is evidence to suggest a causal relationship between the study intervention and the AE. [master:10.2.2] (note: differs from site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger)\n- Not Related - There is not a reasonable possibility that the administration of the study intervention caused the event, there is no temporal relationship between the study intervention and event onset, or an alternate etiology has been established. [master:10.2.2] (note: differs from site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger)','\n',char(10)),'At each visit (including telephone interviews), the participant will be asked if they have had any problems or symptoms since their last vis','["safety"]',3303,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:30','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): General concomitant medication allowance and recording (When an AE requires medication or significant non-drug treatment, record that treatment in the concomitant therapy records.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Other medicines (allowed, restricted, banned): General concomitant medication allowance and recording (When an AE requires medication or significant non-drug treatment, record that treatment in the concomitant therapy records.)\n- Throughout the study, participants may be prescribed concomitant medications deemed necessary to provide adequate care. [master:7.5] (applies to: all participants throughout the study)\n- All concomitant medications and significant non-drug therapies, including supplements, received by a participant should be recorded on the appropriate source document and electronic Case Report Form (eCRF). [master:7.5] (applies to: all participants)\n- In addition to the assessments evaluated below, participants will provide information on their demographics, past medical history, including ALS, as well as concomitant medication usage. [master:9.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details on medications that may not be taken during the trial will be included in the RSA. [master:7.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- The medications indicated below are excluded during the study (unless otherwise specified) (See Section 5.9 for prohibited medications). [regimen_g:s092] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)\n- The lists below are not exhaustive; the use of any concomitant medication is at the discretion of the investigator who knows the participant’s medical history. [regimen_g:s092] (applies to: DNL343 regimen)','\n',char(10)),'Throughout the study, participants may be prescribed concomitant medications deemed necessary to provide adequate care.','["safety"]',3330,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:31','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When an immediate safety concern requires a protocol change or a safety procedure is missed, follow the applicable deviation documentation and notification requirements.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When an immediate safety concern requires a protocol change or a safety procedure is missed, follow the applicable deviation documentation and notification requirements.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["safety"]',3331,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:32','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Informed consent: Reconsent triggers and timing (When new safety information changes the information needed for continued participation, assess and complete required informed reconsent.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Reconsent triggers and timing (When new safety information changes the information needed for continued participation, assess and complete required informed reconsent.)\n- Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: participants already enrolled; Master Protocol amendments)\n- Addition of regimens will not require re-consent. [master:1.4.1] (applies to: addition of regimens)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the Master Protocol or a regimen if new procedures or information is added in the future. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant''s next visit. [master:6.2] (applies to: participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from regimen_g:6)\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants needing reconsent) (note: differs from master:6.2)','\n',char(10)),'Written informed re-consent for continued participation in the study may be required by participants already enrolled in the Master Protocol','["safety"]',3332,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:33','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: EDC and IRT termination steps (When a safety event causes early study termination, complete the EDC and IRT termination steps as well as the safety follow-up.)','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: EDC and IRT termination steps (When a safety event causes early study termination, complete the EDC and IRT termination steps as well as the safety follow-up.)\n- - Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n- If a subject permanently discontinues study drug or completes the trial: [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 1. Click the Subject ID and select Discontinue Subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 2. Enter the Date of Discontinuation and select the primary reason (e.g., Adverse Event, Withdrawal by Subject). [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- 3. Confirm the transaction. This stops all future automated resupply shipments for this subject. [site_irt_guide:3.5] (applies to: IRT subject discontinuation)\n- Note: Unblinding a subject automatically discontinues them from further double-blind protocol treatment. Suvoda will prevent any future double-blind kits from being dispensed to this subject. [site_irt_guide:5] (applies to: unblinded subjects)','\n',char(10)),'- Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completi','["safety"]',3333,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:4','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Recording and EDC','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Recording and EDC\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. It is also important to report all AEs, especially those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational product being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible AEs. All AEs will be documented on source and CRFs designed specifically for this purpose. All AEs will be collected and reported in the EDC system and compiled into reports for monthly reviewing by the MM for the Master Protocol. The Master Protocol MM shall promptly review all information relevant to the safety of the investigational product, including all SAEs. Special attention will be paid to those that result in permanent discontinuation of the investigational products) being studied, whether serious or non-serious. [master:10.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- All clinical Adverse Events and Key Study Events (e.g., Mortality, Pregnancy, PAV, and Tracheostomy) are recorded in the participant''s records. The site enters the AE and Key Study Event information into the EDC system as soon as possible after learning of the event or receiving an update on an existing event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Entries on the AE Log (and into the EDC system) will include the following: description of the event, severity, seriousness, date of onset, date of resolution, relationship to investigational product, action taken, and primary outcome of event. [master:10.2.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- - The SAE Trigger Question: On the AE log, the question "Is this adverse event serious?" acts as a system trigger. Checking "Yes" will instantly generate a dynamic Expedited Reporting Evaluation form. You must complete and save this form to alert the Pharmacovigilance (PV) Safety Gateway. [Rave Standard Study Default – Data Management Confirmation Required] Ensure CTCAE v5.0 grading and attribution (Unrelated to Definite) are completed prior to saving. [site_edc_guide:adverse-events-ae-the-sae-trigger] (applies to: SAE EDC entry) (note: differs from master:10.2.1; master:10.2.2)\n- A: Site users do not have security permissions to hard-delete log lines. To remove the entry, check the "Inactivate" box on that specific log line and click Save. The line will strike through. Do not overwrite the line with a different AE. [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: duplicate AE log line)\n- A: You must complete the End of Treatment form, the End of Study / Early Termination form (selecting "Withdrawal of Consent" as the primary reason), and ensure all Adverse Events have End Dates or are explicitly marked as "Ongoing at Study Exit." [site_edc_guide:8] (applies to: withdrawal of consent during ATE)\n- Every entry on the Concomitant Medication log requires an Indication. This indication must logically map to an existing term on either the Medical History or Adverse Event form. [site_edc_guide:concomitant-medications-restrictions] (applies to: concomitant medication entry)','\n',char(10)),'The SI will carefully monitor each participant throughout the study for possible adverse events. All AEs will be documented on CRFs designed','["safety"]',3304,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:5','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Expedited reporting','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Expedited reporting\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["safety"]',3305,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:6','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Safety monitoring board','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Safety monitoring board\n- The DSMB will receive blinded and unblinded summary reports of the frequency of all clinical adverse events and safety laboratory tests for each trial regimens at planned periodic meetings throughout the study as specified in the DSMB Charter. The DSMB will receive separate reports per trial regimen to review independently. Placebo data from all trial regimens will be compiled into a single report for review. [master:12.4] (applies to: DSMB)\n- Summaries of serious adverse events and enrollment per trial regimen will be provided to the DSMB by the Study Biostatisticians as specified in the DSMB Charter. AE(s) of Special Interest events occurring within 24 hours of dosing for any given treatment arm, and any severe unexpected serious adverse events for a treatment arm are considered events of interest and will be reported in real-time (within 1 business day of CC awareness) to the DSMB. [master:12.4] (applies to: DSMB reporting)\n- The DSMB can ask to receive the SAE reports more frequently. As necessary, the DSMB can review the frequencies of clinical and laboratory abnormalities. Recommendations for modification or termination of the trial based on safety data will be made by the DSMB to the Sponsor. The DSMB will review safety data throughout the trial and may stop enrollment into a treatment arm for safety if they determine that there is a significant difference in the rate of a particular adverse event that would indicate a risk that is greater than the possible benefit of the investigational product. A notable increase in the frequency of any adverse event should be examined by the DSMB although it may not lead to a recommendation by the DSMB. [master:12.4] (applies to: DSMB)\n- During the trial, a regimen may be discontinued early due to futility or safety concerns. [master:2.3] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (applies to: SI)','\n',char(10)),'The DSMB will receive blinded and unblinded summary reports of the frequency of all clinical adverse events and safety laboratory tests for ','["safety"]',3306,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:7','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Overdose and medication errors','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Overdose and medication errors\n- Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA. [master:7.4]\n- Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following: [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343 [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343) [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- These safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness. [regimen_g:5.8] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Details on the actions to take in the event of an overdose will be included in the RSA.','["safety"]',3307,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:8','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Safety-related dose changes','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Safety-related dose changes\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- A SI or designee may terminate a research participant if a medical condition or other situation occurs such that continued participation would not be in the best interest of the participant. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- All participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible. [regimen_g:5.6.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 3. The system will ask: "Is the subject experiencing a dose-limiting toxicity requiring a dose reduction?" [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If No, the system assigns the standard 200 mg QD supply (Cartons containing 14 stick packs, instructing 2 packs daily). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)\n- - If Yes, the system logs the dose reduction to 100 mg QD and adjusts resupply algorithms accordingly. [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G dispensation)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["safety"]',3308,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:safety:9','piece','','Safety, adverse events and reporting > Unscheduled safety assessments','Safety, adverse events and reporting',replace('## Unscheduled safety assessments\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["safety"]',3309,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:1','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Screening collection and repeats','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Screening collection and repeats\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Collect blood samples for clinical screening assessments (including coagulation parameters) and, for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP), for pregnancy test [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Collect blood biomarker sample [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screening period)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants re-screened after the initial screening window)','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eli','["samples"]',3701,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:10','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Week 4 and Week 8 PK timing','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Week 4 and Week 8 PK timing\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 non-morning visits)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4; dosing before clinic arrival)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4; dosing before clinic arrival)\n- For the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 8 Visits)\n- 3. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 4, postdose only on Week 8) o For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 8 Visits)\n- a) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 predose PK)\n- b) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 8 postdose PK)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection.','["samples"]',3710,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:11','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Week 16 and Week 24 PK timing','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Week 16 and Week 24 PK timing\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 Visit)\n- 8. PK blood collection (postdose only on Week 16) o Postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 Visit)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- 8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24)\n- a) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 predose PK)\n- b) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 postdose PK)','\n',char(10)),'The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:','["samples"]',3711,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:12','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Unscheduled safety and PK collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Unscheduled safety and PK collection\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["samples"]',3712,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:13','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > ATE Week 4 and Week 8 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## ATE Week 4 and Week 8 collection\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- 4. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE)\n- 5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 ATE; women of childbearing potential)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 ATE; women of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following proce','["samples"]',3713,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:14','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > ATE Week 16 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## ATE Week 16 collection\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE; women of childbearing potential)\n- 11. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)\n- 12. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["samples"]',3714,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:15','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > ATE Week 28 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## ATE Week 28 collection\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- The Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- 6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE; women of childbearing potential)\n- 12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)\n- 13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G Week 28 ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["samples"]',3715,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:16','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > ATE Week 40 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## ATE Week 40 collection\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G Week 40 ATE; women of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The fo','["samples"]',3716,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:17','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > ATE Week 52 and later collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## ATE Week 52 and later collection\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete. [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- The Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE; women of childbearing potential)\n- 13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- 14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G Week 52 ATE)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed: [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- 5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- 6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52; women of childbearing potential)\n- 12. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)\n- 13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G ATE after Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit p','["samples"]',3717,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:18','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Early termination assessments','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Early termination assessments\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early termination)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing study drug but completing the protocol)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early termination)\n- If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early termination before Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates.','["samples"]',3718,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:19','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Central laboratory collection schedules','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Central laboratory collection schedules\n- Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections. [site_lab_manual:3] (applies to: central laboratory collections)\n- - Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: WOCBP; placebo-controlled period)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.2; differs from master:9.3.4)\n- - Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- - Pharmacokinetics (PK Plasma) (Central): done at Wk 4 (Day 28) Pre, Post; Wk 8 (Day 56) Post; Wk 16 (Day 112) Post; Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) Pre, Post [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - Biomarker Blood (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - Biomarker Urine (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - DNA Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - CSF Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0) X (Bio), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) X (Bio/PK) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: placebo-controlled period)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE)\n- - Biomarker Blood (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE)\n- - Biomarker Urine (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections.','["samples"]',3719,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:2','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Baseline collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Baseline collection\n- This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, please refer to the RA for applicability. [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs, biomarkers, and optional DNA sequencing [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- Collect blood biomarker sample [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit)\n- 6. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit)\n- 7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit; women of childbearing potential)\n- 11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screen','["samples"]',3702,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:20','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Sample identification, storage and consent','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Sample identification, storage and consent\n- All samples will be labeled with a code. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- The code will not include any identifiable information but will be linked to a specific participant, visit, and sample by data entry of the code into the EDC. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- There is no scheduled date on which the samples will be destroyed. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- Samples may be stored for research until they are used, damaged, decayed or otherwise unfit for analysis. [master:9.4] (applies to: all samples)\n- Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their sample used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- However, it will not be possible to destroy samples that may have already been used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- Specific instructions regarding the collection, processing, storage, and shipment of these samples will be provided in the study Laboratory Manual. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid samples)\n- Details of any additional specific biofluid samples required for a regimen will be described in the corresponding RSA. [master:9.4] (applies to: regimen-specific biofluid samples)\n- A participant Neurological Global Unique Identifier (NeuroGUID), or its derivitives'', will be used as the identifier for participant''s samples participating in this study. [master:12.5] (applies to: participant samples)','\n',char(10)),'All samples will be labeled with a code.','["samples"]',3720,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:21','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Sample and PK entry in EDC','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Sample and PK entry in EDC\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- 4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: out-of-window safety labs, unscheduled PK draws, repeated screening procedures)\n- - In-Clinic Dosing & PK: For Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24, exact dosing times must be reconciled with PK draw times. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4, 8, 16, and 24)\n- - Record the Dose Start Time in 24-hour format (e.g., 09:15). [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK reconciliation)\n- - Edit Checks: The EDC utilizes hard logic comparing PK times to the Dose Start Time. Pre-dose PK samples must be logged between 0 and 2 hours prior to dosing. Post-dose samples must be logged between 1 and 8 hours post-dose. Violating these windows will fire a system query requiring a protocol deviation explanation. [site_edc_guide:drug-administration-pk-reconciliation] (applies to: Regimen G PK entry)','\n',char(10)),'If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit f','["samples"]',3721,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:22','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Collection kits and tubes','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Collection kits and tubes\n- Kits are pre-assembled specifically for each visit. [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G kits)\n- Do not substitute tubes between kits, as lot numbers and expiration dates are tracked centrally. [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G kits)\n- All necessary secondary packaging (biohazard bags, absorbents) is included in each kit. [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G kits)\n- - Visit / Time-Point: Screening, Days -42 to -1 | Kit #: KIT #1 | Assessments & Components: Clinical Safety & Pregnancy: (1) 8.5-mL Gold SST (Serum), (1) 3.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA (Whole Blood), (1) Urine Collection Cup + Transfer Tube; Coagulation: (1) 2.7-mL Light Blue Sodium Citrate Tube [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G Screening)\n- - Visit / Time-Point: Baseline, Day 0 | Kit #: KIT #2 | Assessments & Components: Clinical Safety & Endocrinology: (2) 8.5-mL Gold SST, (1) 3.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA, (1) Urine Collection Cup + Transfer Tube; Biomarker Blood: (2) 6.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA → (6) 1.8-mL Cryovials; Optional Genomics / DNA: (1) 8.5-mL PAXgene / Specialized DNA Tube; Optional CSF: (1) 15-mL Sterile Conical Tube → (4) 2.0-mL External Thread Cryovials [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- - Visit / Time-Point: Week 4 & 24, Pre & Post Dose PK | Kit #: KIT #3 | Assessments & Components: PK Plasma (Pre-Dose & Post-Dose): (2) 4.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA, (4) 1.8-mL Cryovials (Purple Caps); Clinical Safety: (1) 8.5-mL Gold SST, (1) 3.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA, (1) Urine Collection Cup + Transfer Tube. *Week 24 includes additional tubes for Endocrinology, Biomarkers, and Optional CSF (identical to Baseline kit setup). [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 24)\n- - Visit / Time-Point: Week 8 & 16, Post-Dose PK | Kit #: KIT #4 | Assessments & Components: PK Plasma (Post-Dose Only): (1) 4.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA, (2) 1.8-mL Cryovials (Purple Caps); Clinical Safety: (1) 8.5-mL Gold SST, (1) 3.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA. *Week 16 includes Coagulation (1) 2.7-mL Light Blue and Biomarkers. [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 8 and 16)\n- - Visit / Time-Point: ATE Weeks (Standard Clinic Visits) | Kit #: KIT #5 | Assessments & Components: Clinical Safety: (1) 8.5-mL Gold SST, (1) 3.0-mL Lavender K2EDTA, (1) Urine Collection Cup + Transfer Tube. *Selected ATE weeks include Biomarker Blood/Urine components. [site_lab_manual:4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Kits are pre-assembled specifically for each visit.','["samples"]',3722,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:23','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Blood volumes','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Blood volumes\n- - Clinical Chemistry/Endo (SST): Screening 8.5 mL | Baseline 17.0 mL | Wk 4 8.5 mL | Wk 8 8.5 mL | Wk 16 8.5 mL | Wk 24 17.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) 8.5 mL [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- - Clinical Hematology (EDTA): Screening 3.0 mL | Baseline 3.0 mL | Wk 4 3.0 mL | Wk 8 3.0 mL | Wk 16 3.0 mL | Wk 24 3.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) 3.0 mL [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- - Coagulation (Citrate): Screening 2.7 mL | Baseline - | Wk 4 - | Wk 8 - | Wk 16 2.7 mL | Wk 24 - | ATE Wks (Typical) - [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- - PK Plasma (EDTA): Screening - | Baseline - | Wk 4 8.0 mL | Wk 8 4.0 mL | Wk 16 4.0 mL | Wk 24 8.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) - [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- - Biomarker Blood (EDTA): Screening - | Baseline 12.0 mL | Wk 4 - | Wk 8 12.0 mL | Wk 16 12.0 mL | Wk 24 12.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) 12.0 mL* [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- * Only collected at specific ATE visits (Wk 16, 28, 52). [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: ATE biomarker blood volumes)\n- - Genomics/DNA (Optional): Screening - | Baseline 8.5 mL | Wk 4 - | Wk 8 - | Wk 16 - | Wk 24 - | ATE Wks (Typical) - [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)\n- - TOTALS (mL): Screening 14.2 mL | Baseline 40.5 mL | Wk 4 19.5 mL | Wk 8 27.5 mL | Wk 16 30.2 mL | Wk 24 40.0 mL | ATE Wks (Typical) 11.5 - 23.5 mL [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: central laboratory assessments)','\n',char(10)),'- Clinical Chemistry/Endo (SST): Screening 8.5 mL | Baseline 17.0 mL | Wk 4 8.5 mL | Wk 8 8.5 mL | Wk 16 8.5 mL | Wk 24 17.0 mL | ATE Wks (T','["samples"]',3723,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:24','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Draw order and specimen processing','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Draw order and specimen processing\n- Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- Blood must be collected in the sequence: Light Blue → Gold SST → Lavender EDTA. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- Always program centrifuges using Relative Centrifugal Force (RCF or g), not RPM. [site_lab_manual:5]\n- - Tube Type / Color: Light Blue (Sodium Citrate) | Target Assay: Coagulation | Processing Instructions: Invert 3-4x. Centrifuge at 1,500 x g for 15 min at Room Temp (20-25°C). Transfer plasma to cryovial. Freeze immediately. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: coagulation samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Gold SST (Serum Separator) | Target Assay: Safety Chem, Endocrinology | Processing Instructions: Invert 5x. Allow to clot upright for 30 min. Centrifuge at 1,500 - 2,000 x g for 10-15 min at Room Temp. Ship ambient. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety chemistry and endocrinology samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Lavender EDTA (3.0 mL) | Target Assay: Safety Hematology | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Do Not Centrifuge. Ship as ambient whole blood on day of collection. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: safety hematology samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Lavender EDTA (4.0 mL / 6.0 mL) | Target Assay: PK Plasma & Biomarkers | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Centrifuge at 1,500 - 2,000 x g for 10-15 min at 4°C (Refrigerated). Separate plasma into cryovials. Freeze at -70°C within 60 mins. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: PK plasma and biomarker samples)\n- - Tube Type / Color: Specialty Tubes | Target Assay: Genomics / DNA | Processing Instructions: Invert 8-10x. Do Not Centrifuge. Freeze immediately at -70°C. [site_lab_manual:5] (applies to: genomics and DNA samples)','\n',char(10)),'Strict adherence to the Order of Draw (CLSI H3-A6) is required.','["samples"]',3724,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:25','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Courier booking and shipment timing','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Courier booking and shipment timing\n- To arrange sample pick-up, contact World Courier. [site_lab_manual:2]\n- Please provide the World Courier representative with your specific Site Account Number, the destination (Labcorp Central Laboratory), and specify that the shipment requires Dry Ice (for frozen samples) or Ambient handling. [site_lab_manual:2]\n- Call the local numbers below: [site_lab_manual:2]\n- - United States: +1 800 221 6600 or +1 516 354 2600 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: United States)\n- - Canada: +1 800 555 1234 (toll free) [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Canada)\n- - United Kingdom: +44 207 9287256 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: United Kingdom)\n- - Ireland: +353 1 800 771660 (toll free) [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Ireland)\n- - Germany (Frankfurt): +49 69 6970080 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Germany)\n- - France (Paris): +33 1 41 84 31 31 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: France)\n- - Italy: +39 02 3800 3900 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Italy)\n- - Spain (Madrid): +34 91 7461010 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Spain)\n- - Netherlands: +31 20 6534141 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Netherlands)\n- - Belgium: +32 2 7125060 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Belgium)\n- - Switzerland (Geneva): +0800 81091 (toll free) [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Switzerland)\n- - Australia: +61 2 9669 3500 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Australia)\n- - Japan: +81 3 5708 6100 [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: Japan)\n- - Other Regions: See Site Activation Packet [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: other regions)\n- Note on Scheduling: Because personnel are not available to receive frozen specimens on weekends at the depot, samples should NOT be shipped on Friday unless a Saturday delivery surcharge is pre-approved and dry-ice top-up is guaranteed by the courier. [site_lab_manual:2] (applies to: frozen specimen shipments)','\n',char(10)),'To arrange sample pick-up, contact World Courier.','["samples"]',3725,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:26','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Packing and temperature control','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Packing and temperature control\n- All specimens must be transported in accordance with national regulations and IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations for UN 3373 Biological Substance, Category B. [site_lab_manual:6] (applies to: all specimens)\n- Use the specific supplies provided by Labcorp/World Courier. [site_lab_manual:6] (applies to: all specimens)\n- - Ensure all primary tubes and cryovials are tightly sealed. [site_lab_manual:step-1-place-samples-into-diagnostic-spe] (applies to: packing step 1)\n- - Place samples into the clear plastic STP 741 bag. [site_lab_manual:step-1-place-samples-into-diagnostic-spe] (applies to: packing step 1)\n- - Insert the absorbent material strip (STP 152) into the bag alongside the tubes. [site_lab_manual:step-1-place-samples-into-diagnostic-spe] (applies to: packing step 1)\n- - Remove excess air and seal the bag using the adhesive strip. [site_lab_manual:step-1-place-samples-into-diagnostic-spe] (applies to: packing step 1)\n- - Place the sealed plastic STP 741 bag inside the white Tyvek/paper STP 740 bag (if provided) or directly into the insulated thermal box. [site_lab_manual:step-2-place-bag-into-secondary-packagin] (applies to: packing step 2)\n- - For Ambient Shipments: Place the sealed bag into the standard ambient cardboard box. [site_lab_manual:step-3-temperature-control-thermal-box-d] (applies to: ambient shipments; packing step 3)\n- - For Frozen Shipments: Place the sealed bag into the EPS foam insulated thermal box (M9.5). The World Courier driver will pour the appropriate amount of Dry Ice (minimum 5.0 kg to 17.0 kg depending on transit time) over the samples. [site_lab_manual:step-3-temperature-control-thermal-box-d] (applies to: frozen shipments; packing step 3)','\n',char(10)),'All specimens must be transported in accordance with national regulations and IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations for UN 3373 Biological Substa','["samples"]',3726,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:27','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Shipping documentation','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Shipping documentation\n- Each shipment tendered to the courier must be accompanied by the following documentation: [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: each shipment)\n- - House Air Waybill (HAWB): Affix to the exterior of the shipment. This is your internal tracking form provided by World Courier. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: each shipment)\n- - Sample Requisition Form (PRF/GSR): Place the completed Requisition Form inside the shipping container, but outside the sealed biohazard bag. Retain a photocopy for the subject''s chart. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: each shipment)\n- - Customs Invoice (International Only): Print the Customs Invoice (Appendix 3) on your hospital/institution letterhead and sign it. Required for cross-border shipments to declare "No Commercial Value". [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: international shipments)\n- - USDA Statement (International to USA): Required for animal/human material transit to the US. See Appendix 3. [site_lab_manual:mandatory-documentation] (applies to: international shipments to USA)\n- Include this form in the shipping container. [site_lab_manual:appendix-2-sample-requisition-form-templ] (applies to: Sample Requisition Form)\n- Form fields: Patient ID Number; Patient Initials; Date of Birth; Sex (Male / Female); Date Collected; Time Collected; Hospital Name; Investigator Name; Visit Name; Fasting Status (Yes / No); Discrepancies / Notes. [site_lab_manual:appendix-2-sample-requisition-form-templ] (applies to: Sample Requisition Form)\n- Note: This document must be printed on your hospital''s letterhead paper, signed, and included in triplicate for any shipments crossing international borders into the United States. [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: international shipments into the United States)\n- Customs Invoice & USDA Statement. Fields: Date; Shipper (Site Name & Address); Consignee: Labcorp Central Laboratory Services, 8211 SciCor Drive, Indianapolis, IN 46214, USA; Contents: Human Other: Blood / Serum / Plasma / CSF / Urine samples; Quantity (mL). [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: Customs Invoice & USDA Statement)\n- Non-Infectious specimens from a normal, non-risk population. For Clinical Laboratory Testing Only. Human material containing no animal material and not of tissue culture origin. The material was not obtained from Humans or Non-human primates that have been inoculated with or exposed to any livestock or poultry disease agent exotic to the United States. The material is not zoonotic. [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: Customs Invoice & USDA Statement)\n- Value: No Commercial Value - For Customs Purposes Only $10 USD. Signature and Job Title. [site_lab_manual:appendix-3-customs-invoice-usda-statemen] (applies to: Customs Invoice & USDA Statement)','\n',char(10)),'Each shipment tendered to the courier must be accompanied by the following documentation:','["samples"]',3727,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:28','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["samples"]',3728,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:29','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Clinical safety laboratory tests','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Clinical safety laboratory tests\n- After a participant is assigned to a regimen, RSA " Clinical safety labs include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, liver function tests, and urinalysis. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'After a participant is assigned to a regimen, RSA " Clinical safety labs include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry pane','["samples"]',3729,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:3','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Week 4 and Week 8 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Week 4 and Week 8 collection\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit)\n- Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done at Baseline) [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4 Visit)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.3)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8 Visit)\n- These visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 8 Visits) (note: differs from master:8.5; differs from master:8.6)\n- 4. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4 and 8 Visits; women of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the ','["samples"]',3703,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:30','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Pregnancy testing at screening and as needed\n- Pregnancy testing is only repeated as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Pregnancy testing is only repeated as applicable if there is a concern for pregnancy.','["samples"]',3730,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:31','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Informed consent: Information and consent before procedures (Before collecting research samples, obtain informed consent before any study-specific procedure.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Information and consent before procedures (Before collecting research samples, obtain informed consent before any study-specific procedure.)\n- At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), participants will be informed about the nature and purpose of the Master Protocol, participation/termination conditions, and risks and benefits. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- Participants will be given adequate time to ask questions and become familiar with the Master Protocol prior to providing consent to participate. [master:1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Written informed consent will be obtained from participants before any study procedures or assessments, not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, weight), are done and after the study objectives and endpoints, methods, anticipated benefits, and potential hazards are explained. [master:6.2] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:8)\n- No study procedures are performed prior to the signing of the Master Protocol ICF. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- All participants will sign the Master Protocol ICF prior to undergoing any study tests or procedures. [master:8] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from master:1.3; differs from master:6.2)\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol Screening Visit)','\n',char(10)),'At the first visit, prior to initiation of any study related procedures not performed as clinic visit standard of care (e.g., vital signs, w','["samples"]',3731,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:32','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Informed consent: Sample consent and withdrawal (For optional sample collection or withdrawal of permission to use samples, follow the sample-consent and withdrawal requirements.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Sample consent and withdrawal (For optional sample collection or withdrawal of permission to use samples, follow the sample-consent and withdrawal requirements.)\n- Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their sample used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid collection)\n- However, it will not be possible to destroy samples that may have already been used. [master:9.4] (applies to: biofluid sample consent withdrawal)\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- - DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- - CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 11. Optional CSF collection [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Participants have the option of declining participation in this portion of the study at any time by withdrawing their consent to have their ','["samples"]',3732,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:33','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required samples are missed or collection, processing, or PK timing departs from protocol, document the applicable protocol deviation.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When required samples are missed or collection, processing, or PK timing departs from protocol, document the applicable protocol deviation.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["samples"]',3733,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:34','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Consent-linked adverse event recording (When laboratory results show a clinically significant abnormality, apply the consent-linked AE recording requirements.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Consent-linked adverse event recording (When laboratory results show a clinically significant abnormality, apply the consent-linked AE recording requirements.)\n- Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Participants will be monitored for adverse events from the time they sign consent for the Master Protocol until completion of their participation as defined in the RSA. [master:10.1.1]','\n',char(10)),'Clinically significant laboratory abnormalities, such as those that require intervention, are those that are identified as such by the Parti','["samples"]',3734,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:35','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When sample results or collection complications reveal a reportable safety event, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When sample results or collection complications reveal a reportable safety event, notify the coordinating center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["samples"]',3735,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:36','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When PK sampling requires an in-clinic dose, follow preparation and in-use stability rules, including the second-person check.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When PK sampling requires an in-clinic dose, follow preparation and in-use stability rules, including the second-person check.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["samples"]',3736,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:37','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Discontinuation and follow-up (When missing safety laboratory data or abnormal results raise safety concerns, apply the treatment-discontinuation and continued-follow-up requirements.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug dosing and dose changes: Discontinuation and follow-up (When missing safety laboratory data or abnormal results raise safety concerns, apply the treatment-discontinuation and continued-follow-up requirements.)\n- If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected), then the investigational product may need to be discontinued. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- A research participant may choose to discontinue the investigational product at any time for any reason. However, the SI or designee will encourage the research participant to follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle (ITT). These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: participants with specified positive C-SSRS responses during treatment)\n- At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- The in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants discontinuing study drug)','\n',char(10)),'If the SI or designee is concerned about the use of a prohibited medication or other safety issues (including lack of safety data collected)','["samples"]',3737,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:38','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At Week 24 PK and CSF collection, complete the ATE supply transition so the subsequent dose uses the correct ATE supply.)','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (At Week 24 PK and CSF collection, complete the ATE supply transition so the subsequent dose uses the correct ATE supply.)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD','["samples"]',3738,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:4','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Week 16 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Week 16 collection\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.5)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs (including coagulation parameters) and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 Visit) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- 9. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16 Visit; women of childbearing potential)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the','["samples"]',3704,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:5','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Week 24 and Week 36 collection','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Week 24 and Week 36 collection\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24 Visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit) (note: differs from master:8.10)\n- 9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24; participants continuing in the ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6; differs from site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24)\n- 10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit; women of childbearing potential)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed: [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)\n- Collect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)\n- Collect urine for urinalysis [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)\n- Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)\n- Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Week 36 Visit)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in pre','["samples"]',3705,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:6','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Clinical safety tests and retesting','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Clinical safety tests and retesting\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Hematology with differential panel: complete blood count with differential (hematocrit, hemoglobin, platelet count, RBC indices, Total RBC, Total WBC, and WBC & differential) [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Blood chemistry panel/Liver function tests (LFTs): alanine aminotransferase (ALT (SGPT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST (SGOT)), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate, blood urea nitrogen, calcium, chloride, creatinine (estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate [eGFR] will be calculated using the using the CKD-EPI 2021 equation), cystatin C, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, potassium, sodium, total bilirubin and total protein [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) [master:9.1.2] (applies to: WOCBP)\n- Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: prior to the second lumbar puncture) (note: differs from site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24)\n- All participants will have clinical safety laboratory tests at the designated visits outlined in the schedule of activities. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: participants requiring additional testing)\n- In addition to procedures in the Master Protocol, the following DNL343-specific procedures will be conducted during the study: [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 6. PK blood collection [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 8. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood adrenocorticotropic hormone [ACTH], cortisol, free thyroxine [fT4]) (Baseline and Week 24 Visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.7)\n- 9. Urine Pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G; women of childbearing potential)\n- 11. Optional CSF collection [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- * Endocrinology notes: Blood samples for ACTH and Cortisol should be collected at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit to control for diurnal variation. [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: scheduled ACTH and Cortisol collection)','\n',char(10)),'The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following v','["samples"]',3706,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:7','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > DNA and blood biomarkers','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## DNA and blood biomarkers\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- All participants will provide blood samples for biomarker assessments. [master:9.3.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- Biofluid samples (blood, urine, and CSF) that will be used for biomarker assessments for Regimen G are captured in the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Additional biofluid samples (blood) will be collected for Regimen G for evaluation of plasma PK at scheduled timepoints described in the SOA. [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing d','["samples"]',3707,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:8','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > Lumbar puncture procedure','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## Lumbar puncture procedure\n- The lumbar puncture (LP) procedure will be performed to collect CSF, as described in each RSA. [master:9.3.4]\n- Study staff should document the time of'' study drug dose administered Coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: all participants)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: prior to the second lumbar puncture) (note: differs from site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24)\n- The SI will discuss all potential LP risks to the subjects including: [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Local pain at injection site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Reaction to anesthetic agents [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Bleeding at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Infection at needle entrance site [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- Post-LP low-pressure headache [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing LP)\n- The procedure must be performed by the SI or another staff member with the appropriate qualifications, experience, and training in performing LPs per institutional policy. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: LP procedures)\n- When possible, LPs should be performed with an atraumatic Sprotte needle to reduce the risk of post-LP headache. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: LP procedures)','\n',char(10)),'The lumbar puncture (LP) procedure will be performed to collect CSF, as described in each RSA.','["samples"]',3708,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:samples:9','piece','','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping > CSF collection schedule and conditions','Samples: collection, timing, handling, shipping',replace('## CSF collection schedule and conditions\n- Perform LP for CSF collection (as described in the RSA) [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed: [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit)\n- Perform LP for CSF collection (or as described in the RSA) [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16 Visit) (note: differs from regimen_g:8)\n- It should be noted that the CSF biofluid collection is optional and collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24 (rather than Baseline and Week 16 as noted in the Master Protocol). [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit)\n- 9. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (biomarkers only; no CSF PK sample collected at baseline) [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline Visit)\n- CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline CSF collection)\n- The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule: [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit)\n- 12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples) [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit)\n- a) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 CSF collection)\n- b) CSF collection for the ET visit will be performed only if the participant has consented to the lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G ET CSF collection)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G ET CSF collection)\n- " CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Perform LP for CSF collection (as described in the RSA)','["samples"]',3709,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:1','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Schedule framework and visit-date calculation','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Schedule framework and visit-date calculation\n- Each regimen will follow these procedures and may add additional procedures as described in the corresponding RSA. [master:8] (applies to: all regimens)\n- No study procedures are performed prior to the signing of the Master Protocol ICF. All participants will sign the Master Protocol ICF prior to undergoing any study tests or procedures. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 1. Visit windows are consecutive calendar days and the target visit dates are calculated from the Baseline Visit - Day 0 (first day of investigational product administration). [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- The primary endpoint will be assessed for all interventions every 4 weeks, either on-site or via phone. [master:4.1] (applies to: all interventions)\n- As per the Schedule of Activities (SOA) below, visits must occur every 4 weeks and will be clinic-, phone-, or telemedicine based, as applicable. There is a maximum 24-week duration of placebo-controlled treatment for a regimen. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:protocol-summary)\n- Treatment duration of the placebo-controlled period is a maximum of 36-weeks for each regimen. The duration of the Active Treatment Extension (ATE) period will be described in the respective RSAs. [master:protocol-summary] (applies to: all regimens) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- Including the placebo-controlled treatment and the ATE periods, the total planned amount of time for a participant in Regimen G is a minimum of approximately 86 weeks and a maximum of approximately 112 weeks. This duration assumes a 6-week screening window, 24-week placebocontrolled treatment period, 52- to 78-week ATE period, and 4-week safety follow-up period. [regimen_g:s009] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The Master Protocol allows a 36-week duration of placebo-Safety Call Follow-Up Refer to Phone RSA The Master Protocol sets out the minimum visit and assessment requirements that must be incorporated into each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Each regimen will follow these procedures and may add additional procedures as described in the corresponding RSA.','["visit_schedule"]',3501,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:10','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > ATE scheduled visits','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## ATE scheduled visits\n- This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Review concomitant medications\n2. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n3. Perform drug compliance check-in\n4. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, containers, to the next visit\n5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.1] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 2)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n7. Review concomitant medications\n8. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n9. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n11. Perform study drug compliance\n12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 4)\n- This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.3] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 8)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.4] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 12)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThis visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n12. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n14. Dispense study drug to participant\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.5] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 16)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.6] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 20)\n- This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit • Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.7] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 24)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs, including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. ROADS\n16. PGI-C\n17. PGI-S\n18. CGI-C\n19. Dispense study drug to participant\n20. Perform study drug compliance\n21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.8] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 28)\n- The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. -lead ECG\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.9] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 40)\n- Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n16. ROADS\n17. PGI-C\n18. PGI-S\n19. CGI-C\n20. Perform study drug compliance [regimen_g:6.10.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Week 52)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following pr','["visit_schedule"]',3510,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:11','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > ATE assessment mapping','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## ATE assessment mapping\n- - Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [9], All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- [9]For participants returning for another visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE dispensing at Week 52 or later)\n- 2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: ATE ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, CGI-C)\n- 3. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE telephone visits)\n- [13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE participants subject to contraception review)','\n',char(10)),'- Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurri','["visit_schedule"]',3511,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:12','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Remote-visit allowances','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Remote-visit allowances\n- If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. The following procedures should be completed in the suggested order and as per the Schedule of Activities:\nALSFRS-R, AE Review, Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, Concomitant Medications, IP Accountability/Compliance, and shipment of IP as applicable; each RA may describe additional activities. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G designated placebo-controlled clinic visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nIn addition to the procedures in the Master Protocol that should be conducted during the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. CNS-BFS (Weeks 8 and 16 only)\n2. ROADS (Week 16 and 24 only)\n3. PGI-C (Week 16 and 24 only)\n4. PGI-S (Week 16 and 24 only)\nDetails on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled remote visits)\n- 7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G designated ATE visits)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nDuring the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. ALSFRS-R (Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET)\n2. CNS-BFS (Week 8, 16, 28, 40, 52 only)\n3. ROADS (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n4. PGI-C (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n5. PGI-S (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET) [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time 12 Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'If needed to protect the safety of the participant, an in-clinic visit may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not av','["visit_schedule"]',3512,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:13','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Follow-up after stopping treatment','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Follow-up after stopping treatment\n- These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period of the study, following the Schedule of Activities. At a minimum, collection of the ALSFRS-R, AEs, Concomitant Medications, C-SSRS, and other outcome measures should be encouraged Loss to follow-up should be prevented whenever possible. For participants followed under the ITT principle, an in-person Early Termination Visit will be completed after they discontinue follow-up under the ITT principle. A Follow-Up Safety Call at the time study drug is discontinued is not be required, as long as the participant is followed under the ITT principle for a minimum of 28 days. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed. [regimen_g:s008] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G participants stopping study drug but completing protocol)','\n',char(10)),'These research participants will be encouraged to follow the study visits, off treatment, up to the week 36 visit and through the ATE period','["visit_schedule"]',3513,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:14','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Safety-call timing and procedures','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Safety-call timing and procedures\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety after their last dose of study drug. Additional details, including the timing of this call, can be found in the RSA. The following procedure will be performed at a minimum:\n• Assess and document AEs [master:8.11] (applies to: all participants)\n- 10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug.\nParticipants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase.\nThe following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.12)\n- Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. Participants will complete this visit who: [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.8)\n- 1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early)\n2. Complete the ATE [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.10.12] (applies to: Regimen G ATE Follow-Up Safety Call)','\n',char(10)),'Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety after their last dose of study drug. Additional details, including the timing of this call, can be','["visit_schedule"]',3514,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:15','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Early-termination visits and combined safety follow-up','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Early-termination visits and combined safety follow-up\n- Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe outcome measures that are required to be collected for each regimen, and the timing of and activities for the Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call, will be detailed in the RSA. [master:8.12] (applies to: early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early terminations)\n- 6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)\n- Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call. [regimen_g:6.10.13] (applies to: Regimen G ATE early terminations)','\n',char(10)),'Participants who terminate their participation in the study early will be asked to be seen for an in-person Early Termination Visit and comp','["visit_schedule"]',3515,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:16','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Unscheduled visits and repeat assessments','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Unscheduled visits and repeat assessments\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- All participants will have clinical safety laboratory tests at the designated visits outlined in the schedule of activities. The SI may order additional local testing or a retest at the central laboratory, if needed, to further assess an AE, or if there is any suspicion that a participant may be pregnant, or if a result could not be obtained throughout the course of the study. If additional testing is required to obtain results, the participant may be asked to return to repeat blood collection for such missing results. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: all participants)\n- If a subject requires an out-of-window safety lab, unscheduled PK draw, or repeated screening procedure, you must add an Unscheduled Visit folder: [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)\n- 1. Navigate to the Subject Page matrix.\n2. Locate the Add Event dropdown menu at the bottom of the screen.\n3. Select Unscheduled Visit and click Add.\n4. In the new folder, select the specific forms you need to complete (e.g., Unscheduled Labs) and enter the assessment date. Do not alter scheduled visit dates for out-of-window procedures. [site_edc_guide:dynamic-unscheduled-folders] (applies to: Regimen G EDC)','\n',char(10)),'Unscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any ti','["visit_schedule"]',3516,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:17','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Missed visits and procedures','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Missed visits and procedures\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14] (applies to: all participants)\n- Q: A subject missed their Week 4 visit entirely. How do I record this?\nA: Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: Regimen G missed Week 4)','\n',char(10)),'Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., parti','["visit_schedule"]',3517,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:18','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > PK sampling and clinic dosing timing','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## PK sampling and clinic dosing timing\n- 15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic. [regimen_g:5.4] (applies to: Regimen G predose PK visits)','\n',char(10)),'15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take ','["visit_schedule"]',3518,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:19','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > CSF and DNA collection windows','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## CSF and DNA collection windows\n- All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing during the Baseline Visit. The DNA sample can be collected at any time after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained for any reason or the sample is not usable. [master:9.3.1] (applies to: participants consenting to DNA collection)\n- 7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional DNA collection)\n- 16. CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G optional CSF collection) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- For the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit. [regimen_g:6.9] (applies to: Regimen G early termination CSF)\n- It should be noted that the CSF biofluid collection is optional and collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24 (rather than Baseline and Week 16 as noted in the Master Protocol). Additional biofluid samples (blood) will be collected for Regimen G for evaluation of plasma PK at scheduled timepoints described in the SOA. [regimen_g:8] (applies to: Regimen G) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.3.4] (applies to: participants undergoing second lumbar puncture)','\n',char(10)),'All participants consenting to DNA collection will provide a blood sample for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) extraction for genome sequencing d','["visit_schedule"]',3519,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:2','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Assessment order and time of day','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Assessment order and time of day\n- 2. Assessment of vital capacity should be performed at the start of a visit so as not to fatigue the participant with other testing. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- 3. The remaining clinical assessments should be administered in the same sequence as listed in the sections below and at approximately the same time of the day. [master:8] (applies to: all participants)\n- a. Any additional assessments that are included in an RSA for a given visit should be completed once the Master Protocol SOA for that visit is complete, unless otherwise specified in the RSA. [master:8] (applies to: all regimens)\n- 17. PGI-C and PGI-S should be completed by the participant prior to other clinical assessments scheduled during the visit whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 18. CGI-C should be completed after all clinical assessments are completed by the investigator whenever possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- * Endocrinology notes: Blood samples for ACTH and Cortisol should be collected at approximately the same time in the morning for each scheduled visit to control for diurnal variation. [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G endocrinology assessments)','\n',char(10)),'2. Assessment of vital capacity should be performed at the start of a visit so as not to fatigue the participant with other testing.','["visit_schedule"]',3502,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:20','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Laboratory assessment calendar','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Laboratory assessment calendar\n- Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections. [site_lab_manual:3] (applies to: Regimen G laboratory schedule)\n- - Serum Pregnancy (WOCBP) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1)\n- Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Baseline (Day 0), Wk 4 (Day 28), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Coagulation (PT, PTT, INR) (Central): done at Screening (-42 to -1), Wk 16 (Day 112)\n- Endocrinology (ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Pharmacokinetics (PK Plasma) (Central): done at Wk 4 (Day 28) Pre, Post; Wk 8 (Day 56) Post; Wk 16 (Day 112) Post; Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) Pre, Post\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 8 (Day 56), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0), Wk 16 (Day 112), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168)\n- DNA Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0)\n- CSF Collection (Optional) (Central): done at Baseline (Day 0) X (Bio), Wk 24 / ET (Day 168) X (Bio/PK) [site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled laboratory assessments) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- - Clinical Safety (Hem, Chem, UA) (Central): done at ATE Wk 4 (Wk 28), ATE Wk 8 (Wk 32), ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 40 (Wk 64), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Blood (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76)\n- Biomarker Urine (Central): done at ATE Wk 16 (Wk 40), ATE Wk 28 (Wk 52), ATE Wk 52 (Wk 76) [site_lab_manual:active-treatment-extension-ate-period] (applies to: Regimen G ATE laboratory assessments)\n- * Only collected at specific ATE visits (Wk 16, 28, 52). [site_lab_manual:maximum-research-blood-volume-table] (applies to: Regimen G ATE biomarker blood)\n- The following laboratory tests will be performed for safety at the Master Protocol Screening to determine eligibility and at the following visits: Screening/Baseline as described in the RSA, Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 24 and Week 36/ET [master:9.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol clinical safety laboratory tests)\n- Serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) for WOCBP (collected only at Master Protocol Screening Visit, and as necessary throughout course of study) Additionally, coagulation parameters (Prothrombin time, International normalized ratio, Activated partial thromboplastin time) will be collected for all participants at the Master Protocol Screening Visit. Coagulation parameters will be collected a second time, prior to the second lumbar puncture, as defined in the RSA. [master:9.1.2] (applies to: WOCBP pregnancy tests; all participants coagulation tests)\n- 5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period)\n- 2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Note: Only in-clinic visits are shown; telephone visits (e.g., Wk 2, Wk 12) require no lab collections.','["visit_schedule"]',3520,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:21','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Other assessment timing','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Other assessment timing\n- Assessments will be performed at designated time-points throughout the study for clinical evaluation. [master:9.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- Details of any additional specific clinical assessments required for a regimen will be reported in the corresponding RSA [master:9.1] (applies to: all regimens)\n- Height in em or in will be measured and recorded at the Master Protocol Screening Visit (Visit 1) only. This one-time height measurement must be used throughout the duration of the study. [master:9.1.1] (applies to: all participants)\n- 4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- A standard 12-lead ECG will be performed for safety at the screening visit to determine eligibility and for safety as outlined in the schedule of activities. [master:9.1.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- 12. -lead ECG (to be done at all in-clinic visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 8. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood adrenocorticotropic hormone [ACTH], cortisol, free thyroxine [fT4]) (Baseline and Week 24 Visits) [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010, regimen_g:s011)\n- Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit. [regimen_g:5.10.2] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Assessments will be performed at designated time-points throughout the study for clinical evaluation.','["visit_schedule"]',3521,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:22','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Vital-status follow-up timing','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Vital-status follow-up timing\n- Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive and date last known to be PAV-free, will be determined by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 visit, as indicated in the SOA. At approximately the time of the last patient last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants to assess vital status. [master:8.15] (applies to: all randomized participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:7.7)\n- Vital status may also be ascertained at later time points. [master:8.15] (applies to: all randomized participants)\n- 11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- 1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. At approximately the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled portion of the regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status.\nWe may also ascertain vital status at later time points. [regimen_g:7.7] (applies to: Regimen G randomized participants) (note: differs from master:8.15)\n- Drug 9 Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or death equivalent or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebo- of the last participant''s last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled period of a given regimen. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive and date last known to be PAV-free, will be','["visit_schedule"]',3522,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:23','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Reconsent at scheduled or remote visits','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Reconsent at scheduled or remote visits\n- Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. If the participant’s next in-clinic visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures: [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G participants requiring reconsent)\n- a) The site staff sends copy of the informed consent form to the participant.\nb) The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign.\nc) The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant.\nd) The participant signs the informed consent form and returns the original signed consent form back to the site.\ne) Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant signs the informed consent form. [regimen_g:6] (applies to: Regimen G remote reconsent)','\n',char(10)),'Participants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. Should a participant need','["visit_schedule"]',3523,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:24','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Visit-system mapping and transition steps','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Visit-system mapping and transition steps\n- - Rave Folder Name: Screening | Protocol Equivalent: Days -42 to -1 | Key eCRFs Included: ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, Baseline Symptoms.\n- Rave Folder Name: Baseline | Protocol Equivalent: Day 0 | Key eCRFs Included: Pre-dose Labs, ECG, Baseline PROs (ALSFRS-R, ROADS, ALSAQ-40).\n- Rave Folder Name: Week X (Placebo-Controlled) | Protocol Equivalent: Weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 | Key eCRFs Included: Drug Administration, AEs, ConMeds, PK/Biomarkers, Safety Labs. (Note: Wks 2, 12, 20 are phone visits).\n- Rave Folder Name: ATE Week X (Open-Label) | Protocol Equivalent: Weeks 26 through 102 | Key eCRFs Included: Dynamically populates only after the Week 24 visit is marked complete.\n- Rave Folder Name: Early Termination (ET) | Protocol Equivalent: Out of sequence ET | Key eCRFs Included: Triggered via the "Study Completion / ET" form if subject withdraws early. [site_edc_guide:schedule-of-assessments-soa-mapping] (applies to: Regimen G EDC visit folders)\n- 2. Select the current visit from the dropdown (e.g., Week 4). (Note: Telephone visits at Weeks 2, 12, and 20 do not require IRT dispensation). [site_irt_guide:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G IRT scheduled visits)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks).\n\n1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit.\n2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply.\n3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period.\n4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 transition and ATE visit sequence)','\n',char(10)),'- Rave Folder Name: Screening | Protocol Equivalent: Days -42 to -1 | Key eCRFs Included: ICF, Demographics, Med Hx, Inclusion/Exclusion, Ba','["visit_schedule"]',3524,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:25','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Screening timing and visits','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Screening timing and visits\n- Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Protocol -42 to -1 Master Clinic Days','["visit_schedule"]',3525,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:26','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Screening pregnancy testing and Master Protocol consent\n- Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phone " During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Master Protocol informed consent form (ICF). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Serum pregnancy testing will occur in women of child-bearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessarv Refer to Phon','["visit_schedule"]',3526,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:27','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Vital signs and examinations','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Vital signs and examinations\n- Height in cm measured at Master Protocol Screening Visit only. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Height in cm measured at Master Protocol Screening Visit only.','["visit_schedule"]',3527,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:28','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > DNA and blood biomarkers','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## DNA and blood biomarkers\n- Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Day 168 Week Clinic 2412 7 The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is no','["visit_schedule"]',3528,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:29','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Later vital-status ascertainment and telephone accountability exception\n- We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'We may also ascertain vital status at later time points by using publicly available data 13 Drug accountability will not be done at phone vi','["visit_schedule"]',3529,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:3','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Screening and baseline timing','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Screening and baseline timing\n- 1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- 13. Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0). [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- The following procedures will be performed in-person, after Master Protocol informed consent is obtained, to determine the participant''s eligibility for the Master Protocol and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nAssess Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion criteria:\nObtain ALS and medical history\nObtain demographics\nPerform Vital Capacity\nPerform physical examination\nPerform neurological examination\nMeasure vital signs including height and weight\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nPerform 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG)\nCollect blood samples for clinical screening assessments (including coagulation parameters) and, for women of child bearing potential (WOCBP), for pregnancy test Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood biomarker sample Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Schedule the Regimen Specific Screening Visit if the participant passes screening for the Master Protocol [master:8.1] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- Once a participant''s Master Protocol eligibility is confirmed the participant will be randomly assigned to a regimen and will undergo informed consent for that regimen at their scheduled Regimen-Specific Screening Visit. [master:8.1] (applies to: participants meeting Master Protocol eligibility)\n- This visit will take place in-person after random assignment to a regimen. This visit may be combined with the Baseline Visit, please refer to the RSA for applicability. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nObtain written regimen-specific informed consent from participant Assess regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria Concomitant medication review Assess and document AEs [master:8.2] (applies to: Regimen Specific Screening Visit)\n- 2. During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- 3. At the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit, participants will have regimen-specific eligibility criteria assessed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G screening)\n- This visit will take place in-person after the regimen-specific Screening Visit. This visit may be combined with the Regimen Specific Screening Visit, please refer to the RA for applicability.\nThe following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nRandomize within the regimen\nCollect vital signs\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs, biomarkers, and optional DNA sequencing Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood biomarker sample Perform LP for CSF collection (as described in the RSA) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Baseline questionnaire Administer first dose of investigational product for the regimen, per the instructions in the RSA.\nDispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Instruct participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 4 visit [master:8.3] (applies to: Baseline Visit)\n- This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-S\n12. -lead ECG\n6. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4)\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (biomarkers only; no CSF PK sample collected at baseline)\n▪ CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed.\n10. Dispense study drug\n11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed\n12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.1] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)\n- 8. Administer the first dose of study drug only after the Baseline Visit procedures are completed. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G Baseline)','\n',char(10)),'1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screenin','["visit_schedule"]',3503,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:30','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Telephone compliance checks and return reminders','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Telephone compliance checks and return reminders\n- A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period of their follow-up (generally the Week 36 Visit, as indicated). [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'A drug compliance check in will be conducted during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and controlled period','["visit_schedule"]',3530,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:31','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > CSF collection schedule and conditions','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## CSF collection schedule and conditions\n- " CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'" CSF collection may differ from the Schedule of Activities, and will be performed as described in each RSA.','["visit_schedule"]',3531,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:32','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When visits or procedures are missed or PK timing departs from protocol, record reportable deviations while applying the specified illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Records, data entry, deviations and notifications: Protocol amendments and deviations (When visits or procedures are missed or PK timing departs from protocol, record reportable deviations while applying the specified illness, disability and failed-attempt exceptions.)\n- Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor. [master:1.4.1]\n- Master Protocol amendments will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1] (note: differs from master:12.1)\n- As regimens are added to the Platform Trial, they will be submitted for approval to the sIRB prior to implementation. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each regimen will be added as an appendix to the Master Protocol prior to any participant being assigned to that regimen. [master:1.4.1]\n- Regimens that are stopped for futility or ended early due to success (if applicable) will not result in an amendment to the Master Protocol. [master:1.4.1]\n- Each SI must adhere to the protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB prior to implementing the change unless it is to address immediate participant safety. [master:12.1] (note: differs from master:1.4.1)\n- A protocol deviation is any noncompliance with the sIRB approved clinical trial protocol. [master:8.13]\n- The noncompliance may be either on the part of the participant, the SI, or the study site staff. [master:8.13]\n- Patient reported outcomes (c.g. ALSAQ-40) not performed due to a participant language barrier, and procedures that were attempted but failed will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.13]\n- A minor protocol deviation will be considered a departure from the protocol of relatively minor degree (such as study visit a day or two out of window due to scheduling conflicts). [master:8.13]\n- A major deviation will be considered any significant deviation from the protocol that the Sponsor determines negatively affect: (1) the rights or welfare of the participant; (2), risk benefit assessment; or (3) the integrity of the data (ability to draw valid conclusions from the study data). [master:8.13]\n- As a result of deviations, corrective and preventative actions are to be developed by the site with review by the Coordination Center (CC) and Study Monitors and implemented promptly. [master:8.13]\n- All deviations from the protocol will be recorded in the participant''s source documentation. [master:8.13]\n- Protocol deviations must be entered in the Protocol Deviations Log in the EDC system. [master:8.13]\n- Deviations will be reported to the sIRB per their guidelines. [master:8.13]\n- Missed visits and any procedures not performed (not attempted) for reasons other than illness, injury or progressive disability (i.e., participant is physically unable to perform test) will be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Procedures or visits not performed due to illness, injury or disability, including procedures that were attempted but failed (e.g., blood samples unable to be drawn after multiple attempts, or weight unable to be obtained due to participant immobility will not be reported as protocol deviations. [master:8.14]\n- Investigational product compliance that is outside the limits set in the RSA will be reported as a protocol deviation (see RSA for further details). [master:8.14]\n- Details and specific instructions regarding protocol deviations, including any exceptions to this standard procedure, are found in the study Manual of Procedures. [master:8.14]\n- Missed assessments due to pandemic-related restrictions must be documented as such in the source documentation and in the EDC. [master:8.14]\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G remote visits)\n- Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP. [regimen_g:s060] (applies to: Regimen G ATE remote visits)\n- If the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded. [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Week 4)\n- Navigate to the Subject ID, click on the Action menu, and select Missed Visit. Choose Week 4 from the dropdown. This ensures the system skips the Week 4 dispensation logic and prepares the subject''s timeline for the Week 8 visit. [site_irt_guide:6] (applies to: missed Week 4 visit)\n- Each Site Investigator (SI) must adhere to the Master Protocol detailed in this document and agree that any changes to the protocol must be approved by the sIRB. [master:1.5]','\n',char(10)),'Any change to the Master Protocol will be documented in a protocol amendment, issued by the Sponsor.','["visit_schedule"]',3532,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:33','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a scheduled visit includes clinic dosing, follow clinic preparation requirements, including the second-person check and in-use stability limits.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Preparation and in-use stability (When a scheduled visit includes clinic dosing, follow clinic preparation requirements, including the second-person check and in-use stability limits.)\n- Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepared at home by the subject/caregiver. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- In-Use Stability / Beyond Use Date (BUD): Once the stick pack is opened and the granules are mixed with water or soft food, the dose MUST be administered within 15 minutes. Do not prepare doses in advance. [site_pharmacy_manual:5] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs, a clean administration cup or dosing syringe, and room-temperature potable water. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Carefully tear open both stick packs and empty the entire contents into the administration cup. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 3. Reconstitute: Add approximately 30 mL to 50 mL of water to the cup. [Standard CDMO Default – Sponsor to confirm exact minimum volume, especially for tube feeding restrictions]. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 4. Mix: Swirl or stir gently until the granules are adequately suspended. (Note: Granules may not dissolve completely; a suspension is expected). [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: preparation in water)\n- 5. Draw Up (Tube Feeding Only): If administering via G-tube, J-tube, or NG-tube, draw the suspension into an appropriately sized enteral syringe. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-a-preparation-in-water-oral-or-en] (applies to: tube feeding)\n- 1. Gather Supplies: Two (2) assigned stick packs and approximately 2 to 4 tablespoons of soft food (e.g., applesauce or yogurt). Do not mix with hot foods. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 2. Empty Granules: Tear open both stick packs and empty the contents completely onto the soft food. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 3. Mix: Stir the granules into the food until evenly distributed. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)\n- 4. Administer: The subject must consume the entire portion of the mixture immediately (within 15 minutes). Fluid thickeners may be used concurrently if required for dysphagia. [site_pharmacy_manual:method-b-preparation-in-soft-food-oral-a] (applies to: preparation in soft food)','\n',char(10)),'Depending on the visit schedule (e.g., PK sampling days vs. take-home dosing), the IMP may be prepared in the clinic by site staff or prepar','["visit_schedule"]',3533,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:34','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Remote dispensing (When an eligible clinic visit is conducted remotely, follow the remote dispensing requirements for any study drug supplied.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: Remote dispensing (When an eligible clinic visit is conducted remotely, follow the remote dispensing requirements for any study drug supplied.)\n- Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Details on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP. [regimen_g:s049] (applies to: Regimen G)','\n',char(10)),'Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not availabl','["visit_schedule"]',3534,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:35','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (When the Week 24 visit starts ATE, obtain open-label kit assignment through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind kits.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Study drug supply, storage, dispensing and accountability: ATE supply transition (When the Week 24 visit starts ATE, obtain open-label kit assignment through IRT and do not dispense leftover double-blind kits.)\n- At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD for up to 78 weeks). [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 1. Select the Subject ID and execute the Week 24 (End of Double-Blind / Start of ATE) visit. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 2. The system will automatically recognize the transition to open-label supply. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 3. Authenticate and Confirm. Suvoda will assign Open-Label ATE Kits. Do not dispense any leftover double-blind kits to a subject entering the ATE period. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE transition)\n- 4. Subsequent ATE visits (ATE Wk 4, ATE Wk 8, ATE Wk 16, etc.) will be available sequentially in the subject''s dropdown menu. [site_irt_guide:3.4] (applies to: ATE)','\n',char(10)),'At the Week 24 Visit, the subject completes the double-blind period and transitions to the Open-Label ATE period (receiving DNL343 200 mg QD','["visit_schedule"]',3535,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:36','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (When study drug is discontinued, arrange unused-product returns and complete discontinuation documentation even if scheduled follow-up continues.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Stopping treatment and early termination: Product return and discontinuation documentation (When study drug is discontinued, arrange unused-product returns and complete discontinuation documentation even if scheduled follow-up continues.)\n- Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging, if applicable. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: participants discontinuing investigational product)\n- Upon termination, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, including empty packaging material, if applicable. [master:6.5.2] (applies to: participants terminating participation)\n- For all research participants, the reason for permanent discontinuation of investigational product must be recorded in the Case Report Form (CRF). These data will be included in the trial database and reported. [master:6.5.1] (applies to: all research participants)','\n',char(10)),'Upon discontinuation of investigational product, the research participant is instructed to return any unused investigational product, includ','["visit_schedule"]',3536,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:37','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a scheduled, remote or unscheduled assessment identifies a reportable safety event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Expedited reporting (When a scheduled, remote or unscheduled assessment identifies a reportable safety event, notify the Coordination Center within 24 hours of site awareness.)\n- The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all events that meet the above criteria for SAEs, or any unexpected AE that is deemed related to the study drug, which would have implications for the conduct of the study (e.g., requiring a significant and usually safety-related change to the protocol such as revising inclusion/exclusion criteria or including a new monitoring requirement, informed consent or investigator''s brochure). [master:10.3] (applies to: all participants)\n- Please note: SAEs must be entered into the EDC system and reported to the CC within 24 hours of site awareness of the SAE. [master:12.2.2] (applies to: all SAEs)\n- The timelines for reporting dosage changes are described in each RSA as applicable. [master:10.3]\n- The MM also reviews AE reports, compiled by Data Management, as described in the safety management and medical monitoring plan. The MM will review blinded study data on enrollment, abnormal laboratory results and protocol deviations. These reports will collectively be known as the Medical Monitor Report. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- The MM communicates with the IND Holder / Sponsor, the DSMB, and the CC as needed for reporting of SAEs to the FDA within the required timeframe per FDA investigational new drug application (IND) regulations. [master:10.3] (applies to: MM)\n- All AEs that meet the criteria for a serious, unexpected, suspected adverse drug reaction (SUSAR), for which there is a reasonable possibility that the investigational product caused the event, in the opinion of the IND Holder / Sponsor, will be submitted to FDA in an expedited fashion. [master:10.3] (applies to: SUSARs)\n- Death, respiratory failure and hospitalization for routine procedures (i.e., g-tube placement) will not be reported individually in an expedited manner because they are anticipated to occur in the study population at some frequency independent of drug expose. The DSMB will review aggregate analysis of these events and the sponsor will submit an IND safety report if the aggregate analysis indicates these events occur more frequently in any of the active drug treatment groups, as compared to the concurrent placebo group. [master:10.3]','\n',char(10)),'The following are considered reportable events and must be reported to the CC within 24 hours of the site being notified of the event: all e','["visit_schedule"]',3537,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:38','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When a visit''s C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety assessment and response procedures.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Safety, adverse events and reporting: Suicidality assessment and response (When a visit''s C-SSRS assessment identifies suicidality, follow the required safety assessment and response procedures.)\n- At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s lifetime. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants)\n- At all other visits, the Since Last Visit version of the C-SSRS will be administered. This version of the scale assesses suicidality since the participant''s last visit. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: all participants) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010; regimen_g:s011)\n- If there is a positive response to question 4 or 5 on the severity of ideation subscale or any positive response on the suicidal behavior subscale of suicide attempt or suicidal ideation by the participant during the administration of the C-SSRS during the treatment period, the appropriately qualified clinician will be notified during the study visit to determine the appropriate actions required to ensure the participant''s safety. The site must ensure that the participant is seen by a licensed physician (or other qualified individual as required by local institutional policy) before leaving the study site. The SI will determine whether the participant should remain on study drug. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: treatment period)\n- Reference to the Clinical Triage Guidelines Using the C-SSRS can be found here https://cssrs.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/C-SSRS-triage-and-workflow-examples-2021.docx It is recommended that a medically licensed physician, nurse, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant to assess the C-SSRS. All evaluators must be certified to perform the C-SSKS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- Certification is required prior to performing the C-SSRS. [master:9.1.6] (applies to: C-SSRS evaluators)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)\n- - Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from master:9.1.6)','\n',char(10)),'At the Baseline Visit, the C-SSRS Baseline version will be administered. This version is used to assess suicidality over the participant''s l','["visit_schedule"]',3538,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:39','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (When screening restarts after the initial window or a previously randomized participant is considered for reassignment, obtain the required renewed Master Protocol consent.)','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Also applies, from Informed consent: Rescreening and reassignment consent (When screening restarts after the initial window or a previously randomized participant is considered for reassignment, obtain the required renewed Master Protocol consent.)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants rescreened after the initial screening window)\n- The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants whose regimen participation is discontinued)','\n',char(10)),'If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Pr','["visit_schedule"]',3539,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:4','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Repeat screening and reassignment','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Repeat screening and reassignment\n- If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to enrollment, interval following last use of an investigational treatment, or other criteria as applicable), then that participant may become eligible immediately after resolution of the disqualifying condition if resolution occurs within the screening period and re-screening is not required. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing time-dependent eligibility criteria)\n- If a participant fails to meet the vital capacity screening criterion on the day of the Master Protocol Screening visit, vital capacity may be reassessed on a different day during the initial screening period. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing vital capacity screening)\n- Safety labs required for the site investigator to determine eligibility, can be repeated during the Master Protocol screening period, if necessary. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: Master Protocol screening)\n- If a participant is re-screened for Master Protocol eligibility after the initial screening window ends, they must complete a full Master Protocol Screening Visit, including informed consent, as outlined in section 8.1. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: re-screening after initial screening window)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a specific regimen, but does not meet additional regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria for that regimen, then the research participant may be re-assigned immediately to a different regimen. If it is known at the time of regimen assignment that the participant is not eligible for the regimen, a participant may be administratively screen-failed for a regimen prior to regimen consent and screening. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: participants failing regimen-specific eligibility)\n- If a research participant meets Master Protocol eligibility, is assigned to a regimen, and is randomized within that regimen, but their participation in that regimen is discontinued, that participant may have the opportunity to be re-screened for the Master Protocol. The research participant must provide written informed re-consent to the Master Protocol and must still meet the Master Protocol inclusion and exclusion eligibility criteria in order to be re-assigned into a different regimen. Re-screening for the Master Protocol and potential re-assignment into a different regimen, if available, may occur due to the following situations: [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants)\n- A regimen is stopped due to futility or success\n• The research participant may be re-screened after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer\n• A research participant completes their Week 36 Visit in the placebo-controlled period and terminates their participation in the study\n• The research participant may be re-screened after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer\n• A research participant discontinues participation in a regimen for personal reasons (e.g., inconvenience of the intervention, mode of administration, required assessments) or due to an adverse event, or is discontinued by the SI\n• The research participant may not be re-screened until after the date that their 36-Week Visit would have occurred, had they completed participation in that regimen. [master:8.1.2] (applies to: previously randomized participants)\n- Upon completion of a regimen, a research participant may be re-screened into the Master Protocol and assigned to another regimen after 30 days or 5 half-lives (if known), whichever is longer. [master:11.2.4] (applies to: participants completing a regimen)','\n',char(10)),'If a research participant fails to meet a time-dependent Master Protocol eligibility criterion (e.g., duration of stable medication prior to','["visit_schedule"]',3504,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:5','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Placebo-controlled visit calendar','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Placebo-controlled visit calendar\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 Days [13] | Regimen Specific Screening [1] / Clinic / –41 to 0 Day [13] | Baseline [1] / Clinic / Day 0 | Week 2 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [12] / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [12] / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [12] / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 or ET Visit [19] / Clinic / Day 168 ± 7 | Follow-Up Safety Call 10 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from master:8.7, master:8.8, master:8.9, master:8.10)\n- This visit will take place in-person 28 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed:\nCollect vital signs\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done at Baseline) Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA Schedule next Study Visit Remind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 8 Visit [master:8.5] (applies to: Week 4) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place in-person 56 + 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed:\nCollect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.6] (applies to: Week 8) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 84 = 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed:\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 16 Visit [master:8.7] (applies to: Week 12) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place in-person 112 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in the preferred order to be completed:\nCollect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs (including coagulation parameters) and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform LP for CSF collection (or as described in the RSA) Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.8] (applies to: Week 16) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 140 - 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 24 Visit [master:8.9] (applies to: Week 20) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place in-person 168 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nCollect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Dispense investigational product to participant, per instructions in the RSA [master:8.10] (applies to: Week 24) (note: differs from regimen_g:s010)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 196 - 3 days after the Bascline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In [master:8.9-60] (applies to: Master Protocol Week 28)\n- This visit will take place via telephone 224 + 3 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nReview and document concomitant medications\nAssess and document AEs\nDrug Compliance Check-In\nSchedule next Study Visit\nRemind participant to bring in investigational product to the Week 36 Visit [master:8.9-61] (applies to: Master Protocol Week 32)\n- This visit will take place in-person 252 - 7 days after the Baseline Visit. The following procedures will be performed and are listed in preferred order to be completed:\nCollect vital signs including weight\nPerform SVC\nAdminister ALSFRS-R questionnaire\nAsk participant to complete Patient Reported Outcomes\nPerform 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG)\nCollect blood samples for Clinical Safety Labs and, for WOCBP, for pregnancy test if applicable Collect urine for urinalysis Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses Collect blood for optional DNA sequencing (if not done previously) Review and document concomitant medications Assess and document AEs Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire Perform investigational product accountability and compliance Ask participant to complete the Exit Questionnaire [master:8.10-62] (applies to: Master Protocol Week 36)\n- As per the Schedule of Activities (SOA) below, visits occur every 4 weeks and will be clinic-, phone-, or telemedicine-based, as applicable. [master:protocol-summary]','\n',char(10)),'Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 D','["visit_schedule"]',3505,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:6','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Regimen G placebo-controlled visit procedures','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Regimen G placebo-controlled visit procedures\n- This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.2] (applies to: Regimen G Week 2)\n- The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nIf the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded.\nFor the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained.\nThese visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. CNS-BFS [Week 8 only]\n12. -lead ECG\n3. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 4, postdose only on Week 8) o For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n4. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n6. Dispense study drug\n7. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.3] (applies to: Regimen G Weeks 4 and 8) (note: differs from master:8.5, master:8.6)\n- This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.4] (applies to: Regimen G Week 12) (note: differs from master:8.7)\n- This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. CNS-BFS\n2. ALSAQ-40\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (postdose only on Week 16) o Postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n9. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n11. Dispense study drug\n12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.5] (applies to: Regimen G Week 16) (note: differs from master:8.8)\n- This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of concomitant medications and adverse events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner [regimen_g:6.6] (applies to: Regimen G Week 20) (note: differs from master:8.9)\n- The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThis visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE)\n10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples)\na) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection.\nb) CSF collection for the ET visit will be performed only if the participant has consented to the lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.\n13. Dispense study drug\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit [regimen_g:6.7] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET) (note: differs from master:8.10, regimen_g:s010)','\n',char(10)),'This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen sc','["visit_schedule"]',3506,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:7','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > Placebo-controlled assessment mapping','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## Placebo-controlled assessment mapping\n- - Written Informed Consent [2] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1]\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1] [3]\n- ALS & Medical History Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Demographics Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Physical Examination Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Neurological Exam Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Muscle Strength Assessment Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-C 17 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-S 17 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CGI-C 18 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- 12-Lead ECG Regimen: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Week 16 [12]\n- Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all participants prior to ATE)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] Regimen: done at Week 4 [12] (pre, post), Week 8 [12] (post), Week 16 [12] (post), Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (pre, post)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1]\n- CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Assignment to the Regimen Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Randomization within the Regimen Master: done at Baseline [1]\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Baseline [1] [8], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [20], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12 [20], Week 16 [12], Week 20 [20], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Exit Questionnaire Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Vital Status Determination [11] Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled period) (note: differs from site_lab_manual:placebo-controlled-period-weeks-0-24, regimen_g:6.7, master:9.1.6)\n- 9. Study drug will only be dispensed at this visit if the participant continues in the ATE. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G Week 24 or ET)\n- 19. The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled early termination)\n- 20. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G telephone visits)\n- [21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements. [regimen_g:s010] (applies to: Regimen G participants subject to contraception review)','\n',char(10)),replace('- Written Informed Consent [2] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1]\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review Ma','\n',char(10)),'["visit_schedule"]',3507,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:8','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > ATE duration and completion','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## ATE duration and completion\n- Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extension (ATE) where all participants will receive DNL343 200 mg QD for up to an additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:s004] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- The planned duration of the placebo-controlled treatment period of the study is 24 weeks. The duration of the ATE period is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks (i.e., 76 weeks total treatment duration). The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks of ATE (i.e., 102 weeks total from Baseline visit). [regimen_g:s007] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Following 24 weeks of double-blinded treatment, all participants will enter the active treatment extension (ATE) period to receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks. [regimen_g:3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- A participant is considered to have completed the placebo-controlled period of the Regimen when they have completed Week 24 or the Early Termination (ET) Visit. [regimen_g:s025] (applies to: Regimen G placebo-controlled completion)\n- Participants are considered to have completed their participation in the ATE period of\nRegimen G if planned ATE period visits, including the last visit, ET Visit, or the last scheduled procedure shown in the Schedule of Activities (SOA), have been completed. [regimen_g:s026] (applies to: Regimen G ATE completion)\n- The end of the placebo-controlled period in Regimen G occurs when all randomized participants have completed their participation in the placebo-controlled period as defined in Section 3.2.\nThe end of the ATE period in Regimen G occurs when all participants who initiated study drug in the ATE period have completed their participation in that period as defined in Section 3.2. [regimen_g:3.3] (applies to: Regimen G)\n- Following the placebo-controlled period, participants will continue in the ATE as outlined in the SOA.\nThe ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE (ie, 102 weeks total from the Baseline visit). The Industry Partner may consider extending or discontinuing the ATE period depending on the results from the primary analysis. [regimen_g:6.10] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)\n- 5. The duration of the ATE is a minimum of 52 weeks with a maximum of 78 weeks per participant and will be completed when all active participants have completed the 52-week Visit in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE)','\n',char(10)),'Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extens','["visit_schedule"]',3508,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','packs','piece:visit_schedule:9','piece','','Visit schedule and windows > ATE calendar and later-visit intervals','Visit schedule and windows',replace('## ATE calendar and later-visit intervals\n- Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [7] / Week 32 / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Week 36 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [7] / Week 40 / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Week 44 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 / Week 48 / Phone / Day168 ± 3 | Week 28 [7] / Week 52 / Clinic / Day 196 ± 14 | Week 40 [7] / Week 64 / Clinic / Day 280 ± 14 | Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 / Week 76 / Clinic / Day 364 ± 14 | All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] / Week 89 and Week 102 / Clinic / 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks | Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G ATE) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11, regimen_g:s011)\n- 8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. Participants will continue in the ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [regimen_g:s011] (applies to: Regimen G after ATE Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:6.10.11, regimen_g:s011)\n- Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit [regimen_g:6.10.11] (applies to: Regimen G after ATE Week 52) (note: differs from regimen_g:s011)','\n',char(10)),'Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 ','["visit_schedule"]',3509,'regimen_g=2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7; master=6.0 (14 Oct 2024) sha256:28e3263947cc; site_lab_manual=2.0 (Dec 2023) sha256:9239cb6e1799; site_pharmacy_manual=2.0 sha256:9586af7f3f43; site_edc_guide=1.0 sha256:c631de9ef9f3; site_irt_guide=sha256:7c030baf67bb');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:1','section','1','INTRODUCTION','','','Introduces the appendix''s background information on DNL343.','["background"]',12,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:1.1','section','1.1','DNL343 Background Information','1 INTRODUCTION','','Introduces background information on DNL343 and its supporting studies.','["background"]',14,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:1.2','section','1.2','DNL343 Therapeutic Rationale','1 INTRODUCTION > 1.1 DNL343 Background Information > Phase 1 Study (DNLI-F-0007): A Drug-Drug Interaction Study of the Effect of DNL343 on Midazolam Pharmacokinetics in Healthy Participants (Preliminary Data)',replace('Nonclinical studies have implicated dysregulation of the ISR in the pathogenesis of ALS. Chronic activation of the ISR can lead to cellular dysfunction (Pakos-Zebrucka et al 2016), including the loss of active eIF2B complex. ISR pathway activation is evident in multiple animal models of ALS (Nagata et al 2007; Cheng et al 2018; Sonobe et al 2018; Westergard et al 2019), and inhibition of the ISR has been shown to be neuroprotective and results in functional benefits in a range of neurodegenerative models (Kim et al 2013; Hetz and Saxena 2017;\nLarhammar et al 2017; Moreno et al 2013; Wong et al 2019). The ISR pathway appears to be similarly overactive in ALS, as several markers of the ISR pathway are elevated in tissues of patients with ALS, including p-eIF2 and ATF4 (Ilieva et al 2007; Hetz et al 2009).\nDNL343 inhibits the ISR in stressed cells, restores normal protein synthesis, and inhibits stress granule formation. The nonclinical neuroprotective findings of ISR pathway inhibition, combined with the observation of increased ISR activity in patients with ALS, indicate that inhibition of the ISR pathway by DNL343 activation of eIF2B could be beneficial in the treatment of ALS. Additional information regarding the disease and target is provided in Section 2 of the DNL343 IB.','\n',char(10)),'Explains the nonclinical and disease-mechanism rationale for treating ALS by inhibiting the integrated stress response through eIF2B activation.','["background"]',19,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:2','section','2','OBJECTIVES','','','Introduces the study objectives and endpoints.','["assessments"]',20,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:2.1','section','2.1','Study Objectives and Endpoints','2 OBJECTIVES',replace('Primary Efficacy Objective:\n1. To evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 as compared to placebo on ALS disease progression\nSecondary Efficacy Objective:\n2. To evaluate the effect of DNL343 on selected secondary measures of disease progression, including survival\nSafety Objective:\n3. To evaluate the safety of DNL343 in participants with ALS\nExploratory Objectives:\n4. To evaluate the effect of DNL343 on selected biomarkers and endpoints\n5. To evaluate DNL343 concentrations in plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)\nPrimary Efficacy Endpoint:\n6. Change from baseline through Week 24 in disease severity as measured by the\nAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Functional Rating Scale-Revised (ALSFRS-R) total score and survival.\nSecondary Efficacy Endpoints:\n7. Change in ALSFRS-R total score from baseline to Week 24\n8. Combined Assessment of Function and Survival (CAFS) to Week 24\n9. Change in respiratory function as assessed by slow vital capacity (SVC) from baseline to Week 24\n10. Change in muscle strength as measured isometrically using hand-held dynamometry and grip strength from baseline to Week 24\n11. Survival evaluated as time to death or permanent assisted ventilation (PAV)\n12. Survival evaluated as time to death\n13. Change in log-transformed serum neurofilament light protein (NfL) concentration from baseline to Week 24\nSafety Endpoints:\n14. Treatment-emergent adverse events and serious adverse events\n15. Changes in laboratory values and treatment-emergent and clinically significant laboratory abnormalities\n16. Changes in ECG parameters and treatment-emergent and clinically significant ECG abnormalities\n17. Treatment-emergent suicidal ideation and suicidal behavior\nExploratory Endpoints:\n18. Changes in patient reported outcomes (PROs) (Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis\nAssessment Questionnaire-40 [ALSAQ-40], Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar\nFunction Scale [CNS-BFS], Rasch-built Overall Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis\nDisability Scale [ROADS], Patient Global Impression of Change [PGI-C], Patient Global Impression of Severity [PGI-S])\n19. Change in body weight\n20. Changes in biofluid biomarkers (urine, plasma, optional CSF)\n21. DNL343 plasma and optional CSF concentrations and CSF-to-plasma concentration ratios\n22. Changes in clinician reported outcome (Clinician Global Impression of Change [CGI-C])','\n',char(10)),'Defines efficacy, safety, and exploratory endpoints, including function, survival, respiratory and muscle measures, laboratory and ECG changes, suicidality, biomarkers, and plasma/CSF drug concentrations.','["assessments", "safety", "samples"]',21,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:3','section','3','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN','',replace('Regimen G is a multi-center, randomized, placebo-controlled trial, testing active dose of DNL343 200 mg administered orally QD versus placebo. Participants will be randomized 3:1 active:placebo. Following 24 weeks of double-blinded treatment, all participants will enter the active treatment extension (ATE) period to receive DNL343 200 mg QD for a minimum of additional 52 weeks to a maximum of additional 78 weeks.\nFigure 1: DNL343 Regimen-Specific Appendix Study Schema\nAbbreviations: BL, baseline; QD, once daily.','\n',char(10)),'Specifies 3:1 randomized oral DNL343 200 mg daily versus placebo for 24 weeks, followed by DNL343 200 mg daily for a 52–78-week active extension.','["dosing", "visit_schedule", "extension_period"]',22,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:3.1','section','3.1','Scientific Rationale for Regimen-Specific Appendix Design','3 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN',replace('This Regimen-Specific Appendix (RSA) is designed to correspond with the design of the Master Protocol and the goals of the Platform Trial.\nIn addition to the objectives in the Master Protocol, Regimen G includes a secondary objective evaluating a CAFS and exploratory objectives to characterize the PK of DNL343 in participants with ALS, additional exploratory measures to measure health-related quality of life in individuals with ALS (ALSAQ-40), functional changes (ROADS, CNS-BFS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C), body weight, and exploratory biomarkers.','\n',char(10)),'Explains how the regimen design aligns with the Master Protocol and why additional functional, quality-of-life, PK, and biomarker objectives are included.','["background"]',23,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:3.2','section','3.2','End of Participation Definition','3 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN','','Introduces definitions for the end of participant participation.','["visit_schedule", "early_termination"]',24,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:3.3','section','3.3','End of Regimen Definition','3 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN > Active Treatment Extension Period',replace('The end of the placebo-controlled period in Regimen G occurs when all randomized participants have completed their participation in the placebo-controlled period as defined in Section 3.2.\nThe end of the ATE period in Regimen G occurs when all participants who initiated study drug in the ATE period have completed their participation in that period as defined in Section 3.2.','\n',char(10)),'Defines each regimen period''s end as completion of participation by all randomized participants or all participants who initiated ATE study drug, respectively.','["visit_schedule", "extension_period"]',27,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4','section','4','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT','','','Introduces regimen-specific enrollment requirements; no standalone instructions are provided.','["eligibility"]',28,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4.1','section','4.1','Number of Study Participants','4 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT','Approximately two hundred-forty (n=240) participants will be randomized in Regimen G.','Describes the planned randomization of approximately 240 participants in Regimen G.','["statistics"]',29,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4.2','section','4.2','Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria','4 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT','In order to be randomized to Regimen G, participants must meet the Master Protocol eligibility criteria. In addition, participants meeting all of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria will be allowed to enroll in Regimen G.','Enroll and randomize only participants meeting both Master Protocol eligibility requirements and regimen-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria.','["eligibility"]',30,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4.2.1','section','4.2.1','Regimen-Specific Appendix Inclusion Criteria','4 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT > 4.2 Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria','There are no additional RSA Inclusion Criteria beyond those described in the Master Protocol.','Apply the Master Protocol inclusion criteria; there are no additional regimen-specific inclusion criteria.','["eligibility"]',31,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4.2.2','section','4.2.2','Regimen-Specific Appendix Exclusion Criteria','4 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT > 4.2 Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria',replace('Participants who meet any of the following criteria will be excluded from study entry:\n1. Diagnosis of epilepsy currently requiring antiepileptic therapy, or seizure of any cause within 6 months of randomization\n2. Hypersensitivity to DNL343 or any of the excipients contained within the DNL343 drug product\n3. Use of prescription or over-the-counter (OTC) medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9)\n4. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9)\n5. Use of prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for breast cancer resistance protein (BCRP) or organic anion transporter (OAT)3 transporters and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose administration or anticipated use during the study treatment period (see Section 5.9)\nThere are RSA-specific requirements that apply to Master Protocol Exclusion Criterion #6.\nFor further details of contraceptive requirements for this RSA, please refer to Section 5.10.2.','\n',char(10)),'Exclude participants with specified seizure history, DNL343 hypersensitivity, or prohibited medication exposure or anticipated use, and apply regimen-specific contraceptive requirements.','["eligibility", "concomitant_medications", "contraception_pregnancy"]',32,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:4.3','section','4.3','Treatment Assignment Procedures','4 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX ENROLLMENT','Each participant who meets all eligibility criteria for the Regimen will be randomized to receive either DNL343 200 mg or placebo for approximately 24 weeks of placebo-controlled treatment and will continue on to the ATE period for up to an additional 78 weeks.','Randomize eligible participants to DNL343 200 mg or placebo for approximately 24 weeks, followed by ATE treatment for up to 78 additional weeks.','["eligibility", "dosing", "extension_period"]',33,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5','section','5','INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT','','','Introduces investigational-product provisions; no standalone instructions are provided.','["drug_supply", "dosing"]',34,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.1','section','5.1','Investigational Product Manufacturer','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT','DNL343 drug product and placebo will be provided for this trial; both are comprised of granules filled into stick packs. DNL343 drug product and placebo are manufactured at the same site, Catalent Germany Schorndorf GmbH, located at Steinbeisstrasse 1-2, 73614 Schorndorf, Germany. Secondary packaging, labeling, and initial distribution of DNL343 drug product and placebo is performed at Fisher Clinical Services located at 7554 Schantz Rd, Allentown, PA 18106 USA.','Identifies the manufacturer and packaging, labeling, and initial distribution facilities for DNL343 and placebo granule stick packs.','["drug_supply"]',35,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.10.1','section','5.10.1','Known Potential Benefits and Risks','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration > 5.10 DNL343 Known Potential Risks and Benefits',replace('The benefit and risks of DNL343 treatment in participants with ALS have not been established. Based on the mechanism of action of DNL343, inhibition of the ISR is being investigated for its therapeutic potential in individuals with ALS. The risks of DNL343 treatment are based on extensive evaluation in nonclinical studies and evaluation in clinical studies in approximately 95 healthy participants for up to 14 days and 20 participants with ALS for up to 10 months to characterize the safety profile. DNL343 was generally safe and well-tolerated in participants with ALS. The potential risks of participation in the current study are primarily those associated with adverse reactions to the study drug and study procedures.\nMore detailed information regarding the known and expected benefits, risks, and reasonably anticipated adverse reactions associated with DNL343 are provided in the DNL343 IB.','\n',char(10)),'Describes unestablished ALS benefits and risks, prior safety experience, potential adverse reactions, and the Investigator''s Brochure as the detailed risk-information source.','["background", "safety"]',44,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.10.2','section','5.10.2','Contraceptive Requirements for Participants in Regimen G','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration > 5.10 DNL343 Known Potential Risks and Benefits',replace('Participants will be asked to provide verbal confirmation of the contraceptive method that they are using at Screening and at each in clinic visit for the duration of the double-blind and ATE periods of the study. The contraceptive method(s) will be documented in the CRF at each clinic visit.\nParticipants must adhere to the following requirements for contraceptive use throughout the trial.\n1. For female participants, one of the following must apply:\n1. Postmenopausal (defined as no menses for ≥ 12 consecutive months before screening without an alternative medical cause, with a follicle-stimulating hormone level of > 40\nIU/L or mIU/mL at screening)\n2. Surgically sterilized (eg, hysterectomy, bilateral salpingectomy, bilateral oophorectomy) at least 6 months before dosing\nOR\nc. For participants of childbearing potential: both the participant and the male partner must use highly effective contraception at the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug. Highly effective contraception consists of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. The other must be a highly effective method (as defined in Appendix III), which includes one of the following:\n1. Intrauterine device\n2. Intrauterine hormone-releasing system\n3. Bilateral tubal ligation\n4. Abstinence, defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ (calendar, symptothermal, postovulation methods) is considered not reliable and would not be acceptable.\nOR\nd. For participants of childbearing potential with an azoospermic male partner, the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential. Note: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview.\n2. For male participants:\na. When engaging in sex with a female partner of childbearing potential, both the male participant and his female partner must use highly effective contraception consisting of two forms of birth control, one of which must be a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom, and the other of which must be a highly effective form of birth control (as defined in Appendix III) used by the female partner. This is required from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug.\nOR\nb. For azoospermic male participants with a woman partner of childbearing potential the male partner must use a male barrier method such as a latex or polyurethane condom.\nMale participants must not donate sperm at any time from the start of dosing, throughout the study period, and for 90 days after the final administration of study drug.','\n',char(10)),'Verify contraceptive methods at screening and each clinic visit through double-blind and ATE treatment, document them in the CRF, and enforce reproductive-status and contraception requirements, including 90-day post-dose protection where specified.','["eligibility", "contraception_pregnancy", "visit_schedule", "extension_period", "records_reporting"]',45,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.2','section','5.2','Labeling, Packaging, and Resupply','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT',replace('Both DNL343 drug product and placebo are packaged into individual stick packs made from aluminum foil at Catalent. The aluminum foil is composed of three layers of polyester, aluminum, and low-density polyethylene. The granules are only in contact with low-density polyethylene, which is on the inside of the stick pack.\nThe individual stick packs, or packets, are packaged into secondary cartons at Fisher Clinical Services and each carton contains 14 packets segmented into 7 doses containing 2 packets each. Each packet contains DNL343 100 mg or placebo. Each packet and each carton are labeled at Fisher Clinical Services.\nThe final labeled cartons are shipped to the Central pharmacy for distribution to clinical sites.\nAll shipments to clinical sites are controlled using an Integrated Response Technology solution.','\n',char(10)),'Describes labeled cartons containing fourteen 100-mg or placebo packets in seven two-packet doses, distributed to sites through the central pharmacy using IRT-controlled shipments.','["drug_supply"]',36,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.3','section','5.3','Acquisition, Storage, and Preparation','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT',replace('DNL343 drug product and placebo should be stored at room temperature (store below 30°C).\nAll drug supplies must be stored in a secure, temperature-controlled area with limited access. For batch-specific storage instructions, see the packaging.\nAll study drug will be transported, received, stored, and handled in accordance with the container or product label, the instructions supplied to the research site and its designated pharmacy, the site’s standard operating procedures, and the applicable country regulations. Appropriate storage temperature and transportation conditions will be maintained for the study drug from the point of manufacture up to delivery of the study drug to each participating clinical site.\nUpon receipt by the study site, the research pharmacy staff will transfer the shipment to the appropriate environmentally controlled storage area. The shipment and temperature monitoring devices (if applicable) will then be examined to verify that the study drug was received in acceptable condition. Once inspected and deemed acceptable, the study drug will then be stored in a restricted access, secured area with access limited to authorized research site staff, under physical conditions consistent with the study drug’s specific requirements.\nThe investigator or designee must confirm that appropriate temperature conditions have been maintained during transit for all study drug received and that any discrepancies are reported and resolved before use of the study drug.\nRefer to the Manual of Procedures (MOP) for additional instructions on handling and storage of study drug.','\n',char(10)),'Store drug securely below 30°C per applicable instructions, inspect received shipments and transit temperatures, and report and resolve discrepancies before use.','["drug_supply", "records_reporting"]',37,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.4','section','5.4','Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT',replace('DNL343 drug product and placebo will be administered reconstituted in water for oral or feeding tube administration (eg, gastrostomy tube [G-tube], jejunostomy tube [J-tube], or nasogastric tube) or coadministered with soft food (eg, applesauce or yogurt). Participants can take the drug with fluid thickener as needed.\nStudy drug administered are described in Table 2.\nTable 2: Study Drug Administered\nTable columns:  | Placebo-Controlled Period | ATE Period\n- Arm Name | Placebo-Controlled Period: DNL343 | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo | ATE Period: DNL343 ATE\n- Intervention Name | Placebo-Controlled Period: DNL343 | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo | ATE Period: DNL343\n- Type | Placebo-Controlled Period: Drug | ATE Period: Drug\n- Dose Formulation | Placebo-Controlled Period: Granules in stick pack | ATE Period: Granules in stick pack\n- Unit Dose Strength(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 100 mg | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo | ATE Period: 100 mg\n- Dose Level(s) | Placebo-Controlled Period: 200 mg QD | Placebo-Controlled Period: Placebo QD | ATE Period: 200 mg QD\n- Route of Administration | Placebo-Controlled Period: Oral or feeding tube | ATE Period: Oral or feeding tube\n- Use | Placebo-Controlled Period: Experimental | Placebo-Controlled Period: Sham comparator | ATE Period: Experimental\nAbbreviations: ATE, active treatment extension; QD, once daily.\nParticipants should take the study drug at approximately the same time each day in the morning without regard to food. All dosing should be completed within 15 minutes. On clinic visit days with predose PK assessments, participants will be administered their dose of study drug in the clinic.\nParticipants may take their missed dose as soon as they remember to take it in the same day. If the participant remembers the day after the dose was missed, only the next dose should be taken. Only one dose is to be taken per day.','\n',char(10)),'Administer assigned once-daily treatment, including DNL343 200 mg in ATE, by permitted oral or feeding-tube methods, complete morning dosing within 15 minutes, and dose in clinic on predose-PK visit days.','["dosing", "extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments"]',38,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.5','section','5.5','Justification for Dosage','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration',replace('The DNL343 dose selected for this study (200 mg QD) is based on safety, PK, and PD data from the Phase 1 study in healthy participants (Study DNLI-F-0001) and interim data from the Phase 1b study in ALS participants (Study DNLI-F-0003) and is further supported by neuroprotection data from DNL343-treated mouse models of neurodegeneration.\nIn Study DNLI-F-0001, DNL343 oral doses ranging from 45 to 260 mg QD for 14 days were generally safe and well tolerated in healthy participants. In Study DNLI-F-0003, DNL343 was generally safe and well tolerated in ALS participants based on an interim analysis when 20 participants had completed 28-day double-blind treatment period. A summary of human exposure at a dose of 200 mg and associated safety margins to the NOAEL and lowest-observedadverse-effect level (LOAEL) in nonclinical toxicity studies are provided in Table 3. A dose of 200 mg is estimated to provide a margin of safety of 7 to 8 × (Cmax–area under the concentrationtime curve from time zero to 24 hours [AUC24] range) to total and 2 to 3 × (Cmax–AUC24 range) to unbound AUC24 and Cmax values associated with the male rat NOAEL in the 6-month Good Laboratory Practice (GLP) toxicity study (most sensitive sex and species, based on unbound exposure).\nIn nonclinical models, plasma and brain exposures achieving or exceeding the in vitro EC90 (22 nM) estimated from H4 human neuroglioma cells provided consistent and maximal efficacy. In the optic nerve crush mouse model, reduced retinal ganglion cell loss and axonal degeneration were observed with DNL343 doses that achieved coverage of the EC90. In the eIF2BR191H mouse model, DNL343 exposures at the concentration producing 75% of maximum effect (EC75) were associated with improvements in motor function as measured by the balance beam test and reductions in the plasma neurodegeneration biomarker neurofilament light chain (Nf-L) and exposures that exceeded the concentration producing 90% of maximum effect (EC90) led to further improvement, resulting in normalization of these endpoints. These effects were associated with a 35% and 64% reduction in the expression of the brain ISR genes ATF4 and CHAC1, respectively, compared with vehicle treated baseline.\nPK/PD modeling of data from healthy participants and participants with ALS showed decreases in ISR pathway biomarkers (CHAC1 gene expression and ATF4 protein levels) in stimulated PBMCs, with unbound EC90 values estimated to range from 55 to 69 nM, suggesting that concentrations higher than that required to see effects in animals may be needed for pharmacologic effects in humans. Based on the evidence above, a DNL343 dose of 200 mg QD is expected to provide (on average) trough coverage above the EC90 values from PK/PD modeling of data from clinical studies. The dose selected for the current study (200 mg QD) is estimated to result in approximately ≥ 60% reduction in CHAC1 gene expression and ATF4 protein levels based on PK/PD modeling.\nIn summary, DNL343 200 mg QD administered orally is estimated to decrease relevant ISR pathway biomarkers and have exposures at or above concentrations required for potential clinical efficacy, based on PK/PD modeling of data from the clinical studies, Study DNLI-F-0001 and Study DNLI-F-0003, and also based on data from in vivo mouse models of neurodegeneration.\nThe exposures achieved at DNL343 200 mg QD was shown to be safe and well tolerated in\nStudy DNLI-F-0001 for 14 days and to have adequate safety margins to the rat and monkey NOAELs. In addition, interim analysis of safety and tolerability data from the ongoing Phase 1b study (Study DNLI-F-0003) in ALS patients supports a dose of 200 mg for a longer-term dosing in the late-stage studies in ALS patients.\nTable 3: Predicted Safety Margins for Proposed DNL343 Clinical Doses of 200 mg Once Daily in\nRelation to NOAEL and LOAEL Exposures in the Good Laboratory Practice Toxicity Studies\nTable columns: Nonclinical Study Dose Level | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h)\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: GLP 3-month rat toxicity study\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: M, 50 mg/kg (LOAEL) c | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM): 8 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 9 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 3 ×\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: GLP 6-month rat toxicity study\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: F, 25 mg/kg/d (NOAEL) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM): 8 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 10 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 3 ×\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: M, 25 mg/kg/d (NOAEL) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM): 7 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 8 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM): 2 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 3 ×\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: GLP 3-month monkey toxicity study\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: M + F, 25 mg/kg/d (NOAEL) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM): 11 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 11 ×\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: GLP 9-month monkey toxicity study\n- Nonclinical Study Dose Level: M + F, 25 mg/kg/d (NOAEL) | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / Cmax (µM): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Total / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 3 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / Cmax (µM): 11 × | Exposure Multiples to 200 mg QD a / Unbound b / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 12 ×\nAbbreviations: AUC24, area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to 24 hours; Cmax, maximum concentration; F, female; fu, fraction unbound; GLP, Good Laboratory Practice; LOAEL, lowest-observedadverse-effect level; M, male; NOAEL, no-observed-adverse-effect level; QD, once daily.\n1. Exposure multiples are calculated using total and unbound AUC24 and Cmax from the nonclinical toxicity studies relative to observed steady-state total and unbound AUC24 and Cmax in healthy participants at a dose of 200 mg QD (total AUC24 = 243 µM ∙ h and total Cmax = 16.2 µM; unbound AUC24 = 2.92 µM ∙ h and unbound Cmax = 0.194 µM).\n2. Unbound multiples derived from total plasma exposure using fu in rats (0.004), cynomolgus monkeys (0.046), and humans (0.012).\n3. LOAEL is defined as the mean Cmax and AUC exposure in male rats following a single dose of 50 mg/kg in the 3-month GLP toxicity study (mean total Cmax = 129 µM and total AUC24 = 2130 µM ∙ h).','\n',char(10)),'Explains selection of DNL343 200 mg daily using prior clinical safety, PK/PD, nonclinical efficacy, and toxicology safety-margin data.','["background"]',39,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.6.1','section','5.6.1','Dose Modification for an Individual Participant','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration > 5.6 Dosage Changes',replace('The DNL343 dose (200 mg QD) was selected given that it is considered to have the highest potential to demonstrate efficacy based on data from preclinical studies, clinical\nbiomarker engagement, and clinical safety (see Section 5.5). To adequately evaluate the efficacy of DNL343 participants should be maintained on the randomized treatment. For situations of substantial intolerability (moderate or severe adverse events [AEs], clinically significant abnormal laboratory test results, or other safety parameter, as judged by the investigator or medical monitor), the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the randomized dose within 28 days from the dose reduction or dose interruption. The blind will be maintained throughout the dose modification procedure.\nAll participants in the ATE will be dosed with DNL343 200 mg QD regardless of whether they had a dose reduction during the placebo-controlled period. In situations that may necessitate initiation of DNL343 at the reduced dose (100 mg) in the ATE, the investigator should discuss with the medical monitor before entry into the ATE. As in the placebo-controlled period, the site investigator, in consultation with the medical monitor, may withhold study drug (dose interruption), temporarily reduce the dose (administer one stick pack rather than two stick packs), or discontinue study drug for an individual participant. Investigators should consider resuming treatment at the 200 mg dose level if possible.','\n',char(10)),'Maintain assigned dosing when possible; consult the medical monitor for intolerance-related interruption, reduction, or discontinuation, preserve blinding, and normally start ATE at 200 mg daily.','["dosing", "safety", "extension_period"]',40,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.7','section','5.7','Participant Compliance','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration',replace('When participants are dosed at the site, they will receive study drug directly from the investigator or designee, under medical supervision. The date and time of each dose administered in the clinic will be recorded in the source documents and electronic case report form (eCRF). The dose of study drug and study participant identification will be confirmed at the time of dosing by a member of the study site staff other than the person administering the study drug.\nWhen participants self-administer study drug at home, compliance with study drug will be assessed at each study visit. Participants will be instructed to bring all their unused packets of study drug with them to all clinic visits. Compliance with home administration of study drug will be performed by the study staff by counting the number of unused packets and will be recorded.\nDeviation(s) from the prescribed dosage regimen should be recorded.','\n',char(10)),'Supervise clinic dosing with independent dose and identity verification, record clinic dose times in source and eCRF, count and record unused packets at visits, and document dosing deviations.','["dosing", "drug_supply", "assessments", "records_reporting"]',41,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.8','section','5.8','Overdose or Medication Administration Error','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration',replace('Certain safety events that occur in association with DNL343 may require reporting. These safety events include, but are not limited to, the following:\n1. Overdose of DNL343, where ‘overdose’ is defined as the accidental or intentional use of a drug in an amount higher than the dose being studied. No safety data related to overdosing of DNL343 are available.\n2. Suspected abuse/misuse of DNL343\n3. Inadvertent or accidental exposure to DNL343\n4. Medication error involving study drug (with or without participant exposure to DNL343)\nThese safety events should be reported to the Coordination Center whether they result in an AE/SAE or not. Safety events associated with an AE/SAE should also be reported in the electronic data capture. In the event of overdose, study staff should monitor the participant and provide supportive care as needed. The Site Investigator should also contact the Medical Monitor within 24 hours of the Site Investigator’s awareness.','\n',char(10)),'Report overdose, misuse, accidental exposure, and medication errors to the Coordination Center regardless of AEs; enter associated AEs/SAEs in EDC and monitor overdoses with medical-monitor contact within 24 hours of awareness.','["dosing", "safety", "records_reporting"]',42,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:5.9','section','5.9','Prohibited Medications','5 INVESTIGATIONAL PRODUCT > 5.4 Study Medication/Intervention, Administration, Escalation, and Duration',replace('Participants may not take any prescription or OTC medications (including herbal medicines such as St. John’s wort) that are strong CYP3A4/5 inducers within 2 weeks or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV Table 4).\nParticipants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 5 for common examples of sensitive substrates). Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increase based on the respective medication’s prescribing information and discretion of the investigator and/or medical monitor.\nParticipants may not take any prescription or OTC medications that are substrates for BCRP or OAT3 and have a narrow therapeutic index within 7 days or 5 half-lives (whichever is longer) of the first dose of study drug, and must not anticipate needing to take any of these medications during the study treatment period (see Appendix IV, Table 6 for common examples of sensitive substrates).\nThe medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates and BCRP and OAT3 substrates (See Appendix IV, Table 5 and 6 for common examples) would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication.\nFor all excluded therapies described in this section, rescreening of participants is allowed after the defined window has passed.','\n',char(10)),'Apply specified washouts and treatment-period prohibitions for strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and narrow-index CYP3A4/5, BCRP, or OAT3 substrates, and monitor permitted wide-index CYP3A4/5 substrates for reduced efficacy.','["eligibility", "concomitant_medications", "safety"]',43,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6','section','6','REGIMEN SCHEDULE','',replace('In addition to procedures in the Master Protocol, the following DNL343-specific procedures will be conducted during the study:\n1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-S, PGI-C\n5. CGI-C\n6. PK blood collection\n12. -lead ECG (to be done at all in-clinic visits)\n8. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood adrenocorticotropic hormone [ACTH], cortisol, free thyroxine [fT4]) (Baseline and Week 24 Visits)\n9. Urine Pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n11. Optional CSF collection\nUnscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment.\nParticipants may be required to reconsent to the regimen if new procedures or information are added in the future. Should a participant need to reconsent, this should occur during the participant’s next in-person visit. If the participant’s next in-clinic visit is conducted remotely, reconsent may also be completed remotely using the following procedures:\na) The site staff sends copy of the informed consent form to the participant.\nb) The participant reads through the consent form but does not sign.\nc) The Site Investigator, or other study staff member approved and delegated to obtain informed consent, contacts the participant and reviews the informed consent form with the participant.\nd) The participant signs the informed consent form and returns the original signed consent form back to the site.\ne) Once received at the site, the individual who consented the participant signs the informed consent form.','\n',char(10)),'Conduct regimen-specific assessments and sample collections, obtain additional PK samples for specified safety events, and arrange required reconsent at the next in-person visit or through the prescribed remote process.','["visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "consent"]',46,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.1','section','6.1','Baseline Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place on Day 0. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-S\n12. -lead ECG\n6. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4)\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (biomarkers only; no CSF PK sample collected at baseline)\n▪ CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visit. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed.\n10. Dispense study drug\n11. Administer first dose of study drug in clinic after all Baseline procedures have been completed\n12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Complete Day 0 baseline assessments, safety and reproductive checks before the first clinic dose; collect optional baseline CSF within the preceding week after eligibility confirmation, dispense drug, and remind participants to return unused packets.','["visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "eligibility", "dosing", "drug_supply"]',48,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10','section','6.10','Active Treatment Extension','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Following the placebo-controlled period, participants will continue in the ATE as outlined in the SOA.\nThe ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE (ie, 102 weeks total from the Baseline visit). The Industry Partner may consider extending or discontinuing the ATE period depending on the results from the primary analysis.\nUnscheduled visits for safety assessments that are deemed medically appropriate and necessary by the Investigator may be performed at any time, independent of scheduled in-clinic visits. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or any severe TEAE that requires an unscheduled visit for assessment.','\n',char(10)),'Continue ATE per the SOA until all active participants have at least 52 ATE weeks, with an individual maximum of 78 weeks unless the period is changed; allow medically necessary unscheduled safety visits and collect additional PK as needed for specified treatment-emergent adverse events.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "safety", "assessments", "samples", "dosing"]',57,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.1','section','6.10.1','Week 2 ATE Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place via telephone 14 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Review concomitant medications\n2. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n3. Perform drug compliance check-in\n4. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, containers, to the next visit\n5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the ATE Week 2 telephone visit 14 ± 3 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 to review medications and contraception, assess/document adverse events including Key Study Events, check compliance, and remind participants to return drug and containers.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply", "contraception_pregnancy"]',59,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.10','section','6.10.10','Week 52 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 52 ATE Visit will take place in-person 364 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n14. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n16. ROADS\n17. PGI-C\n18. PGI-S\n19. CGI-C\n20. Perform study drug compliance','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 52 visit 364 ± 14 days after placebo-controlled Week 24, holding drug until procedures finish; complete specified clinical/outcome assessments, safety and blood/urine biomarker sampling, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE documentation, drug dispensing, and compliance checks.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "dosing", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "drug_supply"]',68,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.11','section','6.10.11','Visits after Week 52','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Visits that occur after 52 weeks of enrollment in the ATE will occur every 13 weeks (91 days ± 14 days) until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks in the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. Visits after the 52-week Visit will take place inperson. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'After ATE Week 52, conduct in-person visits every 91 ± 14 days up to the 78-week maximum, completing listed assessments, safety and biomarker samples, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE documentation, drug dispensing, compliance checks, and drug-return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply"]',69,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.12','section','6.10.12','Follow-Up Safety Call','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose of study drug. Participants will complete this visit who:\n1. Have their last dose of study drug prior to completing the ATE (those who discontinue early)\n2. Complete the ATE\nThe following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'For participants who discontinue drug before completing ATE or complete ATE, conduct a safety call 28 ± 3 days after their last dose to assess/document adverse events and review participant/partner contraception.','["extension_period", "early_termination", "visit_schedule", "safety", "records_reporting", "contraception_pregnancy"]',70,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.13','section','6.10.13','Process for Early Terminations','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the ATE period before reaching Week 52, all assessments that are collected at the ATE Week 52 in-clinic visit should\nbe conducted. For early discontinuations after Week 52 in ATE, assessments for ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S, and CGI-C are not required. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within 28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call.','\n',char(10)),'After ATE withdrawal or early termination, request prompt in-person ET assessments using the Week 52 set, omitting ALSAQ-40, ROADS, PGI-C, PGI-S and CGI-C after Week 52; conduct the safety call about 28 days after last dose, combining it with ET only when ET occurs at 28 ± 3 days.','["extension_period", "early_termination", "consent", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "safety", "samples", "contraception_pregnancy"]',71,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.2','section','6.10.2','Week 4 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place in-person 28 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n5. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n7. Review concomitant medications\n8. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n9. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n11. Perform study drug compliance\n12. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 4 visit 28 ± 7 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with vital signs, SVC, ALSFRS-R, safety blood tests, pregnancy/contraception checks, medication review, AE/Key Study Event documentation, C-SSRS, ECG, compliance checks, and drug-return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply"]',60,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.3','section','6.10.3','Week 8 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place in-person at 56 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. -lead ECG\n12. Dispense study drug to participant\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 8 visit 56 ± 7 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with specified clinical/outcome assessments, safety blood tests, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE/Key Study Event documentation, drug dispensing, compliance checks, and drug-return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply"]',61,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.4','section','6.10.4','Week 12 ATE Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place via telephone at 84 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the ATE Week 12 telephone visit 84 ± 3 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with ALSFRS-R, medication and contraception reviews, AE/Key Study Event assessment and documentation, compliance check-in, and a drug-return reminder.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply", "contraception_pregnancy"]',62,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.5','section','6.10.5','Week 16 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThis visit will take place in-person at 112 ± 7 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Collect urine sample for biomarker analyses\n12. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n14. Dispense study drug to participant\n15. Perform study drug compliance\n16. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug, packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 16 visit 112 ± 7 days after placebo-controlled Week 24, holding drug until procedures finish; perform specified assessments, safety and blood/urine biomarker sampling, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE documentation, drug dispensing, compliance checks, and return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "dosing", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "drug_supply"]',63,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.6','section','6.10.6','Week 20 ATE Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place via telephone at 140 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebocontrolled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit\n6. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the ATE Week 20 telephone visit 140 ± 3 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with ALSFRS-R, medication and contraception reviews, AE/Key Study Event assessment and documentation, compliance check-in, and a drug-return reminder.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply", "contraception_pregnancy"]',64,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.7','section','6.10.7','Week 24 ATE Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place via telephone at 168 ± 3 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n2. Review concomitant medications\n3. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n4. Perform drug compliance check-in\n5. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit • Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the ATE Week 24 telephone visit 168 ± 3 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with ALSFRS-R, medication and contraception reviews, AE/Key Study Event assessment and documentation, compliance check-in, and a drug-return reminder.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply", "contraception_pregnancy"]',65,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.8','section','6.10.8','Week 28 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants should be instructed to hold study drug on the day of the study visit. study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThe Week 28 ATE visit will take place in-person 196 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs, including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. ALSAQ-40\n5. CNS-BFS\n6. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n7. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n8. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n9. Review concomitant medications\n10. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of the Master Protocol)\n11. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n12. Collect urine sample biomarker analyses\n13. Collect blood sample for biomarker analyses\n12. -lead ECG\n15. ROADS\n16. PGI-C\n17. PGI-S\n18. CGI-C\n19. Dispense study drug to participant\n20. Perform study drug compliance\n21. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 28 visit 196 ± 14 days after placebo-controlled Week 24, holding drug until procedures finish; complete specified clinical/outcome assessments, safety and blood/urine biomarker sampling, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE documentation, dispensing, compliance checks, and return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "dosing", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "drug_supply"]',66,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.10.9','section','6.10.9','Week 40 ATE Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('The Week 40 ATE Visit will take place in-person 280 ± 14 days after the Week 24 Visit of the placebo-controlled portion of the trial. The following procedures will be performed:\n1. Collect vital signs including weight\n2. Perform SVC\n3. Administer ALSFRS-R questionnaire\n4. CNS-BFS\n5. Collect blood samples for clinical safety laboratory tests\n6. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n7. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n8. Review concomitant medications\n9. Assess and document AEs, including Key Study Events (see Section 10.3 of Master Protocol)\n10. Administer the C-SSRS Since Last Visit questionnaire\n11. Dispense study drug to participant\n12. -lead ECG\n13. Perform study drug compliance\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the in-person ATE Week 40 visit 280 ± 14 days after placebo-controlled Week 24 with specified clinical/outcome assessments, safety blood tests, pregnancy/contraception and medication reviews, AE/Key Study Event documentation, drug dispensing, compliance checks, and drug-return reminders.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "samples", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy", "concomitant_medications", "records_reporting", "dosing", "drug_supply"]',67,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.2','section','6.2','Week 2 Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place 14 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the Week 2 telephone visit 14 ± 3 days after Baseline to check drug compliance, remind participants to return unused drug, and review concomitant medications, adverse events, and participant/partner contraception.','["visit_schedule", "dosing", "drug_supply", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy"]',49,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.3','section','6.3','Week 4 and 8 Visits','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('The Week 4 visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 4 visit the participant should take their daily dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nIf the Week 4 Visit cannot occur in the morning, the participant should take their dose at home at their usual dosing time rather than receiving the dose in-clinic. Two post dose PK blood samples would be obtained if dosing has already occurred prior to arriving at the clinic. The two PK collections should occur at least one hour apart without a dose administered in between and a minor protocol deviation recorded.\nFor the Week 8 Visit, the participant may take their daily dose at home or in clinic, depending on the time of the visit and one postdose PK blood sample would be obtained.\nThese visits will take place on Days 28 ± 7 and 56 ± 7 days, respectively. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. CNS-BFS [Week 8 only]\n12. -lead ECG\n3. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 4, postdose only on Week 8) o For visits with a predose PK sample collection, participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n4. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n5. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n6. Dispense study drug\n7. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct Week 4/8 visits on Days 28/56 ± 7 with specified assessments, ECG, pregnancy/contraception checks, drug dispensing and return reminders; follow dose/PK timing, using two postdose samples at least one hour apart and recording a minor deviation if Week 4 morning in-clinic dosing is not possible.','["visit_schedule", "dosing", "assessments", "safety", "samples", "contraception_pregnancy", "drug_supply", "records_reporting"]',50,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.4','section','6.4','Week 12 Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place 84 ± 3 days after the Baseline visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of Concomitant Medications and Adverse Events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the Week 12 telephone visit 84 ± 3 days after Baseline to check drug compliance, remind participants to return unused drug, and review concomitant medications, adverse events, and participant/partner contraception.','["visit_schedule", "dosing", "drug_supply", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy"]',51,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.5','section','6.5','Week 16 Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place on Day 112 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. CNS-BFS\n2. ALSAQ-40\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (postdose only on Week 16) o Postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n9. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n10. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n11. Dispense study drug\n12. Remind participant to bring in study drug, including unused study drug packets, to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the Week 16 visit on Day 112 ± 7 with specified outcome assessments, ECG, a PK sample 1–8 hours postdose, pregnancy testing and contraception review, study-drug dispensing, and a drug-return reminder.','["visit_schedule", "assessments", "safety", "samples", "contraception_pregnancy", "drug_supply"]',52,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.6','section','6.6','Week 20 Telephone Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('This visit will take place 140 ± 3 days after the Baseline Visit via telephone. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. Perform drug compliance check-in (remind of the importance of compliance)\n2. Remind participant to bring any unused study drug to the next visit\n3. Review of concomitant medications and adverse events\n4. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct the Week 20 telephone visit 140 ± 3 days after Baseline to check drug compliance, remind participants to return unused drug, and review concomitant medications, adverse events, and participant/partner contraception.','["visit_schedule", "dosing", "drug_supply", "concomitant_medications", "safety", "contraception_pregnancy"]',53,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.7','section','6.7','Week 24 Visit or Early Termination Visit','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('The Week 24 Visit should be scheduled in the morning to accommodate the pre/post dose PK collection. On the day of the Week 24 Visit, the participant should not take any dose from the placebo-controlled period and take their ATE dose in-clinic. Study drug should not be taken until after study visit procedures are complete.\nThis visit will take place on Day 168 ± 7 days. The following procedures will be performed for the regimen schedule:\n1. ALSAQ-40\n2. CNS-BFS\n3. ROADS\n4. PGI-C\n5. PGI-S\n6. CGI-C\n12. -lead ECG\n8. PK blood collection (predose and postdose on Week 24) o For the predose PK sample collection, participants should take their ATE dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual.\na) The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the last dose.\nb) The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose.\n9. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, TSH, fT4) (for participants continuing in the ATE)\n10. Urine pregnancy testing for women of childbearing potential\n11. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner\n12. Optional lumbar puncture for CSF collection (collect both biomarker and PK samples)\na) CSF collection may occur on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled at the Week 24 Visit, provided CSF collection occurs within the allowed window for the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, optional CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose from the ATE period. The date of the other assessments (ie, not the date of CSF collection) will be considered the date of the Week 24 Visit. CSF collection at Week 24 will only be performed on participants with baseline CSF collection.\nb) CSF collection for the ET visit will be performed only if the participant has consented to the lumbar puncture with baseline CSF collection and the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.\n13. Dispense study drug\n14. Remind participant to bring study drug, including unused study drug packets to the next visit','\n',char(10)),'Schedule Week 24/ET assessments at Day 168 ± 7, with Week 24 morning ATE dosing after procedures and no placebo-period dose; perform specified outcomes, ECG, timed PK, pregnancy/contraception checks, ATE endocrine safety labs, and optional CSF within the visit window before the first ATE dose.','["visit_schedule", "early_termination", "extension_period", "dosing", "assessments", "safety", "samples", "contraception_pregnancy"]',54,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.8','section','6.8','Follow-Up Safety Call','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call 28 + 3 days after their last dose of study drug.\nParticipants who continue into ATE will not complete the follow up safety call during placebocontrolled phase.\nThe following procedures will be performed:\n1. Assess and document AEs\n2. Review contraceptive methods being used by participant and partner','\n',char(10)),'Conduct a safety call 28 + 3 days after the last study-drug dose to assess and document adverse events and review contraception; omit the placebo-controlled-phase call for participants continuing into ATE.','["visit_schedule", "safety", "records_reporting", "contraception_pregnancy", "extension_period"]',55,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:6.9','section','6.9','Process for Early Terminations','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE > Modifications to Regimen Schedule',replace('Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate from the study and do not complete the protocol will be asked to be seen for an in-person ET Visit and complete a Follow-Up Safety Call.\nThe in-person ET Visit should be scheduled as soon as possible after a participant early terminates. If the participant early terminates or withdraws consent during the placebo-controlled portion of the Regimen, all assessments that are collected at the Week 24 in-clinic visit should be conducted. The Follow-Up Safety Call should be completed approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug.\nIf the ET Visit occurs approximately 28±3 days after the last dose of study drug, the information for the Follow-Up Safety Call can be collected during the ET visit, and a separate Follow-Up Safety Call does not need to be completed. If the in-person ET Visit does not occur within\n28±3 days of the last dose of study drug, the Follow-Up Safety Call should occur approximately 28 days after the last dose of study drug and the ET Visit will be completed after the Follow-Up Safety Call.\nIf a participant has to discontinue study drug, but will complete the protocol, an in-person ET Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call is not necessary. Every effort should be made to retain participants in the study, including the ATE period, regardless of whether they have discontinued study drug.\nFor the ET Visit, optional CSF collection will be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.','\n',char(10)),'After withdrawal or early termination, request prompt in-person Week 24 assessments and a safety call about 28 days after last dose, combining them if ET occurs at 28 ± 3 days; no ET visit/call is needed for drug discontinuation alone, retain participants where possible, and limit optional ET CSF to those with Baseline CSF at Week 12 or later.','["early_termination", "consent", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "safety", "samples", "dosing", "extension_period"]',56,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7','section','7','OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS','','','Introduces the protocol''s outcome measures and assessment procedures.','["assessments"]',72,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.1','section','7.1','Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Assessment Questionnaire-40 (ALSAQ-40)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS',replace('The ALSAQ-40 is a patient self-report health status patient-reported outcome. The ALSAQ-40 consists of forty questions that are specifically used to measure the subjective well-being of patients with ALS and motor neuron disease.\nParticipants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form, and should not provide any assistance with determining answers.','\n',char(10)),'Administer the 40-question ALSAQ-40 as participant self-report; caregivers or staff may transcribe participants'' responses but must not help determine answers.','["assessments"]',73,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.2','section','7.2','Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar Function Scale (CNS-BFS)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS',replace('The CNS-BFS is a patient self-report scale that has been developed for use as an endpoint in clinical trials and as a clinical measure for evaluating and following ALS patients. The CNS-BFS consists of three domains (swallowing, speech, and salivation), which are assessed with a 21-question, self-report questionnaire.\nParticipants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed, but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form, and should not provide any assistance with determining answers.','\n',char(10)),'Administer the 21-question CNS-BFS covering swallowing, speech and salivation as participant self-report; caregivers or staff may transcribe responses but must not help determine answers.','["assessments"]',74,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.3','section','7.3','Rasch-built Overall Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Disability Scale (ROADS)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS',replace('The ROADS is a PRO of function (Fournier et al 2020). It is a 28-question, validated, linear, self-report questionnaire created with Rasch modeling. Rasch modeling uses statistical principles to enhance scale validity (Vanhoutte et al 2015). Prior to analysis, ROADS raw scores are converted to normed whole numbers. The scale and conversion table are publicly available (https://med.emory.edu/departments/neurology/programs_centers/emory_als_center/_documents/ roads.pdf).\nParticipants will be handed the questionnaire and asked to write their answers themselves. Caregivers or study staff can also help, if needed but should only assist in transferring the participant’s responses to the form and should not provide any assistance with determining answers.','\n',char(10)),'Administer the 28-question ROADS as participant self-report, allowing transcription assistance but no help determining answers; raw scores are converted to normed whole numbers before analysis.','["assessments"]',75,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.4','section','7.4','The Patient Global Impression of Change (PGI-C)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS','PGI-C is a PRO that reflects the respondent’s belief about the efficacy of treatment. PGI-C is a 7-point scale that captures an individual’s self-rating rating of overall improvement. Respondents rate their change relative to baseline as “very much improved,” “much improved,” “minimally improved,” “no change,” “minimally worse,” “much worse,” or “very much worse” (Hurst and Bolton 2004).','Describes the PGI-C patient-reported assessment of overall change from baseline on a seven-point improvement-to-worsening scale.','["assessments"]',76,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.5','section','7.5','The Patient Global Impression of Severity (PGI-S)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS','PGI-S is a PRO scale that may be used to rate the severity of a specific condition. It is a simple, direct, easy to use 1-item questionnaire that asks an individual patient to rate the severity of a specific condition (single-state scales) at a given point in time. The PGI-S item asks the respondent to best describe the level of severity of their symptoms now on a 4-point scale scored as: “normal” (1), “mild” (2), “moderate” (3), or “severe” (4) (Viktrup et al 2012).','Describes the PGI-S patient-reported assessment of current symptom severity on a four-point normal-to-severe scale.','["assessments"]',77,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.6','section','7.6','Clinician Global Impression of Change (CGI-C)','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS','The CGI-C is a stand-alone assessment of the clinician''s view of the patient''s global functioning prior to and after initiating a study medication (Busnar et al 2007). CGI-C asks the clinician one question, which is rated on a seven-point scale: “Compared to the patient’s condition at admission the project (prior to study drug initiation), this patient’s condition is 1=very much improved; 2=much improved; 3=minimally improved; 4=no change from baseline; 5=minimally worse; 6=much worse; 7=very much worse since the initiation of study drug treatment”.','Describes the CGI-C clinician assessment of change from the participant’s pre-treatment condition on a seven-point scale.','["assessments"]',78,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:7.7','section','7.7','Vital Status Determination','7 OUTCOME MEASURES AND ASSESSMENTS',replace('Vital status, including determination of dates of death and initiation of PAV or dates last known alive and last known PAV-free, will be recorded by site study staff for each randomized participant throughout the placebo-controlled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated in the SOA) and at the conclusion of the ATE participation. At approximately the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the placebo-controlled portion of the regimen, a second vital status check will be completed by site study staff for each randomized participant. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will contact all randomized participants or their caregivers to assess vital status.\nWe may also ascertain vital status at later time points. An outside vendor will be used to ascertain death or date of last known alive for all randomized participants by using publicly available data sources. When prompted by the Coordination Center, sites will provide demographic information (e.g., participant name, date of birth, last known address) to the vendor using a secure method.','\n',char(10)),'Sites must record death/PAV or last-known-alive/PAV-free dates during placebo-controlled follow-up and at ATE conclusion, repeat vital-status checks near LPLV, and contact participants/caregivers or securely provide vendor demographics when prompted.','["assessments", "visit_schedule", "extension_period", "records_reporting"]',79,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:8','section','8','BIOFLUID COLLECTION','','Biofluid samples (blood, urine, and CSF) that will be used for biomarker assessments for Regimen G are captured in the Master Protocol. It should be noted that the CSF biofluid collection is optional and collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24 (rather than Baseline and Week 16 as noted in the Master Protocol). Additional biofluid samples (blood) will be collected for Regimen G for evaluation of plasma PK at scheduled timepoints described in the SOA.','Collect biomarker biofluids per the Master Protocol and plasma PK blood per the SOA; optional CSF collection for Regimen G occurs at Baseline and Week 24, not Week 16.','["samples", "assessments", "visit_schedule"]',80,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:9','section','9','DNL343-SPECIFIC STATISTICAL CONSIDERATIONS','','','Introduces DNL343-specific statistical considerations.','["statistics"]',81,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:9.1','section','9.1','Deviations from the Default Master Protocol Trial Design','9 DNL343-SPECIFIC STATISTICAL CONSIDERATIONS','The statistical design for Regimen G will be in accordance with the statistical design described in Appendix I of the Master SAP, unless otherwise noted.','States that Regimen G follows the statistical design in Master SAP Appendix I unless otherwise specified.','["statistics"]',82,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:9.2','section','9.2','Regimen Specific Operating Characteristics','9 DNL343-SPECIFIC STATISTICAL CONSIDERATIONS','Clinical trial simulation was used to quantify operating characteristics for Regimen G (refer to the Regimen G statistical analysis plan for further details).','Describes use of clinical trial simulation to quantify Regimen G operating characteristics, with details in its statistical analysis plan.','["statistics"]',83,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:9.3','section','9.3','Sharing of Controls from other Regimens','9 DNL343-SPECIFIC STATISTICAL CONSIDERATIONS','The primary or supportive analyses of Regimen G will include sharing of controls from other regimens. This is justified by the minor differences in inclusion/exclusion criteria of the RSAs, such that there are no expected systematic differences in the primary, secondary endpoints, or safety outcomes between the controls across regimens.','Describes and justifies sharing controls from other regimens in primary or supportive Regimen G analyses.','["statistics"]',84,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:front','section','','Front matter','',replace('HEALEY ALS Platform Trial - Regimen G for DNL343\nNCT05842941\nDocument Date: 18 DEC 2023\nRefer to NCT04297683 for the HEALEY ALS Platform\nTrial Master Protocol document\nREGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX G','\n',char(10)),'Identifies Regimen G for DNL343, the document date and trial registration, and the associated Master Protocol.','["admin"]',0,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s001','section','','FOR DNL343','',replace('Regimen-Specific Appendix Date: 12/18/2023\nVersion Number: 2.0\nTABLE OF CONTENTS\nPage\n2.0,\nTABLE OF CONTENTS (CONT’D)\nPage\nTABLE OF CONTENTS (CONT’D)\nPage\nContraceptive Guidance for Male Participants with Female Partners of\nChildbearing Potential………………………………………………………… 65\nList of Tables','\n',char(10)),'Provides the appendix version, date, table of contents, and list of tables.','["admin"]',1,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s002','section','','SIGNATURE PAGE','',replace('I have read the attached Regimen-Specific Appendix (RSA) entitled, “REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX G FOR DNL343” dated December 18, 2023 (Version 2.0) and agree to abide by all described RSA procedures. I agree to comply with the International Conference on\nHarmonisation Tripartite Guideline on Good Clinical Practice, applicable FDA regulations and guidelines identified in 21 CFR Parts 11, 50, 54, and 312, central Institutional Review Board (IRB) guidelines and policies, and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA).\nBy signing the RSA, I agree to keep all information provided in strict confidence and to request the same from my staff. Study documents will be stored appropriately to ensure their confidentiality. I will not disclose such information to others without authorization, except to the extent necessary to conduct the study.\nSite Name:\nSite Investigator:\nSigned: Date:','\n',char(10)),'Requires the investigator to agree to protocol and regulatory compliance, protect confidential information, and store study documents confidentially.','["records_reporting"]',2,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s003','section','','LIST OF ABBREVIATIONS','',replace('Table: Abbreviation Definition\n- ACTH adrenocorticotropic hormone AE adverse event AESI adverse event of special interest ALS amyotrophic lateral sclerosis ALSAQ-40 Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Assessment Questionnaire-40 ALSFRS-R Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Functional Rating Scale-Revised ATF4/ATF4 activating transcription factor 4 ATE active treatment extension AUC∞ area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to infinity AUC24 area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to 24 hours BCRP breast cancer resistance protein CAFS Combined Assessment of Function and Survival CGI-C Clinician Global Impression of Change CHAC1 ChaC glutathione–specific gamma-glutamylcyclotransferase 1 Cmax maximum concentration CNS central nervous system CNS-BFS Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar Function Scale CSF cerebrospinal fluid C-SSRS Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale CYP cytochrome P450 EC75 concentration producing 75% of maximum effect EC90 concentration producing 90% of maximum effect ECG electrocardiogram eCRF electronic case report form eIF2B eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B ET early termination fT4 free thyroxine G-tube gastrostomy tube GLP Good Laboratory Practice IB Investigator’s Brochure ISR integrated stress response J-tube gastrostomy tube LOAEL lowest-observed-adverse-effect level LPLV last participant, last visit MOP Manual of Procedures Nf-L neurofilament light chain NOAEL no-observed-adverse-effect level OAT organic anion transporter\n- OTC over-the-counter PAV permanent assisted ventilation PBMC peripheral blood mononuclear cell PD pharmacodynamic(s) p-eIF2 phosphorylated eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2a PGI-C Patient Global Impression of Change PGI-S Patient Global Impression of Severity PK pharmacokinetic(s) PRO patient reported outcome QD once daily ROADS Rasch-built Overall Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Disability Scale RSA Regimen-Specific Appendix SAE serious adverse event SOA Schedule of Activities SVC slow vital capacity TEAE treatment-emergent adverse event TSH thyroid-stimulating hormone WOCBP women of childbearing potential','\n',char(10)),'Defines abbreviations used throughout the regimen-specific appendix.','["admin"]',3,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s004','section','','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY','',replace('Regimen-Specific Appendix G For DNL343\nRationale and Regimen-Specific Appendix Design\nDNL343 is a central nervous system (CNS)–penetrant eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (eIF2B) small molecule activator that is being developed by Denali Therapeutics Inc. (Denali) as a potential treatment for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). The effect of DNL343 treatment is the inhibition of the integrated stress response (ISR) pathway. Inhibition of the ISR is a novel potential therapeutic opportunity for treatment of ALS.\nRegimen G of the HEALEY ALS Platform Trial will evaluate the efficacy, safety, pharmacokinetics (PK), and pharmacodynamics (PD) of DNL343 200 mg once daily (QD) to participants with ALS. The principal aim of Regimen G is to investigate the efficacy of DNL343 in slowing clinical progression of disease as compared to placebo; and the secondary aim is to investigate the safety and tolerability of DNL343. The study will also explore the PK and PD of DNL343 to determine whether DNL343 affects biomarkers related to ISR, neurodegeneration, and ALS pathophysiology at exposures predicted to provide clinical efficacy. Following completion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants in Regimen G will enter an active treatment extension (ATE) where all participants will receive DNL343 200 mg QD for up to an additional 78 weeks.','\n',char(10)),'Describes DNL343''s rationale and efficacy, safety, PK, and PD evaluation, with 200 mg daily treatment and an active extension after the 24-week controlled period.','["dosing", "assessments", "safety", "visit_schedule", "extension_period"]',4,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s005','section','','Allocation to Treatment Regimens','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY',replace('Participants must first be screened under the Master Protocol and before they are randomly assigned to a regimen.\nAssignment to the regimen will stop if pre-defined criteria for futility for the regimen are met or after the target number of randomized participants has been reached.','\n',char(10)),'Requires Master Protocol screening before regimen assignment and stopping assignments when predefined futility criteria or the target enrollment are reached.','["eligibility"]',5,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s006','section','','Number of Planned Participants and Treatment Groups','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY',replace('The number of planned participants for Regimen G is approximately 240.\nThere are two treatment groups for Regimen G: Active and Placebo. Participants will be randomized in a 3:1 ratio to active treatment or placebo (i.e., approximately 180 active:60 placebo).\nPlanned Number of Sites\nResearch participants will be enrolled from up to 80 centers in the United States (US).','\n',char(10)),'Describes approximately 240 participants, a planned 3:1 active-to-placebo allocation, and enrollment at up to 80 US centers.','["statistics"]',6,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s007','section','','Treatment Duration','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY','The planned duration of the placebo-controlled treatment period of the study is 24 weeks. The duration of the ATE period is planned for a minimum of 52 weeks (i.e., 76 weeks total treatment duration). The ATE will continue until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks of ATE (i.e., 102 weeks total from Baseline visit).','Sets a 24-week controlled treatment period followed by an extension lasting at least 52 weeks for active participants and no more than 78 weeks per participant.','["dosing", "visit_schedule", "extension_period"]',7,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s008','section','','Follow-up Duration','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY','At the conclusion of the 24-week placebo-controlled treatment period, all participants will continue in the ATE period and receive DNL343. Once a participant completes the final visit in the ATE and discontinues study drug, a 28-day follow-up safety phone call will be scheduled. Any participant who stops study drug early will be encouraged to remain in the study and follow the study protocol under the intent-to-treat principle. If the participant discontinues study medication and decides not to continue follow-up under the intent-to-treat principle, an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Phone Call should be completed.','Requires extension treatment and a safety call 28 days after drug cessation; encourages continued follow-up after early drug stopping or an ET visit and safety call if follow-up is declined.','["dosing", "extension_period", "visit_schedule", "safety", "early_termination"]',8,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s009','section','','Total Planned Trial Duration','REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX SUMMARY','Including the placebo-controlled treatment and the ATE periods, the total planned amount of time for a participant in Regimen G is a minimum of approximately 86 weeks and a maximum of approximately 112 weeks. This duration assumes a 6-week screening window, 24-week placebocontrolled treatment period, 52- to 78-week ATE period, and 4-week safety follow-up period.','Describes approximately 86–112 weeks of participation, including six-week screening, 24-week controlled treatment, 52–78-week extension, and four-week safety follow-up.','["visit_schedule", "extension_period"]',9,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s010','section','','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD','',replace('As per the Schedule of Activities (SOA) below, visits must occur every 4 weeks and will be clinic-, phone-, or telemedicine based, as applicable. There is a maximum 24-week duration of placebo-controlled treatment for a regimen.\nSchedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD). Columns: Master Protocol Screening [1] / Clinic / –42 to –1 Days [13] | Regimen Specific Screening [1] / Clinic / –41 to 0 Day [13] | Baseline [1] / Clinic / Day 0 | Week 2 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [12] / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [12] / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [12] / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 or ET Visit [19] / Clinic / Day 168 ± 7 | Follow-Up Safety Call 10 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days\n- Written Informed Consent [2] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1]\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1] [3]\n- ALS & Medical History Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Demographics Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Physical Examination Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Neurological Exam Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Vital Signs [4] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Muscle Strength Assessment Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-C 17 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- PGI-S 17 Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- CGI-C 18 Regimen: done at Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- 12-Lead ECG Regimen: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Week 16 [12]\n- Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (for all participants prior to ATE)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] Regimen: done at Week 4 [12] (pre, post), Week 8 [12] (post), Week 16 [12] (post), Week 24 or ET Visit [19] (pre, post)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- DNA Collection [7](optional) Master: done at Baseline [1]\n- CSF Collection [16] (optional) Regimen: done at Baseline [1], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Adverse Event Review [6] Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1], Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Baseline [1], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Assignment to the Regimen Master: done at Master Protocol Screening [1]\n- Randomization within the Regimen Master: done at Baseline [1]\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Baseline [1] [8], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 16 [12], Week 24 or ET Visit [19] [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [20], Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12 [20], Week 16 [12], Week 20 [20], Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Review contraception method [21] Regimen: done at Regimen Specific Screening [1], Baseline [1], Week 2, Week 4 [12], Week 8 [12], Week 12, Week 16 [12], Week 20, Week 24 or ET Visit [19], Follow-Up Safety Call 10\n- Exit Questionnaire Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\n- Vital Status Determination [11] Master: done at Week 24 or ET Visit [19]\nAbbreviations: ACTH, adrenocorticotropic hormone; ALS, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis; ALSAQ-40, Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Assessment Questionnaire-40; ALSFRS-R, Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Functional Rating Scale-Revised; ATE, active treatment extension; CBC, complete blood cell count; CGI-C, Clinician Global Impression of Change; CNS, central nervous system; CNS-BFS, Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar Function Scale; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; ECG, electrocardiogram; ET, early termination; fT4, free thyroxine; ICF, informed consent form; INR, international normalized ratio; PK, pharmacokinetic; ROADS, Rasch-built Overall Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Disability Scale; PAV, permanent assisted ventilation; PGI-C, Patient Global Impression of Change; PGI-S, Patient Global Impression of Severity; PK, pharmacokinetic; PT, prothrombin time; PTT, partial thromboplastin time; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event; TSH, thyroid-stimulating hormone.\n1. Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible.\n2. During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF.\n3. At the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit, participants will have regimen-specific eligibility criteria assessed.\n4. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n5. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n6. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n7. The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable.\n8. Administer the first dose of study drug only after the Baseline Visit procedures are completed.\n9. Study drug will only be dispensed at this visit if the participant continues in the ATE.\n10. Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n11. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol.\n12. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n13. Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0).\n14. Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments.\n15. For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment.\n16. CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.\n17. PGI-C and PGI-S should be completed by the participant prior to other clinical assessments scheduled during the visit whenever possible.\n18. CGI-C should be completed after all clinical assessments are completed by the investigator whenever possible.\n19. The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period.\n20. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses.\n[21]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'Requires controlled-period visits and procedures within specified windows, including screening consent and eligibility checks, clinical assessments, an ET visit when applicable, and post-dose safety follow-up.','["visit_schedule", "consent", "eligibility", "assessments", "safety", "early_termination"]',10,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s011','section','','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION','',replace('Schedule table (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION). Columns: Week 2 / Week 26 / Phone / Day 14 ± 3 | Week 4 [7] / Week 28 / Clinic / Day 28 ± 7 | Week 8 [7] / Week 32 / Clinic / Day 56 ± 7 | Week 12 / Week 36 / Phone / Day 84 ± 3 | Week 16 [7] / Week 40 / Clinic / Day 112 ± 7 | Week 20 / Week 44 / Phone / Day 140 ± 3 | Week 24 / Week 48 / Phone / Day168 ± 3 | Week 28 [7] / Week 52 / Clinic / Day 196 ± 14 | Week 40 [7] / Week 64 / Clinic / Day 280 ± 14 | Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 / Week 76 / Clinic / Day 364 ± 14 | All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] / Week 89 and Week 102 / Clinic / 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks | Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6 / Phone / 28 days after last dose ± 3 days\n- Vital Signs [1] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- 12-lead ECG Regimen: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Slow Vital Capacity Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSFRS-R Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ALSAQ-40 Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CNS-BFS Regimen: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- ROADS Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- PGI-S Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- CGI-C Regimen: done at Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [11]\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Blood Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Biomarker Urine Collection Master: done at Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Concomitant Medication Review Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Adverse Event Review [3] Master: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale Master: done at Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug Master: done at Week 8 [7], Week 16 [7], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8 [9], All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7] [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance Master: done at Week 2 [12], Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12 [12], Week 16 [7], Week 20 [12], Week 24 [12], Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\n- Review contraception method [13] Regimen: done at Week 2, Week 4 [7], Week 8 [7], Week 12, Week 16 [7], Week 20, Week 24, Week 28 [7], Week 40 [7], Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7], Follow-Up Safety Call 4,6\n- Vital Status Determination [10] Regimen: done at Week 52 or ET Visit 6,8, All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) [7]\nAbbreviations: ALSAQ-40, Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Assessment Questionnaire-40; ALSFRS-R, Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Functional Rating Scale-Revised; ATE, active treatment extension; CBC, complete blood cell count; CGI-C, Clinician Global Impression of Change; CNS, central nervous system; CNS-BFS, Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar Function Scale; ECG, electrocardiogram; ET, early termination; PAV, permanent assisted ventilation; PGI-C, Patient Global Impression of Change; PGI-S, Patient Global Impression of Severity; PK, pharmacokinetic; ROADS, Rasch-built Overall Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Disability Scale; RSA, Regimen-Specific Appendix; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event.\n1. Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n2. Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n3. Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained.\n4. All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n5. The duration of the ATE is a minimum of 52 weeks with a maximum of 78 weeks per participant and will be completed when all active participants have completed the 52-week Visit in the ATE.\n6. Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n7. Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason.\n8. All visits after the 52-week Visit in the ATE will be in-clinic visits that occur at 13-week (96 days ±14 days) intervals. Participants will continue in the ATE until all active participants have completed a minimum of 52 weeks of the ATE; no participant will exceed 78 weeks in the ATE. [9]For participants returning for another visit only.\n1. Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol.\n2. Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE.\n3. Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses.\n[13]For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'Specifies extension clinic and phone visits, assessment timing and windows, ET procedures, and a safety call 28 ± 3 days after the last dose.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "assessments", "safety", "early_termination"]',11,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s013','section','','Regimen G: DNL343','1 INTRODUCTION','DNL343 is a central nervous system (CNS)–penetrant eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (eIF2B) small molecule activator that is being developed by Denali Therapeutics Inc. (Denali) as a potential treatment for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS).','Describes DNL343 as a CNS-penetrant eIF2B activator being developed as a potential ALS treatment.','["background"]',13,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s015','section','','Description of DNL343','1 INTRODUCTION > 1.1 DNL343 Background Information',replace('DNL343 is a selective, orally bioavailable, CNS–penetrant, activator of eIF2B that is being developed by Denali as a potential treatment for ALS. DNL343 is an activator of eIF2B that overcomes phosphorylated eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2a (p-eIF2) mediated inhibition of protein translation. The effect of DNL343 treatment is the inhibition of the integrated stress response (ISR) pathway as observed by reduction in activating transcription factor 4 (ATF4) translation, re-initiation of normal protein synthesis, and decreased stress granule formation in otherwise stressed cells. See the DNL343 Investigator’s Brochure (IB) for detailed information on relevant nonclinical and clinical studies, both completed and ongoing.\nComprehensive nonclinical safety pharmacology and toxicity studies with up to 9 months of daily dosing were conducted with DNL343 to characterize the safety profile, define no-observedadverse-effect levels (NOAELs), establish a safe starting dose for the first-in-human study, support chronic daily dosing in humans, and inform the clinical monitoring strategy in human trials. Clinical signs consistent with possible CNS and/or peripheral nervous system disturbance were observed in rats and monkeys and included tremor, whole body twitching, excessive salivation, and ataxia in monkeys, and tremors, ataxia, and convulsion-like activity in rats at exposures exceeding those to be administered in the clinic (see Section 5.5, Justification for Dose). The toxicity studies indicate that the adrenal, pituitary, and thyroid glands are potential target organs for DNL343 based on microscopic evaluation. The findings showed evidence of recovery following a treatment free period and are considered nonadverse, based on their nature, mild severity, and lack of functional impact. In addition, the CNS and/or peripheral nervous system are considered possible target organ systems based on the nature of the clinical signs in rats and monkeys. Refer to the DNL343 IB for more information.\nThe safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics (PK) of orally administered DNL343 has been evaluated in multiple Phase 1 clinical studies. Three Phase 1 studies have been completed, a Phase 1 study in healthy volunteers (Study DNLI-F-0001), and two Phase 1 clinical pharmacology studies (Study DNLI-F-0002 and Study DNLI-F-0004); a description of the findings can be referenced in the DNL343 IB. Preliminary findings from two ongoing studies (Study DNLI-F-0003 and Study DNLI-F-0005) are summarized below. Collectively, DNL343 doses up to 260 mg once daily (QD) for up to 14 days in healthy participants and up to 200 mg for up to 10 months in participants with ALS have been generally well tolerated and support continued evaluation of DNL343 in participants with ALS.','\n',char(10)),'Describes DNL343''s mechanism and nonclinical safety findings, including nervous-system effects and potential endocrine target organs.','["background"]',15,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s016','section','','Phase 1b Study (DNLI-F-0003): Interim Analysis for Multiple-Dose Safety and Tolerability in Participants with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (Preliminary Data)','1 INTRODUCTION > 1.1 DNL343 Background Information',replace('Study DNLI-F-0003 is an ongoing randomized, placebo controlled, double blind 28-day\nPhase 1b study, followed by an 18-month open-label extension, designed to evaluate the safety, PK, and pharmacodynamics (PD) of DNL343 in participants with ALS. As of the interim analysis (n=20 completing the double-blind 28-day treatment period) cutoff date of 08 September 2022, ALS participants were randomized to DNL343 100 mg (n=7), 200 mg (n=7), or placebo (n=6). DNL343 was generally safe and well-tolerated in participants with ALS. At the interim analysis, there were no deaths, no serious adverse event (SAEs), and no adverse events of special interest (AESIs) reported during the double-blind period. All treatment-emergent adverse event (TEAEs) were Grade 1 or 2. There was one discontinuation for TEAE of Grade 2 rash, considered by the investigator to be related to study drug. There were two dose reductions during the double-blind period for TEAEs: one participant with “brain fog” (Grade 1) reduced from 200 mg to 100 mg, one TEAE of “shivers” (Grade 1) led to dose reduction from 100 mg to placebo. TEAEs in ≥ 2 participants and more common in DNL343 vs placebo were fatigue and headache, all Grade 1 in the DNL343 groups. There were no dose-dependent or clinically meaningful changes in safety laboratory tests, electrocardiogram (ECG), vital signs neurological exam, or Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS).\nAt the Phase 1b interim analysis, PK demonstrated low variability, slow absorption, long halflife, extensive blood-brain barrier penetration with predictable dose-related increases in exposure supporting QD dosing. PK results in ALS participants (Table 1) were generally consistent with results in healthy participants.\nTable 1: Summary of Preliminary PK Parameters in Participants with ALS\nTable columns: Dose (mg QD) | Statistic | Day 1 / Tmax (h) | Day 1 / Cmax (µM) | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h) | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h) | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM) | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h) | Ctrough (µM)\n- Dose (mg QD): 100 (n = 6) | Statistic: Mean | Day 1 / Tmax (h): 3 | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 1.24 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 21.0 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): 2.0 | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 5.54 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 88.7 | Ctrough (µM): 3.39\n- Dose (mg QD): 100 (n = 6) | Statistic: SD | Day 1 / Tmax (h): [2–24] | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 0.357 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 7.44 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): [1–4] | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 1.15 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 18.4 | Ctrough (µM): 1.10\n- Dose (mg QD): 100 (n = 6) | Statistic: %CV | Day 1 / Tmax (h): – | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 28.8 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 35.4 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): – | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 20.7 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 20.7 | Ctrough (µM): 32.4\n- Dose (mg QD): 200 (n = 5) | Statistic: Mean | Day 1 / Tmax (h): 4 | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 1.94 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 34.5 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): 4 | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 11.8 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 194 | Ctrough (µM): 6.99\n- Dose (mg QD): 200 (n = 5) | Statistic: SD | Day 1 / Tmax (h): [2–24] | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 0.650 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 8.98 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): [1–4] | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 2.52 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 55.1 | Ctrough (µM): 2.43\n- Dose (mg QD): 200 (n = 5) | Statistic: %CV | Day 1 / Tmax (h): – | Day 1 / Cmax (µM): 33.5 | Day 1 / AUC24 (µM ∙ h): 26.0 | Day 28 / tmax,ss (h): – | Day 28 / Cmax,ss (µM): 21.4 | Day 28 / AUC24,ss (µM*h): 28.5 | Ctrough (µM): 34.7\nAbbreviations: AUC24, area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to 24 hours; AUC24,ss, area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to 24 hours at steady state; Cmax, maximum concentration; Cmax,ss, maximum concentration at steady-state ; Ctrough, trough concentration; CV, coefficient of variation; SD, standard deviation; tmax, time to reach maximum concentration; tmax,ss, time to reach maximum concentration at steady state. Note: tmax shown as median [range]. Some participants were excluded from the population included in this table due to down-dosing or discontinuation of treatment prior to the PK collection or issues with sample collection (such as thawing of samples) that made the PK unevaluable for a given participant on a given day.\nPhase 1b (IA) PD analysis evaluated inhibition of two ISR biomarkers (ATF4 protein, [ChaC glutathione–specific gamma-glutamylcyclotransferase 1] CHAC1 gene expression) in ex-vivo stimulated peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). DNL343 achieved robust inhibition of ATF4 protein levels (73%–79%) and CHAC1 gene expression (56%–89%) at both the 100 mg and 200 mg QD doses studied in ALS participants.','\n',char(10)),'Summarizes preliminary Phase 1b ALS safety, tolerability, dose-reduction, and PK results supporting once-daily dosing.','["background"]',16,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s017','section','','Phase 1 Study (DNLI-F-0005): A Fixed-Sequence, Drug-Drug Interaction Study Evaluating the Effect of the Cytochrome P450 3A Inhibitor Itraconazole on DNL343 in Healthy Participants','1 INTRODUCTION > 1.1 DNL343 Background Information','Study DNLI-F-0005 is a Phase 1, single-center, open-label, fixed-sequence DDI study designed to evaluate the PK, safety, and tolerability of a single dose of DNL343 in the absence and presence of itraconazole (ITZ), a potent cytochrome P450 (CYP3A) inhibitor. The study conduct is complete and the clinical study report is complete. Twenty-four healthy participants were enrolled and received at least one dose of study drug (DNL343 or ITZ). No deaths, other SAEs, or AESIs were reported. All reported TEAEs were Grade 1 or 2, and no TEAEs were considered to be related to DNL343 by the investigator. There were no clinically meaningful trends in ECG results, vital sign measurements, or safety laboratory test results, and DNL343 was generally safe and well tolerated in the presence and absence of itraconazole. PK data was obtained after administration of DNL343 (40 mg) in the presence and absence of multiple doses of ITZ (200 mg). Coadministration of DNL343 with ITZ resulted in a slight increase in the overall exposure of DNL343 (area under the concentration-time curve from time zero to infinity [AUC∞] increased 13%) and a slight decrease in peak exposure (maximum concentration [Cmax] decreased 14%). The results indicate that DNL343 is not a sensitive substrate of CYP3A4 and that DNL343 can be coadministered with strong, moderate, or weak inhibitors of CYP3A4. Additionally, these preliminary results show a minor contribution of CYP3A4 to the metabolism of DNL343, indicating that coadministration with moderate CYP3A4 inducers are unlikely to affect the PK of DNL343 and can be coadministered with DNL343 freely in this trial.','Explains that itraconazole interaction results support allowing strong, moderate, or weak CYP3A4 inhibitors and moderate CYP3A4 inducers with DNL343 in this trial.','["concomitant_medications"]',17,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s018','section','','Phase 1 Study (DNLI-F-0007): A Drug-Drug Interaction Study of the Effect of DNL343 on Midazolam Pharmacokinetics in Healthy Participants (Preliminary Data)','1 INTRODUCTION > 1.1 DNL343 Background Information','Study DNLI-F-0007 is a Phase 1, single-center, open-label, fixed-sequence DDI study to investigate the effect of DNL343 on the PK of oral MDZ (a sensitive index substrate of CYP3A). The study conduct is complete and the clinical study report is in progress. Based on preliminary study results, 16 healthy participants were enrolled and received at least one dose of study drug (DNL343 or MDZ). Preliminary review of safety and tolerability data demonstrate no deaths, or other SAEs. One participant had a laboratory abnormality meeting the AESI criteria (AST >= 3xULN). All TEAEs were Grade 1 and TEAEs occurring in more than one participant included abnormal dreams in 4 (25%) participants and fatigue in 3 (18.8%) participants. There were no clinically meaningful trends in ECG results, vital sign measurements, or safety laboratory test results. Preliminary PK data was obtained after administration of MDZ (1 mg) in the presence and absence of DNL343 (200 mg QD). Midazolam AUC∞ and Cmax were reduced by 60% and 55% respectively in the presence of DNL343. The preliminary results indicate that DNL343 is moderate CYP3A inducer; medications that are CYP3A substrates may have increased hepatic clearance and decreased exposure when taken with DNL343.','Summarizes preliminary midazolam interaction results indicating that DNL343 moderately induces CYP3A and may reduce exposure to CYP3A substrates.','["background"]',18,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s025','section','','Placebo-Controlled Period','3 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN','A participant is considered to have completed the placebo-controlled period of the Regimen when they have completed Week 24 or the Early Termination (ET) Visit.','Defines completion of the placebo-controlled period as completion of the Week 24 visit or the Early Termination visit.','["visit_schedule", "early_termination"]',25,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s026','section','','Active Treatment Extension Period','3 REGIMEN-SPECIFIC APPENDIX DESIGN',replace('Participants are considered to have completed their participation in the ATE period of\nRegimen G if planned ATE period visits, including the last visit, ET Visit, or the last scheduled procedure shown in the Schedule of Activities (SOA), have been completed.','\n',char(10)),'Defines ATE participation completion as completion of planned visits, including the last visit, ET Visit, or last scheduled SOA procedure.','["extension_period", "visit_schedule", "early_termination"]',26,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s049','section','','Modifications to Regimen Schedule','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE',replace('Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, and Week 16) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nIn addition to the procedures in the Master Protocol that should be conducted during the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. CNS-BFS (Weeks 8 and 16 only)\n2. ROADS (Week 16 and 24 only)\n3. PGI-C (Week 16 and 24 only)\n4. PGI-S (Week 16 and 24 only)\nDetails on collection of the CNS-BFS, ROADS, PGI-C, and PGI-S and dispensing of study drug during remote visits are described in the MOP.','\n',char(10)),'Use remote visits at designated weeks only for specified safety or medical reasons, complete applicable remote assessments, and follow the MOP for missed-procedure documentation and drug dispensing.','["visit_schedule", "assessments", "safety", "drug_supply", "records_reporting"]',47,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s060','section','','Modifications to Regimen Schedule','6 REGIMEN SCHEDULE',replace('Designated visits in the SOA (i.e., Week 4, Week 8, Week 16, Week 28, Week 40 in ATE) may be conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services instead of in-person if needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or other medically necessary reason. If a planned in-clinic visit is conducted via telemedicine (or phone if telemedicine is not available) with remote services, only selected procedures will be performed. Instructions on how to document missed procedures are included in the MOP.\nDuring the phone or telemedicine and remote visits, the following regimen-specific procedures should be completed:\n1. ALSFRS-R (Week 16, 20, 24, and 78/ET)\n2. CNS-BFS (Week 8, 16, 28, 40, 52 only)\n3. ROADS (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n4. PGI-C (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)\n5. PGI-S (Week 16, Week 28 and Week 52/ET)','\n',char(10)),'Use telemedicine, or telephone if unavailable, with remote services for designated visits only when needed for participant safety; complete the listed visit-specific assessments and follow MOP instructions for documenting missed procedures.','["visit_schedule", "extension_period", "safety", "assessments", "records_reporting"]',58,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s087','section','','APPENDIX I: THE ALSAQ-40','','','Identifies the appendix containing the ALSAQ-40 questionnaire form.','["admin"]',85,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s088','section','','APPENDIX II: THE CENTER FOR NEUROLOGIC STUDY BULBAR FUNCTION SCALE (CNS-BFS)','','','Identifies the appendix containing the Center for Neurologic Study Bulbar Function Scale questionnaire form.','["admin"]',86,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s089','section','','APPENDIX III: CONTRACEPTIVE GUIDANCE FOR FEMALE PARTICIPANTS OF CHILDBEARING POTENTIAL','',replace('Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom)\n- • Intrauterine device (IUD)\n- • Intrauterine hormone-releasing system (IUS) b\n- • Bilateral tubal ligation c\n- • Azoospermic male partner of woman of childbearing potential participantd\n- Alternative Highly Effective Method\n- • Abstinence defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire duration of the study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ is considered not reliable and would not be acceptablee\nNotes: Male condom and female condom should not be used together (due to risk of failure with friction).\n1. Contraceptive use by participants should be consistent with local regulations regarding the use of contraceptive methods for those participating in clinical studies.\n2. Failure rate of < 1% per year when used consistently and correctly. Typical use failure rates differ from those when used consistently and correctly. c Essure fallopian tube coil placement is not accepted, as the permanent contraception failure rate is likely greater than 1%.\n1. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential and the absence of sperm has been confirmed. If not, an additional highly effective method of contraception should be used. Spermatogenesis cycle is approximately 90 days.\nNote: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview.\n2. Periodic abstinence (calendar, symptothermal, post ovulation methods), withdrawal (coitus interruptus),\nspermicides only, and lactational amenorrhea method are NOT acceptable methods of contraception.','\n',char(10)),'Ensure female participants of childbearing potential use an accepted highly effective method with a male barrier or continuous abstinence through 90 days after the last dose; reject listed unreliable methods and confirm/document azoospermia when relied upon.','["contraception_pregnancy", "records_reporting"]',87,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s090','section','','APPENDIX III: CONTRACEPTIVE GUIDANCE FOR MALE PARTICIPANTS WITH FEMALE PARTNERS OF CHILDBEARING POTENTIAL','',replace('Table: Highly Effective Methods a to be used in combination with a male barrier method (e.g. condom)\n- • Intrauterine device (IUD)\n- • Intrauterine hormone-releasing system (IUS) b\n- • Bilateral tubal ligation c\n- • Implantable progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation\n- • Combined (estrogen- and progestogen-containing) hormonal contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation − oral − intravaginal − transdermal − injectable\n- • Progestogen-only hormone contraception associated with inhibition of ovulation) − Oral − injectable\n- • Azoospermic male participantd\n- Alternative Highly Effective Method\n- • Abstinence defined as refraining from heterosexual intercourse during the entire duration of the study and for 90 days after last dose of study drug. ‘Periodic abstinence’ is considered not reliable and would not be acceptablee.\nNotes: Male condom and female condom should not be used together (due to risk of failure with friction).\n1. Contraceptive use by participants should be consistent with local regulations regarding the use of contraceptive methods for those participating in clinical studies.\n2. Failure rate of < 1% per year when used consistently and correctly. Typical use failure rates differ from those when used consistently and correctly.\n3. Essure fallopian tube coil placement is not accepted, as the permanent contraception failure rate is likely greater than 1%.\n4. Azoospermia is a highly effective contraceptive method provided that the partner is the sole sexual partner of the woman of childbearing potential and the absence of sperm has been confirmed. If not, an additional highly effective method of contraception should be used.\nSpermatogenesis cycle is approximately 90 days.\nNote: documentation of azoospermia for a male participant can come from the site personnel’s review of the participant’s medical records, medical examination, or medical history interview.\n5. Periodic abstinence (calendar, symptothermal, post ovulation methods), withdrawal (coitus interruptus), spermicides only, and lactational amenorrhea method are NOT acceptable methods of contraception.','\n',char(10)),'Ensure male participants with female partners of childbearing potential follow the listed highly effective contraception plus male-barrier requirements or continuous abstinence through 90 days after the last dose, with confirmed azoospermia when relied upon.','["contraception_pregnancy"]',88,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s091','section','','APPENDIX IV: CONCOMITANT MEDICATION RESTRICTIONS','','','Introduces the appendix specifying concomitant medication restrictions.','["concomitant_medications"]',89,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s092','section','','Excluded Medications','APPENDIX IV: CONCOMITANT MEDICATION RESTRICTIONS','The medications indicated below are excluded during the study (unless otherwise specified) (See Section 5.9 for prohibited medications). The lists below are not exhaustive; the use of any concomitant medication is at the discretion of the investigator who knows the participant’s medical history.','Do not use excluded medications during the study unless otherwise specified; investigators must assess other concomitant medications using the participant’s history because the lists are not exhaustive.','["concomitant_medications"]',90,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s093','section','','Strong CYP3A4/5 Inducers','APPENDIX IV: CONCOMITANT MEDICATION RESTRICTIONS',replace('DNL343 is a CYP3A4/5 substrate based on in vitro data; use of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers may decrease DNL343 plasma concentrations.\nThe medications in Table 4 are examples of strong CYP3A4/5 inducers and are excluded during the study. If needed, it is recommended that a tertiary source, such as the Drug Interactions Flockhart Table (found at https://drug-interactions.medicine.iu.edu/MainTable.aspx), be used for more information regarding other medications.\nTable 4: Strong CYP3A4/5 Inducers with the Drug Half-Life and Washout Periods\nTable columns: Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-life | Recommended Washout\n- Generic Drug Name: apalutamide | Drug Half-life: 4.2 d | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: carbamazepine | Drug Half-life: 15 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: phenytoin | Drug Half-life: 24 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: rifampin | Drug Half-life: 3.5 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: St. John’s wort | Drug Half-life: 36 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\n- Generic Drug Name: bosentan | Drug Half-life: 5 h | Recommended Washout: 2 weeks\nAbbreviations: CYP, cytochrome P450.\nSource: US Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Drug Development and Drug Interactions:\nTable of Substrates, Inhibitors and Inducers. FDA website. Published 2020. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-interactions-labeling/drug-development-and-drug-interactionstable-substrates-inhibitors-and-inducers.','\n',char(10)),'Exclude strong CYP3A4/5 inducers during the study; the listed examples have recommended two-week washouts, and additional interaction references may be consulted.','["concomitant_medications"]',91,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s094','section','','Sensitive CYP3A4/5 Substrates','APPENDIX IV: CONCOMITANT MEDICATION RESTRICTIONS',replace('The clinical DDI study was conducted in healthy participants with midazolam, a sensitive substrate of CYP3A (DNLI-F-0007). The preliminary data showed DNL343 is a moderate inducer of CYP3A4 metabolism, indicating that sensitive CYP3A substrates may have reductions in exposure when coadministered with DNL343 which may lead to reduction in efficacy if the therapeutic index is narrow.\nThe medications in Table 5 are examples of sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates. The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. Medications that are sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates with a wide therapeutic index are permitted; participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of loss of efficacy and/or may require dose increases based on the respective medication’s prescribing information.\nThe medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 5 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication.\nTable 5: Sensitive CYP3A4/5 Substrates\nTable columns: Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-Life | Recommended Washout\n- Generic Drug Name: buspirone | Drug Half-Life: 5.0 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: darunavir | Drug Half-Life: 3–5 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: dronedarone | Drug Half-Life: 1.6 ± 0.2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: eplerenone | Drug Half-Life: 3–6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: felodipine | Drug Half-Life: 4.8 min, 1.5 h, and 9.1 h (IV); 11–16 h (IR tablet) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: indinavir | Drug Half-Life: 1.8 ± 0.4 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: lurasidone | Drug Half-Life: 18 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: maraviroc | Drug Half-Life: 13.2 (10.3–8.3) h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: nisoldipine | Drug Half-Life: 3.2 h (young), 6.5 h (elderly) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: quetiapine | Drug Half-Life: 6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: Quinine | Drug Half-Life: 11 ± 2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: saquinavir | Drug Half-Life: 7–12 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: sildenafil (when used for PAH) | Drug Half-Life: 2.4 ± 1.0 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: sirolimus | Drug Half-Life: 62.3 ± 16.2 h | Recommended Washout: 13 days\n- Generic Drug Name: tacrolimus | Drug Half-Life: 31.9 ± 10.5 h (based on radioactivity); 48.4 ± 12.3 h (based on tacrolimus concentrations) | Recommended Washout: 10 days\n- Generic Drug Name: ticagrelor | Drug Half-Life: 6.8 h (IV); 8.1 h (oral) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Generic Drug Name: tipranavir | Drug Half-Life: 4.8 h (healthy volunteers); 5.5 h (female patients); 6.0 h (male patients) | Recommended Washout: 7 days\nAbbreviations: CYP, cytochrome P450; IM, intramuscular; IR, immediate release; IV, intravenous; PAH, pulmonary arterial hypertension.\nNote: Sensitive substrates are drugs that demonstrate an increase in area under the concentration-time curve of ≥ 5-fold with strong inhibitors of a given metabolic pathway in clinical drug-drug interaction studies.\nSource: US Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Drug Development and Drug Interactions: Table of Substrates, Inhibitors and Inducers. FDA website. Published 2020. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/druginteractions-labeling/drug-development-and-drug-interactions-table-substrates-inhibitors-and-inducers.','\n',char(10)),'Exclude narrow-therapeutic-index sensitive CYP3A4/5 substrates; monitor permitted wide-index substrates for loss of efficacy and consider dose increases per prescribing information, with listed seven-day washout recommendations.','["concomitant_medications", "safety", "dosing"]',92,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s095','section','','Substrates of Major Transporters','APPENDIX IV: CONCOMITANT MEDICATION RESTRICTIONS > Sensitive CYP3A4/5 Substrates',replace('DNL343 has a risk of inhibition of BCRP and OAT3 transporters at clinically relevant concentrations based on preliminary in vitro transporter inhibition studies. Medications that are substrates for these transporters may have increased exposure when taken with DNL343.\nThe medications in Table 6 are examples of substrates of BCRP or OAT3. The list below is not exhaustive and if any of these medications are considered to have a narrow therapeutic index, they should be excluded during the study. Medications that are considered to have a wide therapeutic index are permitted, and participants taking them are to be monitored for signs of AEs noted in the respective medication’s prescribing information.\nThe medical monitor may be consulted to assess whether any of the medications listed in Table 6 would be considered to have narrow therapeutic index for the individual participant taking the medication.\nTable 6: Substrates of BCRP or OAT3 Transporters\nTable columns: Substrate | Generic Drug Name | Drug Half-Life | Recommended Washout\n- Substrate: BCRP Substrates | Generic Drug Name: rosuvastatin | Drug Half-Life: 7.48 ± 1.65 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: BCRP Substrates | Generic Drug Name: sulfasalazine | Drug Half-Life: 22.45 ± 6.366 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: adefovir | Drug Half-Life: 7.48 ± 1.65 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: cefaclor | Drug Half-Life: 11 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: ceftizoxime | Drug Half-Life: 1.8 ± 0.8 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: famotidine | Drug Half-Life: 0.8 ± 0.2 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: furosemide | Drug Half-Life: 20 ± 6 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: methotrexate | Drug Half-Life: 15 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: oseltamivir carboxylate | Drug Half-Life: 6 to 10 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\n- Substrate: OAT3 Substrates | Generic Drug Name: penicillin G | Drug Half-Life: 7.6 ± 3.4 h | Recommended Washout: 7 days\nAbbreviations: BCRP, breast cancer resistance protein; OAT3, organic anion transporter 3Source: US Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Drug development and drug interactions: table of substrates, inhibitors and inducers. FDA website. Published 2020. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-interactionslabeling/drug-development-and-drug-interactions-table-substrates-inhibitors-and-inducers.','\n',char(10)),'Exclude narrow-therapeutic-index BCRP/OAT3 substrates; monitor permitted wide-index substrates for adverse effects, with listed seven-day washout recommendations and optional medical-monitor consultation.','["concomitant_medications", "safety"]',93,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:s096','section','','REFERENCES','',replace('Busnar J and Targum S. The Clinical Global Impression Scale. Psychiatry (Edgmont). 2007 Jul; 4(7): 28–37\nCheng W, Wang S, Mestre AA, et al. C9ORF72 GGGGCC repeat-associated non-AUG translation is upregulated by stress through eIF2α phosphorylation. Nat Commun. 2018;9(1):51.\nFournier CN, Bedlack R, Quinn C, et al. Development and validation of the Rasch-built overall amyotrophic lateral sclerosis disability scale (ROADS). JAMA Neurol. 2020;77:480-488.\nHetz C, Saxena S. ER stress and the unfolded protein response in neurodegeneration. NatRev Neurol. 2017;13(8):477–91.\nHetz C, Thielen P, Matus S, et al. XBP-1 deficiency in the nervous system protects against amyotrophic lateral sclerosis by increasing autophagy. Genes Dev. 2009;23(19):2294–306.\nHurst H, Bolton J. Assessing the clinical significance of change scores recorded on subjective outcome measures. J Manipulative Physiol Ther. 2004;27:26-35\nIlieva EV, Ayala V, Jové M, et al.Oxidative and endoplasmic reticulum stress interplay in sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Brain. 2007;130(Pt 12):3111–23.\nKim HJ, Raphael AR, LaDow ES, et al. Therapeutic modulation of eIF2α phosphorylation rescues TDP-43 toxicity in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis disease models. Nat Genet. 2013;46(2):152–60.\nLarhammar M, Huntwork-Rodriguez S, Jiang Z, et al. Dual leucine zipper kinase-dependent PERK activation contributes to neuronal degeneration following insult. Elife. 2017;6: e20725.\nMoreno JA, Halliday M, Molloy C, et al. Oral treatment targeting the unfolded protein response prevents neurodegeneration and clinical disease in prion-infected mice. Sci Transl Med. 2013;5(206):206ra138.\nNagata T, Ilieva H, Murakami T, et al.Increased ER stress during motor neuron degeneration in a transgenic mouse model of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. NeurolRes. 2007;29(8):767–71.\nPakos-Zebrucka K, Koryga I, Mnich K, et al. The integrated stress response. EMBO Rep. 2016;17(10):1374–95.\nSonobe Y, Ghadge G, MasakiK, et al. Translation of dipeptide repeat proteins from the\nC9ORF72 expanded repeat is associated with cellular stress. Neurobiol Dis. 2018;116:155–65.\nVanhoutte EK, Hermans MC, Faber CG, et al. Rasch-ionale for neurologists. J Peripher Nerv Syst. 2015;20(3):260-268.\nViktrup L, et al., Construct validation of patient global impression of severity (PGI-S) and improvement (PGI-I) questionnaires in the treatment of men with lower urinary tract symptoms secondary to benign prostatic hypertrophy. BMC Urol. 2012;12:30.\nWestergard T, McAvoy K, Russell K, et al. Repeat-associated non-AUG translation in C9orf72ALS/FTD is driven by neuronal excitation and stress. EMBO Mol Med. 2019;11(2): e9423.\nWong YL, LeBon L, Basso AM, et al. eIF2B activator prevents neurological defects caused by a chronic integrated stress response. Elife. 2019;8:e42940.','\n',char(10)),'Lists literature references supporting the protocol’s scientific rationale and assessment instruments.','["background"]',94,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-baseline','visit','','Baseline','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Baseline (Clinic), window Day 0. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- Muscle Strength Assessment (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- ALSAQ-40 (Regimen)\n- CNS-BFS (Regimen)\n- ROADS (Regimen)\n- PGI-S 17 (Regimen)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] (Regimen)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection (Master)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection (Master)\n- DNA Collection [7](optional) (Master)\n- CSF Collection [16] (optional) (Regimen)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Randomization within the Regimen (Master)\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug (Master) [8]\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[1] Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible.\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[7] The DNA sample can be collected after the Baseline Visit if a baseline sample is not obtained or the sample is not usable.\n[8] Administer the first dose of study drug only after the Baseline Visit procedures are completed.\n[14] Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments.\n[16] CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Baseline (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1002,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-follow-up-safety-call','visit','','Follow-Up Safety Call','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Follow-Up Safety Call (Phone), window 28 days after last dose ± 3 days. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[10] Participants will only have a Follow-Up Safety Call at this time if they terminate early from the study. Otherwise, participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Follow-Up Safety Call (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1010,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-master-protocol-screening','visit','','Master Protocol Screening','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Master Protocol Screening (Clinic), window –42 to –1 Days. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Written Informed Consent [2] (Master)\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review (Master)\n- ALS & Medical History (Master)\n- Demographics (Master)\n- Physical Examination (Master)\n- Neurological Exam (Master)\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Assignment to the Regimen (Master)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[1] Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible.\n[2] During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF.\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[13] Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0).','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Master Protocol Screening (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1000,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-regimen-specific-screening','visit','','Regimen Specific Screening','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Regimen Specific Screening (Clinic), window –41 to 0 Day. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Written Informed Consent [2] (Master)\n- Inclusion/Exclusion Review (Master) [3]\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[1] Master Protocol Screening procedures must be completed within 42 days to 1 day prior to the Baseline Visit. The Regimen-Specific Screening visit and Baseline Visit should be combined, if possible.\n[2] During the Master Protocol Screening Visit, participants will be consented via the Platform Trial ICF. After a participant is randomly assigned to a regimen, participants will be consented a second time via the regimen-specific ICF.\n[3] At the Regimen-Specific Screening Visit, participants will have regimen-specific eligibility criteria assessed.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[13] Master Protocol Screening and Regimen-Specific Screening Visit windows are relative to Baseline (Day 0).\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Regimen Specific Screening (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1001,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-12','visit','','Week 12','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 12 (Phone), window Day 84 ± 3. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master) [20]\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[20] Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 12 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1006,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-16','visit','','Week 16','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 16 (Clinic), window Day 112 ± 7. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- Muscle Strength Assessment (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- ALSAQ-40 (Regimen)\n- CNS-BFS (Regimen)\n- ROADS (Regimen)\n- PGI-C 17 (Regimen)\n- PGI-S 17 (Regimen)\n- CGI-C 18 (Regimen)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Coagulation Laboratory Tests (PT, PTT, INR) (Master)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection (Master)\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] (Regimen) (post)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[12] Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n[15] For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 16 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1007,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-2','visit','','Week 2','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 2 (Phone), window Day 14 ± 3. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master) [20]\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[20] Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 2 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1003,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-20','visit','','Week 20','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 20 (Phone), window Day 140 ± 3. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master) [20]\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[20] Drug accountability will not be done at phone visits. A drug compliance check in should be held during phone visits to ensure participant is taking drug per dose regimen and to note any report of missed doses.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 20 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1008,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-24-or-et-visit','visit','','Week 24 or ET Visit','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 24 or ET Visit (Clinic), window Day 168 ± 7. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- Muscle Strength Assessment (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- ALSAQ-40 (Regimen)\n- CNS-BFS (Regimen)\n- ROADS (Regimen)\n- PGI-C 17 (Regimen)\n- PGI-S 17 (Regimen)\n- CGI-C 18 (Regimen)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Endocrinology Safety Laboratory Tests (blood ACTH, Cortisol, fT4) [14] (Regimen) (for all participants prior to ATE)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection (Master)\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] (Regimen) (pre, post)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection (Master)\n- CSF Collection [16] (optional) (Regimen)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug (Master) [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\n- Exit Questionnaire (Master)\n- Vital Status Determination [11] (Master)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[9] Study drug will only be dispensed at this visit if the participant continues in the ATE.\n[11] Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each randomized participant at the end of the placebocontrolled portion of their follow-up (generally the Week 24 Visit, as indicated). If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol.\n[14] Endocrinology safety laboratory tests (blood ACTH, cortisol, fT4) may be performed additionally during the study as clinically indicated based on signs and symptoms. Collect blood sample for measurement of ACTH/cortisol at approximately the same time in the morning for each endocrinology laboratory assessment, if possible. It is not considered a protocol deviation if not taken at the same time in the morning as previous endocrinology laboratory assessments.\n[15] For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment.\n[16] CSF collection is scheduled to occur for this Regimen during the placebo-controlled period. CSF collection may be performed on a separate date from the other assessments scheduled for the visits. For the Baseline visit, CSF may be collected at any time within one week prior to administration of the first dose of study medication after Regimen G eligibility is confirmed. Post treatment CSF collection will only be collected for participants with baseline CSF collected. For the Week 24 Visit, CSF may be collected at any time within the allowed visit window of the Week 24 Visit as defined in the protocol. For participants continuing into ATE, CSF collection must occur prior to taking the first dose of study medication from the ATE period. For the early termination (ET) visit, CSF collection is to be completed in participants with a Baseline CSF collection and only if the ET visit occurs at or after the Week 12 visit.\n[19] The assessments listed must be completed for Early Termination Visit that occur prior to Week 24 in the placebo-controlled treatment period.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 24 or ET Visit (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1009,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-4','visit','','Week 4','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 4 (Clinic), window Day 28 ± 7. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] (Regimen) (pre, post)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[12] Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n[15] For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 4 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1004,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v1-week-8','visit','','Week 8','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD',replace('Visit checklist: Week 8 (Clinic), window Day 56 ± 7. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [4] (Master)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- Muscle Strength Assessment (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- CNS-BFS (Regimen)\n- 12-Lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [5] (Master)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection (Master)\n- Pharmacokinetic Blood Collection [15] (Regimen) (post)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [6] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Administer/Dispense Study Drug (Master)\n- Study Drug Accountability/Compliance (Master)\n- Review contraception method [21] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[4] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[5] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[6] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs that require an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained as needed.\n[12] Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n[15] For visits with a predose PK sample (Week 4 and Week 24), these visits should be scheduled for the morning and participants should take their dose in the clinic at approximately the same time in the morning as usual. The predose sample should be collected prior to dosing (ie, any time 0 to 2 hours before the dose) and approximately 24 hours after the most recent dose. The postdose sample can be collected anytime between 1 to 8 hours postdose. For the Week 4 Visit, if it is not possible to collect a predose PK sample, 2 postdose samples should be collected (at least 1 hour apart). The Week 24 Visit is the first day of the ATE and, therefore, the ATE dose would be provided in the clinic with a predose and postdose PK sample obtained. Additional unscheduled PK samples should be collected as needed as part of assessment for TEAEs leading to dose reduction or dose interruption or for severe TEAEs requiring an unscheduled visit for assessment.\n[21] For female participants of childbearing potential and for men with a female partner of childbearing potential, review and document current contraceptive method(s) and ensure they meet study requirements.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Week 8 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – PLACEBO-CONTROLLED TREATMENT PERIOD), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1005,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v2-all-visits-occurring-after-week-52-every','visit','','Study Week in ATE: All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Week 89 and Week 102','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION',replace('Visit checklist: Study Week in ATE: All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Week 89 and Week 102 (Clinic), window 84 days (± 14 days) from last visit after 52 weeks. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Vital Signs [1] (Master)\n- 12-lead ECG (Regimen)\n- Slow Vital Capacity (Master)\n- ALSFRS-R (Master)\n- CNS-BFS (Regimen)\n- Clinical Safety Laboratory Tests [2] (Master)\n- Biomarker Blood Collection (Master)\n- Biomarker Urine Collection (Master)\n- Concomitant Medication Review (Master)\n- Adverse Event Review [3] (Master)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (Master)\n- Administer/ Dispense Study Drug (Master) [9]\n- Study Drug Accountability/ Compliance (Master)\n- Review contraception method [13] (Regimen)\n- Vital Status Determination [10] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[1] Vital signs include weight, systolic and diastolic pressure, respiratory rate, heart rate, and temperature. Height will be measured in cm at the Master Protocol Screening Visit only.\n[2] Clinical safety laboratory tests include hematology (CBC with differential), complete chemistry panel, thyroid function, and urinalysis. Serum pregnancy testing will occur in participants of childbearing potential at the Master Protocol Screening Visit and as necessary during the study. Urine pregnancy testing will occur at every in clinic visit for women of childbearing potential.\n[3] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained.\n[7] Visit may be conducted via phone or telemedicine with remote services instead of in-person if this is needed to protect the safety of the participant due to a pandemic, disability, or a medically necessary reason.\n[9] Vital status, defined as a determination of date of death or PAV status or date last known alive, will be determined for each participant at the end of the ATE portion of their follow-up. If at that time the participant is alive, his or her vital status should be determined again at the time of the last participant, last visit (LPLV) of the Regimen ATE. We may also ascertain vital status (death or date last known alive only) at later time points by using publicly available data sources as described in Section 8.15 of the Master Protocol.\n[10] Not required for early terminations that occur after Week 52 in the ATE.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Study Week in ATE: All visits occurring after week 52 (every 13weeks) = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Week 89 and Week 102 (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1021,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
INSERT INTO guide VALUES('NCT05842941','regimen_g','regimen_g:v2-follow-up-safety-call','visit','','Study Week in ATE: Follow-Up Safety Call = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Follow-Up Safety Call','SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION',replace('Visit checklist: Study Week in ATE: Follow-Up Safety Call = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Follow-Up Safety Call (Phone), window 28 days after last dose ± 3 days. From: SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION.\nRequired at this visit:\n- Adverse Event Review [3] (Master)\n- Review contraception method [13] (Regimen)\nFootnotes that apply:\n[3] Adverse events that occur after signing the consent form will be recorded. If a TEAE is reported that results in dose modification or dose interruption or for a severe TEAE that requires an in-clinic evaluation, an unscheduled PK sample should be obtained.\n[4] All participants will have a Follow-Up Safety Call (as described in the body of this RSA) after their last dose of study drug during the ATE period. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.\n[6] Participants who withdraw consent or early terminate during the ATE will be asked to complete an Early Termination Visit and Follow-Up Safety Call as described in the body of this RSA. Refer to Section 6.9 for additional details regarding the process for early terminations.','\n',char(10)),'what must happen at Study Week in ATE: Follow-Up Safety Call = Study Week from Baseline Visit: Follow-Up Safety Call (SCHEDULE OF ACTIVITIES – ACTIVE TREATMENT EXTENSION), its window and footnotes','["visit_schedule", "assessments"]',1022,'2.0 (18 Dec 2023) sha256:2308a883adb7');
